var WK_INDONESIA = [
{"id":"indonesia-rendang","name":"Rendang","nameAlt":"Slow-fried Sumatran beef in coconut","aliases":["Rendang","Beef Rendang","Rendang Daging","Rendang Padang","Randang","Kalio","Indonesian Dry Beef Curry"],"course":"main","type":["main","slow-cooked","curry","beef","spiced","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","celebration","make-ahead","guests"],"ingredients":"180g beef chuck · 160ml coconut milk · 60g shallots · 20g red chilli · 15g garlic · 12g galangal · 12g lemongrass · 10g fresh ginger · 15g desiccated coconut · 8g macadamia nuts · 5g ground turmeric · 5g tamarind paste · 4g salt · 3g cinnamon","method":"Start with the cut, because rendang is one of the few dishes where the cheap piece is not a compromise but the only correct choice. Use the 180g of beef chuck and do not trim it clean. Chuck is shoulder — it is threaded with collagen and seams of fat, and over the next three hours that collagen dissolves into gelatine and gives the meat the soft, slightly sticky give that rendang is famous for. A lean piece like rump or fillet has nothing to dissolve; it simply dries into fibres and no amount of time will save it. Cut the chuck across the grain into chunks about 3cm square. Across the grain matters: it shortens the muscle fibres so each piece falls apart in the mouth rather than shredding into strings.\n\nNow the bumbu, which is the paste that does all the work. Peel and roughly chop the 60g of shallots, the 15g of garlic, the 12g of galangal and the 10g of fresh ginger. Trim the 12g of lemongrass down to the pale bottom third and slice it thinly across — lemongrass is woody and a blender will not break it down, so pre-slicing it thinly is what stops you finding splinters in the finished dish. Add the 20g of red chilli, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 5g of ground turmeric and the 3g of cinnamon. Blitz all of it to a genuine paste in a small processor, and here is the important part: LOOSEN IT WITH A LITTLE OF THE COCONUT MILK, NOT WITH WATER. Take about 30ml off the 160ml and use that. Water dilutes the dish and then has to be boiled away again, which wastes an hour; coconut milk is going into the pot anyway. Blitz until there is no grit left between your fingers when you rub a little between them. Grit in the paste is grit in the finished rendang, and it never disappears with cooking.\n\nThe macadamias are standing in for candlenuts, and they are doing a real job rather than a decorative one. Candlenut is used across Indonesia purely as a thickener and an emulsifier — it is faintly bitter and mildly toxic raw, and nobody eats it for flavour. What it provides is fat and starch that hold the paste together and give the sauce body. Macadamia is the standard substitute worldwide because the fat content is close, and in South Africa it happens to be the local nut rather than the imported one.\n\nMake the kerisik next and do not skip it, because it is the difference between a good coconut stew and rendang. Put the 15g of desiccated coconut in a dry frying pan, no oil, over medium-low heat, and stir it more or less continuously for 5 to 8 minutes. It will go from white to pale cream to biscuit to a deep nutty brown, and it moves fast at the end — the last thirty seconds are where it burns, so pull it off the heat while it is still the colour of dark toast and not the colour of coffee. Tip it straight out of the pan onto a cold plate; a hot pan carries on cooking it. Then grind it, in a mortar or a spice grinder, until the oil comes out and it clumps into a coarse, damp, fragrant paste rather than a powder. That toasted coconut paste is a thickener, a sweetener and a smoky bottom note all at once, and it is why real rendang tastes of something that has been near a fire.\n\nNow the step that goes against everything else you know about braising: DO NOT BROWN THE MEAT. There is no searing in rendang, and no frying of the paste first either. Put the raw beef, the bumbu, the remaining 130ml of coconut milk and the 5g of tamarind paste into a wide heavy pan — wide, not deep, because you want surface area for evaporation — and stir it into one pale, slack, uniform mass while it is still cold. Then bring it up to a bare simmer. The browning in this dish happens at the very end, in the fat that separates out, and it happens to the meat and the paste together. Sear it first and you get two separate flavours instead of one.\n\nSimmer it uncovered, on the lowest heat that keeps a lazy bubble breaking the surface, for about two hours. Stir every fifteen or twenty minutes, and scrape the bottom of the pan properly each time — coconut solids and the paste both settle and catch, and a single scorched patch flavours the entire pot bitter. Do not cover it. A lid returns every drop of steam to the pan and you would be simmering forever. Over these two hours the liquid drops from a soupy white to a thick golden gravy and the beef goes from firm to just yielding.\n\nAround the two-hour mark you have a dish with its own name. When the sauce is a rich golden-brown gravy that still coats a spoon and pours, that is KALIO — the wet, half-way rendang eaten all over West Sumatra in its own right, and if you stop here, salt it and serve it, nobody has made a mistake. But rendang proper is further on.\n\nStir in the kerisik and the 4g of salt now, and start paying attention, because the next thirty minutes contain the moment the dish changes character. Keep the heat low and stir more often, then almost constantly. The gravy thickens, then goes glossy, then starts to look slightly broken and grainy — and then a clear, faintly golden oil comes out of it and pools around the meat. THIS IS PECAH MINYAK, THE BREAK, and it is the whole point. Coconut milk is an emulsion of coconut oil held in water by protein. As the last of the water leaves, the emulsion cannot hold and the oil comes out on its own. Nothing is wrong. What has happened is that your pot has quietly stopped being a simmer and become a shallow fry, and the beef is now frying in pure coconut oil coated in its own spice paste.\n\nFrom the break, give it another 20 to 30 minutes and DO NOT STOP STIRRING for more than a few seconds at a time, because there is no water left to protect anything from the base of the pan. The colour deepens from red-brown to dark mahogany, almost black at the edges, and the paste stops being a sauce and becomes a dry crust clinging to each piece. This is where the flavour you cannot get any other way is made — the sugars in the shallots and the coconut are browning in hot oil, not stewing in water. It is done when you can drag a spoon across the base and the track stays open for a moment before the oil creeps back, and when the meat looks almost dry but glistens.\n\nTaste it and adjust the salt at the very end, never earlier. This dish reduces to somewhere near a fifth of its starting volume, so a pot salted correctly at the beginning is inedible by the end. Let it stand at least twenty minutes off the heat before eating. And if you can leave it until tomorrow, do — the spice paste keeps moving into the meat overnight, and rendang made today and eaten tomorrow is a noticeably better dish than rendang eaten hot from the pan.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Simmer 2 hr · Fry down 30 min","kcal":"~610 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 39g · Carbs 14g · Fat 44g · Sodium ~880mg","storage":"Rendang is the rare dish that is better on day two and day three than it is on day one, and it is worth knowing why: the paste is still migrating into the meat, and the fat sets and re-melts carrying flavour with it. Cool it uncovered to room temperature, then fridge it in a closed container for up to 5 days. It will set solid with a cap of pale coconut fat on top — stir that fat back in when you reheat, it is the dish, not waste. Reheat gently in a pan with a tablespoon of water to loosen it, never in a hot pan on its own, or the crust catches. It freezes better than almost anything else in this book, 3 months with no loss at all, because there is very little water left in it to form ice crystals. Two honest warnings about the folklore. Properly dry rendang genuinely does keep for weeks unrefrigerated in West Sumatra — the water is gone, the fat seals the surface and the spices are antimicrobial — but that applies to rendang taken all the way to black and dry, in a scrupulously clean pot, and a Pretoria kitchen in February is not the place to test the theory. Fridge it. And do not try to shortcut the standing time by keeping it warm on a low plate for hours; it dries past pleasant into leathery.","chefNotes":"Chuck, not a lean cut, cut across the grain. Loosen the bumbu with coconut milk instead of water. No searing — everything goes into a cold wide pan together. Then wait for pecah minyak, the moment the oil separates out and the simmer becomes a fry, and give it another half hour of near-constant stirring after that. Salt only at the end, because the pot reduces to a fifth.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice and nothing clever — rendang is intensely concentrated and the rice is there to carry it, not to compete. What it really wants alongside is something raw, sharp and cooling: sliced cucumber, a wedge of lime, and a spoonful of sambal on the side of the plate for heat that is bright rather than deep. A plate of fried tempeh gives it crunch, and a simple boiled green vegetable stops the whole meal being rich in one register.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Tempe Goreng","Sambal Terasi","Cucumber"],"trivia":"Rendang is not the name of a dish. It is the name of a technique — from the Minangkabau word merandang, meaning to slowly reduce something until the liquid is gone — which is why the same method applied to chicken, jackfruit, egg or tempeh is still rendang, and why the wet halfway stage has its own separate name, kalio, rather than being called unfinished rendang. What looks like an elaborate curry is really a preservation method wearing a curry's clothes. Take the water out of a food and there is nothing left for bacteria to live in; finish it in coconut oil and you have sealed it; do it with galangal, turmeric, lemongrass and chilli and you have added a genuinely antimicrobial spice load on top. The result keeps for weeks at tropical room temperature without a fridge.\n\nThat is precisely what it was for. Minangkabau culture in West Sumatra has a long tradition called merantau — young men leaving the home village to make their way in the wider world — and rendang was the food that travelled with them, in a tin, for a journey measured in days or weeks. It is also why Padang restaurants, the Minangkabau eating houses now found in every Indonesian city, stack their rendang in the front window at room temperature all day and nobody worries. The same logic explains rendang's ceremonial weight: it is the dish cooked for weddings and for the end of Ramadan, brought as a gift to a house, because it is one of the very few celebration foods that can be made days in advance and improves by waiting. The Minangkabau are also matrilineal, with houses and land passing down the female line, and the deep knowledge of when a pot has broken and when it is finished has traditionally passed the same way — by watching, in a kitchen, not by any written time.","howThisFeels":"The moment the sauce quietly gives up and a slick of clear oil rises around the meat, and the smell in the kitchen turns from coconut to toasted spice in about ninety seconds.","leftovers":["(easy) Rendang on hot white rice with a fried egg on top and a squeeze of lime is the standard next-day lunch across Indonesia, and it is better than it sounds because the runny yolk does the job the missing sauce used to do.","(easy) Chopped small and pressed into a toasted sandwich with a slice of mild cheese — the fat is already there, so it needs no butter, and it crisps in the pan beautifully.","(medium) Stirred through fried rice at the last minute, off the heat, with the rice fried in a spoonful of the set coconut fat lifted off the top of the container.","(creative) Shredded with two forks, mixed with a little of its own fat, and rolled into small pastry parcels or spring roll wrappers to fry — the classic martabak-style filling, and the best possible use for the last dry spoonfuls at the bottom of the dish."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Rendang Ayam — Chicken (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":63,"howThisFeels":"Done inside ninety minutes instead of three hours, and the smell hits the same note about halfway through.","trivia":"Chicken rendang is not a lesser substitute invented for people in a hurry — rendang ayam is a standard of the Padang repertoire in its own right, and in much of Malaysia it is the more common of the two. What changes is the arithmetic of time. Chicken has far less collagen than beef chuck and nothing that needs hours to dissolve, so if you simmer it for three hours it disintegrates into threads long before the coconut milk has reduced. The trick is to reduce the liquid first and add the meat to a pot that is already most of the way there.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef chuck","to":"160g chicken thighs"},{"from":"160ml coconut milk","to":"130ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Change the order, because chicken cannot take three hours. Use 160g of chicken thighs, bone out, skin on if you like, cut into pieces about 4cm — thighs and never breast, because breast is lean and goes to cotton wool in a reducing sauce. Make the bumbu and the kerisik exactly as described. Then put the bumbu and the 130ml of coconut milk into the wide pan WITHOUT the meat and simmer it, uncovered, stirring often, for about 25 minutes until it has thickened into a loose golden paste. Now add the chicken and the tamarind, and simmer for 35 to 40 minutes until the chicken is fully tender. Stir in the kerisik and the salt and carry on over low heat, stirring, until the break comes and the oil separates — 10 to 15 minutes at most, because there is much less water to lose. Take it off within about five minutes of the break. Chicken rendang wants to stay just a little glossier and less dry than the beef, or the meat goes stringy while you wait for a crust."}]}},{"name":"Rendang Daging — the Full Dry Rendang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":74,"howThisFeels":"Three hours in the same room as it, doing almost nothing, and then twenty minutes of not being able to leave the stove.","trivia":"There is a real argument in West Sumatra about how far to take it, and the two ends have different names. Rendang kering — dry rendang — is taken to almost black, with the paste welded onto the meat, and it is the version that keeps for weeks and travels. Rendang basah, wet rendang, is stopped shortly after the break while a little dark sauce still clings. What is remarkable is that a single pot passes through both, plus kalio before them, so three named dishes are separated by nothing but the hour at which you take the pan off the heat. Cooks who grew up with it do not use a clock or a thermometer for any of the three; they read the oil."},{"name":"Rendang Tempe — Fermented Soy Bean Cake (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":89,"howThisFeels":"Chewy, savoury and unexpectedly meaty, with the toasted coconut tasting even louder without beef in the way.","trivia":"Rendang tempe sits on the counter of Padang restaurants alongside the beef, and not as a concession — it is one of the standard cheap everyday rendangs, along with rendang jackfruit and rendang egg. Tempe suits the method for a structural reason: it is whole soya beans bound by a living mould into a firm cake, so it holds its shape in a long reduction where tofu would collapse. It is also porous in a way meat is not, which means it drinks the bumbu instead of merely being coated by it. Because there is no collagen to dissolve and no fat to render, the whole thing is finished in under an hour.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef chuck","to":"120g tempeh"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the tempe first, which is the one place this version does sear. Cut 120g of tempeh into slabs about 1cm thick and then into 3cm pieces, and shallow-fry them in a little oil until deep gold on both sides, 3 to 4 minutes a side. Do this before anything else and set them aside. The frying seals the outside so the pieces survive an hour of stirring, and it takes the raw, faintly grassy edge off the mould that a wet braise leaves in place. Then make the bumbu and kerisik as described, and reduce the bumbu with the coconut milk and tamarind in the wide pan on its own, uncovered, stirring, for about 25 minutes, until it is a thick golden paste. Add the fried tempe and the kerisik and the salt and keep going on low heat, stirring often and gently so the pieces do not break up, for 15 to 20 minutes until the break comes and the oil runs clear. Take it off about five minutes after that. Tempe absorbs salt much faster than beef, so taste before you add the full 4g."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","name":"Sambal Terasi","nameAlt":"Charred chilli and shrimp paste sambal","aliases":["Sambal Terasi","Sambal Belacan","Indonesian Chilli Sambal","Sambal Bakar","Sambal Bawang","Sambal Matah","Balinese Raw Sambal"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","condiment","sambal","spicy","no-cook","quick","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","braai","guests","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"30g red chilli · 20g shallots · 10g tomato · 5g garlic · 3g shrimp paste · 3g jaggery · 10ml lime juice · 2g salt","method":"Understand what you are making before you start, because sambal is not a hot sauce. A hot sauce is a liquid you add to food. Sambal is a coarse, crushed paste that sits on the side of the plate and is taken in tiny amounts with each mouthful, and every part of the method below exists to keep it coarse. Get it smooth and you have made something else.\n\nFirst, deal with the 3g of shrimp paste, because raw terasi is the single most common reason a homemade sambal tastes wrong. It is fermented krill, pressed into a dark solid block, and straight out of the packet it is aggressive and faintly soapy in a way that does not cook out later. It has to be toasted. Wrap the 3g in a small square of foil, flatten it, and put it in a dry pan over medium heat for about 2 minutes a side, or hold it over a gas flame in a spoon. You will know it is ready by smell rather than sight: the sharp ammonia edge drops away and what is left smells savoury, roasted, somewhere between anchovy and hard cheese. The block also becomes brittle, so it crumbles between your fingers instead of smearing. Toast it in foil rather than loose unless you want the whole house to smell of it for a day, which is a real risk and the reason most Indonesian kitchens do this step outside.\n\nNow char the vegetables, dry, with no oil at all. Put a heavy frying pan over medium-high heat until it is properly hot. Put in the 30g of red chilli whole and unslit, the 20g of shallots peeled and halved, the 5g of garlic in whole unpeeled cloves and the 10g of tomato in a thick slice. Leave them alone for a couple of minutes at a time and turn them only occasionally, until each one is blistered and blackened in patches and gone soft — about 6 to 8 minutes for the lot. Garlic left in its skin is deliberate: the skin insulates the clove so it steams sweet inside while the outside chars, and naked garlic in a dry hot pan turns bitter in well under a minute. Slip the skins off afterwards. The chillies will hiss and pop and may spit a seed at you.\n\nThe charring is doing two separate things and both matter. It takes the raw green sting out of the chilli, which is a harsh, thin heat, and replaces it with a rounder, smokier one, so the sambal tastes hot rather than merely sharp. And it drives water out of the shallot and the tomato while browning their sugars, which is what stops the finished paste being watery and gives it a faint sweetness with no sugar involved. A raw sambal is a real thing, and it is version three below, but it tastes nothing like this one.\n\nNow crush it, and USE A MORTAR AND PESTLE IF YOU HAVE ONE. This is not nostalgia. A blender blade spins and cuts, which shears the cell walls into a fine purée and whips air through it at the same time; the result is a pale pink, frothy, slightly bitter cream that oxidises to a duller colour within an hour. A pestle presses and tears, which bursts the cells and releases the oils without aerating anything, and it leaves flecks of skin and seed intact so the paste has texture and colour depth. If you only have a machine, use short pulses and stop while it is still visibly rough — never let it run.\n\nStart with the salt and the hard things: put the 2g of salt, the toasted crumbled terasi, the charred garlic and the 3g of jaggery into the mortar and work them into a rough paste. The salt is doing double duty here as an abrasive; it gives the pestle something to grind against and breaks the garlic down far faster than pounding alone. Then add the chillies and pound them in. Then the shallots and the tomato last, working them in with a scraping, rolling motion rather than hammering, until you have a thick, uneven, deep red-brown paste with recognisable pieces still in it.\n\nJaggery instead of the palm sugar you would use in Java is the honest local route, and it is a good one. Gula jawa is the dark, soft, slightly smoky sugar pressed from coconut or sugar-palm sap, and jaggery — pressed from cane or palm sap in much the same way, sold in blocks in every Indian grocer in the country — carries the same molasses depth that plain white sugar cannot. Three grams is not there to sweeten the sambal. It is there to round off the sharp corner of the chilli, and you would notice its absence long before you noticed its presence.\n\nStir in the 10ml of lime juice at the very end, off the heat and off the pestle. Acid added earlier fights the charring and dulls the colour; added last it lifts everything and makes the heat taste brighter. Taste it now, on a piece of plain bread or rice rather than off your finger, because neat sambal will simply overwhelm your palate and tell you nothing useful. It should be salty, hot, faintly sweet and faintly funky, in that order, and it should taste like too much on its own — that is correct. On the plate, in a half-teaspoon at a time, it will be exactly right.","cookTime":"Toast 4 min · Char 8 min · Pound 6 min","kcal":"~45 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g · Carbs 7g · Fat 0.5g · Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Sambal terasi keeps far better than it looks like it should, for the same reason pickles do — it is salty, acidic and made almost entirely of things that resist spoilage. In a clean sealed jar in the fridge it holds 2 weeks comfortably, and the flavour settles and deepens over the first two or three days as the terasi works through it. Two rules make the difference. Press it down flat in the jar so there is no trapped air, and cover the surface with a thin film of oil if you want a month rather than a fortnight; the oil is a seal, not an ingredient. And always take it out with a clean dry spoon — a wet spoon or a spoon that has been in your food introduces the water and starch that grow mould, and a jar of sambal almost never spoils on its own. It also freezes usefully in an ice cube tray, one cube being roughly a family serving, which is the most efficient way to make a big batch. Do not water-bath or preserve it at room temperature; it has nowhere near enough acid for that, whatever a recipe online says.","chefNotes":"Toast the terasi in foil before anything else, or the sambal will taste soapy no matter what you do next. Char the chillies, shallots, garlic and tomato dry, with the garlic left in its skin. Then crush, do not blend — the texture is the dish. Lime juice goes in last, off the heat.","pairsWith":"Everything, which is the point — sambal is the one thing an Indonesian table is never without. It belongs next to rendang, next to fried tempe, next to plain rice and a fried egg, and it is spectacular on grilled fish and on anything off a braai. A spoonful stirred into mayonnaise makes the best possible chip dip. Keep it alongside something cooling on the same plate — cucumber, or the plain rice itself — because sambal is designed to be eaten against a blank, not on its own.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Rendang","Tempe Goreng","Ayam Goreng Kalasan"],"trivia":"The word sambal is older than the chilli in it, which is the genuinely surprising part. Chillies are American plants; they reached the Indonesian archipelago with Portuguese and Spanish traders in the 1500s, moving through Malacca and Manila, and they were so immediately at home in the cooking that within a couple of generations nobody thought of them as foreign. But heat had been part of the food for centuries before they arrived. It came from cabya, the Javanese long pepper, from black pepper — which the archipelago grew and sold to the world — and from ginger and galangal. The oldest Javanese references to a pounded chilli-and-shallot relish under the name sambal come from around the 1700s, by which time the imported plant had simply moved into a slot that was already there and pushed the long pepper out of it. Cabya is now so obscure in Indonesia that it is grown mostly as a medicinal curiosity.\n\nWhat followed is one of the more extreme cases of regional specialisation anywhere in food. Indonesia has hundreds of named sambals, and they are not variations on a theme so much as genuinely different objects: sambal matah from Bali is raw and sliced, not pounded at all; sambal ijo from Padang is made with green chillies and is pale and mild-looking and is not; sambal dabu-dabu from Manado is closer to a fresh salsa; sambal pecel is a solid dried brick you reconstitute. Terasi itself is worth a note. It is krill or tiny shrimp salted and fermented for weeks and then pressed, and it exists in a family that runs the length of the region under different names — belacan in Malaysia, kapi in Thailand, bagoong in the Philippines — and in the same evolutionary slot as the Roman garum and the fish sauces of Vietnam. It is one of the oldest tricks in cooking: let protein break itself down with salt and time, and you get a concentrated savouriness that no fresh ingredient can give you.","howThisFeels":"The pop and hiss of whole chillies blistering in a dry pan, and then the particular scraping rhythm of a pestle that means somebody in the house is about to eat properly.","leftovers":["(easy) A teaspoon stirred through hot rice with nothing else is a real meal in Indonesia and a genuinely good one — nasi sambal, eaten standing up.","(easy) Beaten into softened butter and rolled in baking paper into a log, chilled, then sliced onto a hot steak or corn on the cob straight off the braai.","(medium) Loosened with a little water and lime and used as a marinade for chicken thighs or fish before grilling — a heaped tablespoon per 500g, an hour is enough, and the sugar in it means it will catch, so keep it away from the hottest part of the fire.","(creative) Fried briefly in oil with extra shallots and a splash of tamarind until it darkens into sambal goreng, the cooked keeping-sambal, which turns a fortnight in the fridge into two months and tastes entirely different — deeper, sweeter, less bright."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sambal Bawang — Chilli and Shallot Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Four things in a bowl and hot oil poured over them, and it is on the table in five minutes.","trivia":"Sambal bawang is the sambal of warungs and fried chicken stalls, and it is made in enormous quantities because it takes almost nothing and almost no time. There is one technique in it and it is worth having: the raw pounded chilli and shallot are finished by pouring smoking hot oil straight over them rather than by frying them. The oil hitting the paste flash-cooks the surface of everything at once, which knocks out the raw sting in a couple of seconds without any of the browning a pan would give, so the sambal stays a bright screaming red and tastes fresh and violent rather than smoky and round. It is the same trick as a Chinese chilli oil, arrived at independently.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"10g tomato"},{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"3g jaggery"}],"addIng":[{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the toasting and skip the charring entirely. Pound the 30g of red chilli, the 20g of shallots, the 5g of garlic and the 2g of salt together RAW in the mortar, into a rough wet paste with visible pieces still in it, and scrape it into a small heatproof bowl. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil in a small pan until it is genuinely smoking — a scrap of shallot dropped in should fizz hard immediately — and pour it straight over the paste. It will hiss and foam up and the smell will change in about two seconds from raw onion to something cooked. Stir it through, add the lime juice, and leave it five minutes. Taste before adding more salt; the hot oil concentrates everything."}]}},{"name":"Sambal Terasi Bakar — Charred, with Shrimp Paste","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Blackened blistered chilli skins going into the mortar, and a colour in the bowl at the end that no jar in a shop has ever managed.","trivia":"Bakar means grilled or burnt, and the charring is not a garnish step but the entire difference between this and the raw sambals. There is chemistry in it that a home cook can taste directly. Blistering the skin of a chilli in dry heat drives off water and starts browning the sugars in the flesh, which is why a charred sambal tastes sweeter and deeper even though nothing sweet has been added, and why it needs so little sugar to balance. In West Sumatra and Java the same job is often done in the ashes of a wood fire rather than a pan, which is worth trying on a braai the next time the coals are dying — bury the chillies and whole shallots in the hot ash at the edge for ten minutes and the sambal comes out with a smokiness a stove cannot reproduce."},{"name":"Sambal Matah — Bali's Raw Sliced Sambal (Vegan)","icon":"🌿","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Sharp, crunchy and startlingly fresh — closer to a fierce salad than to anything you would call a sauce.","trivia":"Matah means raw, and sambal matah breaks the two rules the others keep: nothing is cooked and nothing is pounded. Everything is sliced as finely as a knife allows and then bruised together by hand with salt and hot oil. The reason is textural — matah is meant to crunch — and the reason it works at all is the lemongrass, which is fibrous and inedible in most raw preparations but becomes wonderful sliced paper-thin across the grain. It is a Balinese dish and Bali is the Hindu island in a mostly Muslim country, which shows up in its food in a hundred small ways; the classic matah is finished with coconut oil rather than a neutral one, and it belongs beside grilled fish more than beside anything braised.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"10g tomato"},{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"3g jaggery"}],"addIng":[{"item":"8g lemongrass"},{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Nothing is cooked and nothing is pounded, so the knife work is the whole dish. Slice the 20g of shallots into the thinnest possible rings, slice the 30g of red chilli finely across so it comes off in little rings too, mince the 5g of garlic, and take the 8g of lemongrass down to the pale bottom third and slice it across the grain as fine as you can physically manage — this is the one non-negotiable cut, because lemongrass sliced thick is woody splinters and lemongrass sliced fine is the best thing in the bowl. Put it all in a bowl with the 2g of salt and squeeze and rub it between your fingers for a minute until it wilts slightly and smells enormous; the salt and the bruising are standing in for cooking. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil until just smoking and pour it over, stir, then add the lime juice. If you can get a Thai lime leaf, roll it into a cigar and shred it in hairline strips as well. Eat it the same day — matah is a fresh thing and it goes flat and dull by tomorrow, which is exactly the trade for the crunch."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","name":"Tempe Goreng","nameAlt":"Fried spiced tempeh","aliases":["Tempe Goreng","Tempeh Goreng","Fried Tempeh","Tempe Bumbu Kuning","Tempe Kering","Kering Tempe","Tempe Mendoan","Sambal Goreng Tempe"],"course":"side","type":["side","snack","fried","fermented","vegan","protein","budget","quick"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox","guests","meat-free"],"ingredients":"120g tempeh · 50ml water · 40ml sunflower oil · 5g garlic · 4g coriander seeds · 3g ground turmeric · 3g salt","method":"Look at the block before you cut it, because tempe tells you plainly what condition it is in and it is the one ingredient here that can be wrong. A good block is firm enough to pick up in one hand without bending, dense, with the individual soya beans clearly visible through a white felt of mycelium that binds them into a solid cake. It should smell of mushrooms and fresh bread and very slightly of the beans themselves. Grey or black patches, usually around the pinholes in the wrapper or along an edge, are NOT spoilage — that is the mould sporulating where it found air, it is entirely normal, and those patches simply taste stronger and nuttier. What you do not want is sliminess, a strong ammonia smell, or any colour that is not white, grey or black. Yellow, pink or green means something else moved in and the block goes in the bin.\n\nCut the 120g of tempe on a slight diagonal into slices about 5mm thick. The angle is not decoration; it gives you a longer face for the same weight, which means more crust per piece. And 5mm is the deliberate middle of a real range: at 3mm the slices go crisp all the way through like a chip and lose the soft nutty interior, at 10mm you keep a creamy middle but the outside will never properly snap. Five is where you get both.\n\nNow the brine, which is the step people skip and which is doing more than seasoning. Crush the 5g of garlic, the 4g of coriander seeds, the 3g of ground turmeric and the 3g of salt together into a rough paste — mortar, or the flat of a knife — and stir it into the 50ml of water in a shallow bowl. This is bumbu kuning, yellow paste, in its simplest form, and every Indonesian household has some version of it. Lay the tempe slices in the liquid in a single layer and leave them 15 to 20 minutes, turning them once.\n\nWhat that does is specific. Tempe is porous in a way meat is not — it is thousands of whole beans held in a mesh with air between them — so it drinks a thin salted liquid all the way to the centre in a quarter of an hour. Rub a dry spice paste on the outside instead and you get a seasoned crust wrapped around bland beans, which is the difference between tempe that tastes of something and tempe people describe as cardboard. Coriander seed is the signature: warm, faintly citrus, and it is in nearly every Javanese bumbu for a reason. Turmeric is doing colour and a slight bitterness that cuts the richness of the fry.\n\nThen DRY THE SLICES PROPERLY before they go anywhere near the pan. Lift them out, let them drain a moment and pat both faces firmly with a cloth or kitchen paper until the surface is no longer wet to the touch. Wet tempe in hot oil does two bad things: it spits violently, and the water on the surface has to boil off before any browning can start, which means the inside overcooks while the outside is still pale. You want the interior of the slice damp and the exterior dry.\n\nHeat the 40ml of sunflower oil in a frying pan over medium heat. This is a shallow fry, not a deep one — about 3 to 4mm of oil, enough that the slices sit in it rather than on it. Get it to a moderate 170°C or thereabouts. Test it with a corner of a slice: it should fizz steadily and continuously, not explode and not sit there quietly. Moderate heat matters here more than in most frying. Too hot and the outside browns in forty seconds while the beans inside are still chalky and taste raw and beany; the mould and the starch in the beans both need a little time in real heat to turn nutty. Too cool and the slices absorb oil and go greasy and limp.\n\nFry in a single layer with space between the slices, 3 to 4 minutes a side, turning once. Do it in two batches rather than crowding the pan — crowded slices drop the oil temperature, steam each other and never crisp. They are done when they are a deep even gold, the edges have gone slightly darker and a little frilled, and a slice tapped with a spoon sounds hard rather than soft. Take them out onto a rack rather than onto paper if you can, because paper traps steam under the bottom face and softens the crust you just made.\n\nSalt them lightly the moment they come out of the oil, while the surface is still hot enough to hold it, and eat them within about ten minutes. Fried tempe is at its best almost immediately, then goes progressively chewier as the moisture inside works back out to the surface, and while it is still good half an hour later it is not the same thing. If you need to hold it, keep it in a single layer in a warm oven with the door slightly open — never covered, never stacked.","cookTime":"Brine 20 min · Fry 8 min","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 21g · Carbs 12g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~700mg","storage":"The uncooked block is the part worth being careful with, because tempe is a live culture and it does not stop when you buy it. In the fridge the mould slows but keeps working, which means the block goes progressively darker, softer and stronger-smelling over about 5 to 7 days — not spoiled, but heading past what most people want. Wrap it and use it inside a week. It freezes extremely well for 3 months and the freezer is genuinely the right home for it, because freezing stops the culture dead and the texture afterwards is barely changed; slice it before freezing so you can take out what you need. Thaw in the fridge, then dry the slices well since they will weep a little. Fried tempe is a different matter and does not keep in any satisfying way — it will sit in the fridge for 3 days and reheats respectably in a hot oven or air fryer for 4 to 5 minutes, but it never comes back to that first crispness. The exception is the sweet crisp version in the second variation below, which is deliberately fried past all its moisture and keeps for weeks in a jar, and that is not an accident but the entire reason it exists.","chefNotes":"Slice on the diagonal at 5mm — thinner loses the soft middle, thicker never snaps. Brine the slices in turmeric, coriander, garlic and salt water for twenty minutes so the seasoning reaches the centre, then dry both faces properly before frying. Moderate oil at about 170°C, single layer, two batches, and out onto a rack rather than paper.","pairsWith":"Plain white rice, sambal and a spoonful of something braised is the classic plate, and fried tempe alongside rendang gives that meal the crunch it is missing. It is also very good simply as a snack with sambal to dip into, and it is the thing that turns gado-gado from a salad into a meal. A wedge of lime over the hot slices does more than you would expect.","goesWith":["Sambal Terasi","Nasi Uduk","Gado-Gado","Rendang"],"trivia":"Tempe is unusual among the world's great fermented foods, and the reason is structural rather than cultural. Almost every other fermented soya product — miso, doenjang, soy sauce, douchi — is made by breaking the beans down into a paste or a liquid. Tempe leaves them whole and binds them together instead, with the mycelium of a mould called Rhizopus oligosporus, which grows through the mass in a day or two at around 30°C and knits it into a sliceable cake. The mould has to be given a clean start to do it, which is why the beans are soaked in acidified water first: the acid keeps competing bacteria out for long enough for the Rhizopus to take the territory. In that day or two the mould's own enzymes do a real amount of work — proteases that break long soya proteins into shorter, more digestible pieces and free amino acids, lipases that release fatty acids, and phytase that dismantles a chunk of the phytate that otherwise blocks the absorption of iron and zinc. Tempe is not simply soaked soya beans in a new shape; it is soya beans that have already been partly digested for you, which is why it is easier on the stomach than the beans it came from and why it tastes savoury rather than merely beany.\n\nTwo footnotes that are worth having straight. The famous claim that tempe is a vegan source of vitamin B12 is half true and the half that is false matters. The mould makes no B12 at all. What made traditional Indonesian tempe a B12 source was uninvited bacterial company, principally Citrobacter freundii and Klebsiella pneumoniae, living alongside the Rhizopus in open village production; tempe made in a clean modern facility, or at home with a pure starter, contains little or none. Anyone relying on it for B12 should read the label rather than the folklore. And the second: Javanese cooking makes deliberate use of tempe taken well past the point most people would call fresh. Tempe semangit, sometimes tempe bosok, is left until it is dark, soft and pungent, and it is then used in small quantities as a seasoning in dishes like sayur lodeh and sambal tumpang — the same logic as a blue cheese or an aged anchovy. The oldest clear written reference to tempe by name appears in the Serat Centhini, a long Javanese poem committed to paper in Surakarta around 1814, though the food is certainly older than the manuscript.","howThisFeels":"The particular dry snap of the first slice between your teeth, and then the soft nutty middle behind it that no vegetable and no meat quite tastes like.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold fried tempe, chopped, is excellent through any salad and needs nothing but a squeeze of lime — it goes chewy rather than soggy, which is why it survives dressing.","(easy) Crumbled into fried rice at the last second, off the heat, so it keeps a little of its edge.","(medium) Chopped small and simmered ten minutes in coconut milk with a spoonful of sambal into a quick sayur — the slices go soft and drink the sauce, which is a genuinely different dish rather than reheated leftovers.","(creative) Blitzed coarse with garlic, chilli and lime into a thick spread for bread or roti, which is roughly what a Javanese cook does with tempe that has gone too soft to fry neatly."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Tempe Bumbu Kuning — Plain Brined and Fried (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":45,"howThisFeels":"Twenty minutes of waiting and eight of frying, and it is the cheapest properly satisfying protein in the house.","trivia":"This is the everyday form and the one sold from carts by the bag, and its economics are worth stating: soya beans and a mould culture turn into a firm, sliceable, high-protein food with no animal in the chain and almost no equipment. A block of tempe carries roughly a fifth of its weight as protein, comparable to a lean meat, and the fermentation makes more of that protein absorbable rather than less. In Java it has been the cheap protein of the countryside for centuries, which is why the phrase for a poor household's meal is rice, tempe and sambal, and why the price of soya beans is a political matter there in the way the price of bread is elsewhere."},{"name":"Tempe Kering Manis — Sweet Crisp Matchsticks","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Sticky, hot, salty and sweet all at once, and impossible to stop picking at while it cools on the tray.","trivia":"Kering means dry, and this version is deliberately fried past the point of tenderness until every bit of moisture is gone — which is the whole reason it exists. Dry tempe sticks turned in a caramel keep for weeks in a jar at room temperature, so a household could fry a big batch and eat off it, and it travels in a lunch tin without turning soggy. It is one of the standard components of a Padang or a rice-parcel plate for exactly that reason. The technique point is the caramel: the sugar syrup must be taken to the sticky, thread-forming stage BEFORE the tempe goes in, and the tempe must be fully cold and fully crisp when it does, or the moisture in the sticks dissolves the syrup and the whole batch turns tacky instead of shattering.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"15g jaggery"},{"item":"10g red chilli"},{"item":"5g tamarind paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut and fry differently, then make a caramel. Cut the 120g of tempe into matchsticks about 4mm square rather than slices — thin enough to dry out completely. Brine them exactly as described but only for 10 minutes, since there is far more surface for the same weight, and dry them very well. Fry them in the 40ml of oil over medium heat for 6 to 8 minutes, stirring, until they are deep gold, rigid and the bubbling in the pan has gone noticeably quiet; that quiet is the water leaving and it is the doneness cue, because a stick that still bends will be chewy tomorrow. Lift them out and let them cool COMPLETELY on a rack — this is not optional, warm sticks steam and go limp in the syrup. Pour off all but about 10ml of the oil, add the 10g of red chilli finely sliced and fry it 30 seconds, then add the 15g of jaggery, the 5g of tamarind paste and a tablespoon of water. Let it bubble, without stirring more than a swirl, for 2 to 3 minutes until it thickens and a little dripped from the spoon falls in a thread rather than a drop. Take the pan off the heat, tip in the cold tempe sticks and toss fast and hard for about 20 seconds until every stick is glossed. Spread them out on a tray to set, separated, and cool completely before jarring."}]}},{"name":"Tempe Mendoan — Deliberately Half-Fried in Batter","icon":"🌾","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":49,"howThisFeels":"Floppy, pale, hot and completely wrong-looking, and then the best thing on the table with sambal and a cup of tea.","trivia":"Mendoan comes from a Banyumas word meaning half-cooked, and it is one of very few fried foods anywhere that is a mistake made on purpose. The tempe is sliced thin and wide, dipped in a loose spiced batter and lifted out of the oil while the batter is still pale, soft and visibly underdone — no crust, no colour, no snap. It exists because the region eats it fresh from the wok in the rain with sweet tea and a whole green chilli to bite, and a crisp coating would have gone soft on the walk home anyway. There is a real technique behind the floppiness: the batter must be thin enough to cling in a film rather than a jacket, and the oil hot rather than moderate, so the surface sets in well under a minute and comes out before browning has a chance to start.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g flour"},{"item":"10g spring onions"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Slice and batter instead of brining. Cut the 120g of tempe into wide slices about 3mm thick — as thin and as broad as the block allows, which is the opposite of the main method. Crush the garlic, coriander, turmeric and salt as described but whisk them into the 50ml of water WITH the 30g of flour and the 10g of spring onions finely sliced, to make a thin batter about the consistency of pouring cream; if it coats the back of a spoon opaquely it is too thick and needs another spoonful of water. Heat the 40ml of oil hotter than for the main version, near 190°C, so a drop of batter sizzles and sets on contact. Dip each slice, let the excess run off for a second, and fry 45 to 60 seconds a side ONLY. Take them out while the batter is still pale and soft with barely a hint of gold at the edges. It will look undercooked and that is the dish. Eat them immediately, straight off the paper, with sambal and, if you are doing it properly, a raw green chilli to bite between mouthfuls."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-gado-gado","name":"Gado-Gado","nameAlt":"Vegetable salad in peanut sauce","aliases":["Gado-Gado","Gado Gado","Indonesian Peanut Salad","Pecel","Lotek","Karedok","Indonesian Salad with Peanut Sauce"],"course":"main","type":["main","salad","vegetarian","peanut","meat-free","lunch","street-food"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunch","guests","meat-free"],"ingredients":"80g cabbage · 80g firm tofu · 60g green beans · 50g potato · 40g bean sprouts · 40g cucumber · 40g raw peanuts · 1 large egg · 30ml sunflower oil · 100ml water · 15g red chilli · 10g jaggery · 5g garlic · 5g tamarind paste · 3g salt","method":"The single decision that separates good gado-gado from the wet, khaki version people are politely disappointed by is this: COOK EVERY VEGETABLE SEPARATELY, and cook each one only until it is right. It is tempting to put the beans, the cabbage and the sprouts into one pot to save washing up, and it is the whole problem, because green beans need four minutes, shredded cabbage needs ninety seconds and bean sprouts need about fifteen seconds. Cook them together and you are choosing which two to ruin. One pot of boiling salted water, one vegetable at a time, lifted out with a spider or a sieve — the water gets reused, so it is one pot either way.\n\nStart with the 50g of potato, because it is the longest. Peel it, cut it into 2cm pieces and boil until a knife goes in with no resistance, 12 to 15 minutes, then drain and let it steam dry. Next the 60g of green beans, topped and cut into 4cm lengths: 3 to 4 minutes, until they have gone from squeaky raw to just tender with a slight bite left. Then the 80g of cabbage, shredded about 1cm wide: 90 seconds, no more — you want it wilted and still pale green, not grey and floppy. Last the 40g of bean sprouts: 15 to 20 seconds, literally in and out, which is enough to take the raw beany taste away and not nearly enough to lose the snap that they are entirely there for. The 40g of cucumber is never cooked; slice it thickly and leave it raw and cold.\n\nAs each one comes out, spread it on a tray or a cloth in a single layer to cool and DRY. This is the second half of the same principle. Peanut sauce is a coating, not a soup, and a coating cannot cling to a wet surface — the water on a hot drained vegetable thins the sauce exactly where the two touch, and a plate of gado-gado dressed while everything is still steaming turns into a pool within a minute. Room temperature and dry to the touch is the target. Do not rinse anything under cold water to speed this up; you will wash off flavour and add the water you are trying to remove.\n\nFry the 80g of firm tofu while the vegetables cool. Press it first — a plate on top and ten minutes is enough — then cut it into 2cm cubes, pat every face dry, and fry in the 30ml of sunflower oil over medium-high heat until at least two faces of each cube are properly golden and crisp, 6 to 8 minutes in all. Do not move it about for the first two minutes; tofu needs to be left alone long enough to release from the pan on its own, and a cube prodded early tears and sticks. Lift the tofu out and set it aside. Boil the egg in the same pot as the vegetables — 8 minutes from boiling for a just-set yolk that is still slightly glossy in the middle, then straight into cold water, which stops it cooking and makes it peel cleanly. Halve it just before serving.\n\nNow the sauce, and it starts with frying the peanuts rather than boiling them. Put the 40g of raw peanuts into the tofu oil, still over medium heat, and fry them for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until they are a shade or two darker and smell strongly toasted. Watch them properly. Peanuts go from perfect to burnt in about twenty seconds and a single scorched batch makes a bitter sauce that nothing can rescue. Lift them out onto a plate to cool and crisp — they will not be crunchy while hot, so do not judge them in the pan.\n\nPound or blitz the cooled peanuts with the 5g of garlic, the 15g of red chilli, the 10g of jaggery, the 5g of tamarind paste and the 3g of salt into a thick, coarse, sandy paste. Leave it slightly rough. A completely smooth sauce tastes flat and slightly gluey, and the small fragments of peanut are a large part of why this dish is satisfying rather than merely pleasant.\n\nThen loosen it with the 100ml of water, HOT, and add it in three or four goes rather than all at once, stirring hard after each. Hot water does two things cold water does not: it melts the jaggery so there is no grittiness, and it emulsifies the peanut oil into the liquid instead of leaving it to separate and float. You are looking for the consistency of thin cream — thick enough that a spoon drawn through it leaves a track for a second, thin enough to pour. It will thicken as it stands, so stop just short and keep the kettle nearby.\n\nTaste it now and adjust it deliberately, because peanut sauce is a four-way balance and every batch of peanuts, tamarind and chilli is different. It should be savoury first, then nutty, then sour, then sweet, with the heat running underneath all of it. Flat and heavy means it needs tamarind. Harsh and thin means it needs jaggery. Dull means salt. Get this right and the dish is right; the vegetables are only ever as good as the sauce on them.\n\nAssemble on a wide plate rather than in a bowl, so the sauce sits on the vegetables instead of collecting under them. Arrange the potato, beans, cabbage, sprouts, cucumber and tofu in loose separate groups, put the halved egg on top, and spoon the sauce over generously without stirring it in. Gado-gado is meant to be mixed by the person eating it — that is what the name is about — and mixing it in the kitchen means the last mouthful tastes exactly like the first, which is the one thing this dish is not for.","cookTime":"Boil vegetables 20 min · Fry tofu 8 min · Sauce 12 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 27g · Carbs 40g · Fat 33g · Sodium ~820mg","storage":"Store the sauce and the components apart and this becomes one of the best packed lunches there is; store it assembled and it is ruined by morning. The peanut sauce keeps 5 days in a sealed jar in the fridge and thickens to a paste in there, which is normal — loosen it with a splash of hot water and stir hard, and it comes back exactly. It freezes for 3 months, and freezing it in an ice cube tray is worth doing, since two cubes and a kettle is a dressing on a weeknight. The blanched vegetables hold 2 to 3 days in a closed container in the fridge, though the bean sprouts are the exception and go limp within a day, so add those fresh. Fried tofu keeps 3 days and crisps up again in a hot dry pan in two minutes. The boiled egg, unpeeled, holds a week. Assembled gado-gado does not keep at all and should not be refrigerated, because the vegetables weep, the sauce splits and the sprouts collapse; make up only what is going to be eaten.","chefNotes":"One pot, one vegetable at a time — potato 15 minutes, beans 4, cabbage 90 seconds, sprouts 15 seconds — then spread everything out to cool and dry properly before it meets the sauce. Fry the peanuts rather than boiling them, and watch them, because burnt peanuts cannot be fixed. Loosen the sauce with HOT water, a little at a time, to the thickness of thin cream. Spoon it over at the table and let people mix it themselves.","pairsWith":"It is a complete meal on its own, which is unusual for a salad, and the traditional additions are all about texture rather than more food: prawn crackers to scoop with, a spoonful of sambal on the rim for people who want it hotter, and a wedge of lime. A little warm rice or a couple of lontong rice cakes underneath turns it from a large lunch into a proper supper. Fried tempe alongside is the standard Indonesian move and the crunch is welcome.","goesWith":["Tempe Goreng","Sambal Terasi","Prawn crackers","Lime wedges"],"trivia":"Peanut sauce in Indonesia is not usually made from scratch, and the shortcut is more interesting than the long way. In Java the sauce goes by the name bumbu pecel and it is sold in markets as a solid brick — roasted peanuts, chilli, garlic, kencur, tamarind and palm sugar pounded into a dense block that keeps for weeks unrefrigerated with no packaging technology at all. You break off a piece, pour hot water on it, stir, and you have the sauce. It is a shelf-stable concentrate arrived at centuries before anyone used that phrase, and the same logic as a bouillon cube, except that the brick is the honest version and the cube is not.\n\nThe brick also explains the family this dish belongs to, which is far larger than gado-gado alone. Pecel, the Javanese original, is blanched vegetables with the brick sauce poured over. Lotek, from the Sundanese west, is pounded together with the vegetables in the mortar so the whole thing is half-crushed. Karedok, also Sundanese, uses the vegetables entirely raw. Gado-gado is the Jakarta member of the family, the most cosmopolitan of them, and it is the one that picked up the potato, the egg and the fried tofu — the potato in particular arriving with the Dutch, which is why it is in the capital's version and not in the village ones. The name itself is the giveaway: gado means to eat something as a mixed mouthful with no rice under it, and doubling a word in Indonesian and Malay makes it plural, casual or repetitive. Gado-gado is roughly mix-mix, or a bit of this and a bit of that — a name that describes an eating action rather than a recipe, which is precisely why nobody agrees on what goes in it and why arranging it in neat separate piles and letting the eater wreck them is the correct way to serve it.","howThisFeels":"Cold cucumber, warm potato, a still-soft egg yolk and a sauce thick enough to hold all of it together — the rare plate where nothing on it is the same temperature and that turns out to be the point.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover peanut sauce thinned a little further is the best noodle dressing in the house — hot noodles, sauce, sliced spring onion, done in four minutes.","(easy) The blanched vegetables reheat into a fast soup with any stock and a spoonful of the sauce stirred in at the end off the heat.","(medium) Peanut sauce brushed over chicken thighs or aubergine slices and grilled — keep it away from fierce heat because the jaggery in it catches quickly, and brush it on in the last few minutes rather than the first.","(creative) Sauce, cold vegetables and the fried tofu rolled into rice paper or a wrap with fresh herbs, which makes something closer to a summer roll and is better cold than the original ever is."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Pecel — Vegetables and Sauce Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Cheap, green, hot with chilli and startlingly filling for a plate with nothing rich on it.","trivia":"Pecel is the older and plainer parent of gado-gado and it is what most of Java actually eats — blanched vegetables and the peanut sauce, served on a folded banana leaf, often for breakfast. What is worth noticing is that stripping out the tofu and the egg does not make it a lesser meal nutritionally, because peanuts and the vegetables between them still deliver a decent protein load; it makes it a cheaper one. In Javanese markets pecel is also the standard use for whatever greens are going: water spinach, papaya leaf, cassava leaf, bean sprouts and long beans in whatever combination the morning produced, which is a far better principle for a home cook than a fixed ingredient list.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g firm tofu"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"30ml sunflower oil","to":"20ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the tofu and the egg entirely and use the 20ml of oil purely to fry the peanuts. Everything else is unchanged, but since there is now nothing rich on the plate, increase the vegetables rather than the sauce — another 40g of whatever green you have, blanched to its own timing, keeps this a meal rather than a side. This is also the version to use whatever is in the fridge on: spinach, morogo, kale, broccoli, water spinach and long beans all belong here, each cooked separately as described. Serve it slightly warm rather than cold, which is how it is eaten in Java, and put the sambal on the side rather than in the sauce."}]}},{"name":"Gado-Gado — with Fried Tofu and Egg","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A plate with six separate things on it in neat piles, and the small satisfaction of destroying the arrangement with a spoon.","trivia":"The two additions that make this the Jakarta version tell you something about the city. The potato is Dutch — a colonial import that took hold in the capital's cooking and never reached the village pecel — and the fried tofu is Chinese, from the same Peranakan kitchens that gave the archipelago its noodles and its spring rolls. Gado-gado is a colonial port's salad in the most literal sense: a Javanese sauce over a Javanese method with a Dutch tuber and a Chinese protein on top. The egg is the newest arrival of the three and the most negotiable; plenty of Jakarta warungs still serve it without."},{"name":"Karedok — Raw, the Sundanese Way (Vegan)","icon":"🌿","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":46,"howThisFeels":"Loud, cold and crunchy from the first bite to the last, with none of the softness a cooked salad has.","trivia":"Karedok is the West Javanese version and nothing in it is cooked at all — raw long beans, raw cabbage, raw bean sprouts, cucumber and Thai basil, with the peanut sauce pounded in a mortar and the vegetables tossed straight into it. It works because Sundanese food is built around raw vegetables in a way the rest of Indonesian cooking is not; a Sundanese meal often begins with lalapan, a plate of raw leaves and cucumber with sambal, before anything cooked appears. The signature aromatic in a proper karedok sauce is kencur, a small aromatic ginger with a sharp medicinal edge that is not stocked in South Africa. Fresh ginger with a little extra lime is the honest stand-in — it is not the same note, and it is far better than leaving the sauce flat.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"50g potato"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"8g fresh ginger"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cook none of the vegetables. Slice the 60g of green beans very thinly on the diagonal — thin is doing the work that blanching does in the main version, and beans cut in 4cm lengths raw are unpleasant while beans cut in 3mm slices are excellent. Shred the 80g of cabbage as finely as you can, leave the 40g of bean sprouts and the 40g of cucumber entirely as they are, and keep everything cold. Fry the tofu and the peanuts as described. Pound the 8g of fresh ginger in with the peanuts, garlic, chilli, jaggery, tamarind and salt, then loosen with the hot water as before but stop a little thicker — nothing here is going to release water into it. Toss the raw vegetables through the sauce in a bowl rather than plating them separately, since the sauce needs to reach the inside of the shredded cabbage. Eat it within the half hour: raw cabbage and sprouts sitting in a salted dressing wilt and weep, and karedok that has waited an hour is just a wet salad."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","name":"Nasi Uduk","nameAlt":"Jakarta coconut rice","aliases":["Nasi Uduk","Nasi Uduk Betawi","Indonesian Coconut Rice","Nasi Lemak","Nasi Kuning","Yellow Rice","Coconut Rice"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","rice","side","breakfast","vegan","budget","festive"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","breakfast","celebration","guests"],"ingredients":"90g white rice · 100ml coconut milk · 80ml water · 12g lemongrass · 8g galangal · 2 bay leaves · 3g salt","method":"Rinse the 90g of rice, and stop before you have rinsed it too much. Put it in a bowl, cover it with cold water, swirl it with your hand until the water goes milky, pour it off and repeat — three or four times, until the water is cloudy rather than white. What you are washing away is loose surface starch, which is what makes rice claggy and makes separate grains stick into a mass. What you are NOT trying to do is rinse it perfectly clear; that takes eight or nine changes, it strips a little of the outside of each grain, and coconut rice in particular wants a small amount of that starch left behind to give it body. Drain it properly in a sieve for a couple of minutes so you are not adding unmeasured water to a measured liquid.\n\nBruise the aromatics rather than chopping them. Take the 12g of lemongrass, trim it to the pale bottom third, and whack it two or three times along its length with the flat of a heavy knife or the base of a pan until it splits and frays and you can smell it across the kitchen. Do the same to the 8g of galangal, sliced into two or three thick coins first. Bruising ruptures the oil cells so the aromatics release into the liquid, while leaving the pieces big enough to pull out at the end — chop them fine and you get woody splinters through the rice that nobody can pick out. The 2 bay leaves go in whole.\n\nA word about the bay, because honesty is better than a substitution nobody mentions. The leaf in a Jakarta kitchen is daun salam, from a tree unrelated to the Mediterranean bay, and it is milder, slightly more tannic and less eucalyptus-sharp. European bay is the accepted stand-in and it works, but use two leaves and not four; the same quantity that would be right for daun salam will push a bay-leaf note over the coconut and make the rice taste faintly of stew.\n\nNow the part that is the whole recipe. Put the drained rice, the 100ml of coconut milk, the 80ml of water, the aromatics and the 3g of salt into a heavy pot with a tight lid, and note that the liquid is DILUTED — 100ml of coconut milk and 80ml of water, not 180ml of coconut milk. Cooking rice in undiluted coconut milk sounds like it would be better and it is not: the fat coats the grains and slows water absorption so the centres stay hard, and the result is greasy rather than fragrant. Diluting is not economising. It is how the dish is made.\n\nBring it to a boil uncovered, stirring gently once or twice, then turn the heat right down to the lowest it will go, put the lid on, and cook for about 10 to 12 minutes — until the liquid is fully absorbed and the surface of the rice is pitted with small craters where steam has pushed through. This stage has a name in Indonesian, mengaron, and the half-cooked rice it produces is called aron. Lift the lid and check by dragging a spoon to the bottom at the edge: there should be no free liquid at all, and the grains should still be firm in the middle, not done.\n\nWHY IT IS DONE IN TWO STAGES, and this is the thing worth carrying to every other coconut rice you ever make: coconut milk is not water. It carries fat, protein and a little sugar, and all three of them catch on the base of a hot pot. Boiled all the way through, the bottom layer scorches and goes bitter well before the top layer is cooked, and one burnt patch flavours the whole pot. So the coconut milk is used only for the absorption stage, where there is liquid in the pot protecting the base, and the finish is done in steam, where nothing is touching hot metal. This is why Indonesian cooks move the aron into a steamer, and it is why the many recipes that simply boil rice in coconut milk from start to finish produce either a burnt bottom or an undercooked middle.\n\nSo now steam it. The easiest version at home is to leave it in the same pot: keep the lid on, keep the heat at its absolute lowest, and give it another 12 to 15 minutes without lifting the lid once. The trapped steam finishes the grains from the outside in, gently, with no direct contact. If your pot is thin or your smallest flame is still fierce, use a proper steamer — tip the aron into a steamer basket lined with a cloth or baking paper, spread it loosely rather than packed, and steam over boiling water for 15 minutes. That is the traditional method and it is genuinely more forgiving.\n\nThen take it off the heat and LEAVE IT ALONE, lid on, for 10 minutes. This is not resting for the sake of resting. Rice comes off the heat with a moisture gradient — wetter at the bottom, drier at the top — and ten minutes of standing lets that even out through the whole pot. Fluffed immediately, the top is dry and the bottom is wet; fluffed after ten minutes, all of it is the same.\n\nFish out the lemongrass, galangal and bay, then fluff with a FORK, lifting from the bottom of the pot and turning the rice over rather than stirring it. A spoon presses and drags, which crushes cooked grains against each other and releases the starch you carefully rinsed off at the start. A fork separates. The finished rice should smell strongly of coconut, sit in distinct grains, and taste seasoned on its own — if it needs salt at the table, it needed more salt in the pot.","cookTime":"Rinse 5 min · Absorb 12 min · Steam 15 min · Rest 10 min","kcal":"~430 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 7g · Carbs 74g · Fat 12g · Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Coconut rice needs a little more care than plain rice, and the reason is the fat rather than the rice. Cool it fast and uncovered — spread it out on a tray rather than leaving a warm pot on the stove — then fridge it within an hour, in a closed container, for up to 3 days. The reason for hurrying is the same reason all cooked rice is treated carefully: Bacillus cereus survives cooking as a spore and multiplies in rice left sitting at room temperature, and it produces a toxin that reheating does not destroy. Warm rice left out for an afternoon in a Pretoria summer is genuinely the riskiest thing in this recipe, and coconut fat makes it no safer. Reheat it properly hot, either steamed for 5 minutes or with a splash of water in a covered pan, and the texture comes back well; the coconut fat sets in the fridge and re-melts, so cold nasi uduk looks unpromising and reheats beautifully. It freezes for a month in portion bags. Do not reheat it twice.","chefNotes":"Rinse until the water is cloudy, not clear — a little surface starch is wanted here. Dilute the coconut milk with water, roughly half and half, because undiluted coconut milk makes greasy rice with hard centres. Then two stages, always: absorb in the coconut milk on the lowest heat until the liquid is gone and the surface craters, then finish in steam with the lid on so nothing is boiling against hot metal. Ten minutes standing, then fluff with a fork, never a spoon.","pairsWith":"Nasi uduk is a base and it is built on. The classic Jakarta plate is coconut rice with fried chicken or fried tempe, a spoonful of sambal, a scattering of fried shallots, some sliced cucumber and half a boiled egg — pieces added and left off freely. It is also the correct rice under rendang, since the coconut in both makes them read as one dish rather than two, and it is what turns a plate of gado-gado from a big lunch into a full supper.","goesWith":["Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Sambal Terasi","Bawang Goreng","Tempe Goreng"],"trivia":"Nasi uduk is Betawi food, and Betawi is the most interesting answer to the question of what Jakarta's own culture is. The Betawi are the people of the old city itself, and they are a creole formed over roughly three centuries in and around the port: Malay, Sundanese and Javanese, Balinese and Bugis, Chinese traders long settled, Arab and Indian merchants, Portuguese-speaking freed slaves from across the Indian Ocean, and the Dutch administration all in one crowded delta town. The food came out of that mixture rather than out of any single tradition, which is why Betawi cooking pairs a Malay coconut technique with Chinese fried shallots and a Portuguese-descended taste for things stewed with tomato. The name itself carries the history: Betawi is simply the local rendering of Batavia, the name the Dutch East India Company gave the town it built on the ruins of Jayakarta in 1619 — so the people of Jakarta are, in their own language, named after a foreign fort, and the food they made in its shadow belongs to none of the traditions that fort was trading with.\n\nIt is also, properly, a breakfast. Nasi uduk is cart food sold before dawn, spooned onto a banana leaf with whatever the seller has, wrapped into a cone and eaten standing up on the way somewhere — the fried chicken and the sambal and the egg are all optional extras negotiated at the cart, which is why no two descriptions of the dish agree on what is in it. And it has a well-known twin across the water: nasi lemak, the Malaysian and Singaporean national breakfast, is the same coconut rice by another name, and the two countries have a low-grade running argument about ownership that neither can win, since the technique is older than either border. The one real difference is that nasi lemak is usually tied to a fixed set of accompaniments while nasi uduk is not. The third version below, nasi kuning, is the same rice turned yellow with turmeric, and yellow is doing symbolic work there rather than culinary — gold is the colour of prosperity and thanksgiving, which is why nasi kuning is moulded into a cone called a tumpeng, mountain-shaped, and cut at birthdays, weddings and office anniversaries all over the country.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid after twenty-five minutes of not lifting the lid, and the whole kitchen going warm and sweet with coconut in about two seconds.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold nasi uduk fried in a hot pan with a little oil until the bottom catches into a crust — the coconut fat already in it browns better than plain rice ever does.","(easy) Pressed into a small bowl, turned out onto a plate and topped with a fried egg and sambal, which is a complete meal in four minutes.","(medium) Formed into balls around a spoonful of leftover rendang or fried tempe, rolled in a little flour and shallow-fried into crisp rice cakes.","(creative) Left uncovered in the fridge overnight so it dries properly, then crumbled and deep-fried in small clumps as a crunchy topping for soup or salad — the coconut in it makes these far better than plain fried rice crumbs."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk Sederhana — Coconut and Salt Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"Three things in a pot and it still fills the kitchen with the smell that matters.","trivia":"This is what most households actually cook, and the reason is arithmetic rather than laziness: galangal and lemongrass together cost several times more than the rice they are flavouring, and they are bought by the piece rather than the gram. Dropping them costs the dish its high aromatic top note and takes nothing at all off the coconut, which is the flavour anyone would name if asked what nasi uduk tastes like. The two-stage method is unchanged and is doing all the important work — this version is proof that the technique, not the aromatics, is what makes coconut rice good.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g galangal"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"12g lemongrass","to":"6g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the galangal out and halve the lemongrass to 6g, bruised as described. Everything else is identical — the dilution, the absorption stage, the steam, the ten minutes standing, the fork. If you have no lemongrass at all, this still works and is still nasi uduk; add a strip of lime zest to the pot instead, removed with the bay at the end, for a little of the citrus lift the lemongrass was providing."}]}},{"name":"Nasi Uduk Betawi — the Full Aromatic Pot","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Grains that fall apart separately off the fork, each one carrying coconut, lemongrass and something faintly piney underneath.","trivia":"The aromatics in a Betawi pot are chosen to sit under the coconut rather than beside it, and galangal is the one that does the real work. It is not a hotter ginger, whatever it looks like — the compounds are different, and it reads as resinous and faintly medicinal rather than sharp, which is exactly the note that stops a pot of coconut rice tasting flatly sweet. In Jakarta a pandan leaf usually goes in as well, knotted, for a grassy vanilla smell that is hard to describe and instantly recognisable; it is sold frozen in South Africa in Asian grocers and is worth adding if you find it, one knotted leaf, pulled out at the end with the rest."},{"name":"Nasi Kuning — the Yellow Celebration Rice (Vegan)","icon":"🌾","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"A pot of gold-coloured rice that makes an ordinary Tuesday supper look like somebody remembered an occasion.","trivia":"Nasi kuning is nasi uduk plus turmeric, and it exists for a reason that has nothing to do with flavour: yellow is the colour of gold, prosperity and thanksgiving across much of the archipelago, so the rice is what you serve when there is something to mark. It is packed into a cone-shaped mould and turned out as a tumpeng, a mountain of yellow rice ringed with side dishes, and the tip of the cone is cut and given to the guest of honour — at weddings, at birthdays, at the opening of a shop. Turmeric also does something quietly practical: it is mildly antimicrobial and it was a preservative long before it was a decoration, which is part of why yellow rice travelled to feasts in the heat.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"4g ground turmeric"},{"item":"10ml lime juice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add the 4g of ground turmeric to the pot at the very start, whisked into the coconut milk and water before the rice goes in, so the colour is even — sprinkled on top of dry rice it streaks and never distributes. If you have fresh turmeric, use about 15g grated and squeezed, which gives a cleaner colour and less of the dusty note ground turmeric can leave. Stir the 10ml of lime juice in with the liquid too: the acid brightens the yellow noticeably, since curcumin shifts colour with pH, and it lifts what would otherwise be a slightly earthy pot. Cook it in exactly the same two stages. To serve it properly, pack the hot rice firmly into a bowl or a cone of rolled banana leaf, leave it two minutes, and turn it out — it holds its shape while hot and collapses once cool, so mould it at the last moment."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-nasi-goreng","name":"Nasi Goreng","nameAlt":"Indonesian fried rice","aliases":["Nasi Goreng","Indonesian Fried Rice","Nasi Goreng Ayam","Nasi Goreng Kampung","Nasi Goreng Pattaya","Fried Rice Indonesian"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","quick","leftovers","street-food","breakfast","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","breakfast","lunch","quick"],"ingredients":"120g chicken thighs · 80g white rice · 40g cucumber · 30g shallots · 20ml kecap manis · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g red chilli · 10g garlic · 10g spring onions · 5g shrimp paste · 1 large egg","method":"This dish is decided the day before, and no amount of skill on the day will get around it. COOK THE RICE A DAY AHEAD. The 80g of white rice, cooked plain and drained, gives you roughly 200g cooked — spread it on a tray while it is still hot so it cools fast, then refrigerate it uncovered or loosely covered overnight. What happens in those hours is the whole reason nasi goreng exists as a distinct dish: the surface of every grain dries and firms, and the starch retrogrades and stops being sticky. Freshly cooked rice is soft and holds a great deal of water at its surface, and dropped into a hot pan it releases that water as steam and then absorbs the sauce like a sponge. You get a soft, pale, faintly gluey mound. Cold day-old rice fries. It separates into individual grains, takes colour, and stays chewy. If you must use rice cooked today, spread it thin on a tray in front of a fan for an hour and accept that it will be second best.\n\nBreak the cold rice up with your fingers before anything else, in a bowl, until there is not a single clump left. Cold rice comes out of the fridge in a solid block, and clumps that go into the pan intact will still be clumps on the plate — the outside of a lump takes all the sauce and colour and the inside stays white and bland. This takes two minutes and it is worth doing properly.\n\nNow make the bumbu. Pound or blitz the 30g of shallots, the 15g of red chilli, the 10g of garlic and the 5g of shrimp paste into a rough paste. Toast the terasi first if you have not already — wrapped in a little foil, dry pan, about 2 minutes a side — because raw shrimp paste stays harsh right through a fast fry and this is a fast fry. Leave the paste coarse rather than smooth; you want it to fry in flecks rather than smear into a film.\n\nCut the 120g of chicken thighs into pieces about 2cm, slice the 10g of spring onions, keeping the white and green apart, and cut the 40g of cucumber into thick slices for the side of the plate. Have the 20ml of kecap manis measured out in a spoon within reach. From the moment the pan gets hot this dish takes about five minutes and there is no time to look for anything.\n\nHERE IS THE RULE THAT DECIDES WHETHER THIS WORKS AT HOME, and it is the one nobody puts in a recipe: COOK ONE PORTION AT A TIME. A domestic hob delivers a small fraction of the heat a street cart's burner does, and the pan is the only thing holding that heat. Put two portions of cold rice into a home wok and the temperature crashes below the point where water flashes off on contact — so the rice sits in its own steam, goes soft, and comes out grey. One portion in a wide pan, over the highest heat you have, and the pan recovers between additions. If you are feeding four, fry four times, five minutes each, and keep the finished plates warm. That is genuinely how it is done at a warung, one plate to an order, and it is why warung nasi goreng tastes better than anything made in a big batch at home.\n\nGet the pan properly hot before the oil goes in — a drop of water flicked in should skitter and vanish rather than sit and bubble. Add the 20ml of sunflower oil, swirl it, and put in the chicken in a single layer. Leave it undisturbed for a full minute so it takes real colour, then stir it about for another 2 minutes until cooked through. Push it to one side or lift it out.\n\nAdd the bumbu to the hot oil and fry it, stirring, for 60 to 90 seconds. Two things tell you it is done: the smell changes from raw onion and sharp shrimp into something rounded and savoury, and the paste stops looking wet. This second cue matters more than it sounds — a wet paste puts water into a pan that is about to receive rice, and the rice steams. You are frying the water out of the bumbu, not merely warming it.\n\nNow the rice, all at once, and turn up whatever heat is left. Toss and press it against the hot base with the back of a spatula, then lift and turn, then press again — pressing puts grains in direct contact with hot metal, which is where the toasted, faintly nutty flavour comes from, and lifting stops them burning. Keep it moving for 2 to 3 minutes until the rice is hot through, glossy with oil and beginning to catch in places. Add the chicken back and the white of the spring onion.\n\nTHEN THE KECAP MANIS, AND POUR IT DOWN THE SIDE OF THE PAN, NOT ONTO THE RICE. This is the last technical point and the most commonly wrecked. Kecap manis is roughly a third sugar; dropped into the middle of a pile of rice it soaks straight into whichever grains it lands on, so you get a few dark salty-sweet clumps and a lot of plain rice. Poured around the hot edge it hits bare metal, caramelises for a second or two, and then you fold it through as a thin hot syrup that coats everything evenly. The other half of the same rule is that it goes in at the END. Add it early and you have ten minutes of sugar over high heat, which is not caramelising, it is burning — bitter, black flecks, and a dish that tastes scorched rather than smoky. Toss hard for about 30 seconds until every grain has gone an even mahogany, then take it off the heat and stir in the green of the spring onion.\n\nFry the egg separately, in a hot pan with a little oil, hard and fast so the white blisters and the edges go brown and frilly while the yolk stays runny. Slide it on top of the rice, put the cucumber on the side, and eat it immediately. The runny yolk broken into the rice at the table is doing a real job — nasi goreng is dry by design and the yolk is the sauce.\n\nOne honest note on the shortcut, since it is sold in every supermarket: a jar of ready-made nasi goreng paste will get you a decent plate of food and skips the pounding. What it cannot do is give you the toasted terasi note or let you set the chilli where you want it, and it is dearer per portion than the shallots and garlic it replaces. Use it on a weeknight without guilt; do not expect it to taste like this.","cookTime":"Rice a day ahead · Prep 15 min · Fry 5 min per portion","kcal":"~590 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 33g · Carbs 62g · Fat 23g · Sodium ~1080mg","storage":"This section matters more here than in most recipes, and not because of the fried rice — because of the plain rice that goes into it. Cooked rice is the classic vehicle for Bacillus cereus, a bacterium whose spores survive boiling comfortably. Leave cooked rice standing at room temperature and those spores germinate and the cells produce a toxin, and the important part is this: THAT TOXIN IS HEAT-STABLE AND FRYING DOES NOT DESTROY IT. Reheating unsafe rice does not make it safe. So the overnight rice this dish depends on must be cooled fast — spread on a tray, not left in the pot — and into the fridge within an hour, used within a day, and reheated only once. Done that way it is completely safe and always has been; the problem is rice left out overnight on a counter, which is a different thing entirely. The finished nasi goreng is itself a reheated rice dish, which is why it should be eaten straight away rather than kept: fry it, eat it, and do not put it back in the fridge for tomorrow. If you have made too much, that is the one time to break the rule — cool it fast, fridge it, eat it within a day and reheat it properly hot once. Do not freeze it; the rice goes chalky and the egg is ruined. Uncooked cold plain rice, on the other hand, is the whole point and holds its day in the fridge perfectly.","chefNotes":"Cook the rice the day before and chill it uncovered — this is not a shortcut you can skip, it is the dish. Break every clump with your fingers before it goes near the pan. Fry the bumbu until it stops looking wet, because a wet paste steams the rice. ONE PORTION AT A TIME on a home hob. And the kecap manis goes in LAST, poured down the hot side of the pan so it caramelises rather than soaking into a few grains.","pairsWith":"It is built to be eaten with things scattered on and around it rather than with a side dish: a fried egg on top is close to compulsory, prawn crackers for scooping, a spoonful of sambal on the rim, sliced cucumber and tomato for something cold and raw against the heat. Fried tempe or a few sticks of satay alongside turn it from a plate into a meal. Sweet hot tea is the traditional drink and it works better than it sounds against the salt.","goesWith":["Sambal Terasi","Prawn crackers","Cucumber","Fried egg"],"trivia":"Fried rice dishes cluster in hot, rice-eating parts of the world, and the reason is older and more practical than any recipe: before refrigeration, cooked rice left overnight in a tropical climate was genuinely dangerous, and the safest thing to do with it in the morning was to cook it again, hard and fast, with salt and spice and whatever protein was to hand. Frying yesterday's rice was food management before it was a cuisine. The modern kitchen has changed the reasoning without changing the dish — we now know that the culprit, Bacillus cereus, produces a toxin that a hot pan will not destroy, so the fridge does the work that morning frying used to attempt, and the frying is now purely for pleasure.\n\nWhat makes nasi goreng specifically Indonesian rather than simply fried rice is a single bottle. Chinese traders brought the technique of frying cooked rice to the archipelago centuries ago, and it landed in a place that already had a thick sweet soy of its own. Kecap manis is a genuinely local invention rather than an import: soy sauce arrived from China, and Javanese producers reworked it with large quantities of palm sugar into something closer to a syrup, dark, viscous and only mildly salty. That single substitution — sweet soy where a Chinese cook would use light soy and a Thai cook fish sauce — is what turns fried rice mahogany instead of pale, and it is why the dish tastes sweet, savoury and faintly smoky all at once rather than simply savoury. Indonesia's national dish is, in effect, a Chinese method rewritten by an Indonesian condiment.\n\nThe rest of it is ordinary domestic life, which is the best argument for it. Nasi goreng is overwhelmingly a breakfast, eaten early and often standing up, and it is the single most common thing an Indonesian household makes without thinking about it. That is also why nobody can tell you the recipe: it takes whatever is in the kitchen, and the versions with names — kampung with dried anchovies, pattaya wrapped in an omelette blanket, jawa with a heavier hand on the chilli paste — are just households that agreed with each other for long enough.","howThisFeels":"The hiss the second cold rice hits a properly hot pan, and then the smell about four minutes later when the sweet soy goes down the side and catches for a moment before you fold it through.","leftovers":["(easy) Fry it a second time in a hot pan and press it flat into a cake, left alone until the underside forms a crust, then slid out and cut in wedges — better than reheated fried rice has any right to be.","(easy) Packed cold into a lunch box with extra cucumber and a spoonful of sambal, which is how half of Jakarta eats lunch.","(medium) Stirred into a beaten egg with a little flour and fried as thick fritters, which is a real Indonesian use for the last of the rice and turns a small amount into a plateful.","(creative) Pressed firmly into a small bowl, turned out, and pan-fried on both faces until crusted, then topped with a fresh fried egg — a two-minute rebuild that eats like a new dish rather than a leftover."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Nasi Goreng Sederhana — Egg Only (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Five minutes, one pan, one egg, and the smell that says somebody is looking after you.","trivia":"This is the honest everyday version and the one that shows what the dish actually is. Strip out the chicken and the shrimp paste and nothing structural is lost, because the flavour was never coming from the protein — it comes from the bumbu, the kecap manis and the heat of the pan. In Indonesia this is what gets made at eleven at night and at six in the morning, and the fried egg on top is not a garnish but the entire protein content of the meal. It is also the version to learn on: with fewer things in the pan the temperature holds better, so the rice fries harder and you can see clearly what a properly fried grain looks like.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"120g chicken thighs"},{"item":"5g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the chicken and the terasi and go straight from the bumbu to the rice. Without the shrimp paste the bumbu loses its savoury bottom note, so compensate deliberately rather than pretending it is the same: add an extra 5ml of kecap manis and, if you have it, a small pinch of salt with the shallots, and fry the paste a full 90 seconds so the shallots themselves brown and go sweet. With less in the pan everything moves faster, so have the kecap manis in the spoon before you start. Two eggs instead of one is the usual move here, one fried on top and one scrambled through the rice — push the rice to one side of the pan, pour a beaten egg into the space, let it set for twenty seconds and then break it up and fold it through."}]}},{"name":"Nasi Goreng Ayam — with Chicken","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":35,"howThisFeels":"One plate at a time, five minutes each, and standing at the stove eating the first one while the second is frying.","trivia":"Thigh rather than breast, and it is not only about flavour. A wok-fried dish gives a piece of chicken about three minutes of very high heat, and lean breast meat passes from raw through done to dry inside that window — there is no margin. Thigh carries enough fat and connective tissue to stay tender well past the point where it is cooked, so it forgives the extra thirty seconds you will inevitably spend on the rice. The same logic explains why street cooks so often use chicken cut small rather than in chunks: more surface for the same weight means it colours properly in the time available."},{"name":"Nasi Goreng Sayur — with Tempe (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Chewier and nuttier than the original, and the tempe holds the sweet soy better than chicken ever does.","trivia":"Tempe is the natural protein for a vegan nasi goreng rather than a compromise, and the reason is porosity. Chicken is coated by a sauce; tempe drinks it, because it is thousands of whole beans held in a mesh with air between them, so the kecap manis goes into the pieces rather than sitting on them. It also browns hard and holds its shape in a pan that is being tossed constantly. The one thing that has to be handled is the terasi: dropping the shrimp paste takes out the savoury depth that made the bumbu taste like more than fried onions, and the honest replacement is not a substitute ingredient but technique — brown the shallots properly and let the tempe itself take real colour, because both are doing the job the fermented paste was doing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"120g chicken thighs"},{"item":"5g shrimp paste"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"100g tempeh"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the tempe first and fry it properly. Cut 100g of tempeh into 1cm cubes, dry the faces, and fry them in the oil over medium-high heat for 5 to 6 minutes until at least two faces of each cube are deep gold and firm — do not rush this, because pale tempe in a fried rice is bland and slightly chalky while browned tempe is the best thing in the bowl. Lift it out and set it aside, then fry the bumbu in the same oil. With no terasi and no egg the dish needs its savoury weight from browning, so give the bumbu a full 90 seconds and let the shallots colour. Add the rice, then the tempe back, then the kecap manis down the side of the pan as described. Finish with the spring onion greens and, if you want the richness the egg was providing, a spoonful of sambal and a scatter of fried shallots on top."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-perkedel","name":"Perkedel","nameAlt":"Nutmeg potato fritters","aliases":["Perkedel","Perkedel Kentang","Bergedel","Indonesian Potato Fritters","Perkedel Jagung","Frikadel","Potato Patties"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","side","snack","fried","potato","budget","party"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","party","lunchbox","guests"],"ingredients":"150g potato · 60g beef mince · 40ml sunflower oil · 20g shallots · 10g celery · 5g garlic · 3g salt · 2g white pepper · 2g ground nutmeg · 1 large egg","method":"Everything about this recipe hangs on one instruction, and it is the one every home cook gets wrong: DO NOT BOIL THE POTATO. Peel the 150g of potato, cut it into slices about 1cm thick, and shallow-fry the slices in the 40ml of sunflower oil over medium heat for 6 to 8 minutes, turning once, until they are tender right through and pale gold at the edges. Then lift them out and mash them.\n\nHere is why, because it is the difference between a fritter and a disaster. A boiled potato has absorbed water — it went into water and it came out of water, and that water is trapped in the starch. Mash it and you get a wet purée, and a wet purée in hot oil does two things: it spits, and it slumps, because there is nothing holding it together but starch that is already saturated. Every recipe that boils the potato then has to correct for it, which is why so many perkedel recipes quietly add flour or breadcrumbs. Frying takes moisture OUT instead of putting it in. The slices come out drier than they went in, the mash is stiff and slightly crumbly, and it binds on its own with nothing but an egg. No flour, no crumbs, no cornflour. If you find yourself reaching for a thickener, the potato was boiled.\n\nMash while the slices are still hot, with a fork or a masher and never a food processor or a stick blender. Hot potato mashes into fluff; cold potato mashes into glue. And a machine ruptures the starch granules and whips the released starch into a stretchy, gummy paste — the same failure as overworked mashed potato, and once it happens there is no recovering it. Mash until it is mostly smooth with a few small lumps left, then STOP.\n\nWhile it cools, fry the mince. Finely chop the 20g of shallots, the 5g of garlic and the 10g of celery. In whatever oil is left in the pan, fry the shallots and garlic for 2 minutes until soft and fragrant, add the 60g of beef mince, and cook it for 5 to 6 minutes, breaking it up with a spoon, until it is browned and — this is the important part — properly DRY. Keep it on the heat until the liquid in the pan has gone and the mince starts to sizzle rather than steam. Wet mince put into dry mash undoes the work you just did with the potato. Let it cool a little.\n\nThe celery goes in raw at the end rather than being cooked with the mince, and it is not filler. Indonesian perkedel almost always carries either celery leaf or spring onion, and what it provides is a green, slightly bitter freshness cutting through what is otherwise potato, meat and fat. Left raw it keeps that; cooked with the mince it disappears.\n\nMix the mash, the mince mixture, the raw celery, the 3g of salt, the 2g of white pepper and the 2g of ground nutmeg together with your hands. Nutmeg is the signature and it is worth being precise about the quantity: 2g is roughly half a teaspoon of freshly grated, and it is doing the same job it does in a Dutch meatball, which is exactly where this dish gets it from. Less and the fritter tastes of nothing in particular. More and it takes over completely and starts to taste of milk pudding. White pepper rather than black, because black pepper's fruity note fights the nutmeg and the specks look like burnt bits.\n\nNow the egg, and use it as a COATING rather than mixing it in, which is the traditional method and a better one. Beat the 1 large egg in a shallow bowl. Shape the potato mixture into small flattened patties about 5cm across and 1.5cm thick, pressing them firmly between your palms so there are no cracks — a crack is where a fritter breaks apart in the oil. Then CHILL THEM for at least 20 minutes, uncovered, in the fridge. This is not optional and it is not resting for its own sake: cold patties are firm patties, and firm patties survive the two seconds between the egg bowl and the oil. Warm ones sag off the spoon.\n\nEgg mixed through the mixture works, and is how many households do it, but it makes the inside softer and slightly custardy and it makes the fritters harder to shape. Dipping instead gives you a thin sealed skin of set egg on the outside, which browns beautifully, holds the shape absolutely, and keeps the inside light rather than dense. If you would rather mix it in, use only half the egg or the mixture will be too slack to shape.\n\nHeat oil in a frying pan to a moderate 170°C — enough to come halfway up the patties, so about 1cm. Moderate, not hot: these are already cooked through, so all the heat is doing is browning the outside and setting the egg, and fierce oil browns the skin before the inside has warmed. Dip each chilled patty into the beaten egg, let the excess run off for a second, and lower it in. Fry 2 to 3 minutes a side, in a single layer with room between them, turning ONCE. Turning repeatedly is the other way people lose them; each turn is a chance for a fritter to break, and the crust needs an uninterrupted couple of minutes to set hard enough to be handled.\n\nThey are done when they are deep even gold and firm enough that you can pick one up by its edge. Drain on a rack, not on paper — paper traps steam under the bottom face and turns the crust you just built back to soft. Salt lightly and eat them warm.","cookTime":"Fry potato 8 min · Mix 10 min · Chill 20 min · Fry 6 min","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 17g · Carbs 26g · Fat 19g · Sodium ~780mg","storage":"The raw mixture is the part that keeps well and the fried fritters are the part that does not, so work backwards from when you want to eat. Shaped uncooked patties hold 2 days in the fridge on a covered plate in a single layer, and they are actually better on day two because the mixture firms up further and the nutmeg spreads through it. They also freeze raw very well for 2 months — freeze them apart on a tray first, then bag them, and fry from frozen at a slightly lower temperature for a minute or two longer. Fried perkedel keeps 3 days in the fridge but goes soft, and the fix is dry heat rather than a microwave: 6 to 8 minutes in a hot oven or 4 minutes in an air fryer brings the crust back almost completely, while a microwave turns them to damp mash. Do not freeze them cooked; the potato goes grainy and the egg skin weeps. And do not leave them stacked or covered while warm — trapped steam is the enemy of everything you fried them for.","chefNotes":"Fry the potato slices, never boil them — boiled potato is wet and wet mash needs flour to survive the oil, which is the compromise this method avoids entirely. Mash hot, by hand, and stop while a few lumps remain. Cook the mince properly dry. Nutmeg at 2g, white pepper not black, celery raw at the end. Then dip in beaten egg rather than mixing it through, chill 20 minutes, and turn ONCE in moderate oil.","pairsWith":"Perkedel is not really a starter in Indonesia so much as a thing that sits on the side of a plate, and its natural home is next to a bowl of soup — soto ayam above all, where one is broken into the broth and eaten half-dissolved. It belongs on any rice plate alongside sambal, and it is very good with nothing but sambal and a cold drink. A squeeze of lime over hot fritters lifts them noticeably. They are also one of the better things to hand round at a party, because they are just as good warm as hot.","goesWith":["Bakso","Sambal Terasi","Nasi Uduk"],"trivia":"The word is Dutch and the dish is an inversion of the Dutch original, which is the interesting part. Perkedel comes from frikadel, a Dutch meatball that arrived in the archipelago during the colonial period and which itself traces back through French and Italian kitchens. The Dutch frikadel is meat, with a little bread or potato in it to stretch it and hold it together. What came out of Indonesian kitchens is the exact reverse: potato, with a little meat in it for flavour. That flip is not an accident of taste but of arithmetic — meat was expensive and potatoes were not, so the filler became the dish and the dish became the seasoning. Once you see it, you can see the same inversion running through a great deal of colonial food everywhere: the expensive thing becomes a flavouring for the cheap thing, and the result outlives the original as a dish people actually want.\n\nThe nutmeg is the fingerprint left behind. It is in perkedel because it is in a Dutch meatball, and it is in a Dutch meatball because the Netherlands spent the seventeenth century fighting a war over the Banda Islands to control the world's nutmeg — islands which are, of course, Indonesian. So the spice travelled from Indonesia to Europe as the most valuable cargo on earth, became standard in Dutch home cooking, and then came back to its own islands inside a recipe for meatballs, where it stayed. Meanwhile the word kept moving in the other direction: a frikandel in the Netherlands today is not a meatball at all but a skinless deep-fried sausage sold at every snack bar, a completely different food wearing the same name. One word, three foods, two continents, and the only one still made the old way is the Indonesian one.","howThisFeels":"Picking one up by the edge to check it is firm enough, and the small dry crack of the egg crust giving way to something that is almost fluffy inside.","leftovers":["(easy) Broken into a bowl of hot chicken broth and left thirty seconds to half-dissolve, which is exactly how they are eaten with soto and is arguably better than eating them whole.","(easy) Split and pressed into a bread roll with sambal and sliced cucumber — a genuinely good sandwich and a standard Indonesian street filling.","(medium) Crumbled and fried again in a hot dry pan until crisp, then scattered over rice or a salad as a savoury crumb, which uses up even the broken ones.","(creative) Flattened thinner, topped with a fried egg and a spoonful of sambal, and eaten as a breakfast rösti — the nutmeg makes far more sense at breakfast than it has any right to."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Perkedel Kentang — Potato Only (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"Softer, sweeter and more about the nutmeg — the version that tastes most like the inside of a good pie.","trivia":"Without the mince this becomes the plainest and most common form of the dish, and it is worth knowing that the meatless version is not the concession — in most of Indonesia it is the default, and the meat one is what appears when there is meat. It also shows how much work the aromatics are doing: with the beef gone, the shallots, garlic, celery and nutmeg are the entire flavour, so they get slightly increased rather than left as they were. This is the version that goes with soto, where the broth supplies the meatiness the fritter is not carrying.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g beef mince"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20g shallots","to":"30g shallots"},{"from":"10g celery","to":"15g celery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the mince out and increase the aromatics deliberately, because with the beef gone they are all the flavour there is: 30g of shallots and 15g of celery instead of 20g and 10g. Fry the shallots and garlic as described and let them go properly golden rather than merely soft, 4 to 5 minutes — browned shallot is doing the savoury job the mince was doing. Everything else is unchanged, including the fried potato, the hot mash and the 20-minute chill. Without the mince the mixture is a little softer, so press the patties slightly thicker and give them the full 20 minutes cold, and consider adding a second raw green — spring onion greens alongside the celery work well."}]}},{"name":"Perkedel Kentang Daging — with Beef","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"The nutmeg hits first and the beef arrives second, which is not the order you expect from something that looks like a potato cake.","trivia":"Sixty grams of mince to a hundred and fifty of potato is a deliberate ratio and not a mean one. Push the meat much higher and the fritter stops binding — mince contributes no starch, so beyond about a third by weight there is not enough potato left to hold the thing together and you are back to needing flour. It is the same structural limit that governs a fishcake or a croquette. The other reason to keep it low is that the mince must be cooked dry before it goes in, and dry mince is quite intense; at this ratio it reads as a savoury seasoning running through the potato, which is what perkedel is supposed to taste like."},{"name":"Perkedel Jagung — Sweetcorn Fritters","icon":"🌽","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Crisp, ragged edges and whole corn kernels bursting sweet against the salt — a completely different thing wearing the same name.","trivia":"Perkedel jagung is arguably the more popular of the two across Indonesia, and structurally it is not a potato fritter at all. There is no mash and no binding problem to solve, because whole corn kernels sit in a thin spiced batter and the fritter is deliberately loose and lacy with kernels sticking out of the edges. That raggedness is the point: every protruding kernel goes crisp and slightly caramelised in the oil in a way a smooth patty never can. The technique that matters is crushing about a third of the corn before mixing — the crushed kernels release starch and milky liquid which binds the rest, so the batter needs almost no flour, which is the same principle as the fried potato in the main version arriving from a different direction.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g potato"},{"item":"60g beef mince"}],"addIng":[{"item":"180g sweetcorn"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No potato and no mash. Take the 180g of sweetcorn, and crush roughly a third of it — in a mortar, or by pulsing briefly in a processor, or simply by pressing it hard with the back of a fork on a board. You want it broken and milky, not puréed. That released starch is what binds the fritter, so do not skip it and do not crush the lot or you lose the burst of whole kernels that makes the dish. Mix the crushed and whole corn with the finely chopped shallots, garlic and raw celery, the salt, the white pepper, the nutmeg and the whole beaten egg — the egg goes IN here rather than being used as a dip, because there is no patty to coat. The mixture should just hold together in a spoon; if it runs, add a teaspoon of rice flour and no more. No chilling needed. Drop heaped spoonfuls into 170°C oil, flatten each one slightly with the back of the spoon so the edges spread out ragged, and fry 2 to 3 minutes a side until deep gold. Do not tidy the edges — the loose kernels around the rim are the best part."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Goreng","target":"indonesia-nasi-goreng","emoji":"🍛"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sate-ayam","name":"Sate Ayam","nameAlt":"Chicken skewers in peanut sauce","aliases":["Sate Ayam","Satay Ayam","Chicken Satay","Sate","Satay","Sate Tempe","Chicken Skewers Indonesian"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","main","grilled","braai","skewer","street-food","party","chicken"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["braai","party","family","guests","street-food","everyday"],"ingredients":"160g chicken thighs · 30g raw peanuts · 20ml kecap manis · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g shallots · 10g garlic · 10g red chilli · 10g jaggery · 10ml lime juice · 5g coriander seeds · 5g tamarind paste · 3g salt · 4 bamboo skewers","method":"SOAK THE SKEWERS FIRST, before you touch the chicken. Put the 4 bamboo skewers in cold water for at least 30 minutes and leave them there until the moment you thread them. Dry bamboo over coals is kindling — it chars through at the thin end and a skewer snaps in half while you are turning it, usually into the fire. Soaked bamboo has water in the fibres that has to boil off before the wood can burn, which buys you the eight or nine minutes this takes. It is thirty seconds of work and it is the difference between four skewers and three.\n\nNow the cut, which is the first half of what makes sate sate rather than kebabs. Take the 160g of chicken thighs, boned, and lay each one flat. Find the direction the muscle fibres run, then slice ACROSS them into thin flat pieces about 5mm thick and 4cm long — flat slices, not cubes. Two things follow from this. Across the grain shortens the fibres so each piece is tender rather than stringy, which matters far more on a fast fierce cook than in a slow braise. And flat is a heat calculation: a 5mm slice is cooked through in about the same time its surface takes to char, while a 2cm cube needs three or four times as long in the middle and will be dry and grey on the outside before the centre is safe. Every disappointing homemade satay is a cube problem.\n\nMake the marinade and the sauce from one base, because they are the same thing at different dilutions. Pound or blitz the 15g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 10g of red chilli, the 5g of coriander seeds and the 3g of salt into a rough paste. Take about a third of it, mix it with 10ml of the kecap manis and 10ml of the sunflower oil, and turn the chicken through it. Marinate 30 minutes at room temperature, or up to 4 hours in the fridge, and no longer — there is acid coming later but the salt alone will start to cure the surface of thin slices past a few hours and the texture goes faintly hammy.\n\nFor the sauce, fry the 30g of raw peanuts in the remaining 10ml of oil over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until they are a shade darker and smell strongly toasted, then lift them out to cool and crisp. Watch them the whole time; peanuts go from perfect to acrid in about twenty seconds and there is no rescuing a burnt batch. Pound the cooled peanuts with the remaining two-thirds of the paste, the 10g of jaggery and the 5g of tamarind paste, then loosen with hot water a spoonful at a time to a thick pouring consistency, and finish with the 10ml of lime juice off the heat. Keep it coarse.\n\nNow thread, and thread TIGHTLY. Push the slices onto the soaked skewers so each piece is pressed flat against the next with no gaps, filling only the top two-thirds and leaving the bottom third bare as a handle. Gaps are not a cosmetic issue: an air gap lets the fire get at bare wood, and it also lets the two exposed faces of each slice dry out and go leathery. Pressed together, the meat behaves like one continuous strip and cooks evenly. Four skewers from 160g means roughly 40g each, which is about four or five slices — resist loading them heavier, because a fat skewer cannot cook fast, and fast is the entire point.\n\nHERE IS THE FIRE, AND IT IS THE RECIPE. Build or bank your coals so there are TWO ZONES: one area with the coals raked into a deep hot bed, and one area with a thin scattering or none at all. If you are on a gas grill, one burner high and one low or off. Almost everyone cooks satay over a single uniform fierce fire, and it is why the skewers come off with black stripes and raw pink middles, or dry through with no colour at all — a single heat forces you to choose. Two zones let you do both jobs separately.\n\nStart over the HOT zone and leave the skewers alone for 60 to 90 seconds a side. They should hiss immediately. You are after real colour on both faces — brown, with a few genuinely black edges — and this happens fast and needs no help. Do not brush anything on yet and do not fidget with them; meat lifted too early tears and sticks.\n\nThen move them to the COOL zone and only now start glazing. Brush with the remaining kecap manis, turn, brush the other side, turn again, for 2 to 3 minutes in total, until each skewer carries a dark sticky lacquer. Kecap manis is roughly a third sugar, so over the hot zone it would be black and bitter in well under a minute. Over the cool side it thickens, catches and sets. The reason for the order is worth stating plainly: char first over high heat, sweeten second over low. Get it the other way round and the sugar burns long before the meat has browned, and you taste scorched syrup instead of grilled chicken.\n\nThey are done when the meat is firm and springy to the touch, the glaze looks like varnish and the thin edges of the slices are crisp. Total time on the fire is about five minutes, which is why the preparation matters so much — there is no window in which to fix anything. Rest them 2 minutes off the heat, then serve with the peanut sauce in a bowl to dip into rather than poured over, and a wedge of lime.\n\nIf you have no fire at all, this works in a heavy ridged pan indoors, and the two-zone rule becomes a two-temperature rule: pan on high for the char, then turn the heat down to low before the glaze goes anywhere near it. What you lose is smoke, and there is no honest way to fake it.","cookTime":"Soak 30 min · Marinate 30 min · Sauce 12 min · Grill 5 min","kcal":"~490 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 21g · Fat 27g · Sodium ~940mg","storage":"Everything except the grilling holds, which makes this a genuinely good dish to prepare ahead for people. Threaded raw skewers keep 24 hours in the fridge, covered, laid flat in a single layer — and the marinade is doing its work in there, so a batch threaded in the morning is better at six than one threaded at five. Do not push past a day; the salt starts curing the thin slices and the texture turns. Raw threaded skewers also freeze for 2 months, laid apart on a tray first then bagged, and they thaw in the fridge overnight with no loss. The peanut sauce keeps 5 days in a sealed jar and thickens to a paste in the cold, which is normal — loosen it with hot water and stir hard and it comes back exactly; it freezes 3 months and an ice cube tray is the sensible way to do it. Grilled skewers keep 2 days in the fridge and are good cold, straight from it, which is how they are usually eaten the next day. Reheating is the weak point: dry heat in a hot oven for 4 minutes is passable, a microwave turns the glaze wet and the meat tough. Never leave cooked skewers sitting warm for an hour — the glaze goes tacky and the thin slices dry out from both faces at once.","chefNotes":"Soak the bamboo 30 minutes or a skewer will snap into the fire. Slice the thigh ACROSS the grain into flat 5mm pieces, never cubes. Thread tight with no gaps and leave a bare handle. Then build TWO heat zones: char hard over the hot side with nothing brushed on, move to the cool side, and only then glaze with the kecap manis, because it is a third sugar and will be bitter over high heat in under a minute. Char first, sweeten second.","pairsWith":"Sate is street food and travels with plain starch: steamed white rice, or the compressed rice cakes called lontong cut into cubes, which is the classic pairing because they soak up the peanut sauce. Raw sliced shallot and cucumber on the side is not a garnish but the counterweight — the dish is sweet, rich and smoky and it needs something cold and sharp. Sambal for anyone who wants it hotter, and prawn crackers to scoop the last of the sauce. It sits perfectly on a braai alongside anything else on the fire, which is exactly what its South African descendant does.","goesWith":["Acar Kuning","Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Cucumber"],"trivia":"South Africans already know this dish under another name, and the line between them is direct rather than coincidental. The Afrikaans sosatie comes from the Malay words for sauce and for skewered spiced meat, and it arrived at the Cape with people brought from the Indonesian archipelago by the Dutch East India Company from the middle of the seventeenth century onwards — political exiles, enslaved people, and skilled artisans, whose descendants became the Cape Malay community. They brought turmeric, tamarind, coriander seed, chilli and the whole idea of marinating meat in a spice paste before threading it onto sticks over coals. Cape Malay sosaties are, quite literally, Indonesian sate that took a boat, met local apricots and vinegar, and settled in. The braai tradition that South Africans think of as the most local thing they own has an Indonesian skewer sitting near the middle of it.\n\nThe archipelago itself did not invent skewered meat so much as make it a national institution, and the usual account is that the technique arrived with Muslim traders from India and the Middle East — kebab logic meeting Javanese spice pastes — sometime around the fifteenth to eighteenth centuries. What Indonesia added was the sauce and the sweet soy, and then multiplied it. There are dozens of regionally named sates and they are genuinely different dishes: sate lilit from Bali is minced fish and coconut moulded onto lemongrass stalks rather than skewered; sate padang from West Sumatra abandons peanuts entirely for a thick yellow turmeric-and-rice-flour gravy; sate klathak from Yogyakarta is goat threaded onto bicycle spokes, which conduct heat into the middle of the meat and cook it from the inside as well as the outside — a piece of accidental engineering no recipe can improve on. And the peanut itself is a South American plant that arrived on Portuguese and Spanish ships, which means the sauce everyone considers definitively Indonesian is younger than the skewer it is served with.","howThisFeels":"The particular hiss when eight or nine skewers hit hot coals at once, and then the change of smell a few minutes later when the sweet soy hits the cooler side and starts to caramelise.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold straight from the fridge, pulled off the stick and eaten with a spoonful of the sauce — better than reheating, and how most Indonesian households finish them.","(easy) Meat stripped off the skewers and folded through hot rice with the last of the peanut sauce loosened with a splash of water.","(medium) Chopped into a wrap or a roll with shredded cabbage, cucumber and sauce, which turns four leftover skewers into a proper lunch.","(creative) Stripped, chopped small and stirred through cold noodles with the sauce thinned to a dressing and extra lime — a cold noodle salad that is arguably better than the original the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sate Ayam Kecap — Sweet Soy Only, No Sauce (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":35,"howThisFeels":"Smokier and much sharper, with the lime and raw shallot doing all the work the peanuts usually do.","trivia":"Sate kecap is not a stripped-down version so much as the older and plainer one, and in much of Java it is what you actually get unless you ask for peanut sauce. The dip is nothing but kecap manis loosened with lime juice, with raw sliced shallot and chopped chilli dropped straight into it — no cooking at all. It works because it is doing the opposite job to a peanut sauce: instead of adding richness to lean grilled meat it adds acid and sharp raw allium, which cuts the smoke rather than padding it. It is also the honest answer when peanuts are the most expensive thing on the list, and it is what you want beside something fatty rather than beside chicken breast.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"30g raw peanuts"},{"item":"10g jaggery"},{"item":"5g tamarind paste"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20ml kecap manis","to":"30ml kecap manis"}],"addStep":[{"text":"There is no cooked sauce at all, so the whole sauce section disappears. Use 30ml of kecap manis instead of 20ml: 10ml goes into the marinade with the oil and a third of the paste exactly as described, 10ml is brushed on over the cool zone as the glaze, and the last 10ml becomes the dip. For the dip, put that 10ml of kecap manis in a small bowl, stir in the 10ml of lime juice, and add the remaining paste RAW along with a few extra rings of finely sliced shallot and red chilli. Do not cook it and do not dilute it with water — it is meant to be sharp, thin and salty rather than creamy. Everything about the soaking, the cut, the tight threading and the two zones is unchanged, and with no rich sauce to hide behind the char matters even more, so be braver over the hot side."}]}},{"name":"Sate Ayam — with Bumbu Kacang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"Sticky dark lacquer on the outside, and then the peanut sauce arriving thick and warm over the top of the smoke.","trivia":"Thigh and not breast, and on a five-minute cook the reason is stark. Grilling gives a slice of chicken roughly two minutes a side of very high heat, and breast meat crosses from raw to done to dry inside that window with essentially no margin — there is no version of this recipe in which breast is the better choice. Thigh carries enough intramuscular fat and connective tissue that it stays juicy well past the point of being cooked, which is exactly what you need when you are also trying to blacken the outside. The fat also renders into the coals and comes back as smoke, which is a large part of why satay smells the way it does at fifty metres."},{"name":"Sate Tempe — Fermented Soy Skewers (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":74,"howThisFeels":"Chewy and nutty with hard-charred edges, and the glaze clinging to it more thickly than it ever does to chicken.","trivia":"Tempe suits a skewer better than tofu does, and the reason is structural: it is whole soya beans bound into a firm cake by a living mould, so a block can be cut into slabs that hold their shape on a stick over a fire, where tofu tears at the skewer hole and collapses. It is also porous, so it drinks the marinade all the way through in twenty minutes rather than being coated by it. The one real difference in handling is fat. Chicken thigh bastes itself as it cooks; tempe has almost none of its own, so it needs oil brushed on before and during the char or the surface dries to cardboard before it colours — and that is the whole adjustment.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"160g chicken thighs"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g tempeh"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20ml sunflower oil","to":"30ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut 150g of tempeh into slabs about 1cm thick and 4cm square — noticeably thicker than the chicken slices, because tempe is already cooked and firm and thin pieces simply dry out. Thread them tight onto the soaked skewers through the flat face. Marinate 20 minutes only, which is plenty; tempe absorbs fast and a long soak makes it soggy and prone to breaking on the stick. Use 30ml of oil rather than 20ml, because tempe has no fat of its own to baste with: 15ml goes into the marinade and 15ml is brushed on during the char. Over the hot zone give it slightly longer than chicken, 2 minutes a side, and be firm about wanting real black edges — pale tempe on a skewer is bland and slightly chalky, and hard-charred tempe is superb. Then the cool zone and the kecap manis glaze exactly as described. The peanut sauce is already vegan as written, so nothing changes there."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-klepon","name":"Klepon","nameAlt":"Palm sugar rice balls in coconut","aliases":["Klepon","Kelepon","Onde-Onde","Buah Melaka","Indonesian Palm Sugar Rice Balls","Pandan Rice Balls","Klepon Ubi"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","street-food","vegan","steamed","budget","party"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","party","guests","celebration"],"ingredients":"90g glutinous rice flour · 70ml water · 30g jaggery · 25g desiccated coconut · 2g salt","method":"Everything in this recipe exists to serve one moment: the ball goes into your mouth whole and then bursts with hot liquid sugar. Every instruction below is either about creating that liquid or about stopping it escaping before it reaches you. There is no other point to klepon, and a klepon that does not burst is simply a sweet dumpling.\n\nStart with the sugar, because this is where most attempts are lost. Chop the 30g of jaggery into small hard nuggets roughly 5mm across — the size of a coarse grain of rock salt. THEY MUST BE HARD AND DRY. If your jaggery is soft, damp or crumbly, spread the pieces on a plate and leave them uncovered for an hour, or give them two minutes in a low oven and let them cool completely. Here is why it matters: a hard nugget survives being wrapped in wet dough and only melts once it is inside the boiling water, which is exactly when you want it to melt. A soft or damp piece starts dissolving into the dough the moment you enclose it, and instead of a pocket of syrup you get a faintly sweet patch of dough with nothing to burst. Solid going in is what makes it liquid coming out.\n\nJaggery is standing in for gula jawa here and it is a close and honest match — both are unrefined sugars boiled down from cane or palm sap and pressed into blocks, and both carry the dark molasses depth that plain brown sugar does not. White or brown sugar will not do in this recipe, and not for flavour reasons alone: refined sugar melts to a thin colourless syrup that tastes only of sweetness, while the unrefined block melts to something dark, faintly smoky and almost savoury, which is the entire filling.\n\nPrepare the coconut before the dough, because it needs to be ready and cooling when the balls come out of the water. Toss the 25g of desiccated coconut with 1g of the salt and steam it for 5 to 8 minutes — a sieve over a pan of simmering water with a lid on works perfectly — then spread it out to cool. Do not skip this and do not use the coconut straight from the packet. Desiccated coconut is dried hard and it tastes papery and slightly stale dry, with a texture like fine sawdust; five minutes of steam rehydrates it into something soft, white, moist and sweet that actually tastes of coconut. The salt is not optional either. It is the only savoury note in the dish and without it the whole thing is flatly sweet.\n\nNow the dough. Put the 90g of glutinous rice flour in a bowl with the remaining 1g of salt and add the 70ml of water gradually — start with about 55ml, mix with a spoon and then your hand, and add the rest a teaspoon at a time. Flours vary a great deal in how much water they take depending on the mill and the day, so a fixed number is a starting point rather than an instruction. You are looking for a dough that feels like soft modelling clay: smooth, slightly tacky but not sticking to your palm, and holding a shape without cracking when you roll it. Too dry and it will split in the water and dump the sugar; too wet and you cannot seal it. Knead it two or three minutes until it is completely uniform.\n\nGlutinous rice flour is not a substitute for anything and nothing substitutes for it. It contains almost no amylose, the starch that makes ordinary rice grains stay separate, and almost entirely amylopectin, which is what gives cooked glutinous rice its chew and stretch. Plain rice flour makes a dough that boils into something short, crumbly and slightly gritty that cannot hold a liquid centre at all.\n\nNow the sealing, which is the second place people lose it. Take a piece of dough about the size of a walnut, roll it into a ball between your palms, then flatten it into a disc about 5mm thick. Put a nugget of jaggery in the middle — ONE nugget, not a heap, because the sugar liquefies and expands and an overfilled ball splits from the inside. Fold the dough up around it and pinch the edges together, then, and this is the step that matters, ROLL THE WHOLE THING BETWEEN YOUR PALMS UNTIL THE JOIN DISAPPEARS. Not until it is closed — until you cannot see where it was. Hold it up and look. A visible seam is a fault line, and in boiling water it opens, the syrup escapes into the pot, and you are left with a hollow ball and a sweet saucepan. Do all of them, keeping the finished ones under a cloth so they do not dry out.\n\nBring a wide pan of water to a bare, lazy simmer — bubbles breaking gently, not a rolling boil. A hard boil knocks soft dough balls against each other and against the base and tears them open, which is a shame after all that sealing. Lower them in gently and give them room. They will sink to the bottom and sit there.\n\nAfter 3 or 4 minutes they will rise and bob at the surface, AND FLOATING IS NOT DONE. Floating means the outside has cooked and trapped enough steam to lift the ball; the inside is still raw flour paste, and undercooked glutinous rice flour has a distinct chalky centre you cannot mistake for anything else. GIVE THEM 2 TO 3 MINUTES MORE AFTER THEY FLOAT. That extra time is also when the sugar nugget finishes melting, so it is doing double duty. You can see it happen if you look: the balls become slightly translucent and you can just make out a darker patch shifting inside.\n\nLift them straight out with a slotted spoon and roll them in the steamed coconut IMMEDIATELY, while they are dripping wet. The water on the surface is the only glue there is, and thirty seconds later it will have been absorbed and you will be sprinkling coconut onto a bare ball and watching it fall off. Do not rinse them and do not drain them dry.\n\nEat them warm, within the hour, and preferably in one bite each — that is not a suggestion but the design of the thing. Bitten in half over a plate, the syrup runs out and you have missed it. And warm matters: as they cool the melted sugar thickens and eventually sets back towards solid, so a cold klepon has a chewy sweet centre instead of a hot liquid one. Still good. Not the same dish.","cookTime":"Steam coconut 8 min · Shape 20 min · Boil 7 min","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 4g · Carbs 78g · Fat 9g · Sodium ~790mg","storage":"Klepon is a same-hour food and the reason is physics rather than spoilage. The liquid centre is molten sugar, and molten sugar sets as it cools, so the burst you built the whole recipe around is gone by the time they are cold — you are left with a chewy ball with a sweet middle, which is pleasant and is not what you made. On top of that, glutinous rice dough stales the way bread does, by starch retrogradation, and the fridge accelerates it badly: refrigerated klepon go hard and grainy overnight and the coconut turns sour-tasting. So keep them covered at cool room temperature and eat them the same day, within about six hours at the outside. The workaround that genuinely works is to prepare and shape the balls, uncooked, and refrigerate them on a floured tray for up to 24 hours or freeze them for 2 months — freeze them apart first, then bag them, and boil from frozen for about 2 minutes longer. That way the boiling and the coconut happen minutes before eating, which is the only stage that cannot wait. Steamed coconut on its own keeps 2 days in the fridge. Do not freeze finished klepon; the dough weeps on thawing and the syrup leaks out of the ball from the inside.","chefNotes":"The jaggery must be chopped into hard dry nuggets — damp sugar dissolves into the dough and there is nothing left to burst. Steam the desiccated coconut with salt first or it tastes like sawdust. Seal each ball by rolling it between your palms until the join is INVISIBLE, not merely closed, because a seam splits and the syrup ends up in the pot. Bare simmer, never a rolling boil. And floating is only the first signal — give them 2 to 3 minutes more, then into the coconut while still dripping wet.","pairsWith":"Hot unsweetened tea, and specifically something plain and slightly tannic rather than anything creamy or spiced — these are intensely sweet and the tea is there to cut them, which is exactly how they are sold at a market with a glass of black tea. They belong on a plate with other small market sweets rather than alone, and a few alongside sliced fresh fruit makes a very good end to a rich meal, because there is no dairy and no pastry in them at all. Strong black coffee works for the same reason as the tea.","goesWith":["Black tea","Serabi Solo","Es Cendol"],"trivia":"The green is the interesting part, and it is not food colouring. Klepon is coloured and scented with pandan, the long strap-like leaf of Pandanus amaryllifolius, which is one of very few leaves anywhere in the world used the way vanilla is used — as a sweet aromatic in its own right rather than as a herb. What makes it remarkable is the specific compound responsible: 2-acetyl-1-pyrroline. That is the same molecule that gives jasmine rice and basmati their smell, and the same molecule that gives the crust of a freshly baked loaf its smell. It is why pandan is so hard to describe to anyone who has not met it and so instantly familiar once they have — the brain has filed it under new bread and hot rice. The leaves are sold frozen in Asian grocers, and one knotted leaf dropped into the cooking water, or a few blitzed with the dough water and strained, gives both the colour and the scent. Without it you have a perfectly good white klepon, which is also traditional and is what most home cooks make.\n\nKlepon belongs to jajan pasar, the Javanese market-snack tradition — dozens of small steamed, boiled and fried sweets sold from trays before dawn and eaten by the handful, of which klepon is among the oldest and simplest. The naming is a proper tangle worth knowing if you ever shop for them. In Java it is klepon. Across Sumatra, in Malaysia and in Singapore the same thing is called onde-onde, and in parts of Malaysia buah melaka. But in Java, onde-onde means something else entirely — a fried sesame-covered ball with a sweet bean filling, which is a Chinese-descended snack and a completely different food. So the same word names two unrelated sweets depending on which island you are standing on, and both are sold in the same country.","howThisFeels":"The half-second of ordinary chewiness before it goes, and then hot dark syrup arriving all at once with nowhere to put it.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold klepon come most of the way back with 30 seconds of steam — enough to soften the dough and re-melt the centre — which is far better than a microwave, where they go tough and the sugar boils out.","(easy) Split cold ones in half and press them cut-side down into a hot dry pan for a minute until the flat face crisps and caramelises, which turns a stale sweet into a better one.","(medium) Dropped into a bowl of warm coconut milk sweetened with a little jaggery and a pinch of salt, which is essentially kolak and makes leftovers into a pudding.","(creative) Leftover shaped raw balls are the real prize rather than leftover cooked ones — boil four straight from the freezer and you have a hot dessert in seven minutes with no preparation at all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Klepon Putih — Plain White, No Pandan (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Plain white and slightly startling, because nothing about the look prepares you for what is inside.","trivia":"The white version is not the compromise it looks like — it is what most Javanese households actually make, because pandan is a garden plant you cut when you have it and skip when you do not. Leaving it out changes the dish less than you would expect, since pandan is an aroma rather than a flavour with any weight to it, and what you notice is the colour more than the taste. It also makes a good argument for the salt in the coconut: with no pandan there is nothing at all standing between you and pure sugar except that salt, so it stops being a background detail and becomes the thing keeping the sweet in check.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"30g jaggery","to":"25g jaggery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No pandan at all, and reduce the jaggery slightly to 25g rather than 30g. The reason is worth understanding rather than just following: pandan's aroma does a surprising amount of work distracting from pure sweetness, and without it the same quantity of sugar reads as noticeably sweeter. Everything else is identical — the hard dry nuggets, the invisible seal, the bare simmer, the two minutes after floating, the coconut while wet. If you want a little of the aroma without buying anything, drop a strip of lime zest into the boiling water; it is not pandan and does not pretend to be, but it gives the dough a faint fragrance and keeps the dish from tasting one-note."}]}},{"name":"Klepon — Pandan and Palm Sugar","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Vivid green, still warm, and the coconut coming off on your fingers before you have even picked one up.","trivia":"There is a real technical reason klepon is boiled rather than steamed, even though most of its market-tray siblings are steamed. Boiling gives the ball a hard outer skin of gelatinised starch within the first minute, sealing the seam mechanically while the inside is still raw — the water is doing part of the job your palms did. It also gives you the single most useful doneness cue in the whole jajan pasar repertoire, the float, which a steamer cannot offer at all. And the melted sugar needs the sustained 100°C of surrounding water to liquefy fully in the few minutes available; a steamer's gentler heat leaves the nugget half-dissolved and grainy."},{"name":"Klepon Ubi — Sweet Potato Dough (Vegan)","icon":"🍠","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"Softer, orange and a little fudgy, and it gives way sooner so the syrup arrives faster.","trivia":"Adding sweet potato is a standard trick across the region and it is not just about colour or thrift. The cooked potato brings moisture and its own sugars, so the dough is softer, more pliable and much more forgiving to seal — this is the version to make if a batch has split on you before. It also changes the eating: a straight glutinous-flour ball is properly chewy and takes a moment to break, while a sweet potato dough yields almost immediately, which means the syrup releases faster and the whole thing feels less like a trick and more like a pudding. The trade is that it stales faster, because the extra moisture accelerates the dough going firm, so these want eating within a couple of hours rather than six.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"90g glutinous rice flour","to":"60g glutinous rice flour"},{"from":"70ml water","to":"20ml water"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g sweet potato"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cook the sweet potato first and account for its water. Steam or boil 80g of sweet potato, peeled and cut into chunks, until completely soft — 12 to 15 minutes — then drain it thoroughly and let it steam dry for a couple of minutes before mashing it smooth while hot. Wet potato means wet dough, so do not rush that drying. Mix the hot mash into the 60g of glutinous rice flour with the salt, then add the 20ml of water only as needed, a teaspoon at a time; depending on the potato you may not need all of it, and this is the one version where the written water figure is genuinely a maximum rather than a target. Knead to the same soft modelling-clay feel. Everything else is unchanged — hard sugar nuggets, invisible seam, bare simmer, two to three minutes past the float, coconut while wet. These float slightly sooner because the dough is less dense, so watch the clock from the float rather than from the drop."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Perkedel","target":"indonesia-perkedel","emoji":"🥔"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bakso","name":"Bakso","nameAlt":"Springy beef meatball soup","aliases":["Bakso","Baso","Indonesian Meatballs","Bakso Sapi","Bakso Mie","Bouncy Beef Meatballs","Bakso Kuah"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","street-food","beef","noodles","comfort","make-ahead"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","lunch","guests","winter"],"ingredients":"200g beef mince · 500ml stock · 80g noodles · 30ml water · 20g tapioca starch · 10g garlic · 10g spring onions · 5g salt · 3g white pepper · 1 large egg","method":"A proper bakso does something no other meatball does: you press it and it pushes back, and when you bite it there is a distinct springy snap rather than a crumble. That texture has a name in Indonesia, kenyal, and it is the single thing vendors are judged on. It is also not a matter of skill or of a secret ingredient. It is a protein gel, it obeys rules, and the rules are all about temperature. Read this paragraph before you start, because once the meat is out of the fridge the clock is running.\n\nHere is what you are actually making. Muscle contains a protein called myosin, locked inside the fibres. Salt dissolves it out. Once free, and once worked mechanically, those long protein molecules unravel and link into a continuous three-dimensional net, and when that net is heated it sets — trapping water and fat inside an elastic mesh. That mesh is the bounce. Every instruction below either helps that net form or stops it collapsing. And the thing that destroys it is heat: above roughly 12°C the fat in the mince softens and smears, coating the protein and physically blocking it from linking up. Smeared fat gives you a meatball that is grainy, crumbly and slightly greasy — the classic failure, and there is no fixing it once it has happened.\n\nSo begin by getting everything cold, colder than feels reasonable. Put the 200g of beef mince in the freezer for 20 to 30 minutes until it is firm at the edges and just beginning to crystallise but not frozen solid. Put the bowl and the blade of your food processor in the freezer too, and put the 30ml of water in the freezer or add an ice cube to it — you want it barely above freezing. If you have no processor, this can be done in a mortar or by chopping and slapping the meat on a board with a heavy knife or cleaver, which is the traditional method and works well, but it is slower, so keep the meat in the fridge and work in two batches.\n\nUse beef with some fat in it, around 15 to 20 percent — ordinary lean mince is fine, extra-lean is not. Fat is what makes the set gel taste of something and stay tender rather than rubbery; the point of keeping it cold is not to remove it but to keep it in solid pieces until the protein net has already formed around it.\n\nNow the order of adding things, which is not negotiable. Put the near-frozen mince, the 10g of garlic, the 5g of salt and the 3g of white pepper into the cold processor bowl and blitz. THE SALT GOES IN AT THE START, WITH THE MEAT. This is the step most recipes get wrong by treating salt as seasoning to be added to taste at the end. Salt is the reagent here — nothing dissolves the myosin out of the fibres except salt, and if it arrives after the mixing is done there is no time for it to work and no bounce at all. Five grams to 200g of meat is a genuine 2.5 percent, which is high for a meatball and is exactly the level a sausage-maker uses, for exactly this reason.\n\nBlitz in short bursts for about a minute until the meat has gone from crumbly and separate to a smooth, dense, sticky paste. Then add the 20g of tapioca starch and the egg, and with the machine running pour in the ice-cold 30ml of water in a thin stream. Keep going another minute. Stop and feel the bowl with the back of your hand at any point — if it feels anything other than cold, stop, put the whole thing back in the freezer for ten minutes, and carry on afterwards. Interrupting is free. Overheating is terminal.\n\nTapioca starch rather than flour or cornflour is deliberate and it is the second half of the texture. It gelatinises into a clear, springy, slightly slippery gel rather than the soft opaque one wheat flour gives, so it reinforces the protein net instead of padding it. Twenty grams to 200g of meat is about the upper limit of honest — more starch makes a bouncier ball more cheaply, which is precisely why cheap commercial bakso is springy and tastes of almost nothing, and past about 15 percent you are eating starch with beef flavouring.\n\nThe paste is ready when it is visibly tacky and pulls back at you: push a spoon through it and the surface should close behind the spoon rather than staying open. There is a test if you want certainty — drop a marble-sized piece into a bowl of cold water. It should hold a tight round shape and sit firm rather than spreading or falling apart. If it spreads, the mixture is too warm or under-worked; chill it and work it again.\n\nBring a wide pan of water to a BARE SIMMER — steam and the occasional lazy bubble, around 85 to 90°C, and never a boil. This matters as much as the cold did. A rolling boil makes the setting protein net contract hard and squeeze out the water it was supposed to be holding, and the ball comes out tough, dry and pitted with little tunnels. Gentle heat sets the gel without wringing it.\n\nShape and cook in one motion, which is how the carts do it and it is genuinely easier than shaping first. Wet one hand, take a handful of paste, and squeeze it up through the gap between your thumb and forefinger so a smooth ball forms at the top; scoop that ball off with a wet spoon and lower it straight into the water. Around 3cm across is right. Work quickly and keep the bowl of paste in the fridge or over ice between handfuls.\n\nThey will sink. After 6 to 8 minutes they will rise and float, and unlike a dumpling, FLOATING HERE MEANS DONE — the ball becomes buoyant because the gel has set and trapped its water. Give them one more minute at most and lift them out. Overcooking is the last way to lose the texture: past about twelve minutes the net keeps tightening and the springiness turns to rubber.\n\nNow build the bowl, and keep the poaching water. Bring the 500ml of stock to a simmer, and add a ladle or two of the cloudy poaching liquid to it — that liquid has dissolved meat protein in it and it is the difference between a bakso broth and hot stock from a carton. Season it and keep it clear; bakso broth is meant to be thin and light, not enriched, because the meatballs are the substantial part. Cook the 80g of noodles separately, in their own water, and drain them, because starch released into the broth makes it cloudy and gluey.\n\nAssemble in the bowl rather than in the pot: noodles first, then the meatballs, then the hot broth poured over, then the 10g of sliced spring onions on top. Serve with sambal, and with a small spoonful of the broth first before you add anything to it, because you should be able to taste it plain.","cookTime":"Freeze meat 30 min · Blitz 5 min · Poach 8 min · Assemble 10 min","kcal":"~560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 44g · Carbs 44g · Fat 21g · Sodium ~1420mg","storage":"The meatballs keep far better than most things made of minced beef, and the reason is the gel — a set protein net holds its water rather than weeping, so poached bakso stay firm and springy for 4 days in the fridge in a closed container, ideally with a little of their poaching liquid. They freeze extremely well for 3 months and lose almost nothing; freeze them apart on a tray first, then bag them, and reheat straight from frozen in simmering broth for 4 to 5 minutes. Never reheat them in a rolling boil or a microwave: the first tightens the gel into rubber and the second does it faster. The raw paste is a different matter and must be used the day it is made — it will not wait, and freezing it raw is worse than pointless because the ice crystals rupture the protein net you just built and the bounce is simply gone. Store the components apart: noodles sitting in broth turn to porridge within an hour, so cooked noodles go in their own container for up to 3 days and the broth keeps 4 days or freezes 3 months. Assembled bowls do not keep at all.","chefNotes":"Cold is the whole recipe. Near-freeze the mince, chill the bowl and blade, use ice water, and stop the moment anything feels warm — above about 12°C the fat smears and the bounce is gone for good. Salt goes in AT THE START with the meat, because salt is what dissolves the myosin out; salt added at the end seasons but does nothing. Tapioca starch, not flour, and no more than 20g to 200g of meat. Then poach at a bare 85–90°C, never a boil, and lift them out a minute after they float.","pairsWith":"Sambal is not optional at a bakso cart — a spoonful stirred into the broth is how nearly everyone eats it, along with a splash of sweet soy and a squeeze of lime, all added at the table so each bowl ends up different. Fried shallots scattered on top add the crunch the bowl otherwise lacks. Perkedel on the side, broken into the broth, is a standard and very good addition. Beyond that it wants nothing: it is a complete bowl and adding a side dish is missing the point of a one-bowl meal eaten standing next to a cart.","goesWith":["Perkedel","Sambal Terasi","Bawang Goreng"],"trivia":"Three cuisines, working separately and centuries apart, arrived at the same piece of food science, and bakso is one of them. The springiness of a bakso, the snap of a good frankfurter, and the particular firm bounce of Japanese fish cake are all the same phenomenon: cold protein plus salt plus mechanical work, producing an elastic gel that sets on heating. A German sausage-maker chilling the bowl, an Indonesian cart cook slapping beef with a cleaver on a wet board, and a Japanese cook pounding fish paste are all doing one thing, and none of them needed to know the word myosin to work out that the mixture must be kept cold and salted early. It is one of the clearest cases in cooking of technique arriving well ahead of the explanation.\n\nWhat makes Indonesia unusual is that the country turned that texture into the entire basis of judgement. Bakso is not evaluated on seasoning or on broth in the way a ramen or a pho would be; it is evaluated on kenyal, the bounce, and vendors build reputations on it. Push a bakso against the side of the bowl with a spoon and watch whether it springs back is a completely normal thing to do before eating. The consequence is an arms race, and not always an honourable one: since starch produces bounce far more cheaply than beef does, the springiest bakso on a street is quite often the one with the least meat in it, which is exactly why 20g of tapioca to 200g of beef is the honest ceiling rather than a starting point.\n\nThe name gives away where it came from — bakso is Hokkien, from bak-so, meaning minced meat, brought by Chinese traders and settlers. Its ancestor is a pork meatball, and the single change that made it Indonesian was the protein: beef, so that it could be sold to a Muslim-majority country from a cart on a public street. That one substitution is why bakso is beef almost everywhere in Indonesia while the dish it descends from is pork. The carts themselves became an institution, wheeled gerobak announcing themselves down residential streets by rapping a spoon against a bowl, and the form keeps mutating — the current street spectacle is bakso beranak, a single enormous meatball with smaller meatballs sealed inside it, cut open at the table.","howThisFeels":"Pressing one against the side of the bowl with the back of a spoon just to watch it spring back, which is a thing you will do without being told after the first one.","leftovers":["(easy) Meatballs dropped straight from the fridge into any hot clear broth with a handful of greens — four minutes and it is dinner again, and they are arguably better on day two because the gel firms further.","(easy) Halved and fried cut-side down in a dry hot pan until the flat faces brown and caramelise, then eaten with sambal as a snack, which is a completely different and very good use for them.","(medium) Sliced thin and stir-fried with cabbage, garlic and kecap manis into a fast noodle dish, where the springy slices behave more like a sausage than a meatball.","(creative) Skewered three to a stick, brushed with kecap manis and grilled hard over coals until blistered — bakso bakar, a real Indonesian street form and the best possible thing to do with the last six in the container."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bakso Kuah — Meatballs and Broth Only (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42,"howThisFeels":"Clear, hot and startlingly plain, with nothing in the bowl to distract from the texture you just built.","trivia":"Without noodles this becomes the older and simpler form, and it is the one to make the first time because there is nothing hiding the meatball. It is also how a cart serves it to someone who wants a snack rather than a meal — five or six balls in a paper bowl with broth and a spoonful of sambal. There is a practical advantage too: no noodles means no starch anywhere near the broth, so it stays properly clear and light, which is how bakso broth is supposed to be. If you want to make it more of a meal without the noodles, a handful of shredded cabbage or greens wilted in the broth for a minute at the end is the traditional move.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g noodles"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the noodles out entirely and the bowl gets simpler rather than emptier. Everything about the cold meat, the early salt, the tapioca and the bare-simmer poach is unchanged. Because there is no starch going into the bowl, be slightly more generous with the poaching liquid stirred into the stock — three or four ladles rather than one or two — since that dissolved meat protein is now carrying the whole broth. Wilt a handful of shredded cabbage or any leafy green in the simmering broth for 60 seconds before serving and lift it into the bowl first, which gives the bowl some body without clouding it. Serve with more sambal than you think you need."}]}},{"name":"Bakso Mie — with Noodles","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":49,"howThisFeels":"A whole bowl arriving at once — hot thin broth, springy balls and noodles that have not been allowed to go anywhere near each other until this second.","trivia":"Cooking the noodles in their own separate water and never in the broth is the one rule that separates a good bowl from a mediocre one, and it is worth understanding rather than obeying. Noodles shed a surprising quantity of surface starch, and starch in a thin broth does two things: it clouds it, and it thickens it slightly so it coats the mouth and dulls everything else. Bakso broth is deliberately light because the meatballs are the substance — a thickened broth flattens the contrast the whole dish is built on. It is the same reason the assembly happens in the bowl rather than in the pot: anything that sits together for ten minutes tastes like one thing instead of three."},{"name":"Bakso Jamur — Mushroom and Tofu (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"Dense, deeply savoury and satisfying in its own right — softer than the beef, and honest about being a different thing.","trivia":"This version has to be honest about one thing: it cannot bounce, and any recipe claiming otherwise is selling you something. The spring in a bakso comes from myosin, an animal muscle protein, and there is no myosin in a mushroom or a soya bean — no amount of cold or salt or pounding will produce that net. So the goal changes rather than being chased: instead of an elastic gel you are building a dense, well-bound, deeply savoury ball, and the binding is done by starch and by pressing rather than by protein. Mushrooms are the right partner because they bring glutamates, the same savoury compounds that make the beef version taste meaty, and squeezing the water out of them is what stops the mixture being a paste that falls apart.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"200g beef mince"},{"item":"1 large egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150g mushrooms"},{"item":"120g firm tofu"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20g tapioca starch","to":"35g tapioca starch"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The cold no longer matters and the squeezing does, so the whole approach changes. Chop the 150g of mushrooms finely and fry them in a dry pan over high heat for 6 to 8 minutes until they have released their water and it has evaporated and they begin to brown and squeak — this is the single most important step, because mushrooms are around 90 percent water and unsqueezed mushroom makes a mixture that will not hold a shape at any starch level. Let them cool. Press the 120g of firm tofu hard between two plates for 15 minutes, pour off the water, then crumble it and squeeze it in a clean cloth until no more comes out. Blitz the cooled mushrooms, the squeezed tofu, the garlic, the salt and the white pepper to a coarse thick paste — coarse, not smooth, because the texture here comes from the pieces rather than from a gel. Work in 35g of tapioca starch rather than 20g; with no protein net the starch is doing all the binding, and it needs the extra. Skip the water entirely unless the mixture will not come together. Roll firmly into balls by hand, pressing hard so they are dense, and poach them in the same bare 85–90°C water for 8 to 10 minutes. They will not float reliably, so time them instead. Use a well-seasoned vegetable stock and be generous with the sambal, the sweet soy and the fried shallots at the table, all of which are doing more work in this bowl than in the beef one."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Perkedel","target":"indonesia-perkedel","emoji":"🥔"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-rawon","name":"Rawon","nameAlt":"Black beef soup from Surabaya","aliases":["Rawon","Rawon Surabaya","Nasi Rawon","Black Beef Soup","Indonesian Black Soup","Rawon Rusuk","Rawon Setan"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","slow-cooked","spiced","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","celebration","winter"],"ingredients":"180g beef brisket · 500ml water · 40g bean sprouts · 20g shallots · 20ml sunflower oil · 15g red chilli · 12g keluak · 10g garlic · 10g lemongrass · 10ml lime juice · 8g galangal · 5g ground turmeric · 5g tamarind paste · 4g salt · 2 bay leaves","method":"The black is not burnt, not soy, not squid ink and not colouring. It is one ingredient — keluak, the seed of Pangium edule — and it is the only thing in this recipe that cannot be substituted or approximated. Everything else here is an ordinary beef soup. So the first job is the nuts, and it is a job of rejection rather than preparation.\n\nBUY THEM ALREADY BLACK AND ALREADY PROCESSED, which is the only way they are sold. Raw keluak is genuinely poisonous and the detoxification is not a home process — it takes boiling followed by weeks buried in ash or earth, and the black colour is the evidence that it has happened. Nothing in this recipe involves treating a raw nut, and if you are ever offered fresh cream-coloured ones, they are not food.\n\nNow inspect them one at a time, and this is the actual technique. Crack each shell — the back of a heavy knife or a hammer, on a board, they are hard — and scoop out the flesh. GOOD FLESH IS SOFT, ALMOST PASTE-LIKE, DEEP BLACK OR VERY DARK CHOCOLATE BROWN, AND SMELLS FAINTLY OF TAMARIND, COCOA AND SOY. Reject any nut whose flesh is pale, cream, grey, hard, chalky or dry, and reject any that smells sharp, bitter or faintly of almonds. Those are the under-processed ones and they are both the flavour risk and the safety risk. Throw them out and do not try to rescue them by cooking them longer. A single bad nut will make an entire pot taste acrid and there is no fixing it afterwards, which is why the nuts are checked individually and why you should buy a few more than you need. You want 12g of good flesh, which is usually three or four nuts.\n\nMash that flesh with about 40ml of the water into a smooth, loose black paste, then PUSH IT THROUGH A SIEVE and discard what stays behind. The flesh carries fibres and small fragments of shell, and both are gritty in a finished soup in a way that reads as carelessness. This takes a minute and it is the difference between a silky black broth and one that feels sandy on the teeth.\n\nNow the beef, and here is the structural decision that most versions get wrong: RAWON IS A SOUP, NOT A CURRY. The mistake is to fry everything together and stew it into a thick dark gravy, which produces something perfectly edible that is not this dish. Rawon should be a thin, clean, intensely black broth that you could drink from a cup, with tender beef sitting in it. That means building a clear stock first and colouring it second.\n\nCut the 180g of beef brisket into 3cm cubes. Brisket, or another well-worked cut with fat and collagen in it — this simmers for a long time and a lean cut will simply go dry and fibrous. Put it in a pot with the remaining water, bring it to the boil, and then SKIM. For the first five minutes a grey foam will rise; lift it off and discard it, repeatedly, until the surface runs clear. That foam is coagulated protein and impurities, and if it is stirred back in it clouds the broth permanently — which you will not be able to see under the black, but you will taste as a slight muddiness. Once it is clear, drop the heat to a bare simmer.\n\nWhile that goes, make the bumbu. Pound or blitz the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 15g of red chilli, the 8g of galangal, the trimmed pale third of the 10g of lemongrass sliced thinly, the 5g of ground turmeric and the 4g of salt into a rough paste. Fry it in the 20ml of sunflower oil over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring, until it smells rounded rather than raw and stops looking wet. Then add the sieved keluak paste to the pan and fry it for another 2 minutes. Frying the keluak rather than tipping it straight into the pot matters: it is a starchy, faintly bitter paste raw, and a couple of minutes in hot oil turns the bitterness into something closer to cocoa and unlocks the colour, which deepens visibly in the pan from brown-black to true black.\n\nScrape all of it into the simmering beef, add the 5g of tamarind paste and the 2 bay leaves, and simmer uncovered, gently, for 90 minutes to 2 hours until the brisket is completely tender and gives to a spoon. Uncovered, because a little reduction concentrates the broth, and gently, because a hard boil emulsifies the fat into the liquid and makes it cloudy and greasy instead of clean. Top up with hot water if it drops below the beef. Taste for salt at the end, not the beginning.\n\nThe bay leaves are standing in for daun salam and they are a fair stand-in, but use two and not four — the same quantity that suits the milder Indonesian leaf will push a eucalyptus note through a broth this delicate.\n\nStir in the 10ml of lime juice off the heat, right at the end. Acid added during the simmer dulls the black towards grey-brown; added at the end it lifts a soup that is otherwise deep, dark and slightly heavy, and it is the difference between rawon that tastes finished and rawon that tastes like it needs something.\n\nServe it with the 40g of bean sprouts RAW, put into the bowl before the hot broth goes over them, or handed round separately. This is not a garnish and it is worth insisting on. The whole dish is soft, dark and savoury, and short raw bean sprouts are the only crunch and the only fresh note in it — they wilt slightly in the heat and keep their snap. Cooked into the pot they collapse and contribute nothing. Traditionally these are the small stubby sprouts rather than the long ones, and if yours are long, snap them in half.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Skim 5 min · Simmer 2 hr","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 15g · Fat 33g · Sodium ~1180mg","storage":"Rawon is genuinely better on day two, and unusually for a soup the reason is the keluak — its flavour keeps moving into the beef and the broth overnight, and the slight bitterness it carries on the first day softens into something rounder. Cool it uncovered to room temperature and refrigerate in a closed container for up to 4 days. It will set with a dark cap of fat on top; stir that back in on reheating, it is where a good deal of the flavour lives. Reheat gently to a bare simmer, never a rolling boil, for the same reason as the first cook — hard heat emulsifies the fat and turns a clean broth greasy. It freezes very well for 3 months with no loss at all, which makes a double batch the sensible way to cook it given how long it takes and how dear the nuts are. ⛔ Store the bean sprouts entirely separately and add them raw to each bowl — sprouts left sitting in hot broth go limp within ten minutes and slimy by the next day, and they are the only texture the dish has. Cooked rice also keeps separately, never in the soup.","chefNotes":"The nuts are the whole dish, so inspect them one by one and throw out any that are pale, hard, chalky or sharp-smelling — one bad nut ruins the pot and there is no rescuing it. Sieve the mashed keluak or the broth will be gritty. Then remember it is a SOUP, not a curry: skim the beef until the surface runs clear, fry the bumbu and the keluak separately, and only then bring them together. Lime juice off the heat at the very end. Bean sprouts raw in the bowl, never cooked in the pot.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice, either alongside or with the soup ladled straight over it, which is how it is usually eaten and is why the dish is often called nasi rawon. The traditional plate carries a salted duck egg cut in half, a spoonful of sambal terasi, and prawn crackers, and every one of those is there to push against the darkness of the broth — salt, heat and crunch respectively. A wedge of lime on the side lets each person sharpen their own bowl. Fried tempe alongside works for the same reason the sprouts do.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Tempe Goreng"],"trivia":"The same tree that gives this dish its colour was used across the archipelago to poison fish. Pangium edule seeds are loaded with cyanogenic glycosides, compounds that release hydrogen cyanide when the seed tissue is crushed, and crushed seeds thrown into a pool would stun or kill everything in it. Raw keluak is not a mild risk; it is dangerous. What makes rawon extraordinary is that somebody worked out how to disarm it — boil the nuts, then bury them in ash or damp earth and leave them for a month or more, during which the tissue breaks down, the toxins degrade and disperse, and the cream-coloured flesh turns black. The colour is the receipt. It is one of the most striking pieces of food technology anywhere: not a preservation method, not a flavour method, but a deliberate multi-week detoxification, arrived at without any understanding of what was being removed, and then built into a national dish rather than merely tolerated. The nuts are sold ready-processed everywhere they are sold at all, which is why no recipe asks you to do any of this.\n\nRawon itself belongs to East Java and to Surabaya in particular, and it is old — a dish under that name appears in the Javanese Nagarakretagama-era records and in inscriptions discussed as far back as the tenth century, which makes it one of the oldest continuously eaten Indonesian dishes with a recognisable identity. The accompaniments are unusually fixed for Indonesian food, where almost everything else is negotiable at the cart: short raw bean sprouts, a salted duck egg, sambal and crackers appear together so consistently that a bowl without them looks wrong. There is also a small piece of Surabaya restaurant folklore worth knowing — rawon setan, devil's rawon, named not for its heat but for the hours, since the original stall was known for serving it in the middle of the night. And the colour has a practical consequence nobody mentions: keluak stains. It will mark a wooden spoon, a plastic chopping board and a white shirt permanently, which is a reasonable price for the only naturally jet-black soup in the world.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid two hours in and finding something genuinely black in the pot, then the crack of raw bean sprouts against beef that falls apart on the spoon.","leftovers":["(easy) Better the next day as it is — reheat gently to a bare simmer and add fresh raw sprouts, and it will taste rounder and less bitter than it did on the first evening.","(easy) Beef lifted out, shredded, and piled onto hot rice with a spoonful of the reduced broth over it, which turns a soup into a rice bowl.","(medium) Broth reduced hard by half in an open pan until it is thick and glossy, then used as a black sauce over noodles or fried tempe — an entirely different dish out of what is left.","(creative) Shredded beef fried in a little oil with kecap manis and chilli until the edges go crisp, then folded through fried rice, where the black bumbu still clinging to the meat colours everything faintly grey-purple and tastes remarkable."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Rawon Bumbu Instan — the Sachet Route (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":54,"howThisFeels":"Black in the pot in twenty minutes of work instead of an hour, and honestly close.","trivia":"This is the one shortcut in Indonesian cooking that is genuinely worth taking, and the reason is that a bumbu rawon sachet actually contains keluak — it is not a seasoning standing in for the nut but a different way of buying the same thing. That makes the arithmetic very different from most convenience products: a sachet costs about a tenth of what a pack of whole nuts costs to buy, and works out cheaper per plate as well. What you give up is control and the black is usually a shade less intense, because the keluak content is fixed and modest and there is no frying step to deepen it. What you do not give up is the dish. The sachets are specialty-importer stock rather than supermarket shelf, so it is worth buying two when you find them.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"12g keluak"},{"item":"8g galangal"},{"item":"10g lemongrass"},{"item":"5g ground turmeric"}],"addIng":[{"item":"12g rawon spice mix"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The nut inspection, the sieving and the separate bumbu all disappear. Skim the beef exactly as described — that step is about the broth and no sachet does it for you, and it is half of what makes rawon a soup rather than a stew. Fry the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic and the 15g of red chilli in the oil for 3 minutes, then add the 12g of rawon spice mix and fry it for a further 90 seconds, stirring; even a paste from a packet is better for a short fry, because it takes the flat raw edge off and deepens the colour a little. Scrape it into the skimmed beef with the tamarind and bay leaves and simmer the same 90 minutes to 2 hours. Expect a slightly softer, less inky black and a rounder, less bitter flavour. Push the lime juice up to 15ml at the end — the sachet versions are usually a touch sweeter and flatter, and the extra acid is what brings them back into line. Raw bean sprouts in the bowl, unchanged and non-negotiable."}]}},{"name":"Rawon Surabaya — Whole Keluak","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":84,"howThisFeels":"Cracking nuts open one by one and throwing two away, which feels wasteful right up until you taste what the good ones do.","trivia":"Frying the sieved keluak paste in oil before it goes anywhere near the pot is the step that separates a properly black rawon from a murky brown one, and it is worth knowing why rather than taking it on trust. Raw keluak paste is starchy and carries a distinct bitterness; two minutes in hot fat does two things at once — it cooks that starch out, which is what lets the colour disperse cleanly through a broth instead of clouding it, and it converts the raw bitterness into something much closer to dark chocolate. You can watch it happen: the paste shifts from a dull brown-black to a glossy true black in the pan. Tipped in raw, the same nuts give a soup that is greyer, grittier and faintly harsh, and no amount of extra simmering corrects it."},{"name":"Rawon Rusuk — Short Rib on the Bone","icon":"🦴","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":89,"howThisFeels":"A bone standing up out of a black bowl, and broth with enough body to coat the back of a spoon.","trivia":"Rusuk means rib, and this is the version restaurants in Surabaya charge more for. The difference is not the meat but the bone. Beef ribs carry marrow and a great deal of collagen in the connective tissue around them, and over a long gentle simmer both dissolve into the broth — the collagen as gelatine, which gives the liquid a faint silkiness and body that a cube of brisket cannot, and the marrow as fat and flavour. It is the same reason a bone broth behaves differently from a meat stock. The trade is time and patience: bone-in meat needs a longer simmer to reach the same tenderness, and the skimming matters more at the start because bones throw far more foam than muscle does.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g beef brisket","to":"220g beef short rib"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 220g of beef short rib rather than 180g of brisket — the extra weight is bone, so the edible meat is roughly the same. Cut between the bones into individual pieces if they come as a rack. The skimming at the start becomes the important part rather than a tidy habit: bones release considerably more grey foam than muscle, and it will keep rising for a good eight or ten minutes rather than five, so stay with it and keep lifting it off until the surface genuinely runs clear. Then simmer 2 hours 30 minutes to 3 hours, gently and uncovered, until the meat is pulling back from the bone and a skewer slides through it with no resistance. Do not rush it with a harder boil; the collagen needs time at low heat to become gelatine, and a hard boil just makes the broth cloudy. Skim the fat off the top before serving if you want it cleaner, or leave it, which is what most places do. Everything else is identical, including the lime off the heat and the raw sprouts in the bowl."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-gudeg","name":"Gudeg","nameAlt":"Yogyakarta jackfruit stew","aliases":["Gudeg","Gudeg Jogja","Gudeg Yogyakarta","Gudheg","Jackfruit Stew","Young Jackfruit Curry","Gudeg Basah","Gudeg Kering"],"course":"main","type":["main","slow-cooked","vegan","jackfruit","sweet-savoury","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","celebration","winter"],"ingredients":"200g young jackfruit · 200ml coconut milk · 300ml water · 25g jaggery · 15g shallots · 15ml sunflower oil · 10g garlic · 8g macadamia nuts · 6g galangal · 5g coriander seeds · 5g red chilli · 4g black tea · 3g salt · 3 bay leaves","method":"Every other brown stew you have cooked got its colour from heat — a seared surface, a browned onion, a caramelised paste. Gudeg gets almost none of its colour that way, and understanding that is what stops you cooking it wrong. There is no searing step here and nothing gets fried dark. The mahogany comes from two places: a leaf steeped in water, and sugar held for hours just under the boil. So the whole dish is built around leaving things alone, which makes it the least demanding three hours in this book and the easiest to ruin by fussing.\n\nSTART WITH THE JACKFRUIT, BECAUSE THE TIN IS THE RISK. Young green jackfruit is sold here in brine, and that brine is aggressive — it carries a metallic sourness that will sit underneath everything for three hours and never cook out. Drain the 200g, then rinse it in three changes of cold water, then put it in a small pot of fresh water, bring it to the boil and drain it again. Two minutes of work, and the reason it matters is that everything else in this recipe is gentle and sweet; there is nothing loud enough in the pot to hide a tinned edge behind.\n\nNow TEAR IT, DO NOT CHOP IT. Pull the pieces apart with your fingers into rough 3cm chunks, following the grain you can see running through each wedge. This is not fussiness — the fibres are the entire texture of gudeg, and they are what will slowly drink the coconut milk and go soft and stringy and silky. A knife cuts across those fibres and gives you neat cubes that collapse into square pulp instead. Your hands separate along them. Squeeze each piece gently as you go; jackfruit is spongy and holds water, and water it releases into the pot later is liquid you were counting on evaporating.\n\nTHE COLOUR NEXT, AND THIS IS THE SUBSTITUTION THAT MAKES THE DISH POSSIBLE HERE. Traditionally the brown comes from teak leaves, daun jati, thrown into the pot for their tannins. Teak does not grow to hand here, so use strong black tea instead, which is not a compromise dressed up as one — it is the same class of compound doing the same job. Steep the 4g of black tea in the 300ml of water, just off the boil, for 5 minutes, until it is very dark and slightly drying to taste. Then strain the leaf out and throw it away. Do not be tempted to leave it in the pot for the long cook: tannins keep extracting, and past about ten minutes you cross over from colour into a mouth-drying astringency that fights the coconut milk and makes the finished stew taste like cold tea. Five minutes, out, done.\n\nTHE BUMBU, POUNDED NOT BLENDED. Toast the 5g of coriander seeds in a dry pan over medium heat for 60 to 90 seconds, until they smell warm and nutty and one or two start to jump — that dry heat drives off the raw grassy note and is the difference between coriander tasting of spice and tasting of dust. Tip them into a mortar with the 15g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 6g of galangal, the 5g of red chilli and the 3g of salt, and pound to a coarse, slightly rough paste. The macadamias are standing in for candlenuts and it is the standard swap here for a good reason: both are high-fat, low-flavour nuts whose job in a bumbu is body rather than taste. They emulsify into the sauce as they cook and hold the coconut milk together, which is why a gudeg made without any nut at all tends to split into oil and grit by hour two.\n\nFry that paste in the 15ml of oil over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, until it stops smelling raw and sharp and the shallots have gone translucent. STOP THERE — do not take it dark. You are not building colour in this pan, the tea has already done that, and a bumbu fried to brown gives the finished stew a roasted, bitter edge that pulls against the sweetness the whole dish is organised around.\n\nNOW ASSEMBLE IN LAYERS, AND THEN DO NOT STIR IT. Not once, not to check, not at the end. Jackfruit into the pot first, the fried bumbu spooned over the top, the 3 bay leaves tucked in, the 25g of jaggery scattered over, then the strained tea poured in, and the 200ml of coconut milk over everything last. Lid on. The lowest heat your hob will hold — you want the barest tremble at the edge, not a simmer. Three hours minimum. The reason for the no-stirring rule is mechanical: those torn fibres soften long before the flavour is finished, and every stir shears them, so a pot that has been stirred four times is a brown purée with good flavour rather than gudeg. The traditional pot is a clay kendil sat on embers overnight precisely because that heat is too weak to move anything. If you must look, tilt the pot and peer in rather than putting a spoon into it.\n\nTHE JAGGERY IS 25g PER PERSON AND THAT IS NOT A TYPO. It reads as an absurd amount of sugar for a savoury dish and it is correct, because it is not there for sweetness alone. Held for hours in liquid a few degrees under boiling, sugar slowly darkens and turns faintly bitter-caramel, and that is the second half of both the colour and the depth — it is the reason a good gudeg tastes long and slightly smoky rather than merely sweet. Caramelising in water is far slower and gentler than in a dry pan and you cannot hurry it with more heat, because more heat boils the coconut milk and splits it into oil and curds. The clock is the technique.\n\nFINISH, AND SALT ONLY NOW. At three hours the jackfruit should be completely soft, stringy, the colour of dark toffee, sitting in a loose glossy sauce. Taste, and add salt at this point if it needs it — never earlier. Three hours of reduction concentrates whatever salt is in the pot, and gudeg seasoned at the start comes out genuinely inedible. If you want the drier Yogyakarta style, take the lid off and give it another 45 to 60 minutes at the same low heat until the sauce has almost gone and what is left just clings to the fibres. Let it stand off the heat for 10 minutes before serving, which lets the fat settle back in rather than pooling on top.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Steep 5 min · Simmer 3 hr","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 7g · Carbs 42g · Fat 32g · Sodium ~980mg","storage":"Gudeg is one of the few stews that is unarguably better on the third day, and the reason is the same reason it exists in a dry version at all — the jackfruit fibres keep drawing the sweet, tannic, coconut-heavy liquid into themselves long after the pot has come off the heat, so a stew that tasted like sauce and fruit on the first evening tastes like one single thing by the third. Cool it uncovered to room temperature, then refrigerate in a closed container for up to 5 days, which is longer than most coconut-milk dishes and is down to the sugar. Reheat gently and covered, with a splash of water if it has gone tight, and once again resist stirring it — lift and fold once at the very end if you must. The dry version keeps longer still, up to a week refrigerated, and freezes better than the wet one because there is less liquid to separate; both freeze acceptably for 3 months, though the wet version can look split on thawing and needs a slow gentle reheat to come back together. ⛔ Store rice separately, always, and never reheat gudeg twice — the coconut milk turns grainy on a second pass and no amount of gentleness recovers it.","chefNotes":"Rinse and then boil-and-drain the tinned jackfruit before anything else, or a metallic brine note sits under the whole pot for three hours. Tear it along the grain rather than chopping it, because the fibres are the texture. Steep the tea 5 minutes and take the leaf OUT — left in, tannin turns colour into mouth-drying astringency. Fry the bumbu only to translucent, never to brown. Then layer it, lid on, lowest heat, and DO NOT STIR IT ONCE for three hours; stirring shears the soft fibres and gives you purée. The 25g of jaggery is correct and is doing colour and depth, not just sweetness. Salt at the very end only.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice, which is not a default so much as a requirement — gudeg is sweet, dense and soft, and it needs something neutral and dry underneath it to keep from cloying. The classic Yogyakarta plate then adds three things that all push against it in different directions: a fierce spoonful of sambal for heat, something crisp and fried for texture, and an egg. Fried tempe or a potato fritter alongside covers the crunch, and a wedge of cucumber or a handful of raw beansprouts does more work than it looks like it should, because raw cold vegetable is the fastest way to reset the palate between mouthfuls of something this rich. Strong unsweetened tea is the traditional drink with it, which is a neat circle given where the colour came from.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Perkedel","Tempe Goreng"],"trivia":"The brown in a traditional gudeg comes from teak leaves — from the same tree that becomes ship decks, garden benches and floorboards. Teak, Tectona grandis, is a timber tree to most of the world and a dye plant across Java, where the leaves have long been used both to wrap food and to colour it a deep red-brown, and it is genuinely strange that a wood most people associate with patio furniture has an edible leaf sitting on it. What the leaf contributes is tannins, and the word is worth pausing on, because it is not a coincidence: tannin is named after tanning, the business of turning raw hide into leather, and it is named that way because that is what the compounds physically do. Tannins bind to proteins and lock them together. That single mechanism explains three things at once — why they turn skin into leather, why they will stain a wooden spoon and a white shirt, and why strong black tea dries your mouth out, which is tannins precipitating the proteins in your own saliva. It is also why substituting black tea for teak leaves is chemistry rather than desperation.\n\nThe same tannins are the reason the eggs come out of the pot brown all the way through rather than merely stained. Eggs cooked in tannin-rich liquid — teak leaves, shallot skins, guava leaves — are their own named dish across Java, telur pindang, and the colour goes past the shell and marbles into the white, because the tannins are binding the egg's surface proteins in exactly the way they bind a hide. You are, very mildly, tanning an egg.\n\nGudeg belongs to Yogyakarta so completely that the city is nicknamed Kota Gudeg, and there is a street, Wijilan, where the shops sell essentially nothing else. The story told there ties the dish to the founding of the Mataram court in the late 1500s, when forest was cleared at Kotagede and the two things in overwhelming supply were jackfruit and coconut — plausible, and impossible to verify, which is true of most origin stories worth repeating. What is verifiable is why the dry version exists. Gudeg kering, cooked down until there is barely any liquid left, keeps for the better part of a week without refrigeration, and it is traditionally sold packed into a kendil, a small earthenware pot, as something you carry home from Yogyakarta for people who did not come with you. It is one of the few famous dishes whose two forms exist not for taste but for transport: the wet one is dinner, and the dry one is a souvenir.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid after three hours of doing nothing at all and finding something the colour of old furniture, with the whole kitchen smelling of caramel rather than curry.","leftovers":["(easy) Straight out of the fridge on hot rice with a spoonful of sambal, which is genuinely how it is eaten in Yogyakarta and needs no reheating at all.","(easy) Reheated gently, covered, with a splash of water, and served with something fried alongside for the crunch the second day loses.","(medium) Lid off in an open pan until it goes dry and sticky, then piled into a folded flatbread or a soft roll with cucumber and sambal — the wet version turned into the dry one, and into lunch.","(creative) Chopped fine, the fibres pulled apart with two forks, and folded through fried rice at the very end with a little kecap manis, where the sweetness and the tannin give the whole pan a smoky brown depth that nothing else does."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Gudeg Sayur — Jackfruit Alone (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":47,"howThisFeels":"A pot you are actively forbidden from touching, which turns out to be the easiest cooking there is.","trivia":"This is closer to how gudeg is actually eaten most days than the version with eggs and chicken piled on it, and the two things it drops are the two most expensive lines in the recipe. Losing the macadamias costs you something real, so it is worth knowing what: without a fatty nut in the bumbu there is nothing emulsifying the sauce, so it will be thinner and more inclined to look oily where it meets the pot. Cutting the coconut milk back and making up the volume with the tea liquid does the same thing in the other direction. The fix is not to add anything back but to reduce harder at the end, because concentration and emulsification produce a similar glossiness by different routes — you are simply getting there with time instead of fat.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g macadamia nuts"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"200ml coconut milk","to":"120ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the macadamias entirely and pound the bumbu without them — it will be looser and wetter than a nut paste, which is fine. Use 120ml of coconut milk rather than 200ml and make the difference up with the tea liquid, so the total volume going into the pot is unchanged and the three-hour timing still works. Everything else is identical, including the no-stirring rule. Then change one thing at the end: take the lid off for the last 30 to 40 minutes instead of finishing covered, and let the sauce reduce further than you otherwise would, until it is thick and clinging rather than loose. That reduction is doing the job the nut fat would have done, and a thinner gudeg cooked down further tastes closer to the full version than a thin one served wet. Do not raise the heat to speed it up; the coconut milk is more fragile at this dilution, not less."}]}},{"name":"Gudeg Basah with Telur Pindang","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":63,"howThisFeels":"Peeling an egg that went into the pot white and came out marbled brown the whole way through.","trivia":"The eggs are not a garnish and they are not there for protein — they are there because they are the one thing in the dish that visibly records what the pot has been doing. Hard-boiled and peeled before they go in, they spend the last 90 minutes absorbing tannin and caramel, and they come out brown right through the white in a marbled, uneven way that no dye achieves, with a faintly savoury-sweet edge to them. Peeling them BEFORE they go in rather than after is the whole trick and it is often got wrong: an egg in its shell picks up colour only on the shell, because the shell is a barrier the tannins cannot cross in the time available. Peeled, the white is exposed directly, and the tannins bind to its surface proteins the way they would to a hide. Slide them in gently, tuck them down among the jackfruit so they sit under the liquid, and then leave them alone with everything else.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"2 large eggs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Hard-boil the 2 large eggs separately — 9 minutes from boiling for a set but not chalky yolk — then cool them under cold running water and PEEL THEM COMPLETELY before they go anywhere near the gudeg. Unpeeled eggs colour only on the shell and come out white underneath, which is the single most common mistake with this dish. At the 90-minutes-remaining mark, lift the lid for the only time in the cook, slide the peeled eggs in and press them down gently with the back of a spoon until the liquid covers them, then put the lid straight back on and leave it. They will come out mottled brown, faintly sweet at the surface, and slightly firmer than a plain boiled egg because the tannins have tightened the outer white. Halve them to serve so the marbling shows."}]}},{"name":"Gudeg Kering — the Dry Pot with Chicken","icon":"🍯","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":70,"howThisFeels":"The last hour with the lid off, when the sauce stops being liquid and starts clinging to everything it touches.","trivia":"Dry gudeg is not simply wet gudeg left on the stove longer, although that is where it starts. Taking the liquid down to almost nothing changes what the sugar is doing: at low water content the caramelisation that had been creeping along for three hours accelerates sharply, so the last hour contributes more colour and more of that bitter-edged depth than the two before it, and the dish goes from toffee-brown to genuinely dark. That is also why it keeps for the better part of a week — very little free water and a lot of sugar is an unfriendly place for bacteria, which is the same reason jam keeps, and it is why this is the version packed into earthenware pots and carried out of Yogyakarta. Adding chicken changes the timing rather than the method: thighs go in at the halfway mark, because a full three hours of even this gentle heat will take them past tender and into dry and stringy.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"2 large eggs"},{"item":"80g chicken thighs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 80g of chicken thighs, bone in or out, cut into two or three pieces — thigh rather than breast because this is a long wet cook and there is enough fat and connective tissue in a thigh to survive it, where breast simply seizes and goes dry. At the halfway point, 90 minutes in, lift the lid once and add the chicken along with the 2 peeled hard-boiled eggs, pushing both down under the liquid, then cover again. Ninety minutes is deliberate: any longer and the chicken overcooks even at this temperature, any shorter and it has not taken on the bumbu. At the three-hour mark take the lid OFF and let it go a further 45 to 60 minutes at the same low heat, without stirring, until the liquid has almost entirely gone and what is left is thick, dark and just coating the fibres. Watch it in the last 15 minutes rather than walking away — with almost no water left the sugar can catch on the base very quickly, and a burnt bottom taints the whole pot. Tilt and look; still do not stir. It should be dark, sticky and barely wet, and it will thicken further as it cools."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Perkedel","target":"indonesia-perkedel","emoji":"🥔"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-rujak","name":"Rujak","nameAlt":"Sour fruit in chilli palm sugar","aliases":["Rujak","Rujak Buah","Rujak Manis","Rojak","Indonesian Fruit Salad","Spicy Fruit Salad","Rujak Serut","Rujak Petis"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","snack","salad","no-cook","fruit","spicy","quick","street-food"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","party","summer","braai"],"ingredients":"80g pineapple · 70g green mango · 60g cucumber · 60g green papaya · 20g jaggery · 15g peanuts · 5g red chilli · 5g tamarind paste · 3g shrimp paste · 3g salt","method":"This is a fruit salad in the same way that a sambal is a hot sauce, which is to say not really. There is no cream, no honey, nothing soft, and nothing in it is sweet on its own terms. What you are making is hard sour fruit under a dressing that is salty, fiery, sour and dark-sweet all at once, and the whole thing works on a single mechanism that most recipes never mention: THE SALT PULLS THE DRESSING OUT OF THE FRUIT. You never add water to rujak. The liquid in the bowl at the end came out of the pineapple.\n\nSO START AT THE SHOP, BECAUSE UNRIPE IS THE INGREDIENT AND NOT A COMPROMISE. Every instinct you have about buying fruit is wrong here. You want the mango rock hard, pale green, sour enough to make you wince, the kind you would put back on the shelf. You want the pineapple firm and a little sharp, not the fragrant golden one. The papaya must be green and dense with white-ish flesh, nowhere near orange. Choose by feel and by sound rather than by colour — flick the fruit with a fingernail and you want a hard knock, not a dull thud. The reason is structural rather than aesthetic: as fruit ripens its pectin breaks down and its starch converts to sugar, so a ripe mango has neither the crunch to survive being salted nor the acidity to stand up to chilli and shrimp paste. Ripe fruit in rujak does not taste like a sweeter rujak. It collapses into a sweet wet mess within minutes and the dressing has nothing to hold onto.\n\nTHE SHRIMP PASTE, BRIEFLY, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THIS CARD'S LESSON. The 3g of shrimp paste must be toasted before it goes in, or it will taste sharp and faintly soapy and that will not cook out — nothing here gets cooked at all. Wrap it in a small square of foil, flatten it, dry pan over medium heat, about 2 minutes a side, until the ammonia edge drops away and it smells savoury and roasted and crumbles rather than smears. The Sambal Terasi card explains what is happening and why in full; this is the short version, and it is non-negotiable even in a raw dish.\n\nNOW THE DRESSING, AND POUND IT THICK ON PURPOSE. Into a mortar go the 5g of red chilli, the 3g of salt and the toasted shrimp paste, and pound them to a rough paste. Then the 20g of jaggery, broken up, and keep pounding — jaggery is soft and slightly damp and it will smear into the chilli rather than dissolving, which is what you want, because a granular sugar dissolved in liquid gives you sweet water while a smeared soft sugar gives you body. Then the 5g of tamarind paste, worked through. Finally the 15g of peanuts, and this is a deliberate ordering: the peanuts go in LAST and get only a few strokes, because they are texture, not sauce. Pounded early they turn oily and thicken everything into a spread. You are after a dressing you could spoon, with visible pieces of nut in it, and it should look far too thick and far too strong. Taste it. It should be almost unpleasantly intense on a fingertip — salty, hot, sour, sweet, in that order of arrival. If it tastes balanced and pleasant now, it will taste of nothing once the fruit has diluted it, and undersalting the bumbu is the most common failure with this dish.\n\nCUT THE FRUIT BIG, AND CUT IT LAST. Peel the 70g of green mango, the 60g of green papaya and the 60g of cucumber, and cut everything including the 80g of pineapple into chunks roughly 3cm across — bite-sized, but only just. Small dice is a mistake and the reason is surface area: the more cut face you create, the faster salt draws water out, and diced rujak floods in half the time. If the cucumber has large watery seeds, scrape them out with a spoon, because that core carries the most water and the least crunch. Do not salt the fruit, do not rinse it after cutting, and do not chill it in water.\n\nTHEN DRESS IT AT THE VERY LAST MOMENT, AND UNDERSTAND THE WINDOW. Tip the fruit into the mortar with the bumbu, or into a bowl over it, and turn it through with a spoon until every piece is coated. What happens next is osmosis, and it is both the point of the dish and the clock on it. Salt on cut fruit draws juice out through the cell walls, that juice thins the too-thick bumbu into a proper glossy dressing, and for about the first ten minutes the result is perfect — coated, crunchy, sitting in a small amount of intense liquid. Somewhere around twenty to thirty minutes the same process has gone too far: the fruit has given up too much water, the dressing is pale and dilute, and the crunch has gone slack. There is no recovering it. So mix it when people are standing there, not before, and if you need to get ahead then make the bumbu hours in advance and cut the fruit into a dry covered bowl, and put the two together at the table.\n\nServe it in the mortar if you have a stone one, which is how it is sold from carts, and eat it with a spoon or with your fingers straight away. Taste before serving and adjust with a pinch more salt rather than more sugar — if it reads flat, it is almost always salt it wants, not sweetness.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · No cook · Eat within 10 min of mixing","kcal":"~280 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 7g · Carbs 44g · Fat 9g · Sodium ~1250mg","storage":"⛔ Rujak does not store, and this is the one dish in this section where that is a hard rule rather than a preference. Once dressed, salt keeps drawing water out of the cut fruit whether you are eating it or not, so a bowl put in the fridge for later comes out as pale fruit sitting in a pool of thin sweet liquid with no crunch left in it. An hour is already too long. What you CAN do is store the two halves separately, and it works well: the pounded bumbu keeps in a closed jar in the fridge for up to 5 days and genuinely improves after a day as the tamarind and jaggery come together, while cut fruit keeps in a dry sealed container for about 24 hours if you do not salt it. Then mix a fresh portion whenever you want one, which turns a fussy dish into a very fast one. Undressed fruit that has started to weep on its own should be drained before it meets the bumbu, or it will dilute it from the start. Nothing here freezes — freezing ruptures exactly the cell walls whose integrity is the entire texture of the dish.","chefNotes":"Buy the fruit HARD, green and sour — ripe fruit is the failure, not a milder version, because it has neither the crunch to survive salting nor the acid to stand up to chilli. Toast the shrimp paste in foil even though nothing gets cooked. Pound the bumbu much thicker and much stronger than seems sensible, and put the peanuts in last so they stay as texture rather than turning the dressing oily. Cut the fruit in big 3cm chunks, never diced, because cut surface is what lets it flood. Then dress it at the table: perfect for ten minutes, ruined by thirty, and never add water — the liquid comes out of the fruit.","pairsWith":"It belongs before or beside hot, rich, fatty food rather than after it, which is why it turns up at the start of a meal and at the side of a grill. Anything charred and oily is the natural partner — skewers off the fire, fried tempe, a plate of grilled chicken — because the salt and acid in the bowl do the job a squeeze of lemon does for fish, only harder. It is also very good on its own in the middle of a hot afternoon, which is how most of it is actually eaten. Keep the drink plain and cold and unsweetened; anything sugary alongside flattens the dressing completely. If you are serving it as part of a bigger spread, put it next to something with real crunch and something with real chilli heat and let people build their own contrast.","goesWith":["Sate Ayam","Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Pepes Ikan"],"trivia":"In Java, rujak has a job that has nothing to do with eating. At the seven-month point of a pregnancy there is a ceremony — tingkeban, also called mitoni, from pitu, seven — and rujak is made for it and shared among the guests. The taste is then read as an omen: the tradition holds that if the guests find it sharp and fiery the child will be a girl, and if it comes out mild, a boy. It is a folk belief rather than anything more, but it has a lovely internal logic, because a rujak's heat depends on how heavy-handed the mother-to-be was with the chilli, so the prediction is in a real sense hers to make. The same ceremony has a small piece of theatre attached where a drink is symbolically sold to the guests and paid for with kreweng, broken shards of pottery standing in for money. A dish that most of the world would file as a snack is, in its home, closer to a ritual object — and this is not a historical curiosity, it still happens.\n\nThe other thing worth knowing is that rujak is not really a dish at all; it is a grammar. The word names a way of dressing things — something sour, something salty-fermented, chilli, palm sugar — and then it gets applied to almost anything, which is why the family is so wildly wide. Rujak buah is the fruit one here. Rujak petis swaps in a black fermented shrimp-and-fish paste and goes dark and pungent. Rujak cingur, from Surabaya, puts the same dressing on boiled cow's muzzle, which is exactly as startling as it sounds and is a genuinely beloved dish. Rujak serut grates everything and serves it icy, and rujak es krim, from Yogyakarta, goes the whole way and puts ice cream in it. In Malay the cognate word rojak escaped the kitchen altogether and became the ordinary word for a mixture or a jumble — people use it to describe mixed-up speech, the way English borrowed \"salad\" for something patched together. It is a rare case of a food name becoming a general-purpose word for eclecticism, and the food earned it.","howThisFeels":"The first bite is aggressively wrong — salt and chilli on fruit — and by the third one you cannot stop, standing over the mortar eating it with a spoon.","leftovers":["(easy) There are no dressed leftovers, by design, but the bumbu keeps 5 days in a jar and is better on day two — so cut a fresh bowl of fruit and you have the dish again in three minutes.","(easy) Leftover bumbu thinned with a little lime juice makes a fierce dipping sauce for anything fried, which is what most of it gets used for.","(medium) Dressed rujak that has already wept can be drained, the fruit chopped small and folded into a fresh green salad, where the dilute dressing works properly as a vinaigrette.","(creative) The bumbu spooned over hot grilled chicken or fish straight off the fire, where the sugar in it catches slightly on the heat and it stops being a salad dressing and becomes a glaze."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Rujak Manis — Sweet and Sour Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":15,"howThisFeels":"Sharper and cleaner than you expect, and gone from the bowl faster than the full version.","trivia":"Dropping the shrimp paste and the peanuts is not a lesser rujak — rujak manis is its own named thing and it is what children and most vegetarians in Java eat. It is worth knowing exactly what leaves with them, though, because you have to compensate for both. The shrimp paste was carrying the savoury depth, the thing that made the dressing taste like a sauce rather than a syrup, and nothing plant-based replaces that on its own; the fix is more tamarind and noticeably more salt, so the sourness and the saltiness take over the job that fermented depth was doing. The peanuts were the texture, so without them the fruit has to carry all of it, which means the buying advice stops being important and becomes the whole dish: hard, green, sour fruit or nothing. Slightly less sugar too, because with no savoury weight underneath it the sweetness reads much louder than it did.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"15g peanuts"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"5g tamarind paste","to":"8g tamarind paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the shrimp paste entirely, which also means skipping the foil and the toasting, and leave the peanuts out. Use 8g of tamarind paste rather than 5g. Pound the chilli and the 3g of salt first, then the jaggery, then work the tamarind through — and taste it far more carefully than you would the full version, because there is no fermented savoury depth to hide behind and the balance is genuinely more delicate. It should still be too strong on a fingertip. Expect to want a little more salt than the main recipe, not less: salt is now doing the work the shrimp paste was doing, and undersalted rujak manis tastes like sweet chilli fruit. Everything else is unchanged, and the ten-minute window is if anything tighter, since there is no nut in the bowl slowing the dilution down."}]}},{"name":"Rujak Buah — the Full Bumbu with Terasi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"Lifting the pestle and smelling roasted shrimp paste and palm sugar together, which should not work and completely does.","trivia":"The toasted shrimp paste is doing something more specific than adding flavour, and it is the reason this version tastes finished where the sweet one tastes bright. Fermented krill paste is dense in free glutamates and nucleotides, the same compounds that make anchovies, aged cheese and fish sauce taste savoury and mouth-filling, and 3g of it is enough to give a bowl of raw fruit the roundness of something cooked. That is why the pairing sounds absurd on paper and works in the mouth: the fruit brings acid and sweetness and crunch, and the terasi brings the one thing fruit categorically cannot bring. Toasting matters because raw paste also carries volatile ammonia notes that read as sharp and slightly soapy, and dry heat drives those off while leaving the savoury compounds behind. Untoasted, the same 3g will dominate the bowl for the wrong reasons."},{"name":"Rujak Serut — Grated and Iced","icon":"🧊","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"Cold enough to make your teeth ache, and closer to a drink than a salad by the last spoonful.","trivia":"This is the one version that deliberately breaks the rule the main recipe is built on. Serut means grated or shaved, and grating the fruit destroys the crunch and multiplies the cut surface enormously, so the flooding that takes twenty minutes with 3cm chunks happens almost instantly — which here is the intention rather than the failure. What you get is not a salad but a slushy, icy, intensely flavoured bowl somewhere between a fruit salad and a granita, and it is a hot-afternoon street food rather than a starter. Cold is what buys back what the crunch gave up: at fridge temperature the dilute dressing reads as refreshing instead of slack, and the extra lime keeps the whole thing sharp enough to stay interesting. Yogyakarta takes it one step further and puts a scoop of ice cream on top, which sounds like a joke and is a real and popular thing called rujak es krim.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"10ml lime juice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the bumbu exactly as described, then chill it thoroughly — at least an hour in the fridge, because everything in this version depends on being properly cold and a room-temperature dressing will not get there once the fruit is in. Instead of cutting the fruit into chunks, coarsely grate the green mango, the green papaya and the cucumber on the large holes of a box grater, and chop the pineapple as finely as you can rather than grating it, since it shreds into stringy fibres. Keep the grated fruit in the fridge, drained, until the last minute. Then stir in the chilled bumbu and the 10ml of lime juice and serve immediately in a cold bowl — the extra acid is there because dilution happens instantly at this surface area and lime is what keeps it from going flat. Eat it with a spoon while it is still icy. Do not try to hold it: unlike the chunky version, which has a ten-minute window, this one is at its best in the first minute and is a bowl of sweet water inside of ten."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-tahu-gejrot","name":"Tahu Gejrot","nameAlt":"Puffed tofu in sharp chilli broth","aliases":["Tahu Gejrot","Tahu Gejrot Cirebon","Tahu Pong","Smashed Tofu in Chilli Broth","Cirebon Tofu","Puffed Tofu Salad","Tahu Goreng Gejrot"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","snack","no-cook-sauce","fried","tofu","vegan","spicy","street-food"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","party","summer","braai"],"ingredients":"120g firm tofu · 400ml sunflower oil · 100ml water · 15g jaggery · 10ml kecap manis · 10g shallots · 8g garlic · 6g red chilli · 5g tamarind paste · 3g salt","method":"The entire dish rests on one thing being true: THE TOFU HAS TO BE HOLLOW. Not crisp, not golden — hollow, with a leathery skin and an open web of nothing inside it. Everything else here is arranged around getting sauce into that cavity, which is also why the sauce looks wrong. It is thin. Watery, even. Every other bumbu in this section is a thick pounded paste, and this one is deliberately let down until it pours like a vinaigrette, because a thick sauce physically cannot enter a sponge. It sits on the outside and you taste tofu and sauce as two separate things. Thin it properly and the tofu drinks, and you taste one thing.\n\nSO START BY PRESSING THE TOFU, BUT NOT TOO HARD. Take the 120g of firm tofu, wrap it in a clean cloth or a few sheets of kitchen paper, sit a plate on top and leave it 20 minutes. What you are after is a specific middle state, and this is the make-or-break of the whole card: the tofu needs to be dry on the OUTSIDE and still wet INSIDE. Surface water stops the skin forming and makes the oil spit violently. Interior water is the entire mechanism — it is what turns to steam and inflates the thing. Press it flat and dry all the way through and it will never puff; it will just go tough. Use silken tofu and it collapses in the pan, because there is no protein network strong enough to hold a bubble. Firm tofu, blotted outside, damp inside.\n\nCut it into rough 4cm squares or triangles, about 2cm thick. Chunky, not thin — a thin slice has nowhere to be hollow.\n\nNOW THE OIL, AND IT HAS TO BE PROPERLY HOT AND PROPERLY DEEP. Heat the 400ml of sunflower oil in a small deep saucepan to 180°C. If you have no thermometer, drop a cube of bread in: it should turn golden in about 40 seconds. This is a real deep fry and the depth matters as much as the temperature, because the tofu must float freely and inflate on all sides at once — in a shallow pan the underside sets flat against the metal and the piece comes out as a fried slab with a crust rather than a shell with a cavity. Below about 170°C nothing puffs at all; the tofu simply sits there absorbing oil and goes greasy and dense, which is the single most common failure. Too hot, past 200°C, and the skin sets and browns before there is time for the inside to generate enough steam to lift it.\n\nLower the pieces in carefully, a few at a time so the temperature does not crash, and fry 6 to 8 minutes, turning once. Watch what happens rather than the clock: for the first two minutes almost nothing, then the pieces begin to swell and lift and float higher in the oil, and the surface goes from smooth to slightly wrinkled and taut. That wrinkling is the sign — it means the skin has set and is being stretched from inside. Fry until they are a deep even gold and noticeably lighter than they went in, then lift them out onto paper.\n\nAND THEN LET THEM COOL, WHICH IS NOT AN INSTRUCTION ABOUT TEMPERATURE. Give them 10 minutes. As they cool the steam inside condenses and the inflated interior collapses inwards into a loose open web — and THAT web is the sponge the whole dish needs. Tofu used straight from the oil is still pressurised with steam and takes up almost no sauce. This is the one wait in a dish that is otherwise immediate, and skipping it is why homemade versions taste dry in the middle.\n\nMAKE THE SAUCE IN THE DISH YOU WILL EAT FROM. Traditionally that is a cowet, a small unglazed clay plate that is mortar and bowl at once, and if you have any kind of stone or clay mortar use it, because there is no transferring and no washing up and the whole point is that the pounding and the serving happen in one vessel. Put in the 6g of red chilli, the 8g of garlic, the 10g of shallots and the 3g of salt, and pound them to a rough wet paste — rough, with pieces still visible. Then work in the 15g of jaggery until it smears rather than dissolves, then the 5g of tamarind paste and the 10ml of kecap manis.\n\nNOW POUR IN THE 100ml OF WATER AND DO NOT PANIC. It will look far too thin, like dirty water with bits in it, and that is correct. Taste it: it should be sharp, salty, sweet and hot in a bracing, almost drinkable way, more like a seasoning liquid than a sauce. If it tastes right as a sauce it is too concentrated to travel into the tofu, and you will get a strong outside and a bland middle. Cold or room-temperature water, never warm — nothing here is cooked and warm water flattens the chilli.\n\nFINALLY, SMASH THE TOFU INTO IT. Put the cooled pieces straight into the dish and press each one down firmly with the pestle or the back of a spoon — not to pulverise it, just enough to rupture the shell and flatten the piece so the cavity opens and the broth floods in. You will hear it. Turn everything over once, leave it 2 to 3 minutes so the tofu drinks, and eat it immediately with a spoon, broth and all. The tofu should be soft and soaked at the centre with the fried skin still slightly chewy at the edges, sitting in a shallow pool of sharp liquid — and the liquid is part of the dish, not something left behind in the bowl.","cookTime":"Press 20 min · Fry 8 min · Cool 10 min · Assemble 5 min","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 13g · Carbs 22g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~1350mg","storage":"Assembled tahu gejrot has a window of about fifteen minutes and then it is finished — the fried skin, which is the only textural contrast in the dish, goes completely soft and the tofu turns from soaked to waterlogged. It does not keep and it cannot be revived. Store it as two halves instead, which works very well: the sauce keeps in a closed jar in the fridge for up to 5 days and is better on day two once the tamarind, jaggery and raw garlic have settled into each other, and the fried tofu keeps in an open container at room temperature for a day, or 3 days in the fridge. ⛔ Never store the fried tofu sealed while it is still warm — trapped steam softens the skin from the inside and you lose the shell you spent 8 minutes building. Re-crisp refrigerated tofu in a dry pan or a hot oven for 5 minutes and let it cool again before it meets the sauce, because the cooling is what reopens the sponge. The tofu freezes surprisingly well for 2 months and comes back with an even more open, chewier texture, since freezing turns the internal water to ice crystals that tear the network wider — a genuine improvement here, and the one dish in this section where freezing makes something better.","chefNotes":"Press the tofu dry on the OUTSIDE and leave it wet INSIDE — the interior water is what steams and inflates it, so tofu pressed all the way through will never puff. Firm, never silken. 400ml of oil at a genuine 180°C and deep enough for the pieces to float, because a shallow pan sets one face flat and you get a slab instead of a shell. Then COOL IT for 10 minutes: the collapse as the steam condenses is what creates the sponge, and hot tofu absorbs almost nothing. Make the sauce far thinner than looks right — a thick sauce cannot get inside a sponge. Smash each piece to rupture the shell, wait 3 minutes, eat immediately.","pairsWith":"It is a street snack rather than a course, so it goes best where a sharp, hot, wet mouthful earns its place against something dry and rich — beside skewers off the fire, alongside fried tempe, or as the first thing on the table while everything else is still cooking. The broth means it needs no sauce of its own and it actively fights anything creamy, so keep it away from peanut dressings, which will bury it. A bowl of plain steamed rice turns it from a snack into a light meal and is how it is often eaten late at night, and compressed rice cake is the traditional version of that. Something cold and unsweetened to drink — the salt level is high and the chilli is raw and immediate, and a sweet drink makes both worse.","goesWith":["Sate Ayam","Tempe Goreng","Sambal Terasi"],"trivia":"The hollow is the whole dish, and it happens for a reason worth understanding, because once you know it you can never get the tofu wrong again. Drop firm tofu into oil at 180°C and two things start racing each other. The proteins at the surface set and dry into a tough, flexible skin within seconds. Meanwhile the water trapped inside hits boiling point and turns to steam, which needs roughly seventeen hundred times the space the water occupied — and it cannot get out, because the skin has already closed over it. So it pushes, and the skin stretches, and the piece inflates like a small leather balloon. That is what the wrinkling on the surface actually is: a skin under tension from the inside. And then the part nobody explains — as it cools, the steam condenses back to a few drops of water, the pressure vanishes, and the inflated interior falls in on itself into a loose open lattice. The puff makes the shell; the COLLAPSE makes the sponge. Which is exactly why the recipe insists on a ten-minute wait, and why tofu straight from the pan tastes dry in the middle no matter how good the sauce is. Indonesian markets sell this ready-made as tahu pong, and pong means hollow.\n\nThe other thing this dish will not let go of is its plate. Tahu gejrot belongs to Cirebon, a port on the north coast of Java, and it is traditionally made and served in a cowet — a small unglazed earthenware dish that does double duty as the mortar. You pound the chilli and garlic in it, you smash the tofu in it, and you eat out of it, and no part of the food ever leaves the vessel it was made in. That is not romance, it is design: unglazed clay is porous, so it holds a faint memory of everything pounded in it before, and a cowet used daily for years seasons the way a wok or a cast-iron pan does. Vendors do not wash them thoroughly for exactly that reason. It is one of very few dishes anywhere that is named for a sound rather than an ingredient or a place — gejrot is usually explained as the noise of the sauce being splashed out over the tofu — and it is fitting that a dish named after a splash cannot be separated from the bowl it splashes into.","howThisFeels":"The give when the pestle goes through a piece of tofu and the broth rushes into the hole you just made.","leftovers":["(easy) Sauce keeps 5 days in a jar and is better on day two — fry fresh tofu and you have the dish again in fifteen minutes.","(easy) Leftover sauce as a dipping liquid for anything fried, spring rolls and fritters especially, where its thinness is a feature rather than something to apologise for.","(medium) Leftover fried tofu re-crisped in a dry pan, cooled, then torn into a green salad with a few spoonfuls of the sauce as the dressing — the sponge does the same job in a bowl of leaves.","(creative) Fried tofu frozen deliberately and thawed, which tears the internal web wider, then simmered in the sauce for 5 minutes instead of soaked cold — it turns a cold snack into a hot braised side that holds far more liquid than the fresh version can."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Tahu Goreng Gejrot — the Shallow-Fry Route (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":33,"howThisFeels":"A frying pan, a tablespoon of oil and fifteen minutes, and most of the dish still arrives.","trivia":"This is the honest cheap route and the saving is not an ingredient, it is 360ml of oil — which is the single most expensive line in the full version and is the reason a street snack costs what a small meal costs when you make it at home. What you lose is real and there is no way to disguise it: a shallow pan sets the underside flat against the metal, so the piece never inflates and you get fried tofu with a crust rather than a shell with a cavity inside. No cavity means no sponge, which means the sauce can only work on the outside. The compensation is geometry — cut the tofu much smaller so there is far more surface for the sauce to work on, and give it a proper soak instead of the quick 3 minutes, because you are now flavouring the outside of many small pieces rather than the inside of a few big ones. It lands at perhaps three quarters of the full version, which for a tablespoon of oil is a very good trade.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400ml sunflower oil","to":"40ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the tofu exactly as described, but cut it much smaller — 2cm cubes rather than 4cm squares. Heat just 40ml of sunflower oil in a frying pan over medium-high heat until a corner of tofu sizzles immediately on contact. Fry the cubes 8 to 10 minutes, turning them every couple of minutes so that every face gets time on the metal, until they are golden and firm all over with a definite chewy skin. They will not puff and they are not supposed to — do not wait for it and do not turn the heat up trying to force it, which only burns the outside. Drain on paper and still let them cool for 10 minutes, because even without a cavity the surface firms up as it cools and takes sauce better cold than hot. Make the sauce identically, then give the cubes a full 10 minutes in it rather than 3, turning them through twice, so the outside really does soak. Eat with a spoon. Expect a livelier, saltier, less subtle bowl than the deep-fried version, and season the sauce a touch lighter to allow for it."}]}},{"name":"Tahu Gejrot Cirebon — the Hollow Fry","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Watching a flat square of tofu swell and lift in the oil, which never stops being satisfying.","trivia":"The raw garlic in the sauce is doing something the cooked version of itself could not, and it is worth knowing why this dish does not fry its aromatics like almost everything else in Indonesian cooking. Crushing raw garlic ruptures its cells and lets an enzyme meet a stored compound, and the reaction produces allicin — the sharp, hot, faintly metallic bite you get from raw garlic and never from cooked, because heat destroys the enzyme before it can do the work. In a dish where nothing is cooked after the frying, that raw pungency is a whole flavour layer, and it is what stops a sauce of sugar, tamarind and soy from reading as merely sweet-and-sour. It also fades: allicin is unstable and breaks down over hours, which is precisely why the sauce is at its sharpest within the first day and turns rounder and mellower by day three. Both are good. They are not the same dish."},{"name":"Tahu Gejrot dengan Lontong — with Compressed Rice","icon":"🍚","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":51,"howThisFeels":"Cold dense slices of rice cake soaking up broth that would otherwise be left in the bowl.","trivia":"Lontong turns this from a snack into a meal, and it is a genuinely clever piece of rice cookery that has nothing in common with cooking rice normally. Rice is packed loosely into a tight wrapper and then boiled in plenty of water for well over an hour — far longer than rice should ever be cooked. Confined, the grains swell until they have nowhere left to expand, burst, and press against each other until the starch released fuses the whole mass into one dense block that can be sliced with a knife and holds its shape. Ordinary cooked rice cannot do that; it is loose grains and it dissolves into broth. Lontong stays intact while soaking up an enormous amount of liquid, which is why it is the traditional partner for anything served in a thin sauce, and why it exists at all — it was food you could carry and eat with your hands without a bowl.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g rice"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the lontong first, ideally a day ahead, because it must set completely cold before it can be sliced. Rinse the 60g of rice, then pack it loosely — no more than half full — into a small heatproof roasting bag or a double layer of foil rolled into a tight cylinder and folded closed at both ends, leaving room for the rice to swell. Sit it in a saucepan of boiling water so it is fully submerged, weighted down with a plate if it floats, and boil for 1 hour 15 minutes, topping the water up as it drops so it never runs dry. The rice must be overcooked; this is the one time that is the aim. Lift it out, let it cool completely, then refrigerate at least 4 hours or overnight — it will not slice while warm and will crumble if you try. Cut into 1.5cm slices and lay them in the dish first, then the smashed tofu on top, then the thin sauce poured over everything so the rice soaks from underneath. Increase the water in the sauce to 130ml, because the lontong will take up a great deal of liquid and the standard 100ml disappears entirely. Serve cold or at room temperature, which is how it is sold."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-pepes-ikan","name":"Pepes Ikan","nameAlt":"Leaf-wrapped spiced fish","aliases":["Pepes Ikan","Pepes","Pais Ikan","Botok Ikan","Steamed Fish Parcels","Sundanese Fish Parcels","Banana Leaf Fish","Pepes Tahu"],"course":"main","type":["main","fish","steamed","grilled","parcel","spiced","braai","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","braai","summer"],"ingredients":"150g hake · 40g tomato · 15g shallots · 10ml lime juice · 10g garlic · 8g macadamia nuts · 6g red chilli · 5g lemongrass · 5g galangal · 4g basil · 3g ground turmeric · 3g salt","method":"A pepes parcel is doing two different kinds of cooking at once, and almost every mistake with this dish comes from not knowing which one you are in. Sealed inside, the fish is STEAMING — and not in added water, because you never add any. The liquid is the fish's own juice plus what the tomato gives up, held in a closed pocket where it has nowhere to go. Outside, the wrapper is SCORCHING against direct heat. So the order is fixed and it is not interchangeable: steam first until the fish is cooked, then char. Do it the other way round and the wrapper burns through long before the middle is done, and you lose the seal, which is the only thing making the dish work.\n\nFIRST, THE WRAPPER, BECAUSE IT DECIDES EVERYTHING ELSE. Traditionally this is banana leaf, and here that makes it a garden ingredient rather than a shop one — banana plants are common enough in gardens in this part of the world that if you have one, or a neighbour does, you already have the real thing, and it is not sold in shops in any reliable way. If you have access to leaves, cut a piece about 30cm square per parcel, wipe both sides with a damp cloth, and then WILT IT, which is the step people skip and then wonder why their parcel splits. Pass the leaf over a gas flame or lay it in a dry hot pan for 10 to 20 seconds a side. You will see it change — it darkens slightly, turns glossy, and goes from stiff and squeaky to soft and foldable. Raw leaf has rigid parallel fibres and cracks straight along them the moment you fold it; a wilted leaf folds like cloth.\n\nNO LEAF? USE BAKING PAPER INSIDE A LAYER OF FOIL, and be clear-eyed about the trade. Paper and foil reproduce the steam vessel perfectly — arguably better, since they seal tighter — but they contribute no aroma at all and they cannot char, and those are two of the three things pepes is for. You buy them back at the end rather than pretending: open the parcel after steaming, lift the fish out, and finish it for 60 to 90 seconds a side directly over the coals or in a smoking-hot dry pan until the surface takes some real colour. It is not the same as a scorched leaf breathing into the fish for four minutes, but it is honest, and it beats a pale steamed parcel pretending to be a grilled one.\n\nNOW SALT AND ACID THE FISH BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE TOUCHES IT. Pat the 150g of hake dry, then rub it with the 3g of salt and the 10ml of lime juice and leave it 10 minutes. Two things happen and both matter. The salt draws a little moisture to the surface, which will become part of your steaming liquid rather than being lost, and the acid firms the outer layer of protein so a soft, flake-prone fish like hake holds together in the parcel instead of falling into the bumbu. If the fillet has a thick end, slash it twice on the diagonal, about halfway through, so the thick and thin parts finish at the same time.\n\nPOUND THE BUMBU AND THEN — UNUSUALLY — DO NOT FRY IT. Into a mortar go the 15g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 6g of red chilli, the 5g of lemongrass sliced thin first because it is fibrous, the 5g of galangal and the 3g of ground turmeric. Pound to a fairly smooth paste. Almost every other bumbu in Indonesian cooking gets fried before it goes near the main ingredient, and this one deliberately does not, which is worth understanding rather than taking on faith: frying drives off volatile aromatics, and the whole architecture of a sealed parcel exists to TRAP those volatiles against the fish. Fry the paste first and you throw away the compounds the parcel was built to hold. Raw in, sealed, and the twenty minutes of steaming cooks it perfectly well.\n\nASSEMBLE IT FLAT AND KEEP IT DRY. Lay the wilted leaf or the paper down. Spread half the bumbu in a patch the size of the fish, sit the fish on it, then spread the rest over the top and press it on. Scatter the 40g of tomato, cut into thick wedges rather than chopped — thick wedges release their juice slowly across the whole cook, where chopped tomato floods the parcel in the first five minutes and boils the fish rather than steaming it. ⛔ Add no water, no stock and no oil. There is enough liquid coming.\n\nTHEN THE BASIL, LAST AND WHOLE. Lay the 4g of basil on top in whole sprigs, unbruised and unchopped. In a sealed parcel the volatiles have nowhere to escape to, so whole leaves sitting on top will perfume the entire thing as they wilt — while chopped or pounded basil oxidises, goes dark and grassy, and tastes stewed. This is the one herb in the recipe that is a finish rather than a foundation, even though it goes in at the start.\n\nWRAP IT TIGHT. Fold the long sides of the leaf over the fish so they overlap in the middle, then fold both ends up and under, and sit the parcel seam-side down so its own weight holds it closed. Traditionally the ends are pinned with a toothpick or a sliver of bamboo. If you are using paper and foil, wrap the paper first as a tight envelope and then the foil around it, folding the foil edges over twice — the paper stops the acid and salt meeting aluminium, and the foil does the sealing.\n\nSTEAM 20 MINUTES, THEN CHAR 3 TO 4 MINUTES A SIDE. A steamer basket over simmering water, or a plate on a trivet in a covered pan with 2cm of water in the bottom. Twenty minutes at a steady steam for a 150g fillet — a little more if it is thick, but do not go past 25 or hake turns cottony. Then take the parcel out and put it directly over hot coals, or into a dry heavy pan on high heat, for 3 to 4 minutes a side. The leaf will blacken in patches and start to smell like scorched grass and hot tea, and that smell going into the fish is precisely the point of the whole exercise. Blackened is correct. Burnt through is not — if it catches fire, the heat is too high.\n\nREST IT FIVE MINUTES AND OPEN IT AT THE TABLE. The parcel is full of pressurised steam carrying everything volatile in the dish, and if you tear it open in the kitchen that all goes into the extractor fan. Five minutes off the heat also lets the juices settle back into the fish instead of running out the moment it is cut. Open it in front of whoever is eating it, and eat it straight from the leaf.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Cure 10 min · Steam 20 min · Char 8 min · Rest 5 min","kcal":"~310 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 33g · Carbs 9g · Fat 15g · Sodium ~1220mg","storage":"Pepes keeps unusually well for a fish dish, and the parcel is the reason — sealed, it holds its own moisture instead of drying out in the fridge, so it does not go tough and papery the way an open piece of cooked fish does. Cool the parcels unopened to room temperature, then refrigerate still sealed for up to 3 days. Reheat them sealed too, either steamed again for 8 minutes or in a 180°C oven for 12, and only then open them. ⛔ Do not microwave an unopened foil parcel, and do not reheat a banana-leaf parcel that has charred right through, because a broken seal means you are now just baking dry fish. Cooked pepes freezes well in the parcel for 2 months and is one of the better things to have in a freezer — thaw overnight in the fridge and steam from cold for 12 minutes. Leftover bumbu, made and not used, keeps 5 days in a jar and 3 months frozen, and it is worth making a triple batch while the mortar is out. ⚠️ The one thing that does not survive is the char: a reheated parcel tastes steamed rather than grilled, so if you are cooking ahead deliberately, steam the parcels, refrigerate them, and do the charring fresh on the day.","chefNotes":"Steam FIRST, char SECOND — reversed, the wrapper burns through before the fish cooks and you lose the seal the dish depends on. Wilt banana leaf over a flame for 10 to 20 seconds a side or it splits along the grain when you fold it. Salt and lime the fish 10 minutes ahead: the salt makes your steaming liquid and the acid firms hake enough to hold together. Do NOT fry the bumbu — a sealed parcel exists to trap volatile aromatics and frying drives them off. Add no water, ever. Tomato in thick wedges, not chopped, or it floods and boils the fish. Basil whole and on top, never chopped. Then rest 5 minutes and open it at the table, not at the stove.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice and something sharp, and not much else — the parcel arrives with its own sauce and the dish is light enough that anything rich alongside takes it over. Coconut rice works if you want the meal to feel bigger. What it really wants is acid and heat on the side: a spoonful of sambal, and a bowl of something raw, cold and sour. A fierce fruit salad in the Indonesian style is close to perfect next to it, for the same reason a squeeze of lemon works on any grilled fish, only harder. It is also a natural braai dish — the parcels go straight onto the grid after steaming, they do not stick, they do not fall through, and they can sit at the edge of the fire while everything else cooks. Keep drinks cold and dry.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Rujak","Plecing Kangkung"],"trivia":"The wrapper is not from a tree, and that is not pedantry — it is the reason the leaf is big enough to cook in. A banana plant is a herb. Specifically it is the largest herbaceous flowering plant on earth, and what looks like a trunk is a pseudostem: a tightly rolled column of the bases of the leaves themselves, with no wood in it anywhere. Cut one and it is wet and fibrous like a leek, not dry like timber. Because the plant invests almost everything in foliage rather than structure, individual leaves reach two to three metres long, which is what makes a banana leaf a viable cooking vessel where an oak leaf is a garnish. And each pseudostem flowers and fruits exactly once and then dies — the plant carries on from suckers off the underground rhizome, so what looks like one long-lived tree in a garden is a rolling succession of single-use stems. All of which explains why the leaf is a garden ingredient and not really a retail one: a banana plant is generous with leaves and useless as a crop unless you actually want the fruit, so people who have one have more leaf than they can use, and people who do not have none at all.\n\nWhat the leaf does in the cooking is more than sentiment. It carries a waxy cuticle on the underside that softens slightly with heat and stops the leaf disintegrating or sticking, which is why a banana-leaf parcel survives twenty minutes of steam and then four minutes on coals where paper would go to pulp. It also gives up a distinct grassy, tea-like aroma when it scorches, closer to green tea than to smoke, and that aroma is a flavour the dish is built around rather than a side effect — which is exactly why pepes is grilled after steaming instead of simply being steamed and served. The family is wide across Java and worth knowing by name: pepes is the Sundanese word, West Java, and it is the leanest version. Pais is the same thing. Botok, from Central and East Java, adds grated coconut to the parcel so the filling comes out closer to a moist crumble than a fillet in sauce. Same parcel, same fire, entirely different mouthful.","howThisFeels":"Cutting the string on a blackened parcel and getting a face full of steam that smells of hot basil, lime and scorched grass.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated sealed in the parcel, steamed 8 minutes from cold, which is the only leftover fish that does not go tough — though it will taste steamed rather than grilled.","(easy) Cold, straight from the fridge, flaked over rice with a spoonful of sambal, which is genuinely good and needs no reheating.","(medium) The fish flaked and the parcel juices poured over hot rice as a sauce, then folded through with a handful of fresh basil — the juices are the best part of the dish and get thrown away far too often.","(creative) Flaked cold pepes fried briefly in a hot dry pan until the edges catch, then stirred through fried rice at the last moment, where the turmeric and charred-leaf aroma colour the whole pan and it stops tasting like leftovers entirely."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Pepes Sederhana — Paper Parcel, Short Bumbu (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":43,"howThisFeels":"A foil packet out of a home oven that still arrives smelling like something cooked over a fire.","trivia":"The two lines that come out here are the two most expensive things in the recipe per kilo, and neither is the fish — macadamia and galangal together cost more than a third of the bumbu. It is worth being precise about what each was doing, because you compensate for them differently. The macadamias were body: fat and starch that thicken the pounded paste so it clings to the fish rather than sliding off it. Without them the bumbu is looser, so it needs to be spread thinner and pressed on harder. Galangal was the sharp, pine-and-pepper note that is not quite ginger and has no real substitute, so rather than fake it with ginger — which is sweeter and hotter and pulls the dish somewhere else — the honest move is to lean on what remains and lift the lemongrass and the lime instead. What you end up with is a cleaner, simpler, more citrus-forward parcel. It is not a lesser dish, it is a plainer one, and plain is what most weeknight pepes actually is.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g macadamia nuts"},{"item":"5g galangal"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"5g lemongrass","to":"8g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Skip the macadamias and the galangal, and use 8g of lemongrass instead of 5g, sliced very thin before pounding since it is now carrying more of the aromatic load on its own. Expect a looser bumbu without the nut in it — spread it thinner and press it onto the fish firmly so it stays put. Push the lime juice up to 15ml as well; with the galangal gone the dish wants more acid to keep it from tasting flat. Then use baking paper inside foil rather than leaf, and cook the whole thing in a 200°C oven for 22 minutes instead of steaming, which works because a tightly sealed foil parcel in a hot oven IS a steamer — the trapped juice boils and the fish cooks in its own vapour exactly as it would in a basket. Open the parcel, lift the fish out, and give it 60 to 90 seconds a side in a smoking-hot dry pan to get some colour on it, because an oven parcel gives you no char at all and that browning is the closest available stand-in. Pour the parcel juices back over it to serve."}]}},{"name":"Pepes Ikan Sunda — Leaf-Wrapped, Steamed then Charred","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":50,"howThisFeels":"Laying a parcel straight onto coals and hearing the leaf hiss and tighten around the fish.","trivia":"The charring step looks decorative and is chemically the most active four minutes of the cook. Up to that point everything has happened at 100°C, the ceiling of a steamer, which is hot enough to cook protein and soften aromatics and nowhere near hot enough to brown anything — which is why a purely steamed pepes tastes clean, gentle and slightly flat. Direct contact with coals puts the outside of the leaf several hundred degrees higher. The leaf itself pyrolyses, breaking down and releasing aromatic compounds inward, and the thin film of juice and bumbu pressed against the inner surface of the leaf finally gets hot enough to brown properly. So the parcel is doing something a pan cannot: it browns the sauce against the wrapper while the fish inside never exceeds steaming temperature and stays completely moist. That is the trick of the dish, and it is why the sequence cannot be reversed and why the char is not optional."},{"name":"Pepes Tahu Kemangi — Tofu and Coconut (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"Crumbly, herby and steaming, and closer to a savoury pudding than anything you expected out of a leaf.","trivia":"Swapping tofu for fish changes the physics of the parcel, and the grated coconut is not a garnish — it is the fix. Fish releases a considerable amount of liquid as it cooks, and the whole no-added-water rule depends on that. Tofu releases almost none, so a straight substitution gives you a dry parcel with a paste stuck to a block in the middle of it. Crumbling the tofu rather than leaving it whole solves half of it by giving the bumbu enormous surface area to cling to, and the coconut solves the rest: it holds moisture, it brings fat that the fish was providing, and it binds the crumble into something spoonable. What you get is genuinely closer to botok, the Central Javanese version, than to pepes proper — a moist savoury crumble rather than a fillet in sauce — and it is what the parcel does best when there is no fish in it. Firm tofu, never silken, or it collapses into liquid under twenty minutes of steam.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g hake","to":"150g firm tofu"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g desiccated coconut"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Press the 150g of firm tofu under a weight for 20 minutes, then CRUMBLE it coarsely with your fingers rather than cutting it into slices — you want rough pieces from pea to thumbnail size, not a block. Skip the salt-and-lime cure, which has nothing to grip on tofu; instead pound the salt into the bumbu and add the lime juice at the mixing stage. Soak the 25g of desiccated coconut in 30ml of warm water for 10 minutes first so it goes soft and does not steal moisture from everything else in the parcel, then squeeze it lightly and fold it through the crumbled tofu together with all of the bumbu, so every piece is coated — with tofu the bumbu is mixed IN rather than spread on top. Pile the mixture onto the leaf, lay the whole basil sprigs over it, add the tomato wedges, and wrap as normal. Steam for the same 20 minutes and char for the same 3 to 4 minutes a side. It will be crumbly and want a spoon rather than a fork. Season it a little more assertively than the fish version, because tofu is far blander than hake and will take everything you give it."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Rujak","target":"indonesia-rujak","emoji":"🍍"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bika-ambon","name":"Bika Ambon","nameAlt":"Medan honeycomb cake","aliases":["Bika Ambon","Bingka Ambon","Medan Honeycomb Cake","Indonesian Honeycomb Cake","Bika Ambon Keju","Kue Bika","Fermented Tapioca Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","baked","fermented","cake","celebration","make-ahead"],"diet":["vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","party","celebration","everyday"],"ingredients":"70ml coconut milk · 35g tapioca starch · 30g white sugar · 2 large eggs · 5g lemongrass · 2g salt · 1g instant yeast","method":"Cut into a good Bika Ambon and it looks like the inside of a sponge that has been stood on end — dozens of narrow channels running straight from the bottom of the cake to the top, open all the way through. Those channels ARE the dish. A Bika Ambon that has risen nicely and come out with a fine even crumb is a failure, however good it tastes. So everything below is arranged around one idea, and it is a strange one: YOU HAVE TO KEEP THE TOP LIQUID WHILE THE BOTTOM SETS.\n\nHere is why, because once it is clear the whole method stops looking eccentric. The fermenting batter produces carbon dioxide continuously and that gas wants to go up. If the surface of the cake sets early — which is exactly what a normal oven does — the gas is trapped underneath, spreads sideways, and you get scattered round holes in a cake-like crumb. But if the surface stays wet and open while the batter gelatinises from the bottom upward, each stream of bubbles keeps using the same escape route, and the starch sets around that route and holds it open. The channel is the fossil of a bubble path. Which is also why this is made with STARCH AND NOT FLOUR: wheat flour has gluten, gluten forms an elastic film that catches bubbles and turns them into a fine crumb, and a fine crumb is precisely what you are trying not to make. Tapioca starch has no gluten at all, so it cannot trap anything — it can only gel around whatever shape the gas has already carved.\n\nINFUSE THE COCONUT MILK FIRST AND COOL IT COMPLETELY. Bruise the 5g of lemongrass with the flat of a knife, slice it, and warm it in the 70ml of coconut milk over low heat for 5 minutes without letting it boil — boiling coconut milk splits it. Then take it off and let it cool to genuinely lukewarm, no warmer than blood heat. ⛔ This is not a suggestion. Yeast begins dying somewhere above about 45°C, and warm coconut milk poured onto yeast is the most common reason a batter never ferments at all. Strain the lemongrass out.\n\nMIX IT THIN, THEN STRAIN IT. Whisk the 2 large eggs with the 30g of white sugar and the 2g of salt just until combined — do NOT beat air into them, because you want the gas in this cake to come from fermentation only. Air whipped in now makes large irregular bubbles that cut across the vertical channels and ruin them. Whisk in the 35g of tapioca starch, then the cooled coconut milk, then the 1g of instant yeast. It should be thin, about the consistency of pouring cream. Then pass the whole thing through a sieve, which takes ten seconds and removes both starch lumps and the mixing bubbles. A lump becomes a solid patch with no channels through it.\n\nNOW FERMENT IT, AND THIS IS A WINDOW, NOT A MINIMUM. Cover the bowl and leave it somewhere warm. At 28 to 30°C it needs 2 to 3 hours. ⚠️ In a cold kitchen — and a Highveld winter kitchen sits closer to 15 to 18°C — it will take two to three times that, so reckon on 5 to 6 hours, or stand the bowl in a larger bowl of water at about 30°C and change the water when it cools. Do not chase it with real heat.\n\nJUDGE IT BY THREE SIGNS, NOT BY THE CLOCK. The surface should be completely covered in fine bubbles, edge to edge. The volume should be up by roughly a third — it will not dome, because the batter is far too thin to hold a shape. And it should smell distinctly yeasty with a faint sour-alcoholic edge, like bread dough with something sharper behind it. ⛔ THEN STOP. Left too long it goes sharply sour, and worse structurally: gas production peaks and then falls away, and the starch begins to break down so the batter thins. Bake an over-fermented batter and the channels collapse as it cooks and you get a dense, sour, slightly rubbery slab. There is no rescuing it. The risk here is structural rather than microbial — it is a batter you are about to bake, so nothing unsafe is developing, but the good window is a couple of hours wide and then it closes.\n\nPREHEAT THE TIN, AND GREASE IT — NEVER LINE IT. Set the oven to 170°C and put a small greased tin, about 15cm, in to heat for 10 minutes. Butter or oil straight onto the metal, and no paper: the batter needs to grip the sides and climb, and baking paper gives it nothing to hold. A properly hot tin sets the base almost on contact, and that instant floor is what anchors the bottom of every channel. Batter into a cold tin settles and spreads before anything sets, and the channels start from nowhere.\n\nBAKE WITH THE OVEN DOOR PROPPED OPEN. Pour the batter in — it should come no more than 3cm up the tin — and bake at 170°C with the door held ajar by the handle of a wooden spoon for the first 40 minutes. This is the step that sounds wrong and is the entire trick: an open door keeps the top of the cake below setting temperature and lets the steam out, so the surface stays wet and open while the heat coming from below gels the batter upward. You can watch it happen through the gap — the batter goes cloudy from the bottom, and bubbles keep breaking on a still-liquid surface. Then close the door for the last 10 to 15 minutes to set and lightly colour the top. Total 50 to 55 minutes.\n\nIT IS DONE WHEN THE TOP IS SET AND MATTE, springs back to a light press, and a skewer comes out clean — but check the SIDE rather than the top, because that is where the structure shows: the edge should be full of visible open tunnels running vertically. If the sides look solid, it needed longer with the door open, and there is nothing to be done about it this time.\n\nCOOL IT COMPLETELY IN THE TIN BEFORE CUTTING, at least an hour. Warm, the starch gel is still soft and a knife smears the channels closed, which makes a perfect cake look like a failed one. Cut it cold with a thin sharp knife, wiped between slices, and cut generously thick so the channels show on the face of the slice — that cross-section is the whole reason anyone makes this.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Ferment 2-3 hr (5-6 hr in a cold kitchen) · Bake 55 min · Cool 1 hr","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g · Carbs 44g · Fat 14g · Sodium ~820mg","storage":"It keeps far better than a wheat cake and for a structural reason: there is no gluten to go stale and stiffen, and tapioca starch holds moisture well, so Bika Ambon stays chewy and moist for days where a sponge would be dry by the second morning. Keep it in a closed container at room temperature for up to 3 days — it is genuinely as good on day two, and some people prefer it, because the yeast note settles and the coconut comes forward. ⛔ Do NOT refrigerate it. Cold makes the starch gel firm and slightly rubbery and it goes tough and squeaky, which is the one thing that ruins the texture, and it does not fully recover at room temperature. If you must keep it longer, freeze it: wrapped well it takes 2 months and thaws back to almost exactly itself, which cold storage does not. Thaw at room temperature, still wrapped, for a few hours. A short warm in a low oven, 5 minutes at 140°C, brings back the chew in a slice that has been out a while. Cut faces dry out fastest, so store it as a whole cake and cut as you go rather than slicing it all up.","chefNotes":"The holes are the dish — a fine even crumb means it failed. Tapioca starch, never wheat flour, because gluten would trap the gas into a crumb instead of letting it carve channels. Cool the infused coconut milk to lukewarm or the yeast dies. Do not whisk air in, and strain the batter — mixing bubbles and starch lumps both break the channels. Ferment to THREE signs, not a clock: surface fully bubbled, volume up a third, a yeasty smell with a sour edge. Then stop, because over-fermented batter collapses in the oven. Preheat and grease the tin, never line it. Then bake with the oven door propped ajar for 40 minutes so the top stays liquid while the bottom sets. Cool completely before cutting or the knife smears the channels shut.","pairsWith":"It is a cake for the middle of the afternoon rather than the end of a meal, and it wants strong unsweetened coffee or black tea beside it more than anything else — the cake is sweet, rich with yolk and coconut, and quite chewy, so a bitter drink is doing real work rather than just keeping it company. Serve it plain. It needs no cream, no sauce and no fruit, and anything wet poured over it soaks straight down the channels and turns the texture to mush, which is a genuine waste of the one thing that took all the effort. If it is going on a table with other things, put it with dry sweets rather than puddings — steamed rice-flour sweets and fried banana are its natural company, and it is a standard part of a box of mixed cakes taken to somebody's house.","goesWith":["Black tea","Coffee","Klepon"],"trivia":"The original leaven for this cake was not yeast from a packet but a drink, and the reason is a piece of fermentation you cannot switch off. Bika Ambon was traditionally raised with tuak or nira — palm sap, tapped from the cut flower stalk of a coconut or sugar palm. Sap comes out of the tree sweet, and it does not stay that way, because the wild yeasts are already there: living on the tree, on the tapping knife, and coating the inside of the bamboo tube it drips into. Within hours they are converting the sugar, and within a day the sap is properly alcoholic. Keeping palm sap FRESH is the hard part, not fermenting it — collectors traditionally line the tube with lime or a bitter bark specifically to slow the yeasts down, and if you want sweet sap for sugar you have to boil it almost immediately. So in a hot climate with a palm tree, a reliably active fermented liquid was simply always to hand, and it was easier to come by than anything you could buy. The cake was leavened by whatever the tree was already doing, and the faint sourness that came with it was part of the flavour rather than a fault. Commercial yeast gives a cleaner and much more predictable rise, which is why almost every recipe now uses it, but it also gives a blander cake — and some bakers in Medan still put a splash of tuak or fermented coconut water in for exactly the note that packet yeast cannot produce.\n\nThe name is an unsolved problem and it is worth being honest that nobody actually knows. This is a cake from Medan, in North Sumatra, and Ambon is an island in Maluku some three thousand kilometres east — about as far from Medan as London is from Cairo. The usual explanations are that it was first sold on a street in Medan called Jalan Ambon, or that the word comes from a local term for the way the cake is made, and neither is settled. It is a reasonable guess and nothing more, which is a better position than the confident folk etymologies these things usually attract.","howThisFeels":"Propping the oven door open with a wooden spoon and feeling like you are doing something wrong for forty minutes, then cutting into it and seeing the tunnels.","leftovers":["(easy) Day two at room temperature is arguably better than day one — the yeast note settles back and the coconut comes forward. Store whole, cut as you go.","(easy) A thick slice warmed 5 minutes in a low oven at 140°C, which brings the chew back to a piece that has been sitting out.","(medium) Cut into thick fingers and griddled cut-side down in a dry pan until the channels catch and caramelise at the edges, then eaten hot — a completely different and slightly better cake.","(creative) Stale slices torn up and layered with sweetened coconut milk and a little palm sugar, then chilled overnight, where the channels do exactly what channels do and the whole thing sets into something close to a coconut trifle."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bika Ambon Sederhana — One Egg, No Lemongrass (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":12,"howThisFeels":"Paler and plainer out of the tin, with exactly the same tunnels down the side.","trivia":"This drops the two lines that cost the most and keeps the one thing that matters, and it is worth knowing which is which. The channels are made by starch, gas and an open oven door — none of which is affected here — so a budget Bika Ambon has the same structure as the full one, which is unusual for a cheap fork. What the second egg was doing is colour and richness: yolk fat coats the starch and makes the chew softer and more tender, and the yolk pigment is most of why a good Bika Ambon is deep yellow rather than cream. With one egg it comes out noticeably paler and a touch firmer and chewier. The lemongrass was a perfume rather than a structural part, so losing it costs an aroma and nothing else. The one thing to actually adjust is the fat: with one less yolk the batter is slightly thinner, so it ferments a little faster in a warm kitchen and needs watching rather than timing.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"2 large eggs","to":"1 large egg"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"5g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 1 large egg rather than 2 and skip the lemongrass, which also means skipping the infusion — just warm nothing, and use the 70ml of coconut milk straight from the tin at room temperature, checking only that it is not fridge-cold, since cold batter ferments very slowly. Everything else is identical. Two things to watch: the batter will be a little thinner without the second yolk, so it may hit the three fermentation signs 20 to 30 minutes sooner in a warm kitchen, and it is worth looking at it early rather than trusting the clock. And because there is less fat, grease the tin more generously than you think you need to — yolk fat was doing some of the work of stopping it sticking, and a budget Bika Ambon is noticeably keener to grip the metal. Expect a paler, firmer, chewier cake with the same open channels down the cut side."}]}},{"name":"Bika Ambon Medan — the Full Fermented Honeycomb","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"Watching bubbles keep breaking on a surface that refuses to set, which is the moment you know it is working.","trivia":"The lemongrass infusion is doing something less obvious than adding flavour, and it explains why this cake is almost always infused with something aromatic rather than left plain. Fermentation does not only make carbon dioxide — it also makes a small amount of ethanol and a range of by-products that read as sour, bready and faintly beery, and in a cake this thin and this lightly sweetened they are quite noticeable. A cake risen this way and left unperfumed tastes of the ferment. Lemongrass brings a bright citral note that sits directly on top of that and turns it from a slightly odd background sourness into a deliberate savoury-fresh edge, which is why pandan, lemongrass and lime leaf all turn up in different versions doing the same job. It also matters that the infusion is warmed and then cooled rather than boiled in: gentle heat pulls the aromatic oils out of the bruised stalk, while a real boil drives them off and splits the coconut milk at the same time."},{"name":"Bika Ambon Keju — the Bakery Version with Cheese","icon":"🧈","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Salt hitting sweet at the top of a mouthful and then chewy coconut underneath it.","trivia":"Cheese on a sweet cake is a real and thoroughly mainstream Indonesian habit rather than a novelty, and Medan bakeries sell Bika Ambon in a row of flavours with keju — cheese — as one of the standards alongside pandan, chocolate and durian. It works here better than it has any right to, for two reasons. The cake is sweet but not very sweet and it is heavy with coconut fat, so a firm salty cheese lands as seasoning rather than as an ingredient fighting the dessert. And it is added at the very end, in the last stretch of baking with the door closed, so it melts and browns on a surface that has already set — which matters, because cheese put on at the start would sit on a liquid top, sink, and block the channels it is meant to sit above. Grated fine and added late, it stays where it is put.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"20g cheddar"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make and bake exactly as described, right through the 40 minutes with the oven door propped open — do not add the cheese before this, because the top is still liquid and grated cheese will sink into it and seal the channels shut from above, which is the one thing the whole method is arranged to prevent. Grate the 20g of cheddar finely. When you close the oven door for the final stretch, give it 5 minutes to let the surface set and go matte, then open it briefly, scatter the cheese evenly over the top, and close it again for the remaining 8 to 10 minutes until the cheese has melted and taken a few brown patches. Watch it, because 20g grated fine over a 15cm cake is a thin layer and it goes from melted to leathery quickly at 170°C. Cool completely in the tin as normal. Cut it cold — the cheese layer helps here, since it holds the top together and stops the knife dragging."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Klepon","target":"indonesia-klepon","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ayam-goreng-kalasan","name":"Ayam Goreng Kalasan","nameAlt":"Coconut-water braised fried chicken","aliases":["Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Ayam Kremes","Ayam Goreng Kremes","Kremesan","Javanese Fried Chicken","Coconut Water Fried Chicken","Ayam Goreng Lengkuas"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","braised","crispy","street-food","celebration"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","celebration","party"],"ingredients":"400ml sunflower oil · 200ml coconut water · 180g chicken drumsticks · 25g rice flour · 20g shallots · 10g jaggery · 10g garlic · 10g cornflour · 8g macadamia nuts · 5g coriander seeds · 5g galangal · 5g tamarind paste · 3g ground turmeric · 3g salt · 1 large egg · 3 bay leaves","method":"Most fried chicken is a marinade and a fry. This is a braise and a fry, and the whole dish turns on one decision that separates it from every ordinary version: YOU DO NOT POUR THE COOKING LIQUID AWAY. You reduce it until there are two or three tablespoons left, and then you make the best part of the dish out of it. The crisp lace of crumbs piled on top — kremesan — is not a batter. It is the braising liquid, thickened and fried. So everything that leached out of the chicken over forty minutes comes back to the plate as texture.\n\nSTART WITH THE BUMBU. Pound the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 5g of coriander seeds toasted dry for a minute first, the 5g of galangal, the 3g of ground turmeric and the 3g of salt to a fairly smooth paste. The macadamias stand in for candlenut and do the same job they do everywhere in this cooking — fat and starch for body, not flavour.\n\nNOW THE COCONUT WATER, AND IT IS NOT A GIMMICK. Put the 180g of chicken drumsticks in a pan just wide enough to hold them in one layer, add the bumbu, the 200ml of coconut water, the 10g of jaggery, the 5g of tamarind paste and the 3 bay leaves. Coconut water is doing three things plain water cannot. It is mildly acidic, which relaxes the connective tissue and is why the traditional version can use a tough old free-range bird and still come out tender. It carries free sugars, which means the surface will brown properly later where a water-braised bird stays pale and grey. And it brings a faint sweet-savoury minerality that you notice most when it is missing. ⚠️ If you are splitting a whole young coconut for it rather than buying it, use it the same day — once it is out of the shell it starts fermenting quickly.\n\nBRING IT TO A BARE SIMMER AND LET IT GO DOWN. Lid on, lowest heat, 35 to 40 minutes, turning the pieces twice, until a skewer goes into the thickest part with no resistance. Then take the lid OFF and keep going, 5 to 10 minutes more, until the liquid has reduced to two or three tablespoons of thick, dark, intensely concentrated sauce clinging around the meat. ⛔ This is the step to get right and the one people rush. Do not stop while there is still liquid sitting in the pan, because that liquid is where most of the flavour now lives, and either it goes back into the chicken or it becomes the kremesan — it does not go down the sink. Watch the last two minutes properly: there is very little water left and a lot of sugar in there, and it will catch and burn quickly.\n\nLIFT THE CHICKEN OUT AND DRY IT — REALLY DRY IT. Sit the pieces on a rack or paper for at least 10 minutes and blot them. Any wet patch will spit violently in the oil and, worse, will steam rather than fry, so you get a pale soggy stripe down an otherwise crisp piece. Scrape every bit of the remaining paste and reduced liquid out of the pan into a bowl and keep it. That is your kremesan base and it should be about 45 to 60ml of thick dark sludge.\n\nMAKE THE KREMESAN BATTER THIN — THINNER THAN FEELS RIGHT. Into that reserved liquid whisk the 25g of rice flour, the 10g of cornflour and the 1 large egg, then let it down with water until it runs off a spoon in a thin unbroken stream, roughly the consistency of single cream. ⛔ THE THINNESS IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. A thick batter dropped into oil makes a fritter. A thin one scatters into hundreds of separate threads that set instantly and fuse into an open lace, and that lace is what shatters when you bite it. Rice flour rather than wheat, and this matters: rice flour has no gluten, so it cannot form an elastic film, and it fries brittle and stays brittle instead of softening back the way a wheat batter does within minutes.\n\nHEAT THE 400ml OF OIL TO 180°C. Deep enough that the chicken can sit in it without touching the base, because a piece resting on hot metal scorches on one face. Bread cube test: golden in about 40 seconds.\n\nFRY THE CHICKEN BRIEFLY — YOU ARE COLOURING IT, NOT COOKING IT. It is already fully cooked, so it wants 3 to 4 minutes total, turning once, until the skin is deep golden and tight. Longer and you are simply driving moisture out of meat that was tender when it went in, which is the second most common way this dish is ruined. Out onto a rack, never onto a plate, or the underside steams itself soft.\n\nTHEN THE LACE, AND KEEP THE OIL PROPERLY HOT. With the oil back at 180°C, dip your fingers in the thin batter and let it run off them in a stream, moving your hand in circles across the surface of the oil so it lands scattered rather than in one place. A spoon works, held high and drizzled. It will bloom instantly into a pale foam and then set into a raft of threads. Give it 45 to 60 seconds until it stops bubbling hard and turns light gold, then lift the whole sheet out with a slotted spoon and drain it on paper. ⛔ If the oil is below about 170°C the batter sinks and clumps into a solid biscuit instead of a lace — this is the one place where oil temperature is not negotiable. Repeat with the rest of the batter, in two or three batches, letting the oil come back up between them.\n\nPILE IT ON AND SERVE AT ONCE. Chicken on the plate, then crush the kremesan roughly in your hand and heap it over the top so it half-buries the meat. It stays crisp for about twenty minutes and then it starts to soften, which is simply what it does — nobody has ever solved it and nobody serves this dish assembled ahead. Eat with rice and something fiercely hot, and eat the crumbs that fall onto the plate, because that is the point of the whole exercise.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Braise 45 min · Dry 10 min · Fry 10 min","kcal":"~610 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 31g · Fat 38g · Sodium ~1180mg","storage":"Store it as two things, never as one, because the chicken keeps well and the kremesan does not keep at all. Braised-and-fried chicken refrigerates in a closed container for up to 3 days and reheats honestly in a 200°C oven for 8 to 10 minutes, which re-tightens the skin — never in a microwave, which turns crisp skin to leather. It also freezes for 2 months after frying, and thaws and re-crisps in the oven from frozen in about 20 minutes. ⛔ The kremesan is finished within half an hour of leaving the oil and there is no storing, reviving or re-crisping it; the lace collapses as it takes up moisture from the air and an oven only browns it further. Make it fresh, every time, in the ten minutes before people eat. The useful shortcut is to do the braise a day ahead: braise, reduce, and refrigerate the chicken and the reserved liquid separately, then dry, fry and lace on the day. That splits a long recipe into two short ones and improves it, since a night in the fridge lets the bumbu settle further into the meat. ⚠️ The reserved liquid sets almost solid when cold — stir it with a spoonful of water before adding the flours.","chefNotes":"Do NOT pour the braising liquid away — reduce it to two or three tablespoons and turn it into the kremesan, because that is where the flavour went. Coconut water rather than water: mildly acidic so it tenderises, and its sugars are what let the skin brown. Dry the chicken properly for 10 minutes before it goes in the oil or it steams instead of frying. Fry it only 3 to 4 minutes — it is already cooked and you are colouring it, not cooking it. Then make the lace batter THIN, like single cream, and keep the oil at 180°C: cooler and it clumps into a biscuit instead of scattering into threads. Rice flour, not wheat, so it fries brittle and stays brittle. Assemble at the last second.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice and a fierce sambal, and that is genuinely the whole plate — this is rich, sweet-savoury, deep-fried food and it wants heat and neutrality beside it rather than another flavour competing. Something raw and cold does more than you would expect: a few wedges of cucumber and a handful of shredded cabbage eaten between mouthfuls, which is how it is served from warungs and is not garnish. A sharp fruit salad in the Indonesian style works for the same reason. Avoid anything creamy or coconut-based alongside, because the dish already carries that, and avoid a second fried thing on the plate. Cold unsweetened tea.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Plecing Kangkung","Cucumber"],"trivia":"Coconut water arrives sterile, and that fact has been put to a use nobody would guess from a braising pan. Inside an intact green coconut the liquid is sealed off from the outside world entirely — there is no route in for bacteria until the shell is opened — and it carries dissolved sugars, potassium, magnesium and other minerals in roughly the proportions of a body fluid. That combination led to it being used as an intravenous drip in genuine emergencies where sterile saline was not available, in the Pacific during the Second World War and in scattered documented cases since. It is not standard medicine, it is not something to attempt, and it works only from an unopened young nut — but it happened, and it is a remarkable thing to know about the liquid you are simmering a chicken in. It is also the reason coconut water tenderises where plain water does nothing at all: it is not water with a flavour, it is a plant's own nutrient fluid, mildly acidic and full of sugar, and both of those act on meat.\n\nThe other half of it is that the water and the white flesh are the same tissue at two stages of its life. The liquid is endosperm — the nut's food store for the seedling — and it starts out entirely liquid and then gradually solidifies from the shell inward as the coconut matures. So a young green coconut is mostly water with a thin jelly lining, and an old brown one is thick firm flesh with only a little liquid left, and the difference between them is time rather than variety. Which is exactly why the two things are bought differently: split a young nut for water, buy a mature one for flesh, and never expect much of either from the wrong one.\n\nKalasan itself is a small place in Yogyakarta, out towards the Prambanan temples, and its claim on this dish is specific: the version that made it famous was built around ayam kampung, the lean, tough, muscular free-range village chicken that will not go tender in a frying pan. The braise is not a refinement, it is a solution to that bird. Everything good about the recipe — the coconut water, the long simmer, the reduced liquid, the crumbs made from it — follows from the problem of cooking a chicken that has spent its life walking around.","howThisFeels":"Scraping the last dark sludge out of the pan and knowing it is about to become the crunchiest thing on the table.","leftovers":["(easy) Chicken reheated 8 to 10 minutes in a 200°C oven, which re-tightens the skin — with fresh sambal and rice it is barely a leftover at all.","(easy) Cold, pulled off the bone and eaten straight, where the reduced bumbu clinging to the meat tastes stronger than it did hot.","(medium) Meat shredded off the bone and fried hard in a dry pan until the edges crisp, then folded through rice with kecap manis at the last second.","(creative) Shredded chicken simmered 10 minutes in coconut milk with a spoonful of sambal until it thickens, which turns a fried dish into a completely different braised one and is the best possible use of the last drumstick."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Ayam Kremes Sederhana — Water Braise, Shallow Fry (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":39,"howThisFeels":"A frying pan and a tablespoon of oil, and the lace still arrives on top.","trivia":"This drops the four dearest lines and deliberately keeps the kremesan, because the lace is the dish and a budget version without it is just fried chicken. What actually leaves: coconut water, 320ml of frying oil, the macadamias and the galangal. The one that costs you something real is the coconut water, so do not pretend otherwise — without its acidity the meat needs a slightly longer braise to reach the same tenderness, and without its sugars the skin browns less readily, which is why the tamarind goes up and a little extra jaggery goes in. Those two together restore some acid and some sugar, which is precisely what was lost. The shallow fry costs you evenness rather than crispness: you turn the pieces more often and the lace spreads flatter and wider in a shallow pan, so make it in smaller drizzles. It lands close, for well under half the money.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200ml coconut water","to":"200ml water"},{"from":"400ml sunflower oil","to":"80ml sunflower oil"},{"from":"5g tamarind paste","to":"8g tamarind paste"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"8g macadamia nuts"},{"item":"5g galangal"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Braise in 200ml of plain water instead of coconut water, with 8g of tamarind paste rather than 5g, and leave out the macadamias and the galangal. Give the braise 45 to 50 minutes rather than 35 to 40 — without the acidity of coconut water the connective tissue takes longer to give way, and a skewer going in with no resistance is still the test. Reduce it down to the same two or three tablespoons; that part never changes. Then fry in a frying pan in just 80ml of oil, deep enough to come a third of the way up the pieces, turning them every minute or so for about 6 minutes so every face gets colour — a shallow pan will not brown the top of a drumstick on its own. Lift the chicken out and make the lace in the same oil, but in much smaller drizzles, three or four at a time, because a shallow pan gives the threads nowhere to spread and a big drizzle lands as one flat sheet. Keep the oil hot between batches; that requirement does not relax just because there is less of it."}]}},{"name":"Ayam Goreng Kalasan — Coconut Water Braise, Full Kremesan","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":77,"howThisFeels":"The moment the thin batter hits the oil and blooms into a raft of white threads.","trivia":"The lace works because of what rice flour cannot do. A wheat-flour batter forms gluten, gluten makes an elastic film, and that film traps steam and holds the batter together as a continuous shell — which is what you want on a fritter and exactly what you do not want here. Rice flour has no gluten at all, so a thin rice-flour stream hitting hot oil has nothing holding it together: it fragments into separate threads, each one thin enough that its water flashes off almost instantly, and each sets rigid before it can slump back into its neighbours. The cornflour alongside it is there for a second reason — it gelatinises fast and then dries hard, which is what makes the finished lace shatter rather than bend. And because neither flour can form gluten, there is no protein network to soften and go leathery as it takes up moisture. It stays brittle for as long as it stays dry, which is about twenty minutes, and then air wins."},{"name":"Ayam Goreng Lengkuas — Buried in Crisp Galangal","icon":"🌿","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":95,"howThisFeels":"A plate that smells of pine and pepper before it smells of chicken.","trivia":"This is the Sumatran cousin and it uses galangal at a quantity that looks like a mistake — not a few grams in a bumbu but a genuine handful, grated coarse and cooked with the chicken until it dries out and then fried alongside it until the shreds go crisp and brown. Galangal is far more fibrous and much less watery than ginger, and that is what makes this possible: fine ginger shreds given the same treatment turn to bitter black threads within seconds, while galangal has enough structure to dry into something like a crisp savoury hay that holds its shape and its flavour. Cooked this way it loses the raw camphor-and-pine sharpness and turns nutty and peppery. The result is a dish where the aromatic is a texture as well as a flavour, which is unusual anywhere, and it is worth doing at least once to understand what galangal actually is rather than treating it as a hard ginger.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"20g galangal"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add a further 20g of galangal on top of the 5g in the bumbu, coarsely grated rather than pounded — you want visible shreds, not paste. Put it into the braise with everything else, where it will soften and give up its flavour to the liquid. When you reduce down at the end, keep the shreds in the pan rather than picking them out, and let them dry out with the last of the liquid until they look like damp threads rather than anything wet. Lift the chicken out and scrape the galangal shreds aside separately from the sludge you are keeping for the kremesan. Dry the shreds on paper for the same 10 minutes as the chicken. Then fry them FIRST, before the chicken, in the hot oil for 60 to 90 seconds until they turn golden and stop bubbling, and lift them straight out — they go from golden to burnt and bitter in about fifteen seconds and there is no warning, so stand over them and do not walk away. Drain on paper. Fry the chicken and make the lace as normal, then scatter the crisp galangal over the top with the kremesan. Expect the plate to smell strongly of pine and pepper, and expect people to ask what it is."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Rujak","target":"indonesia-rujak","emoji":"🍍"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-urap-urap","name":"Urap-Urap","nameAlt":"Greens in seasoned steamed coconut","aliases":["Urap-Urap","Urap","Urap Sayur","Urab","Javanese Coconut Salad","Vegetables in Seasoned Coconut","Serundeng Kelapa"],"course":"side","type":["side","salad","vegetables","coconut","steamed","vegan","light","braai"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","braai","celebration"],"ingredients":"60g green beans · 50g cabbage · 30g bean sprouts · 30g carrots · 30g desiccated coconut · 10g shallots · 8g garlic · 8g jaggery · 5g galangal · 5g red chilli · 3g shrimp paste · 3g tamarind paste · 2g salt · 2 bay leaves","method":"The vegetables are not the dish. The dressing is, and it is a strange one — not a sauce, not a paste, but a loose, dry, savoury fluff of seasoned coconut that coats each piece of vegetable in separate threads. Getting it to stay loose is the entire skill, and there is exactly one instruction that decides it: YOU STEAM THE COCONUT. Not fry, not stir into a pan, and not leave raw. Everything else here is arranged to protect that.\n\nHERE IS WHY, BECAUSE IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. Coconut is more than a third fat by weight, and coconut fat melts at about 24°C — barely above room temperature. So the moment grated coconut meets a hot pan and a spoon, the fat liquefies, the threads slick together, and what you had as a fluff becomes an oily clump. Frying it is not a slightly different result, it is a different dish. But leaving the bumbu raw is no good either: raw garlic and raw shrimp paste in the mouth are harsh and the garlic drowns everything. Steam solves both at once. It is wet heat with no fat added and no agitation, so it cooks the aromatics through while the coconut is never stirred and never gets hot enough for long enough to render out and bind. You get cooked seasoning on uncooked-textured coconut, which is exactly the point.\n\nSO START BY REHYDRATING THE COCONUT, AND DO NOT SKIP THIS. Indonesia uses coconut grated fresh that morning, which is damp and sweet and soft. Desiccated coconut is the same thing dried hard, and if you steam it dry it stays hard — you get a sandy, chalky dressing that scratches, and no amount of extra steaming fixes it. Put the 30g of desiccated coconut in a bowl, pour over 45ml of warm water, stir, and leave it 15 minutes until it has drunk the lot and gone soft and plump. ⛔ Warm WATER, not coconut milk and not oil. Coconut milk is fat, and adding fat to coconut is how you get the clumping you are trying to avoid. If there is any free liquid left after 15 minutes, squeeze it out gently.\n\nPOUND THE BUMBU COARSE. Into a mortar go the 10g of shallots, the 8g of garlic, the 5g of red chilli, the 5g of galangal, the 2g of salt and the 3g of shrimp paste, and pound to a rough paste — coarse, not smooth, because you want to find flecks of it. Then work in the 8g of jaggery until it smears, and the 3g of tamarind paste. ⚠️ The signature aromatic in a real urap is kencur, sand ginger, which is a third plant entirely — not ginger and not galangal, sharper and more medicinal than either — and it is not sold here. There are two honest routes and it is worth knowing which you are taking. Galangal is the closest structural relative and goes in measure for measure, which is what the 5g here already is — what it lacks is kencur's medicinal, almost camphor-like sharpness, so the result is rounder and less startling. The other route is regular ginger with a pinch of white pepper, and the white pepper is the part that matters: ginger alone is sweeter and hotter and pulls the dish somewhere else, but ginger carrying white pepper's dry back-of-the-throat pungency lands much closer to the warming kick kencur brings. Use one or the other, not both. What is not worth doing is buying imported kencur powder for this — it is specialty-import only here and priced accordingly, and a clean version of something is better than a muddy version of something else.\n\nMIX THE BUMBU THROUGH THE COCONUT WITH YOUR FINGERTIPS, not a spoon. Rub it in lightly until the colour is even and every thread carries some — it should look like damp, faintly orange sawdust. Tuck the 2 bay leaves in. A spoon compresses; fingers separate.\n\nTHEN STEAM IT 15 MINUTES AND LEAVE IT ALONE. Spread it loosely in a shallow bowl or on a plate — loose, not packed, so steam moves through it — and steam over simmering water for 15 minutes. Do not stir it at any point. It will smell raw and sharp for the first five minutes and then turn savoury and rounded, and that shift is the thing you are waiting for. Take it out, discard the bay leaves, and fluff it apart with a fork while it is still warm, which is when it separates most easily. Let it cool to room temperature. ⚠️ Warm coconut is above the melting point of its own fat and will smear if you dress vegetables with it hot.\n\nNOW THE VEGETABLES, AND THE RULE HERE IS DRYNESS. Blanch them separately in well-salted boiling water — the 60g of green beans cut into 4cm lengths, the 50g of cabbage shredded, the 30g of carrots cut into thin batons, and the 30g of bean sprouts last and briefly. Each one comes out when it is done rather than on a shared timer, and the Gado-Gado card explains why that matters in full; what belongs to THIS dish is what happens next. Drain everything, then SPREAD IT OUT on a tray or a clean cloth and leave it 10 minutes to steam itself dry. ⛔ This is the step that separates a good urap from a wet one. A wet vegetable turns the coconut fluff to paste on contact, instantly and irreversibly, because you are adding water to something whose whole character is dryness. Beans and cabbage hold water in their folds; sprouts hold it inside. Blot them if you need to. They should feel dry to the touch and no longer look glossy.\n\nTOSS IT AT THE LAST MINUTE, WITH YOUR HANDS. Put the dry cooled vegetables in a wide bowl, scatter the seasoned coconut over in three additions, and lift and fold with your fingers between each one rather than stirring — you are trying to distribute, not to mix. Three light additions coat far better than one heavy one. Taste, and correct with salt rather than anything else; if it seems flat it almost always wants salt, and if it seems dull it wants a squeeze of lime, off the heat and at the very end.\n\nSERVE IT WITHIN THE HOUR, at room temperature or barely cool, never chilled and never hot. It is at its best about ten minutes after tossing, when the coconut has picked up just enough vegetable moisture to stop tasting dry and not enough to go soft.","cookTime":"Rehydrate 15 min · Prep 20 min · Steam 15 min · Blanch 10 min · Dry 10 min","kcal":"~210 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 6g · Carbs 16g · Fat 13g · Sodium ~880mg","storage":"⛔ Dressed urap does not keep, and the reason is the same mechanism that makes it good: the coconut goes on dry and immediately begins drawing water out of the vegetables, so within a couple of hours the fluff has become a damp paste and the vegetables have gone limp in it. An hour is the window. Refrigerating it makes it worse rather than better, because cold sets the coconut fat hard and the dressing turns waxy and gritty on the tongue. Store it as two halves and it becomes one of the most convenient things in the section: the steamed seasoned coconut keeps in a closed container in the fridge for 4 days and freezes for 2 months, losing nothing at all, and blanched dried vegetables keep 2 days in a sealed box. Then toss a fresh portion in two minutes whenever you want one. ⚠️ Bring the coconut back to room temperature before using it from the fridge, or it will not fluff — cold coconut fat is solid and the threads stay stuck together. A few seconds of steam or 20 seconds in a warm oven loosens it. The dry-fried version, unlike this one, keeps for weeks in a jar, which is the real argument for making it.","chefNotes":"STEAM the seasoned coconut, never fry it — coconut fat melts at about 24°C, so a hot pan and a spoon turn a loose fluff into an oily clump. Rehydrate desiccated coconut in warm WATER for 15 minutes first, never in coconut milk, or the dressing comes out sandy. Mix the bumbu in with fingertips, not a spoon. Do not stir it while it steams, and cool it before it meets the vegetables. Then the real make-or-break: blanch the vegetables, spread them out and let them steam DRY for 10 minutes, because a wet vegetable turns the fluff to paste on contact. Toss with your hands in three additions, at the last minute. Kencur is not sold here: use galangal measure for measure, or ginger with a pinch of white pepper — ginger alone is too sweet, the white pepper is what supplies the missing pungency.","pairsWith":"It is a side and it works hardest next to something rich, fatty and hot off a fire — grilled skewers, fried chicken, anything charred — because a dry savoury coconut dressing cuts fat in a way a wet dressing cannot, and it brings crunch without bringing liquid to the plate. With plain rice and a spoonful of sambal it is a light meal on its own. It is also a standard component of the ceremonial rice cones served at Javanese celebrations, which is why you will see it in the middle of a very large spread rather than as anything precious. ⛔ Do not put it beside another coconut dish or another peanut-dressed one; the flavours are close enough that both get lost, and the Gado-Gado card is the peanut version of this idea rather than a companion to it. Something acidic on the table helps — pickles, or a sharp fruit salad.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Sambal Terasi"],"trivia":"Freshly grated coconut is one of the most perishable foods in any kitchen, and understanding why explains both this recipe and the existence of an entire global industry. Grating a coconut ruptures millions of oil-rich cells at once and exposes their contents to air, to the coconut's own enzymes and to whatever bacteria were on the shell and the blade — and the result is a food that is roughly a third fat and half water sitting at tropical temperature. It sours and goes rancid within hours. Left out overnight it is finished; even refrigerated it is a day or two at most. That single fact shapes the way coconut is handled across South and Southeast Asia: markets grate it to order in front of you, households own a dedicated grater — the kukuran, a low bench with a serrated iron blade that you sit astride and work the half-shell against — and nobody grates more than they are about to use. Desiccated coconut exists because of this problem and nothing else. Drying it to a few percent moisture stops the enzymes, halts the rancidity and turns a food with a half-day shelf life into one with a year of it, which is what made coconut a commodity that could cross oceans at all. What it costs you is the softness and the sweetness, which is precisely what the fifteen-minute soak in warm water is trying to buy back.\n\nThe melting point is the other half of it, and it is why this dish insists on being cool. Coconut fat is more than four fifths saturated, which is unusual for a plant fat, and it melts at around 24 to 25°C — so grated coconut is crumbly and dry-feeling in a cool kitchen and greasy in a warm one, with the change happening over just a couple of degrees. That is the whole reason urap is steamed rather than fried and served at room temperature rather than hot. It is also why the same seasoned coconut, taken deliberately past that point in a dry pan, becomes an entirely different and equally traditional thing: serundeng, golden and crunchy and toasted, which keeps for weeks in a jar where urap keeps for an hour. Same bowl of ingredients, one decision, two dishes with opposite shelf lives.","howThisFeels":"Rubbing spice paste into coconut with your fingertips and watching pale threads turn faintly orange.","leftovers":["(easy) There are no dressed leftovers worth having, but the steamed coconut keeps 4 days — blanch a fresh handful of whatever vegetable is in the fridge and you have the dish again in two minutes.","(easy) Leftover seasoned coconut scattered over plain rice with a fried egg, which is a real Javanese breakfast and not a compromise.","(medium) Dressed urap that has gone damp, tipped into a hot dry pan and cooked until the coconut toasts and the vegetables catch — it becomes a stir-fry and is genuinely good.","(creative) The steamed coconut dry-fried in a pan until golden, then kept in a jar as serundeng and sprinkled over anything for weeks afterwards — soups, rice, roast vegetables. It is the single most useful thing to do with the surplus."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Urap Sayur — No Terasi (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Cleaner and sweeter, and the coconut comes forward instead of hiding behind the shrimp paste.","trivia":"Two things leave here and only one of them costs you anything. Bean sprouts are the most expensive vegetable per kilo in the recipe by a wide margin — they are sold in small punnets rather than by weight and they price accordingly — and losing them costs texture rather than flavour, which extra cabbage replaces almost exactly since both are there for crunch and water. The shrimp paste is the real loss. Three grams of it was supplying the savoury depth that makes a bowl of vegetables and coconut taste like a dish rather than a salad, and there is no plant substitute that does the same job. So the compensation is not to replace it but to lean harder on the two things that remain: more tamarind for sourness and a definite increase in salt. Sweet, sour and salt can carry a dressing between them; they simply carry it differently, and the result is brighter and cleaner rather than deep. Taste it more than once, because without the shrimp paste there is nothing hiding an underseasoned bowl.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"30g bean sprouts"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"50g cabbage","to":"80g cabbage"},{"from":"3g tamarind paste","to":"5g tamarind paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave out the shrimp paste and the bean sprouts, use 80g of cabbage instead of 50g, and 5g of tamarind paste instead of 3g. Pound the bumbu as normal — it will be looser without the solid block of paste in it, which is fine. Push the salt up from 2g to 3g and taste the coconut again after steaming rather than before, because steaming rounds the flavours and an underseasoned bumbu that tasted acceptable raw will taste of nothing once it has been through the steamer. Everything else is identical, including the 15-minute soak, the no-stirring rule and the 10 minutes of drying the vegetables. Shred the extra cabbage finer than you would normally, closer to 3mm, so it brings the same fine crunch the sprouts were bringing rather than sitting in the bowl as broad wet strips."}]}},{"name":"Urap-Urap — Steamed Seasoned Coconut","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid on the steamer and having it stop smelling of raw garlic and start smelling like food.","trivia":"The fifteen minutes of steaming is doing something quite specific to the garlic, and it is the reason urap made properly tastes nothing like a raw coconut salad. Crushing garlic sets off an enzyme reaction that produces allicin, the sharp aggressive compound behind raw garlic's bite — and allicin is unstable and heat-sensitive. Fifteen minutes of wet heat destroys it and converts what is left into a range of milder sulphur compounds that read as savoury and faintly sweet rather than pungent. The shrimp paste is going the other way at the same time: gentle moist heat drives off its volatile ammonia notes while leaving behind the glutamates that make it taste savoury. So the steamer is not simply cooking the dressing, it is removing the two harshest things in it and keeping the two best. Raw, the same bumbu on the same coconut is genuinely unpleasant, and people who have tried urap once and disliked it have almost always eaten it unsteamed."},{"name":"Serundeng Kelapa — Dry-Fried Golden Coconut","icon":"🥘","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":31,"howThisFeels":"Standing over a pan for twelve minutes doing nothing but stirring, and the whole house smelling toasted.","trivia":"This is the same bowl of ingredients taken deliberately past the point the main recipe protects, and it is worth making once precisely because it proves why the main recipe steams. Taken into a dry pan and stirred, the coconut fat renders out, the threads slick together, the sugars in the jaggery and the coconut itself begin to brown, and instead of a loose white fluff you get a golden, nutty, crunchy crumble. Nothing has gone wrong — this is serundeng, and it is as traditional as urap. The trade is in the shelf life and it is dramatic: driving off the moisture and toasting the fat gives you something that keeps for weeks in a sealed jar, where urap is finished in an hour. The two dishes are a lesson in what heat does to coconut, sitting side by side in the same cuisine. ⚠️ It will go from golden to burnt in under a minute at the end, and burnt coconut is acrid and cannot be rescued, so the last three minutes are stand-over-it minutes.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Follow the recipe right through the steaming, then keep going. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil in a wide frying pan over medium-low heat, add the steamed seasoned coconut, and stir it more or less continuously for 12 to 15 minutes. Low and slow, not hot and fast: at high heat the outside toasts before the inside dries and you get a mixture that is simultaneously burnt and damp. Watch it go through three stages — pale and damp, then dry and sandy, then finally golden and starting to smell nutty and toasted. ⛔ The last 3 minutes are the whole job, because it goes from golden to acrid burnt in under a minute and there is no rescuing it. Take it off while it is one shade lighter than you want, since it keeps colouring in the residual heat of the pan. Tip it straight out onto a cold plate to stop it. Cool it completely before it goes anywhere near the vegetables, and expect a crunchier, sweeter, nuttier dish with far less of the fresh sharp edge. Any surplus goes into a sealed jar and keeps for weeks, which is the real reason to make it."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥗"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bawang-goreng","name":"Bawang Goreng","nameAlt":"Crisp fried shallots","aliases":["Bawang Goreng","Fried Shallots","Crispy Shallots","Bawang Merah Goreng","Bawang Putih Goreng","Fried Garlic","Minyak Bawang","Shallot Oil"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","condiment","garnish","fried","vegan","make-ahead","budget"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","guests","party","celebration"],"ingredients":"150ml sunflower oil · 60g shallots · 5g cornflour · 3g salt","method":"Almost nothing in an Indonesian kitchen gets used more than this. Fried shallots go over soup, over rice, over noodles, over porridge, over fried rice that already has them in it — and a home-made batch is a completely different substance from the packet kind, which is usually stale and tastes of old oil. It takes half an hour and two rules, and both of them are counter-intuitive: THEY GO INTO COLD OIL, AND THEY COME OUT LOOKING UNDERDONE.\n\nSLICE THEM THIN AND, MUCH MORE IMPORTANTLY, EVENLY. Peel the 60g of shallots and slice them into rings 1 to 2mm thick. A mandoline is worth using if you own one, and a properly sharp knife and a bit of patience if you do not. Thickness matters less than consistency, and this is the single most common reason a batch fails: in one pan of oil every slice gets exactly the same treatment, so if some are 1mm and some are 3mm, the thin ones will be burnt and bitter by the time the thick ones have stopped being soft. There is no technique that rescues an unevenly sliced batch. Separate the rings with your fingers so none are stuck together in stacks.\n\nTOSS THEM WITH THE CORNFLOUR, DRY. Five grams over 60g of shallots is a light dusting, not a coating — shake off anything loose. It is doing one job: it soaks up the film of surface moisture the shallots weep as they sit, so that they hit the oil dry rather than spitting and steaming, and it adds a very thin layer that fries brittle. Cornflour rather than wheat flour, because cornflour is pure starch with no protein in it, which is why it dries hard and stays hard instead of softening back the way a flour coating does. Skip it and you still get fried shallots; they are simply a little less crisp and they soften sooner.\n\nNOW THE COLD START, WHICH IS THE HALF PEOPLE GET WRONG. Put the shallots into a small saucepan, pour the 150ml of sunflower oil over them so they are just floating, and only THEN put it on the heat, medium-low. Do not preheat the oil. A shallot is mostly water, and dropping it into oil that is already at 180°C boils the surface violently and starts browning the outside while the inside is still wet — so it colours before it dries, and a shallot that browns before it dries can never go crisp. It goes chewy and slightly leathery instead, and that is why most disappointing homemade batches fail — not because they were underfried, but because they were browned in the wrong order. Bringing them up slowly from cold does the two jobs in the right order: first the water leaves, steadily and gently, and only once they are actually dry do they begin to brown. You will hear the change — a loud constant fizz for fifteen or twenty minutes as water boils off, then the sound drops away as it stops.\n\nSTIR OCCASIONALLY AND GIVE IT 20 TO 30 MINUTES. Keep the heat moderate, so that the water leaves faster than the sugars can colour; you are aiming for a lazy steady bubble, never a hard rolling fry, because a hard fry drives the outside past golden while the centre is still wet. Stir every few minutes so that nothing sits against the base, which is hotter than the oil around it and will scorch a slice that rests on it. For most of this they will look pale and nothing will appear to be happening, and that is correct — the whole first two thirds is dehydration, not cooking.\n\nTHEN WATCH THEM, AND PULL THEM EARLY. Once the fizzing quietens they will start to turn colour, and from there it moves fast. ⛔ TAKE THEM OUT AT PALE STRAW GOLD — noticeably lighter than you want them to end up, when your instinct says they need another two minutes. They are thin, sugary and full of hot oil, and they keep cooking hard after they leave the pan; a batch lifted at the colour you actually want will be dark brown and bitter by the time it reaches the plate. Two shades early. Strain them through a sieve set over a bowl, and do it quickly.\n\nSPREAD THEM OUT AT ONCE AND SALT THEM NOW, NOT BEFORE. Tip them onto kitchen paper and spread them into a single layer straight away — a pile traps its own steam and the middle goes soft while the edges crisp, and once softened they do not come back. Scatter the 3g of salt over while they are still hot and glistening so it sticks. ⚠️ Salting before frying is a real mistake and not a stylistic one: salt draws water to the surface by osmosis, which is precisely the water you have spent twenty minutes removing.\n\nAND THEY CRISP AS THEY COOL, NOT IN THE PAN. Straight out of the oil they are still floppy, and it is tempting to conclude they need longer — this is the trap. Shallots have essentially no starch; what makes a fried shallot shatter is its own sugars, concentrated as the water left and then setting hard and brittle as the temperature drops. Give them 10 minutes. If after ten minutes at room temperature they are still bendy rather than snapping, they genuinely were underdone and can go back into the oil for a minute or two — but judge that cold, never hot.\n\nAND KEEP THE OIL. What is left in the bowl is minyak bawang, shallot oil, and it is deeply savoury and quietly one of the most useful things you will make this month. Strain it into a jar once cool. A spoonful stirred into plain noodles, rice, or a soup at the table does more than most sauces.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Fry 25 min · Cool 10 min","kcal":"~90 kcal per serving of shallots","nutrition":"Protein 1g · Carbs 6g · Fat 7g · Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Properly made and properly cooled, they keep 3 to 4 weeks in an airtight jar at room temperature, which is the entire argument for making a big batch rather than a small one. ⛔ The two rules are absolute and they are both about moisture. First, they must be COMPLETELY cold before the lid goes on — a jar sealed while they are even slightly warm traps steam, and by morning the whole batch is soft. Second, keep them somewhere dark and dry and never in the fridge: the crispness is a sugar glass, and sugar glass pulls water straight out of humid air, so a fridge is close to the worst place you could put them. If a batch does go soft, it is recoverable — spread them on a tray and give them 8 to 10 minutes in a 140°C oven, then cool them completely again, and they come back almost fully. The shallot oil keeps separately in a sealed jar for 2 months at room temperature and longer in the fridge, where it will go cloudy and solid and clear again on standing, which is normal and not a fault. ⚠️ Do not store the shallots submerged in their own oil expecting them to stay crisp; they will not, and you get soft shallots in nice oil instead of both.","chefNotes":"Slice them EVENLY above all else — one pan of oil treats every slice the same, so mixed thicknesses means burnt and soft in the same batch, and nothing rescues it. Start them in COLD oil and bring it up slowly, because a shallot that browns before it dries can never crisp. Then take them out two shades lighter than you want: they are thin and sugary and full of hot oil, and they keep cooking hard on the paper. Spread them in a single layer immediately and salt them after frying, never before. Judge crispness once they are cool, not hot — they set as they cool, not in the pan. Cool completely before the lid goes on, and keep the oil.","pairsWith":"The honest answer is almost everything, and that is what makes it a staple rather than a garnish. It is scattered over fried rice, over noodle soups, over beef ball soup, over plain steamed rice with nothing else, and over coconut rice — and in each case it is doing the same job, which is adding a savoury crunch that no wet component on the plate can provide. It sits particularly well on anything soft or soupy, where it is the only texture in the bowl. The shallot oil belongs with plain things: noodles with nothing but oil, soy and shallots is a real and very good meal. ⚠️ One place to hold back is anything already deep-fried and crisp, where it adds nothing and just makes the plate greasy. Keep a jar on the table and let people help themselves, which is how it is actually used.","goesWith":["Bakso","Nasi Goreng","Nasi Uduk","Gudeg"],"trivia":"Shallots are not a garnish in Indonesia, they are a commodity with political weight, and the price of them turns up in national news in a way that is genuinely hard to imagine for a fried topping. Bawang merah — red shallots — go into practically every savoury dish in the country, which means demand is enormous, constant and almost completely unresponsive to price: when they get expensive, people do not stop buying them, they simply pay more. That is a textbook recipe for volatility, and combined with a crop that is perishable, weather-sensitive and harvested in concentrated seasons, shallot prices swing hard. Together with chillies they are among the most-watched items in Indonesia's food inflation figures, they are a recurring reason the monthly inflation number moves, and governments respond with stockpiles, import permits and market interventions in a way normally reserved for fuel or rice. Brebes, on the north coast of Central Java, grows so much of the national crop that the town is essentially synonymous with the bulb. It is a strange thing to hold in your head while slicing sixty grams of them: this is a garnish that shows up in monetary policy discussions.\n\nThe second thing worth knowing is why it has to be shallots and not onions, because the substitution looks harmless and is not. Shallots carry less water and proportionally more sugar and sulphur compounds than a brown onion, and both differences matter here. Less water means there is less to drive off before browning can start, so they dry out within the time it takes to cook them rather than after it. More sugar means more of the material that concentrates and sets into the brittle glass that makes them shatter. An onion put through exactly this method comes out darker, softer and slightly leathery, because it is still giving up water at the point where it should already be crisping, and it browns on the outside while the middle is damp. It is perfectly edible and it is not the same thing — which is why the cheap version below is honest about being a compromise rather than pretending the two are interchangeable.","howThisFeels":"The moment the loud fizzing drops to almost nothing and you know the water has gone and the clock has started.","leftovers":["(easy) They are made to be leftovers — a jar on the table for three or four weeks, over rice, noodles, soup and eggs.","(easy) A batch gone soft, revived on a tray for 8 to 10 minutes in a 140°C oven and cooled completely again.","(medium) The shallot oil stirred through plain hot noodles with a splash of soy sauce, which is a two-minute supper and better than it has any right to be.","(creative) Crushed to coarse crumbs with a little salt and dried chilli and kept as a savoury dust, which turns plain rice, boiled potatoes or roast vegetables into something with a whole extra register."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bawang Goreng Bawang Bombay — Onion, the Cheap Route (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":7,"howThisFeels":"Not quite as brittle, and still miles better than anything from a packet.","trivia":"This is a genuine compromise rather than a hidden upgrade, and it is worth being straight about what changes. A brown onion holds noticeably more water than a shallot and proportionally less sugar, so the drying phase runs longer and the crisping phase has less material to work with. The practical consequences are three: it takes about ten minutes more in the pan, the slices shrink much more dramatically so 60g of onion yields less than 60g of shallots would, and the finished crisp is a shade softer and goes soft sooner in the jar — expect two weeks rather than four. What you do NOT lose is the flavour, which is the surprising part; fried onion and fried shallot taste far more alike than raw onion and raw shallot do, because most of the harsh sulphur compounds that distinguish them raw are driven off or transformed by twenty-five minutes in hot oil anyway. For scattering over soup it is honestly close. For a jar meant to last a month it is not.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"60g shallots","to":"60g onion"},{"from":"150ml sunflower oil","to":"100ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 60g of onion instead of shallots, and 100ml of oil rather than 150ml, which is enough for the smaller volume once the onion starts collapsing. Slice it into rings the same 1 to 2mm and separate them properly — onion rings stick together in stacks much more stubbornly than shallot rings, and a stuck stack steams instead of frying. Cold start exactly as before. The drying phase runs longer, so expect 30 to 40 minutes in total rather than 20 to 30, and do not be tempted to raise the heat to make up the difference, because that reintroduces the exact problem the cold start exists to avoid. Listen for the same signal: a long loud fizz, then quiet. Pull them at pale gold, two shades early, same as the main method. They will look like far less than you started with; that is the water leaving and it is normal. Cool completely and expect them to keep about two weeks rather than a month."}]}},{"name":"Bawang Goreng — Shallots, Cold Start","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Tipping a jar of them over a bowl of soup and hearing them land.","trivia":"The reason a home batch tastes so much better than a packet is not freshness in the vague sense, it is a specific and measurable thing about the oil. Fried shallots are roughly a fifth oil by weight when they come out of the pan, so a commercial packet is not really a dry product — it is a fatty one, and its shelf life is limited by the fat rather than by the shallot. Oil oxidises on contact with air and light, slowly and continuously, producing the flat cardboard-and-crayon flavours we call rancid, and once that has started nothing removes it. A packet that has sat under supermarket lighting for four months is carrying oil that has been oxidising for four months, which is why so many of them taste faintly stale even when they are perfectly crisp. Your batch carries oil that is half an hour old. That is the whole difference, and it is also why an airtight jar in a dark cupboard genuinely matters rather than being fussiness: you are protecting the fat, not the crunch."},{"name":"Bawang Putih Goreng — Crisp Fried Garlic","icon":"🧄","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"A far shorter fuse than the shallots, and the smell arrives about a minute before it is ready.","trivia":"Garlic is the same idea on a much tighter window, and treating it like shallots is how people end up with a pan of bitter black slivers. Two things make it unforgiving. It has less water and much more sugar than a shallot, so it moves from dry to browning to burnt far faster — the useful window is measured in tens of seconds rather than minutes. And burnt garlic is not merely darker garlic; the compounds that develop past a certain point are genuinely acrid and bitter in a way that burnt shallot is not, so there is no cooking through it or masking it. The fix is entirely about lowering the stakes: a lower temperature, a slightly thicker slice, and a pull that is earlier still. Fried garlic is worth the nerve because it does something shallots cannot — it is sharper and more insistent, and a spoonful over a plain bowl of noodles or a soup announces itself. The garlic oil left behind is, if anything, more useful than the shallot version.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"60g shallots","to":"60g garlic"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 60g of peeled garlic cloves in place of the shallots — about two whole heads. Slice them a little thicker than you would shallots, a full 2mm rather than 1mm, which buys you a few crucial extra seconds of margin. Toss with the cornflour as normal. Cold start in the 150ml of oil exactly as before, but keep the heat LOWER — low rather than medium-low — and expect the whole thing to take 15 to 20 minutes rather than 25 to 30, because there is less water to drive off. ⛔ From the moment the fizzing quietens, do not leave the pan and do not look away, because garlic goes from pale to gold to burnt in well under a minute and burnt garlic is bitter in a way that cannot be salvaged or hidden. Pull it at the very first blush of straw colour, earlier than feels sensible even by shallot standards, and strain it immediately — leaving it sitting in the hot oil for another twenty seconds while you find the sieve is exactly how it is lost, so have the sieve ready over the bowl before you start. Spread, salt, cool. It crisps hard and keeps 2 to 3 weeks."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Bakso","target":"indonesia-bakso","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Nasi Goreng","target":"indonesia-nasi-goreng","emoji":"🍳"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-serabi-solo","name":"Serabi Solo","nameAlt":"Coconut pancakes with lacy edges","aliases":["Serabi Solo","Srabi Solo","Serabi","Serabi Notosuman","Surabi","Javanese Coconut Pancake","Serabi Kinca"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","street-food","vegan","coconut","breakfast","budget"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","guests","party","celebration"],"ingredients":"120ml coconut milk · 60g rice flour · 30ml water · 20g white sugar · 2g salt · 1g instant yeast","method":"A serabi is two completely different things at once and it is made that way on purpose. The outer rim is thin, dry, lacy and crisp, browned at the very edge. The centre is thick, pale, soft and barely-set, closer to a custard than a pancake. One batter, one pan, one side of heat — and the reason both exist in the same object is that IT IS NEVER TURNED OVER. Flip a serabi and you have made a small pancake and thrown the entire dish away.\n\nTHE PAN DECIDES EVERYTHING, SO DEAL WITH IT FIRST. A serabi pan is a small bowl — a round-bottomed clay dish about 12cm across with sides that curve up steeply. That shape is not decoration. Poured into a bowl, the batter runs to the bottom and then a thin film clings up the curved sides, and it is that thin climbing film, pressed against hot metal with almost no depth to it, that dries out and goes lacy. A flat frying pan cannot do this: the batter spreads into an even disc, every part of it is the same depth, and it cooks into one uniform texture. So a flat pan does not give you a worse serabi, it gives you something else entirely. ⚠️ What works here is a small wok, a mini wok, a steel ladle wide enough to sit on the hob, or the smallest deep-sided non-stick pan you own with genuinely curved rather than square sides. If the sides go up at a right angle, the batter will not climb.\n\nMAKE THE BATTER THIN AND REST IT. Whisk the 60g of rice flour with the 20g of sugar and the 2g of salt, then beat in the 120ml of coconut milk and the 30ml of water until completely smooth, and finally the 1g of instant yeast. It should be properly thin — it wants to pour like cream, not drop like a batter, because a thick batter is too heavy to climb the sides at all. Cover and rest it 45 minutes somewhere warm. Two things happen in that time and only one of them is fermentation: the rice flour hydrates, its starch granules swelling and softening so the finished serabi is smooth rather than faintly gritty, and the yeast produces enough gas to open the texture into fine bubbles. ⚠️ The yeast is doing a small, short job here — the Bika Ambon card explains in full what a ferment does to a starch batter and why, and this is not that. There you are building channels over three hours; here you want bubbles in forty-five minutes and nothing more. Do not extend it hoping for more, because an over-rested batter thins out and loses the body it needs to climb.\n\nHEAT THE PAN DRY AND HARD, THEN DROP IT. Get the pan properly hot over medium heat — a drop of batter should set with a hiss on contact — and then turn the heat down to medium-low before you pour. This is worth doing in that order rather than simply starting low: you want the initial contact hot enough to seize the film against the sides instantly, and everything after that gentle enough that the custardy middle can set slowly without the base scorching. ⛔ No oil, or the barest smear on a pan you do not trust. Oil makes the batter slide back down instead of gripping, and a batter that slides has no lacy edge.\n\nPOUR IN ONE GO AND SWIRL EXACTLY ONCE. Ladle about 80ml of batter into the centre, then immediately pick the pan up and swirl it once so the batter rides up the sides, and put it straight back down. One swirl. Swirl twice and the first film has already begun setting, so the second wave runs over it and you get a thick doubled edge instead of a thin lacy one. What you want to see is the batter grip and stay high on the sides rather than sliding back to the middle — if it all runs back down, your batter is too thick or the pan is not hot enough.\n\nNOW LID ON, AND THE LID MUST BE DOMED AND MUST NOT TOUCH. Cover it and cook 6 to 8 minutes without moving it, without pressing it and without any thought of turning it. The dome is not there to speed things up; it is there because the top of a serabi is never in contact with anything, so it can only be cooked by trapped steam rising off the batter itself. ⛔ A flat lid is a genuine problem: condensation collects on it and drips straight back down onto the soft surface, pitting it and making it wet, and a serabi with a soggy pocked top is the second most common failure after flipping it. A domed lid runs the condensation down to the rim, away from the food. If your lid is flat, tilt it slightly so the drips run to one side.\n\nIT IS DONE WHEN THE MIDDLE HAS LOST ITS SHINE. Look at the surface rather than the clock. The top should be set and matte with a fine scatter of small holes across it, no longer glossy or liquid anywhere, and still pale — it should not colour at all. Meanwhile the edge, which you can see standing proud of the rim, will have gone dry, thin, faintly translucent and browned. That difference is the dish. ⚠️ If the middle is still shiny after 8 minutes the heat is too low; if the edge is dark before the middle sets, it is too high.\n\nLIFT IT OUT WITH A SPATULA AND FOLD IT. Slide it free — it should release cleanly — and either serve it flat or fold it loosely in half, which is how it is sold and which also keeps the soft centre from drying out. Eat serabi warm and eat it soon. It is at its best in the first ten minutes, while the edge is still crisp and the middle still soft, and it is genuinely a different and lesser thing an hour later.","cookTime":"Rest 45 min · Cook 8 min each · Serves 2 small serabi","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 4g · Carbs 44g · Fat 17g · Sodium ~790mg","storage":"⛔ Serabi is a ten-minute food and no amount of care changes that, because the two textures it is built on move in opposite directions as it sits. The crisp lacy edge takes up moisture from the custardy middle and goes soft, and the soft middle gives that moisture away and goes firm and rubbery, so within an hour you have a uniform chewy disc — which is precisely the thing the whole method exists to avoid. It cannot be revived: an oven crisps the edge and dries the middle further, and a microwave softens everything. Store the BATTER instead, which keeps very well: it holds 2 days covered in the fridge and is arguably better on day two, since the rice flour is fully hydrated by then. ⚠️ Bring it back to room temperature and stir it before using, because cold batter will not climb the sides of the pan and the yeast is sluggish straight from the fridge. It will also have thinned slightly overnight, so a small extra spoonful of rice flour may be needed to get it back to pouring-cream consistency. The kinca sauce is the one component that keeps properly — a week in the fridge in a closed jar, and it sets thick when cold, so warm it gently to pour.","chefNotes":"NEVER turn it over — the whole dish is one batter cooked from one side, and flipping it destroys both textures at once. Use a bowl-shaped pan with curved sides; a flat pan spreads the batter evenly and gives you a pancake instead. Batter thin enough to pour like cream, rested 45 minutes and no longer. Heat the pan hard, then drop to medium-low before pouring. Swirl exactly ONCE, or the second wave doubles the edge and you lose the lace. Domed lid, not touching, or condensation drips onto the soft top and pits it. Done when the middle is matte and still pale while the edge has gone thin and brown. Eat within ten minutes.","pairsWith":"Plain is the Solo way and it is worth eating at least one that way, because the dish is quietly salty as well as sweet and the coconut is the whole flavour. Strong black coffee or unsweetened tea beside it is the traditional pairing and the right one — it is rich and its edges are caramelised, so a bitter drink stops it cloying. If you are serving several, offer the palm sugar sauce on the side in a small bowl rather than pouring it over, since a poured sauce soaks the lacy edge within a minute and the whole point of the edge is that it is dry. It belongs on a table of small sweet things rather than at the end of a large meal, alongside steamed rice-flour sweets and fried bananas, and it is eaten as readily at seven in the morning as at four in the afternoon.","goesWith":["Black tea","Klepon","Es Cendol"],"trivia":"The pan is the interesting thing, because it is not really an Indonesian pan — it is an Indian Ocean one. A small round-bottomed vessel with steeply curving sides, used over a low fire with a domed lid to cook a fermented rice-and-coconut batter into something crisp at the rim and soft in the middle, turns up along a continuous arc of coastline: the appachatti of Kerala and Sri Lanka making appam, the pans of coastal Malaysia, the paired hemispherical moulds of Thailand making khanom krok, and the clay serabi pan of Java. The batters differ in detail and the names have nothing in common, but the object and the idea are recognisably the same, and they sit along exactly the routes that carried rice, coconut, spices, Islam and Hinduism back and forth across that ocean for well over a thousand years. The precise lines of descent are not documented and probably cannot be, but this is not a case of four cultures separately inventing the same specialised pan. Something travelled. It is worth knowing while you are hunting through your cupboard for a small wok, because the shape you are trying to reproduce is one of the older pieces of cooking technology still in daily use.\n\nSolo's own claim rests on one shop. Serabi Notosuman opened in the Notosuman district in 1923 and has been run by the same family since, and it makes serabi and essentially nothing else — plain, or with a scatter of chocolate — cooked over charcoal in rows of small clay pans, rolled and wrapped in banana leaf while still warm, and sold by the dozen. It is now the thing Solo is known for as a food city, and the queue starts early. There is something instructive in it for anyone cooking from this card: the shop has had a century to complicate the recipe and has not. Rice flour, coconut milk, sugar, salt, a little ferment, one side of heat, no flipping.","howThisFeels":"Lifting a domed lid and finding a pale set middle ringed by a brown lace collar standing up off the pan.","leftovers":["(easy) There are no good leftovers of the finished serabi, but the batter keeps 2 days and is better on day two — cook fresh ones in eight minutes whenever you want them.","(easy) Leftover kinca sauce in a jar, spooned over ice cream, bananas or plain yoghurt, where it does the work of a butterscotch sauce.","(medium) Yesterday's serabi torn up and warmed through in coconut milk with a little palm sugar until it softens into a loose pudding, which is far better than trying to re-crisp it.","(creative) Cooked serabi cut into strips, dried out slowly in a low oven until brittle, and used as a crisp topping over fruit or ice cream — turning the failure mode into the point."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Serabi Encer — Thin and Plain (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":7,"howThisFeels":"Crisper at the edge, less rich in the middle, and gone in three bites.","trivia":"Halving the coconut milk changes which of the two textures you get more of, and it is worth knowing which way it goes because it is not the direction most people expect. Coconut fat is what keeps the centre soft and custardy — fat interferes with the starch setting firmly, so a rich batter stays tender and a lean one sets more solidly. Cutting the coconut milk therefore gives you a crisper, drier, more brittle serabi with a thinner and less custardy middle, which is closer to a very thin crumpet than to the Notosuman version. That is a real loss in the centre and a small gain at the rim, since a leaner batter also climbs the pan more readily and dries into a finer lace. It is the everyday version rather than a compromised one, and it is what you make when you are cooking six of them for people who are going to eat them standing up.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120ml coconut milk","to":"60ml coconut milk"},{"from":"30ml water","to":"80ml water"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 60ml of coconut milk and 80ml of water rather than 120ml and 30ml, keeping the total liquid roughly the same so the batter still pours like cream. Rest it the same 45 minutes. Two adjustments in the pan: it will climb the sides more eagerly because it is leaner, so swirl a little more gently or the film goes too high and burns before the middle sets, and it will cook faster — start checking at 5 minutes rather than 6. Because there is less fat, the pan matters more, so make sure it is genuinely hot before you pour and consider the barest smear of oil on the base only, not up the sides. Expect a crisper, thinner, less rich result with a firmer centre. If you want to claw a little richness back for nothing, hold the salt at the full 2g — salt reads as richness in a lean sweet batter and is the cheapest correction available."}]}},{"name":"Serabi Solo Polos — Plain, the Notosuman Way","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Eating one plain and understanding why the shop never bothered to add anything.","trivia":"The reason one batter can hold a sixty-degree temperature difference across ten centimetres comes down to boiling water, and once it is clear the cooking times stop being arbitrary. The centre of a serabi is several millimetres deep and full of water, and water boiling off pins whatever contains it at right around 100°C — it cannot climb past that while there is still liquid there, no matter how hot the pan gets. So the middle poaches gently in its own moisture and stays pale, because browning reactions barely proceed at 100°C. The film clinging to the sides has almost no water in it and loses what it has within a minute, and once dry there is nothing pinning its temperature at all, so it races up past 150°C into proper caramelisation and browning. Same pan, same batter, same flame, two entirely different cooking processes running side by side — which is exactly why the dish only works in a bowl-shaped pan, and exactly why turning it over ruins it."},{"name":"Serabi Kinca — with Palm Sugar Coconut Sauce","icon":"🍯","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"A dark sauce poured at the table so the lace edge survives long enough to hear it crack.","trivia":"Kinca is a two-ingredient sauce that goes wrong for one reason, and the reason is that coconut milk is an emulsion rather than a liquid — droplets of fat suspended in water, held there by the coconut's own proteins. Boil it hard and those proteins denature and let go, the fat separates out, and what should be a glossy sauce becomes oily liquid with grainy curds in it. Sugar makes this worse rather than better, because a high sugar concentration draws water away from exactly the proteins doing the stabilising. So the whole technique is a temperature discipline: a bare tremble, never a boil, and constant gentle movement so no part of the pan gets ahead of the rest. Done properly it thickens as much from reduction as from anything else, and it goes on thickening as it cools, which is why it should come off the heat looking slightly thinner than you want it. The Bandung style serves serabi swimming in this; Solo keeps it on the side, and Solo is right, because a poured sauce softens the lace edge within about a minute.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"25g jaggery"},{"item":"40ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the serabi exactly as described. For the sauce, chop the 25g of jaggery small so it dissolves evenly, and put it in a small pan with the extra 40ml of coconut milk and a pinch of salt. ⛔ Melt it over LOW heat, stirring gently and continuously, and never let it reach a boil — a bare tremble at the edge is as far as it goes. Coconut milk boiled hard splits into oil and curds and there is no bringing it back, and the sugar makes it split more readily rather than less. It needs 4 to 6 minutes: the jaggery dissolves, then the sauce thickens slightly and turns glossy and dark. Take it off while it still looks a little thinner than you want, because it thickens considerably as it cools. Pour it into a small bowl and serve it BESIDE the serabi rather than over it — poured on, it soaks the lacy edge within a minute and you lose the one texture that took the whole method to build. Let people dip. Leftover sauce keeps a week in the fridge and sets firm when cold, so warm it briefly to pour again."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Klepon","target":"indonesia-klepon","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Bika Ambon","target":"indonesia-bika-ambon","emoji":"🍯"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sate-lilit","name":"Sate Lilit","nameAlt":"Balinese minced fish on lemongrass","aliases":["Sate Lilit","Satay Lilit","Balinese Satay","Sate Lilit Ikan","Lemongrass Satay","Minced Fish Satay","Sate Lilit Udang"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","fish","grilled","braai","skewer","street-food","party","coconut"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","braai","party","celebration"],"ingredients":"150g hake · 25g lemongrass · 25g desiccated coconut · 10g shallots · 8g garlic · 5g galangal · 5g macadamia nuts · 5g red chilli · 5g jaggery · 3g coriander seeds · 3g shrimp paste · 2g salt · 1g white pepper","method":"Every other sate is threaded — a stick goes through the meat and holds it. Sate lilit is the opposite: the meat is moulded around the OUTSIDE of a lemongrass stalk with nothing running through it at all, so from the moment it goes near a fire, gravity and steam are both working to make it fall off. Getting it to stay on is the dish. Once you see it as a gripping problem rather than a grilling one, every instruction below stops being fussy.\n\nMAKE THE MINCE A PASTE, NOT A CHOP. Skin the 150g of hake, check it over for pin bones, and cut it up before working it to a smooth sticky paste — a food processor in short bursts, or twenty minutes with a heavy knife and a bit of stubbornness. It needs to be genuinely tacky, so that a spoonful pulled away stretches slightly rather than crumbling, which is the point at which it will hold onto a stalk rather than sitting on one. ⚠️ The Bakso card explains exactly what is happening to the protein when you do this, and why salt is doing more than seasoning; it is the same mechanism and there is no point telling it twice. What matters HERE is temperature and one other ingredient. Keep the fish cold — straight from the fridge, and back in if the kitchen is warm — because fat softens and protein slackens as it warms, so a warm paste is a slack paste that will not hold a shape.\n\nAND THE COCONUT IS STRUCTURAL, WHICH IS THE PART NOBODY EXPECTS. The 25g of desiccated coconut is not in here for flavour, or not mainly. Fish is roughly four fifths water, and a wet paste wrapped around a stalk and put over coals turns that water to steam from the inside — steam that has nowhere to go except outward, pushing the layer off the stick in sheets. Dry coconut is extremely thirsty and it soaks that free water up and holds onto it, so it never becomes steam in the first place. ⛔ DO NOT SOAK THIS COCONUT. If you have made the Urap-Urap card you will have rehydrated coconut in warm water first, and doing that here defeats the entire purpose — you want it bone dry and hungry. Same ingredient, opposite instruction, for opposite reasons.\n\nPOUND THE BUMBU. Toast the 3g of coriander seeds dry for a minute, then pound them with the 10g of shallots, the 8g of garlic, the 5g of galangal, the 5g of macadamia nuts, the 5g of red chilli, the 3g of shrimp paste, the 5g of jaggery, the 2g of salt and the 1g of white pepper to a smooth paste. Take about 5g off the end of one lemongrass stalk, slice it as finely as you possibly can, and pound that in too — the stalks are going to be handles, so this is the only lemongrass that ends up inside the meat.\n\nMIX EVERYTHING TOGETHER AND THEN KEEP GOING FOR A MINUTE LONGER THAN FEELS NECESSARY. Fish paste, bumbu and dry coconut in a bowl, and work it hard with a spoon or your hand until it is completely uniform and noticeably stiffer and stickier than when you started. If it still smears rather than clinging to itself it is not ready, because a mixture that will not grip its own sides will not grip a stalk either. Then cover it and put it in the fridge for 30 minutes. This is not a rest for flavour; it is the coconut finishing its job of drinking the loose moisture, and the whole mixture firming up in the cold. A mixture moulded straight away will sag, so that half an hour of doing nothing saves you four skewers on the fire.\n\nNOW PREPARE THE STALKS, AND THIS IS WHERE MOST FAILURES ACTUALLY START. Trim the remaining lemongrass into 4 lengths of about 12cm, keeping the firmer lower part. Then take the back of a heavy knife and BRUISE each stalk along its whole length, and fray or split one end into a rough brush. Two separate reasons, both real. A smooth stalk is a smooth surface and paste slides straight off it while it cooks; a bruised, split, roughed-up stalk gives the mince something mechanical to key into, and that alone is the difference between four skewers and four skewers in the fire. And bruising ruptures the oil cells along the stem, so as the stalk heats it releases its aromatics into the meat from the inside outward — which is the reverse of a marinade and is why the flavour of a good sate lilit seems to come from the middle.\n\nMOULD WITH WET HANDS. Keep a bowl of cold water beside you and wet your palms before each one, or the paste will stick to you instead of to the stalk. Take a quarter of the mixture, press it into a flat patty in your palm, lay the stalk across the middle, and close your hand around it — then squeeze and roll gently until you have a sausage 8 to 10cm long wrapped evenly around the middle of the stalk, leaving a clean handle at each end. ⛔ Squeeze firmly enough to drive out air pockets. A trapped pocket of air expands hard when it heats and blows a section of the layer clean off the stick, and it always happens over the fire where you can do nothing about it. Even thickness matters more than neatness, because one fire cooks the whole skewer at one rate: a thin patch dries out while a thick one is still raw, and there is no correcting it later.\n\nBACK IN THE FRIDGE FOR 15 MINUTES ONCE MOULDED, uncovered. The surface dries very slightly and firms, and a dry surface grips the grill far better than a wet one and is much less likely to tear when you finally turn it.\n\nGRILL MEDIUM, NOT FIERCE, AND THEN LEAVE THEM ALONE. The Sate Ayam card covers building and managing the fire itself; what matters here is that fierce heat is actively wrong for this dish. High heat sets and colours the outside while the middle is still raw and still full of water, and that water then steams and shears the layer off the stalk — so a blackened sate lilit that has slumped off its stick was cooked too hot, not too long. Medium coals, or a medium-hot griddle pan. ⛔ Lay them down and DO NOT TOUCH THEM for the first 3 to 4 minutes. The first face has to set into a crust that anchors the whole layer, and a sate turned early tears in half and the rest follows. When it releases from the grill without sticking, it is ready to turn; if it grips, it is not.\n\nTHEN TURN AND GIVE IT 3 TO 4 MINUTES A FACE, 12 to 14 minutes in total, until it is firm right through, lightly charred at the high points, and smells of toasted coconut and lemongrass. Rest them 2 minutes off the heat and eat them straight off the stalk. ⚠️ The stalk is a handle and a flavouring, not food — nobody eats it.","cookTime":"Prep 30 min · Chill 45 min · Grill 14 min","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 30g · Carbs 12g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~1080mg","storage":"The best thing you can do with sate lilit is make it ahead RAW, which is how it is handled anywhere it is sold in quantity. Moulded uncooked skewers keep on a tray in the fridge, loosely covered, for 24 hours and they genuinely improve — the coconut goes on absorbing, the paste firms further, and a day-old skewer is noticeably less likely to fall off the stick than a freshly moulded one. They freeze raw very well too, up to 2 months: open-freeze them on a tray until solid, then bag them, and grill from frozen over gentler heat for about 18 minutes rather than 14. ⚠️ Do not thaw them first; a thawed skewer weeps the water the coconut spent all night absorbing and goes slack. Cooked, they keep 2 days in the fridge and are decent cold, but reheating is genuinely poor — fish paste goes rubbery and the coconut dries out, so a leftover skewer is better eaten cold or pulled off the stalk and used in something else. ⛔ Never store cooked skewers stacked or wrapped tightly while warm; the trapped steam softens the crust that was holding everything together and they slump off the stick in the fridge.","chefNotes":"The stick is a former, not a skewer — nothing goes through the meat, so everything is about grip. Work the fish to a genuinely sticky paste and keep it cold. The dry coconut is STRUCTURAL, soaking up the water that would otherwise steam the layer off the stalk, so do NOT soak it first. Bruise and fray the lemongrass: a smooth stalk sheds its meat, a roughed-up one keys into it, and bruising also lets it perfume the meat from the inside. Mould with wet hands and squeeze out every air pocket, because a trapped pocket expands and blows the layer off over the fire. Chill 30 minutes before moulding and 15 after. Then grill MEDIUM, never fierce, and do not touch them for the first 4 minutes — the first crust is the anchor.","pairsWith":"Sambal, rice, and something raw and sharp, which is the standard Balinese plate and hard to improve on. It is rich, sweet-edged and heavy with coconut, so a fierce chilli sambal on the side is doing real work rather than decorating. A crisp sharp vegetable salad is the traditional partner and cuts it exactly right; a fruit-based sour salad does the same job harder. ⛔ Skip peanut sauce with this one, which is worth saying because it is what everyone reaches for with the word sate — the coconut in the meat and the peanut in the sauce fight each other and the lemongrass disappears under both. Balinese sate lilit is not served with it. As braai food it is excellent and unusual: it goes on late, cooks in fourteen minutes, and needs nothing but a plate.","goesWith":["Plecing Kangkung","Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi"],"trivia":"You are grilling on a close relative of the mosquito coil, and it is the same chemistry doing both jobs. Culinary lemongrass is Cymbopogon citratus, and the citronella grown for insect repellent is Cymbopogon nardus and its cousin C. winterianus — the same genus, differing mainly in which volatile oils dominate. Lemongrass runs heavily to citral, the compound responsible for the sharp lemon smell; citronella runs to citronellal and geraniol, which is what ends up in the candle on the patio. But citral is itself a documented insect repellent, and that is not a coincidence or a happy accident — the plant is not making these compounds for us. Terpenes like these are chemical defences, produced and stored in oil cells in the leaf and stem specifically to deter the insects and grazers that would otherwise eat a large, soft, sugar-rich grass. Which means the reason lemongrass smells good to you is that it is designed to smell bad to something else. It also explains, quite precisely, why the recipe insists on bruising the stalks: those defensive oils sit locked inside cells, and crushing is what ruptures them and lets the compounds out. An unbruised stalk is a stalk with its chemistry still in the bottle.\n\nThe dish itself is Balinese and that matters more than it looks. Bali is the one substantially Hindu province in the largest Muslim-majority country on earth, and its cooking runs on a different set of rules as a result — pork is ordinary there and sate lilit is very commonly made with it, alongside the fish version of the coastal villages and minced duck and turtle historically. Its spice paste is base genep, meaning roughly the complete or full seasoning, a standing mixture rebuilt in quantity rather than assembled per dish. And sate lilit is not primarily restaurant food: it belongs to temple ceremonies and village feasts, where the mincing and moulding are done communally by men in large groups, hundreds of skewers at a time. It is one of those dishes whose method is shaped by the fact that many hands are making it at once — a paste anybody can mould around a stick needs no knife skill, no portioning and no equipment, which is exactly what you want when the whole village is cooking.","howThisFeels":"Closing your fist around a lemongrass stalk and feeling the paste take hold of it.","leftovers":["(easy) Make them raw a day ahead — they are better for it, and a day-old skewer clings to the stalk more reliably than a fresh one.","(easy) Cold, straight from the fridge, pulled off the stalk and eaten with sambal, which is far better than reheating them.","(medium) Leftover cooked meat crumbled off the stalks and stirred through fried rice at the last moment, where the coconut and lemongrass carry right through.","(creative) Crumbled cooked sate simmered 5 minutes in coconut milk with a spoonful of sambal until it thickens into a loose curry, then spooned over rice — a grilled dish turned into a braised one, and the best possible end for two lonely skewers."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sate Lilit Ayam — Chicken on Bamboo (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":37,"howThisFeels":"Four skewers off a braai for less than the price of one fish fillet.","trivia":"Two changes here and it is worth being clear that one of them is a real loss. Chicken instead of fish is barely a compromise at all — chicken mince is leaner and less watery than fish, which actually makes it EASIER to keep on the stick, and the coconut has less free moisture to mop up. If anything this is the version to make first while you learn the moulding. Bamboo instead of lemongrass stalks is the genuine loss, and it is not about flavour in the sauce sense: a lemongrass stalk perfumes the meat from the inside as it heats, and a bamboo skewer contributes nothing at all. That is why the lemongrass in the bumbu goes UP rather than staying the same — you are moving the aromatic from the middle to the mix, which is not equivalent but is honest. Bamboo is also smoother than a bruised stalk, so soak the skewers and then rough them lightly, and squeeze harder when you mould.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g hake","to":"150g chicken mince"},{"from":"25g lemongrass","to":"10g lemongrass"}],"addIng":[{"item":"4 bamboo skewers"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 150g of chicken mince rather than hake, and only 10g of lemongrass — but pound ALL of it into the bumbu rather than saving stalks, since the aromatic now has to work from inside the mix instead of from the stalk. Work the chicken to a paste exactly as described; it comes together faster and stiffer than fish and needs less working, so stop when it turns tacky rather than pushing on. Soak the 4 bamboo skewers in water for 30 minutes so they do not scorch through, then scratch each one lightly along its length with a knife before moulding — a bamboo skewer is smooth and gives the paste nothing to key into, and this is a poor substitute for a frayed lemongrass stalk, so anything that roughens it helps. Squeeze noticeably harder when you mould, and do not skip either chilling stage; with a smooth former they are doing more work than usual. Grill the same way, medium and untouched for the first four minutes. Chicken needs a minute or two less than fish, so start checking at 11 minutes."}]}},{"name":"Sate Lilit Ikan — Fish on Lemongrass","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":57,"howThisFeels":"Smelling scorched lemongrass before you smell the fish, which is how you know the stalks are working.","trivia":"The order in which you build this matters in a way that is easy to miss, and it comes down to salt meeting fish before coconut does. Salt pounded into the bumbu goes into the paste with everything else, and salt is what makes a minced protein sticky rather than merely wet — so the mixing stage is when the paste becomes glue. If the dry coconut goes in too early, it starts absorbing water immediately and there is less free moisture available for that process to happen in, and you end up with a mixture that is stiff but not actually adhesive: it holds its shape in the bowl and then slides off the stalk on the grill. Stiff and sticky are not the same property. This is why the method has you work the fish to a tacky paste FIRST, then add bumbu and coconut together, then keep working. You want the glue made before the sponge starts drinking."},{"name":"Sate Lilit Udang — Prawn, the Feast Version","icon":"🦐","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":83,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter, springier and gone first off the platter every time.","trivia":"Prawn is what gets made for a ceremony or a table you are trying to impress, and it behaves differently enough from fish to be worth knowing about rather than simply swapping in. Prawn flesh is springier and more elastic than white fish when it is worked, so it reaches a sticky paste faster and with less effort, and the finished skewer has a distinct bounce that fish does not — closer to a fish cake than to a mince. It is also sweeter, noticeably so, which is why the jaggery drops rather than staying the same; left at the full amount the whole thing tips over into dessert territory against the coconut. The one real trap is overcooking: prawn goes from tender to rubbery over a much narrower window than hake, so this version comes off the fire two minutes sooner and should be watched rather than timed. Chop it coarsely by hand rather than blitzing it all smooth, and leave a few larger pieces in — the texture of actual prawn against the paste is most of the pleasure.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g hake","to":"150g prawns"},{"from":"5g jaggery","to":"3g jaggery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 150g of peeled deveined prawns in place of the hake, and drop the jaggery from 5g to 3g because prawn is markedly sweeter than white fish and the balance tips otherwise. Do not blitz all of it smooth: work about two thirds to a sticky paste and chop the remaining third coarsely by hand, then fold it through, so there are recognisable pieces of prawn in the finished skewer. That contrast is the whole reason to use prawn rather than fish. Everything else is unchanged through the moulding and both chilling stages. ⛔ On the fire it is a shorter cook and a tighter window — prawn goes from tender to rubbery far faster than hake, so give it 3 minutes on the first face and 2 to 3 on each face after, coming off at 10 to 12 minutes total rather than 14. Judge it by feel: firm and springy is right, hard is already too far. Rest 2 minutes and serve immediately, because prawn stiffens further as it cools."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Bakso","target":"indonesia-bakso","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-plecing-kangkung","name":"Plecing Kangkung","nameAlt":"Iced greens under a raw tomato sambal","aliases":["Plecing Kangkung","Plecing","Plecing Sayur","Sambal Plecing","Lombok Water Spinach","Blanched Greens in Raw Sambal","Kangkung Plecing","Sambal Tomat Greens"],"course":"side","type":["side","vegetables","no-cook-sauce","spicy","quick","budget","braai","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","braai","guests","summer"],"ingredients":"150g bunched spinach · 40g tomato · 30g bean sprouts · 15g red chilli · 12g shallots · 10ml lime juice · 6g garlic · 5g jaggery · 3g shrimp paste · 3g salt","method":"Two things make this dish and neither of them is the greens. The first is ninety seconds in boiling water followed immediately by ninety seconds in ice. The second is that the sambal is never cooked, never oiled and never let down with water — the tomato is the only liquid in it. Cold sambal, cold greens, and no fat anywhere in the bowl. Almost every failed plecing is a warm one.\n\nSTART WITH THE GREEN AND SPLIT IT IN TWO. The original is kangkung, water spinach, which grows with hollow floating stalks and broad leaves, and it is not reliably sold here. The honest local route is a bunch of ordinary spinach, and it is a closer match than it looks — the bunched spinach on a market table is a stalk-and-leaf vegetable too, with a thick pale rib and a broad soft leaf, where a bag of baby leaves has no stalk at all and nothing to squeak. So buy it in a bunch, and then STRIP THE LEAF OFF THE STALK with your fingers, running down each rib. Cut the stalks into 4cm lengths and keep them in one pile, leaves whole in another. They are two different vegetables from this moment on: a stalk needs about 90 seconds and a leaf needs about 20, and a bunch dropped in whole gives you grey slippery leaves wrapped around raw squeaky ribs.\n\nGET THE ICE BOWL READY BEFORE THE POT BOILS. A large bowl, cold water, a good handful of ice. This sounds like a detail and it is the load-bearing step of the whole recipe — the shock has to happen the second the green leaves the water, and if you are still filling a bowl at that point you have already missed it.\n\nSALT THE BLANCHING WATER PROPERLY, until it tastes like a soup you would drink. The green is in there for a minute and a half and that is the only chance it gets to be seasoned from the inside; everything that happens afterwards is seasoning sitting on the outside. Underseasoned blanching water is why a plate of dressed greens so often tastes of fierce sambal and then of nothing.\n\nNOW BLANCH, AND KEEP THE LID OFF. Stalks in first, at a hard rolling boil, 90 seconds. Leaves in after, 20 seconds — which is barely long enough to say it out loud. Watch what happens in the first few seconds, because it tells you what you are doing: the colour jumps, suddenly, to a green far louder than the raw leaf was. That is air leaving. A living leaf holds gas in the spaces between its cells and you have been looking at chlorophyll through that gas; boiling drives it out and replaces it with water, and water lets the colour through. Keep going past that point and the reaction reverses on you — the cells break, they release their own acids, and those acids strip the magnesium out of the chlorophyll and turn it olive-grey. That change is on a clock and the clock is heat, which is the entire reason for what happens next. The lid stays off for the same reason: a covered pot traps those volatile acids and drops them straight back into the water, so it hurries the greying along.\n\nSTRAIGHT INTO THE ICE, AND THIS IS THE ONE STEP NOT TO IMPROVISE ON. Lift the stalks out with a spider or a sieve and drop them into the ice water, then the leaves the same way, and push them under. Two things are happening. A hot drained pile of greens carries enough heat in itself to keep cooking for several minutes on the counter, so a perfect 90-second blanch quietly becomes a three-minute one while you are pounding the sambal — the ice stops that dead. And the cold firms the pectin in the stalk while it is still intact, which is where the squeak comes from. Sixty to ninety seconds in the ice is right. Leave them in there for five minutes and they simply take up water and go slack, which is the opposite of the point.\n\nTHEN DRY THEM, PROPERLY. Lift everything out, shake it hard, and press the stalks gently between the folds of a clean cloth. There is no oil in this sambal to help it cling, so surface water is a real problem here rather than a small one — it thins the dressing exactly where the two touch. The Gado-Gado card explains that properly and it is the same principle; it just bites harder in a dish with no fat in it.\n\nTOAST THE TERASI. Wrap the 3g of shrimp paste in a small square of foil, flatten it, and give it about 2 minutes a side in a dry pan until the sharp ammonia edge drops away and it crumbles rather than smears. The Sambal Terasi card explains what is happening in full. It is non-negotiable here for the obvious reason: nothing in this sambal gets cooked, so raw paste stays raw all the way to the plate.\n\nPOUND THE SAMBAL, HARD THINGS FIRST AND THE TOMATO LAST. Into a mortar go the 15g of red chilli, the 12g of shallots, the 6g of garlic, the 3g of salt and the crumbled terasi, and work them to a rough wet paste with pieces still visible. Then the 5g of jaggery, pounded until it smears through rather than sitting in grains. THEN THE 40g OF TOMATO, AND ONLY CRUSHED — press and tear it with the pestle a dozen times and stop while there are still recognisable pieces of flesh in there. This is the whole architecture of the dish: the tomato is the liquid, the body and the acidity all at once, and it has to release its juice without being reduced to a purée. Pounded smooth it goes thin and pink and slides off wet greens; left in pieces it clings in patches and each patch is a mouthful.\n\n⛔ ADD NO OIL AND NO WATER. Both are the standard correction when a sambal looks too thick, and both are wrong here. Oil makes it a sauce that beads and runs off cold greens instead of sticking to them, and water dilutes the one ingredient carrying the flavour. If it genuinely will not come together, the answer is more tomato, not more liquid.\n\nSTIR IN THE 10ml OF LIME JUICE AT THE END, off the pestle, and do not pound after it goes in. Acid worked into a raw tomato early flattens it and dulls the colour; stirred through at the last moment it lifts the whole thing and keeps the tomato tasting freshly cut rather than faintly stewed. Now taste it on a fingertip. It should read salty, then hot, then sour, then very faintly sweet, and it should be far too strong to eat on its own — that is correct, because it is about to be spread over 180g of almost unseasoned green.\n\nASSEMBLE COLD AND SERVE SOON. Lay the drained stalks and leaves out on a flat plate rather than heaping them in a bowl, spoon the sambal over the top in a thick uneven layer, and scatter the 30g of bean sprouts over raw, straight from the packet, for the second and sharper crunch. Do not stir it all together in advance. Salt on cut greens starts drawing water out of them the moment they meet — the Rujak card sets out exactly how that clock runs — so dress it a few minutes before it goes to the table, not an hour. Eat it cold or at room temperature, and never warm it.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Blanch 2 min · Ice 2 min · Pound 8 min","kcal":"~120 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 6g · Carbs 16g · Fat 2g · Sodium ~1340mg","storage":"Dressed plecing is a same-hour plate and there is no way around it — the salt keeps pulling water out of the greens whether anyone is eating them or not, so a covered bowl put in the fridge comes back as pale greens sitting in pink liquid with the squeak gone. Store it as two halves instead, which works so well that it is worth making it this way deliberately. The pounded sambal keeps in a closed jar in the fridge for up to 5 days and is arguably better on day two, once the raw garlic has settled into the tomato and stopped shouting. The blanched, shocked and dried greens keep 2 days in a closed container with a sheet of kitchen paper under them to take up any weeping, and they stay green because the ice already stopped the reaction that would have turned them. Bean sprouts go limp within a day, so buy them for the day. Then a fresh plate takes about two minutes to build. ⛔ Nothing here freezes: freezing bursts the cell walls whose integrity is the entire texture, and a thawed blanched green is a wet grey ribbon. Do not reheat any part of it — this dish has no hot version.","chefNotes":"Strip the leaves off the stalks and blanch them separately — 90 seconds for stalk, 20 for leaf — because a bunch dropped in whole gives grey leaves around raw ribs. Have the ice bowl standing ready BEFORE the water boils; the shock has to be immediate, and it is doing two jobs at once, stopping the carryover that would otherwise keep cooking the greens on the counter and firming the pectin that makes the stalk squeak. Salt the blanching water like soup — it is the only seasoning that ever gets inside. Lid off. Then dry the greens properly, because there is no oil in this sambal to help it cling. Pound the sambal hard-things-first and crush the tomato LAST, leaving pieces: the tomato is the only liquid, and puréed it slides straight off. Add no oil and no water. Lime at the very end, off the pestle. Serve cold, and dress it minutes before it is eaten.","pairsWith":"It exists to sit next to something hot, fatty and charred, and that is where it is best — grilled chicken or fish straight off the coals, skewers, fried chicken, anything that has been near fire. On its home island it is the fixed companion to a fierce charcoal-grilled chilli chicken and the plate makes complete sense the moment you see it: one thing scorching hot and oily, one thing cold, wet and sharp. Plain steamed white rice underneath turns the two of them into a full meal. It is also an unusually good braai side, because it is finished before the fire is lit, it wants no reheating and it is the only cold green thing on a table that is otherwise all meat. Keep the drink cold and unsweetened; sugar alongside flattens the sambal completely.","goesWith":["Ayam Betutu","Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Nasi Uduk","Sate Lilit"],"trivia":"The vegetable this dish is named after is illegal to grow in much of the United States. Kangkung is Ipomoea aquatica, a morning glory and a close relative of the sweet potato, and it sits on the US federal noxious weed list — a permit offence in a string of states, with eradication programmes run against it in Florida waterways. The thing that earned it that status is the same thing that makes it a vegetable: hollow, air-filled stems that let it float in a mat across still water, and nodes along those stems that put out roots wherever they touch wet ground. Break a piece off and it does not die, it starts a new plant. So one stem lost off the back of a truck into a warm canal becomes a colony that blocks drainage and shades out everything under it, and a plant that feeds a great many people across Asia is treated as a hazard where nobody eats it. It is a neat demonstration that a weed is not a category of plant but a category of situation.\n\nThat same vigour is why it is the cheapest green on any market between Java and Vietnam. Kangkung is cut-and-come-again in the strongest sense — harvested by cutting the stems above a node, it regrows to picking size in ten to fourteen days and keeps doing it for months from a single planting, in water or wet mud, with essentially no inputs. A vegetable that reproduces from fragments, tolerates standing water and can be cropped twenty times a year is a very difficult thing to make expensive. So the food it belongs to is honest about what it is: a plate of the least valuable green available, made worth eating by a sambal, which is roughly the founding principle of Indonesian home cooking. And the hollow stem that makes it a legal problem in Florida is the same hollow stem that makes it need a longer blanch than its own leaf does, which is where this recipe started.","howThisFeels":"The squeak of a stalk against your teeth that was in boiling water ninety seconds ago and in ice water ninety seconds after that.","leftovers":["(easy) Keep the two halves apart and there are no real leftovers — the sambal holds 5 days in a jar, so a fresh plate of blanched greens is two minutes away whenever you want one.","(easy) A spoonful of the sambal over a fried egg on hot rice, which is a whole supper and takes four minutes.","(medium) Greens that have already wept, drained hard and chopped small, folded through fried rice off the heat with the pink liquid left behind in the bowl rather than added.","(creative) The sambal loosened with a little extra lime and tossed through cold noodles with cucumber — the same dressing on a completely different carrier, and it is better cold than the greens are the next day."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Plecing Sederhana — Sambal Tomat, No Terasi (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"Brighter, redder and much simpler, with the tomato doing every job in the bowl on its own.","trivia":"Taking the shrimp paste out does not leave a gap so much as change what the sambal is, and it has its own name for that reason — sambal tomat, which is what a meat-free warung puts on the counter and what a great many households make on an ordinary day. Two things change mechanically and both want handling rather than ignoring. The paste was contributing the savoury weight that made a raw dressing taste finished, and nothing plant-based replaces that directly, so salt and acid have to carry more of the load than they did. And the crumbled paste was quietly absorbing tomato juice, so without it the sambal is noticeably wetter — which is why the answer is more tomato crushed less, rather than the same tomato pounded harder. Ten minutes standing before it goes on the greens is worth taking too: raw garlic and chilli need a few minutes in the tomato juice to spread out, and a sambal tomat spooned on the second it is made tastes of garlic and then of nothing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"3g shrimp paste"},{"item":"30g bean sprouts"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"40g tomato","to":"55g tomato"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the shrimp paste out, which also means skipping the foil and the toasting, and leave the sprouts out. Use 55g of tomato rather than 40g and crush it even less than you would in the main version — a dozen presses become half a dozen, because there is nothing in the mortar now soaking up the juice it releases. Pound the chilli, shallots, garlic and salt first as described, then the jaggery, then the tomato. Expect to want more salt than the main recipe rather than less, and add it in small pinches with a taste between each: salt is now doing the job the fermented paste was doing, and an underseasoned sambal tomat reads as sweet chilli tomato and nothing more. Push the lime a little too, up to 12ml. Then let the finished sambal stand 10 minutes before it meets the greens. Everything about the split, the blanch, the ice and the drying is unchanged — the technique is the dish and none of it lives in the terasi."}]}},{"name":"Plecing Kangkung Lombok — Raw Sambal over Iced Greens","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Lifting a fistful of stalks out of ice water and hearing them rattle against the bowl like something raw.","trivia":"This plate is almost never served alone on its home island, and the pairing it belongs to explains why the dish is built cold. Plecing kangkung is the standing companion to ayam taliwang — a young chicken split flat, rubbed with a fierce red chilli paste and grilled hard over charcoal until the edges blacken — and the two arrive together as a matter of course rather than as a chef's suggestion. Look at what each one is doing and the logic is immediate. The chicken is hot, oily, smoky and aggressively spiced, and eaten on its own it flattens the palate within a few mouthfuls. The greens are cold, wet, crunchy and sharp, and they reset it between every bite. Neither is a garnish for the other and neither works as well alone, which is why the greens are never warmed to match the chicken: warming them would remove the one property the plate needed from them. It is worth carrying that reasoning to a braai, where the same problem turns up under a different flag."},{"name":"Plecing Komplit — with Fried Peanuts","icon":"🥜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":31,"howThisFeels":"Hot peanuts hitting cold greens and staying loud right to the last mouthful.","trivia":"Scattering fried peanuts over a plate that is already finished looks like decoration and it is doing something structural, because the Indonesian plate is assembled to a texture rule at least as much as to a flavour one: something soft, something wet, something raw and something crisp, in every meal. It is why fried shallots go on a soup that needs no more flavour, why crackers come with a rice plate, why a lace of fried crumbs is heaped over fried chicken, and why nobody thinks any of that is excessive. Here the crunch is doing real work rather than making up a fourth — a blanched green has given up its rigidity by definition, the sprouts fade within minutes of meeting salt, and the peanut is the only thing on the plate that will still be crisp at the end of it. The one rule is that the peanuts go on WHOLE and at the last second. Pounded into the sambal they release their oil, thicken it into a paste and turn it into a different dressing entirely, and that dressing already has a card of its own.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"30g peanuts"},{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the 30g of peanuts in the 15ml of sunflower oil in a small pan over medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring constantly, until they are a shade or two darker and smell strongly toasted. Watch them the entire time and do not walk away — peanuts go from perfect to acrid in about twenty seconds and a scorched batch cannot be rescued or hidden. Lift them out onto a plate with a slotted spoon, leaving the oil behind, and salt them lightly while they are still hot. Let them cool completely: a peanut is soft when it comes out of the oil and only turns properly crisp as it cools, so judging them in the pan will always have you overcooking them. ⛔ The oil stays in the pan and does not go anywhere near the sambal, which is oil-free by design. Scatter the cold peanuts whole over the assembled plate at the very last moment, after the sambal and the sprouts, and never pound them into the mortar."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Ayam Goreng Kalasan","target":"indonesia-ayam-goreng-kalasan","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ayam-betutu","name":"Ayam Betutu","nameAlt":"Balinese spice-paste slow-roast chicken","aliases":["Ayam Betutu","Betutu","Bebek Betutu","Ayam Betutu Gilimanuk","Ayam Betutu Gianyar","Balinese Spiced Chicken","Base Genep Chicken","Balinese Slow-Roast Chicken"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","slow-cooked","spiced","roast","celebration","make-ahead","festive"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","celebration","winter"],"ingredients":"200g chicken thighs · 30g shallots · 20ml sunflower oil · 12g garlic · 12g red chilli · 10g galangal · 10g lemongrass · 8g macadamia nuts · 8g fresh ginger · 5g jaggery · 4g coriander seeds · 4g ground turmeric · 4g tamarind paste · 4g salt · 3g shrimp paste · 2g black pepper · 2 bay leaves","method":"Weigh the paste against the meat before you start, because that ratio is the dish. Everything listed after the chicken adds up to roughly a quarter of the weight of the chicken itself, and that is not a typo or an enthusiasm — betutu carries more spice paste per gram of meat than almost anything else cooked anywhere. A normal roast gets a rub. This gets a coat, a filling and a crust, and if you halve it out of nerves you will produce a pleasant spiced chicken that is not this.\n\nTHE PASTE HAS A NAME AND THE NAME IS THE POINT. Base genep means the complete spice base — complete in the sense of nothing left out — and a Balinese kitchen builds it in quantity and keeps it. Peel and roughly chop the 30g of shallots, the 12g of garlic, the 10g of galangal and the 8g of fresh ginger. Trim the 10g of lemongrass to the pale bottom third and slice it thinly across first, because it is woody and a blade will not break it down. Toast the 4g of coriander seeds in a dry pan for about a minute until they smell warm and one or two jump. Then pound or blitz the lot with the 12g of red chilli, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 4g of ground turmeric, the 2g of black pepper, the 3g of shrimp paste and the 4g of salt into a thick paste. The macadamias stand in for candlenut and do what candlenut always does here — fat and starch for body, not flavour.\n\nGalangal and ginger BOTH, and not one standing in for the other. They look alike and they are not interchangeable: ginger is hot, sweet and immediate, galangal is resinous, peppery and faintly medicinal, and a base genep with only one of them tastes lopsided in a way that is obvious side by side. The other authentic root, kencur, is a third plant again and is not sold here — galangal at the same weight is the closest honest route, which is part of what the 10g is already doing.\n\nNOW FRY THE PASTE, AND THIS IS A DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS. Warm the 20ml of sunflower oil in a small pan over medium-low heat, add the paste with the 2 bay leaves, and cook it gently for 8 to 10 minutes, stirring, until it darkens by a shade, stops smelling of raw onion and — the cue that matters — stops looking wet, so that the oil begins to separate at the edges. Stir in the 5g of jaggery and the 4g of tamarind paste and give it another minute. The Pepes card deliberately does NOT fry its paste, because a sealed parcel exists to trap volatile aromatics and frying drives them off. This dish is the opposite case, and for two reasons. Raw shallot and garlic sitting on meat for four hours turn sulphurous and acrid rather than sweet. And turmeric, chilli and coriander carry their colour and much of their flavour in fat-soluble compounds, so a few minutes in hot oil pulls those out into the oil, which then travels into the meat — where a raw paste would simply sit on it. Let it cool until it is comfortable to handle. ⛔ Never put a hot paste onto raw chicken.\n\nHERE IS THE TECHNIQUE, AND IT IS A PLACEMENT PROBLEM RATHER THAN A COOKING ONE. Divide the cooled paste into three roughly equal parts, because it goes into three different places and each one is doing a different job.\n\nONE — UNDER THE SKIN, AND THIS IS THE PLACEMENT THAT MATTERS MOST. Take the 200g of chicken thighs, bone in and skin on, and work a finger in at the edge of the skin to separate it from the meat without tearing it, making a pocket over the whole surface. Push the first third of the paste in there and spread it flat with your fingers from the outside. Skin is a barrier and it is a very good one — paste sitting on top of skin seasons a thing most people push to the side of the plate and never reaches the meat at all. Underneath, it is in direct contact, and as the fat in the skin renders over the next four hours it runs through the paste and carries those fat-soluble aromatics down into the meat instead of off the bird and into the dish.\n\nTWO — INSIDE THE MEAT ITSELF. A whole bird is stuffed in the cavity, which is where the second third goes and why betutu is usually a whole-bird dish. With pieces you make your own inside: cut two or three deep slashes down to the bone in the thickest part of each thigh, and pack paste into the cuts with your thumb until they will not take more. This is doing what the cavity does on a whole bird — seasoning from within, on cut surfaces, where nothing has to travel through skin or a fat layer to get anywhere. A bird stuffed with a fistful of paste and cassava leaves is the traditional article; the leaves are not sold here and this version leaves them out rather than pretending.\n\nTHREE — THE OUTSIDE, AND THIS THIRD IS SACRIFICIAL. Smear the last third over the skin and over the base of the dish. It will darken over four hours and by the end it will be very dark indeed at the edges, and that is correct and intended. Its job is not to be eaten as it is — it is a crust that shields the skin from the dry heat and slowly gives up its aromatics into the steam under the cover. At the end it is scraped up and stirred into the juices, where it becomes the sauce.\n\nCOVER IT AND GO LOW. Sit the pieces in a small ovenproof dish that holds them snugly, cover it tightly with a lid or a double layer of foil, and put it into an oven at 150°C. Not 180°C, not 200°C. ⛔ THE LOW HEAT IS FORCED BY THE PASTE VOLUME, and this is the whole reason a four-hour recipe exists. There is a great deal of shallot, chilli and unrefined sugar sitting on the outside of this bird, and every one of those burns well before a bird would normally be considered roasted — at 200°C the outer layer is black and bitter inside forty minutes while the meat under the skin has taken up almost nothing. Low heat lets the paste slowly dehydrate and press its flavour inward over hours instead of scorching in the first one. This is not a slow-cooked dish because chicken needs four hours. It is slow because the paste does.\n\nADD NO LIQUID AT ANY POINT. Covered, the chicken releases its own juice and the dish becomes a steam box; within an hour there will be a centimetre of intensely spiced liquid in the bottom that was not there when it went in. Water added at the start dilutes exactly the thing you are trying to concentrate.\n\nTHREE AND A HALF HOURS COVERED, THEN THE LID COMES OFF. Do not open it before that; every look drops the oven and lets out the steam that is doing the work. Then take the cover off and give it a final 30 to 45 minutes at the same 150°C, which dries the outer crust, tightens the skin and reduces the liquid in the base to a thick dark sauce. If you want more colour, three or four minutes under a hot grill at the very end will do it, and standing over it is not optional at that point because the sugar in the paste goes from dark to burnt very quickly.\n\nIT IS DONE WHEN THE MEAT PULLS AWAY FROM THE BONE with almost no resistance and a skewer meets nothing at all. Four hours takes chicken thigh well past merely cooked and into the soft, giving texture the dish is known for, which is exactly why thigh and never breast — breast has no collagen to convert and nothing to gain from hours, and it would be dry cotton by the end of hour one.\n\nREST IT TEN MINUTES, THEN SCRAPE THE DISH OUT PROPERLY. Everything stuck to the base and sides is concentrated base genep and rendered chicken fat, and it is the best thing in the oven dish. Stir it through the juices, taste it for salt, and spoon it over. Serve it hot, and give people rice, because on its own it is relentless.","cookTime":"Prep 30 min · Fry paste 10 min · Covered 3 hr 30 min · Uncovered 45 min · Rest 10 min","kcal":"~640 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 41g · Carbs 14g · Fat 46g · Sodium ~1560mg","storage":"This is one of the better make-ahead dishes in the section and the reason is the paste — a quarter of the weight of the bird in salt, chilli, tamarind and aromatics is a fairly hostile environment for anything that spoils food, and it also keeps moving into the meat while it sits. Cool it uncovered to room temperature, then refrigerate in a closed container, sauce and all, for up to 4 days, and it is unarguably better on day two. Reheat it covered at 150°C for 20 to 25 minutes with a spoonful of water stirred into the sauce if it has set solid, which it will — never uncovered and hot, which dries the meat and catches the sugar. It freezes very well for 3 months, meat and sauce together, and thaws overnight in the fridge with no loss at all, which makes a double batch the sensible way to spend four hours of oven. ⚠️ The one thing that does not survive is the crisp outer crust; a reheated betutu is a softer, more braised thing than a fresh one, so if you are cooking ahead deliberately, do the covered hours in advance and give it the uncovered 45 minutes on the day. Rice always keeps separately.","chefNotes":"Weigh the paste against the meat — it is roughly a quarter of the bird's weight, and halving it produces a different and lesser dish. FRY the base genep first, unlike the Pepes parcel, because raw shallot over four hours goes acrid and because turmeric and chilli give up their colour and flavour into fat, which then travels into the meat. Then split it in three and place it in three places for three jobs: under the skin (direct contact, and the rendering fat carries it inward), into deep slashes cut to the bone (the pieces version of stuffing a cavity), and over the outside as a sacrificial crust that shields the skin and becomes the sauce. Cover, 150°C, and no liquid ever — the bird makes its own. Three and a half hours covered, then 45 minutes uncovered to dry the crust. The low heat is forced by the paste volume, not by the chicken.","pairsWith":"Plain steamed white rice, and it is not optional here in the way it sometimes is — this is a dish of concentrated spice paste and it needs something blank underneath it to be edible in quantity rather than merely impressive in a mouthful. Beyond that it wants cold, wet and sharp: blanched greens under a raw sambal is the standard Balinese answer and it is the right one, and cucumber, a wedge of lime or anything raw and acidic does the same job on a smaller scale. Keep other spiced dishes off the table — betutu is loud and it flattens anything competing with it. A cold unsweetened drink, and something plain and starchy for anyone who finds the chilli level fierce, because a properly made one is.","goesWith":["Plecing Kangkung","Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Acar Kuning"],"trivia":"The original oven for this dish was a waste product, and it explains the four hours better than any flavour argument does. Betutu is traditionally buried in smouldering rice husk — sekam, the hard outer casing knocked off the grain at the mill. Husk is about a fifth of the weight of harvested rice, it is useless as animal feed because it is largely indigestible and heavily silicated, and it is abrasive enough to wear out machinery, so every rice mill on earth has the problem of what to do with a mountain of the stuff. What it does have is a remarkable burning habit: it does not flame so much as smoulder, at a low steady temperature, for many hours, unattended, without needing tending or topping up. Bury a wrapped bird in a heap of it, light the top, and you have an oven that runs itself overnight at a temperature nothing can burn in. Which means the timing of this dish was never chosen. Nobody decided that six to twelve hours suited chicken; that is simply what a husk fire does, and the recipe was built around the fuel. A domestic oven at 150°C is an imitation of a rice husk heap, not the other way around.\n\nThe modern coda is stranger. Rice husk ash — the pale grey powder left when the smouldering is finished — is between about 85 and 95 percent amorphous silica, and that particular form of silica is highly reactive with the lime released as cement cures. So the ash from the fire has become an industrial commodity: it is used as a pozzolan blended into concrete, where it increases strength and cuts the amount of cement needed, and as a raw source of silica in its own right. A husk that was worthless enough to be burned for the sake of getting rid of it now has a market on both sides of the fire. And the timing that fuel imposed is also why betutu became ceremonial food rather than weeknight food: a dish you bury at night and dig out in the morning is a dish for the day something is happening, which is why it belongs to temple anniversaries and feasts, and why the two famous versions differ by occasion as much as by recipe — the Gianyar style a whole bird cooked long and mellow, the Gilimanuk style served in pieces and genuinely, unapologetically fierce with chilli.","howThisFeels":"Working a finger under the skin and pushing a fistful of orange paste into a pocket that turns out to be far bigger than you expected.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated covered at 150°C with a spoonful of water stirred into the set sauce, which is honestly better than it was on the first day because the paste has kept moving into the meat overnight.","(easy) Pulled off the bone cold and eaten in a roll with cucumber and a spoonful of the sauce, where the spice reads much louder cold than it did hot.","(medium) Shredded and fried hard in a dry pan until the edges catch and crisp, then folded through rice at the last second with the last of the pan sauce.","(creative) The bones and the scrapings simmered 40 minutes in water into a fierce orange broth, then the shredded meat and a handful of greens dropped in — four hours of paste turns into a second whole meal, and it is the best possible use of a carcass."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Betutu Sederhana — Half the Paste, One Placement (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":43,"howThisFeels":"Less paste in the bowl and somehow more of it in the meat, because none of it is being burnt on the outside.","trivia":"This fork takes out the two most expensive lines in the paste and then does something more interesting than economising: it changes where the paste goes. With a smaller quantity you cannot afford a sacrificial outer coat, so all of it goes under the skin and into the slashes — and the honest observation is that the meat itself comes out tasting of very nearly as much, because the outer third was never being eaten as paste in the first place. What you actually lose is the crust and most of the sauce, since the dark scrapings in the base of the dish were largely that outer layer breaking down. So this version is drier and leaner and it wants its rice wetter. It also needs the cover kept on for longer, right to the end, because with no crust protecting it the exposed skin dries and toughens in an uncovered oven rather than crisping.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g macadamia nuts"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"30g shallots","to":"20g shallots"},{"from":"10g galangal","to":"5g galangal"},{"from":"10g lemongrass","to":"5g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the macadamias out and cut the shallots to 20g, the galangal to 5g and the lemongrass to 5g. Fry the paste exactly as described — that step is the flavour and it is free. Then split what you have in TWO rather than three: half under the skin, half packed into deep slashes cut to the bone, and nothing at all on the outside. Without the nut the paste is looser and wetter, so let it fry a couple of minutes longer until it genuinely stops looking wet, or it will run out from under the skin in the first hour. Keep the dish COVERED for the whole four hours instead of uncovering at three and a half: there is no sacrificial crust here to protect the skin, and dry oven heat on bare skin for 45 minutes toughens it rather than crisping it. Expect noticeably less sauce in the base at the end, so serve it with something wetter alongside, and taste for salt before serving because a smaller paste carries less of it."}]}},{"name":"Ayam Betutu Gianyar — Paste in Three Places, Four Hours","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":55,"howThisFeels":"Taking the lid off at three and a half hours and finding a centimetre of dark orange liquid in a dish you never added anything to.","trivia":"The bay leaves are the one line in this paste doing a structural job rather than an aromatic one, and it is worth knowing why they go in at the frying stage rather than into the oven dish. In Bali the leaf is daun salam, and here as elsewhere in this section European bay stands in for it at a reduced quantity because it is sharper and more eucalyptus-forward. But the reason it belongs in the pan with the paste is fat: the aromatic compounds in a bay leaf are almost entirely oil-soluble and barely water-soluble at all, so a leaf simmered in a watery braise gives up a fraction of what a leaf warmed in oil does. Ten minutes in the frying paste extracts far more than four hours in the covered dish would, and by the time it goes into the oven the leaf itself has already done its work and is essentially spent. It is the same reasoning as blooming turmeric in oil, applied to something most cooks treat as a thing you simply drop in and forget."},{"name":"Bebek Betutu — Duck","icon":"🦆","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":77,"howThisFeels":"Fat rising through the paste for the first hour, until the whole dish is orange oil and the smell reaches the other end of the house.","trivia":"Duck is the older and more ceremonial of the two, and the swap changes the arithmetic of the whole method rather than just the protein. A duck carries several times the subcutaneous fat of a chicken, and over a long low cook that fat renders out and effectively confits the meat in the dish — which is why bebek betutu can be taken to five or six hours where a chicken would begin to fall apart, and why it comes out silkier rather than drier. The under-the-skin placement earns its keep even more here, because there is far more fat to travel through the paste on its way down. The trade is that the dish ends up swimming, so the fat has to be poured off before the uncovered stage or the crust never forms and the sauce is greasy instead of concentrated. Save that poured-off fat. Spiced duck fat is one of the best things a kitchen ever produces by accident, and roast potatoes cooked in it are a small revelation.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g chicken thighs","to":"200g duck"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 200g of duck — leg and thigh on the bone, skin on, never breast, which has neither the fat nor the connective tissue this method is built around. Prick the skin all over with the point of a knife BEFORE the paste goes anywhere near it, going through the skin and fat but not into the meat: those holes are the escape routes for the fat, and unpricked duck skin holds it in and steams itself soft from underneath. Place the paste in the same three places. Then give it 4 hours 30 minutes covered rather than 3 hours 30 minutes, because there is more fat and more connective tissue to work through. ⛔ Before the uncovered stage, tilt the dish and spoon or pour off the rendered fat — there will be a surprising amount and if it stays in, the crust cannot dry and the sauce will be an oil slick rather than a reduction. Keep that fat in a jar in the fridge; it is spiced duck fat and it will improve any potato it meets. Then 45 minutes uncovered as described. Duck also takes salt more slowly than chicken, so taste the sauce at the end and expect to add a pinch."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Plecing Kangkung","target":"indonesia-plecing-kangkung","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-es-cendol","name":"Es Cendol","nameAlt":"Iced coconut with palm sugar droplets","aliases":["Es Cendol","Cendol","Dawet","Es Dawet","Dawet Ayu","Chendol","Cendol Putih","Indonesian Coconut Ice Dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","cold","drink","vegan","street-food","summer","budget"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","guests","party","summer"],"ingredients":"250ml water · 100ml coconut milk · 40g jaggery · 40g rice flour · 15g tapioca starch · 2g salt","method":"The whole dish is one trick performed once, and it happens in the air. A thick cooked starch paste is pushed through holes, and in the twenty centimetres between the colander and the water it stops being a thread and becomes a droplet with a little tail. Get that right and everything else is assembly. Get it wrong and you have a pot of grey mush, so read the next three paragraphs before you turn the heat on.\n\nCOOK THE STARCH FIRST — THE BATTER GOES INTO THE COLANDER ALREADY COOKED. This is the part people miss and it is the whole difference. Whisk the 40g of rice flour and the 15g of tapioca starch with 200ml of the water and 1g of the salt in a saucepan until there is not a lump in it, then put it over medium heat and STIR CONSTANTLY with a wooden spoon or a heatproof spatula, getting into the corners. For the first two or three minutes nothing happens. Then it thickens all at once — it goes from milky liquid to a thick, glossy, translucent paste in well under a minute, and if you are not stirring at that moment it will catch on the base and you will be picking grey lumps out of it for the rest of the recipe. Keep cooking and stirring for another 2 minutes after it thickens, because half-gelatinised starch tastes chalky and, worse, has not developed enough body to hold a shape.\n\nTHE CONSISTENCY TEST IS THE WHOLE RECIPE. Lift the spoon: the paste should fall back into the pan in a thick, slow, UNBROKEN RIBBON that sits on the surface for a second before sinking. Too thin and it will simply disperse into the water in threads and dust. Too thick and it will not go through the holes at all, or it will come through in worms that stay worms. If it is too thick, beat in another tablespoon of hot water off the heat; if it is too thin, put it back on the heat and keep stirring. Fix it now, because once it starts cooling you have lost the window.\n\nTWO STARCHES, DOING TWO JOBS. Rice flour is the body and gives the short, slightly grainy bite that cendol is supposed to have. Tapioca starch is the spring — it gelatinises into a clear, elastic, faintly slippery gel that makes the droplets bounce against the teeth instead of dissolving into the coconut milk. Rice flour alone gives you soft droplets that go pasty in the glass within ten minutes. Tapioca alone gives you clear rubber. The proportion is the recipe.\n\nSET UP THE ICE WATER BEFORE YOU START COOKING, not after. A wide bowl, cold water, and a real handful of ice, sitting under the colander and ready. The paste is only workable while it is properly hot, so there is no time to go and fill a bowl.\n\nNOW PUSH IT THROUGH, AND HOLD THE COLANDER HIGH. Tip the hot paste into a colander with holes around 4 to 5mm — a metal one, since a hot paste will soften a cheap plastic one — held 20 to 25cm above the ice water, and press it through with the back of a spoon or a rubber spatula. ⛔ THE HEIGHT IS NOT SHOWMANSHIP. A column of falling liquid cannot stay a column: it is unstable, it necks and pinches, and it breaks into separate rounded drops as it goes. That is precisely what makes the tapered droplet shape with the little tail, and it is why cendol pushed through a colander resting on the rim of the bowl comes out as stubby worms instead. Give the paste the fall and the fall does the shaping for you.\n\nTHE ICE IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE SAME MECHANISM. Hot gelatinised starch is soft and sticky, and droplets landing in room-temperature water stay soft long enough to touch each other and fuse into one grey mass at the bottom of the bowl. Cold water sets the outside of each droplet on contact — the starch firms and stops being tacky within a second or two — so they bounce off each other and stay separate. This is the same reason there is no rescuing a batch dropped into tepid water: by the time you notice, they have already joined.\n\nLet them sit in the ice water for 5 minutes to firm all the way through, then lift them out with a slotted spoon and keep them in a bowl of plain cold water until you build the glasses. Do not drain them dry and leave them sitting; exposed to air the surface dries and they go leathery.\n\nA WORD ON THE COLOUR, HONESTLY. Cendol is usually green, and the green is pandan — the Klepon card sets out what pandan actually is and why it smells the way it does. It is a garden plant rather than a shop one in most of the world and it is not reliably sold here, so this recipe is written for cendol putih, the plain white version, which is a real and common thing and not a compromise invented for this book. If you do find frozen leaves, blitz two or three with the batter water and strain them out before cooking, which gives you the colour AND the smell together. What is not worth doing is reaching for a bottle of green colouring on its own: you get the appearance of the thing and none of the reason anyone wanted it, and a plain white glass is a more honest object than a green one that smells of nothing.\n\nMAKE THE SYRUP THICK AND THE COCONUT MILK SALTY, AND KEEP THEM APART. Melt the 40g of jaggery with the remaining 50ml of water in a small pan over low heat, stirring, until it has dissolved completely, then simmer it 2 to 3 minutes until it is a dark, glossy, pourable syrup a little thicker than it looked when it melted. Let it cool. Separately, stir the remaining 1g of salt into the 100ml of coconut milk — cold, uncooked and UNSWEETENED. This is the structural decision of the finished glass. ⛔ Do not sweeten the coconut milk and do not stir the syrup into it. All the sugar lives in the syrup, all the salt lives in the coconut milk, and keeping them separate is what makes each spoonful different from the last as they slowly meet in the glass. Pre-mixed, you get a single flat sweet drink and the dish loses its entire point.\n\nBUILD IT IN LAYERS AND IN THIS ORDER. Cendol droplets in the bottom of a tall glass. Syrup poured over them — over the cendol specifically, not down the side, so the droplets sit in it. Then crushed or shaved ice, a good handful. Then the salted coconut milk poured over the ice last, so it runs down through it in white ribbons. Serve it immediately with a long spoon and do not stir it before it reaches the table. It is meant to be a mess made by the person eating it.","cookTime":"Cook batter 6 min · Press and set 8 min · Syrup 5 min · Build 2 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 4g · Carbs 82g · Fat 20g · Sodium ~800mg","storage":"Store the three parts separately and this becomes an easy thing to have ready for a hot afternoon; store it built and you have a glass of sweet grey soup within the hour, because the ice melts into it and the droplets soften in the liquid. The cendol droplets keep 3 days in the fridge SUBMERGED IN PLAIN COLD WATER in a closed container, and the water is not optional — droplets stored drained go leathery on the outside and hard in the middle overnight. Change the water on day two. ⛔ Do not freeze them: freezing turns the water inside each droplet to ice crystals that tear the starch gel, and they thaw grainy and collapsed. The palm sugar syrup keeps 2 weeks in a jar in the fridge and thickens as it chills, so let it stand at room temperature for ten minutes or stir in a teaspoon of hot water before pouring. Coconut milk keeps 3 days salted in a covered jug and separates in the cold, which is normal — shake or whisk it back together. Built glasses do not keep at all and there is no reviving one, so build only what is about to be drunk.","chefNotes":"The batter goes into the colander ALREADY COOKED — stir it constantly over medium heat until it thickens suddenly into a glossy translucent paste, then two minutes more. The test is a thick unbroken ribbon off the spoon; fix the consistency then, because it cannot be fixed later. Rice flour for body, tapioca starch for the spring, and the proportion between them is the texture. Hold the colander 20 to 25cm above the bowl: a falling column of liquid breaks into drops on its own, which is what makes the tail, and a colander resting on the rim gives you worms. Ice water underneath, ready before you start, because cold sets the surface on contact and stops the droplets fusing. Then keep the syrup sweet and the coconut milk salty and NEVER mix them — that separation is the dish.","pairsWith":"It is a hot-afternoon thing rather than an end-of-dinner thing, and it is at its best on its own in the middle of the day with nothing else on the table. If it is following food, put it after something fiercely chilli-hot and salty, where cold sweet coconut is doing an obvious job. It sits very well beside small dry market sweets rather than other puddings — steamed rice-flour balls or a slice of dense coconut cake work because they are chewy and the cendol is wet, and two wet sweet things together is too much. Do not serve it with coffee or tea; hot drinks alongside a glass built on ice make no sense and the tannin fights the coconut. Serve it with a long spoon and let people wreck the layers themselves.","goesWith":["Klepon","Serabi Solo","Bika Ambon"],"trivia":"This dessert is several centuries older than the first word in its own name. Dawet — the older Javanese name for the same thing, still what it is called across much of Central Java — is usually traced back to Old Javanese literature of the Kediri period, somewhere around the twelfth century, which puts pounded starch droplets in coconut milk and palm sugar syrup on the table roughly eight hundred years before anyone in the archipelago could reliably make a cold drink cold. For almost all of that history it was eaten at ambient temperature, which in a tropical afternoon is not cold at all. The es on the front is a modern addition, and it means exactly what it says: ice. So the name records a technology upgrade rather than a recipe, and it dates the version everyone now thinks of as the real one.\n\nWhat is striking is how much of the Indonesian dessert repertoire turns out to be dated the same way. Es teler, es campur, es doger, es buah — an entire and much-loved family of national sweets whose names all begin with the equipment. Ice reached the port cities of the region first as an imported cargo, cut from frozen northern lakes in winter, packed in sawdust and carried across the world under sail on a calculated bet about how much of it would survive the voyage, then later as a local product once mechanical ice factories arrived in the late nineteenth century and made it something a city could simply have. Within a couple of generations of that, a shelf of desserts existed which had not existed before, and a much older one had quietly acquired a new first name. It is one of the clearest cases anywhere of a machine writing itself into a cuisine — and it is worth remembering while you are holding a colander over a bowl of ice cubes that the difficult, expensive, world-spanning part of this recipe is the part you got out of a freezer drawer without thinking about it.","howThisFeels":"Watching the first spoonful of paste come through the holes and break into separate tails on the way down, which is the moment you find out whether the consistency was right.","leftovers":["(easy) Droplets kept submerged in cold water are ready for another glass all week — the whole dish is three parts in three containers, so a second serving is a two-minute build rather than a leftover.","(easy) Leftover palm sugar syrup poured over any plain fruit, or over hot rice porridge in the morning, where it behaves like a much better golden syrup.","(medium) Droplets and salted coconut milk warmed gently in a pan with a little of the syrup and a pinch more salt, which turns a cold drink into a warm coconut pudding and is worth doing on a cold evening.","(creative) Droplets, syrup and coconut milk frozen in a shallow tray and forked through every half hour into a coarse granita — the salt in the coconut milk keeps it from setting into a solid brick, so it stays scoopable straight from the freezer."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Cendol Putih Sederhana — Rice Flour Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Softer droplets that give way almost immediately, and a thinner white glass that goes down in about a minute.","trivia":"Dropping the tapioca is the honest cheap route and it costs exactly one property, which is worth naming rather than glossing: the bounce. Rice flour on its own gelatinises into a soft, short, slightly grainy gel with no elasticity in it, so the droplets are tender rather than springy and they begin to soften into the coconut milk after about ten minutes in the glass. That is not a failure — plenty of home cendol is made this way and eaten quickly, which is the point. What it does mean is that the batter has to be cooked a little further and a little thicker to compensate, because with no elastic starch holding the droplet together the only thing giving it structure is how well the rice starch itself has set. Less coconut milk in the glass follows the same logic: a softer droplet in a smaller pool holds its own better than a softer droplet swimming.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"15g tapioca starch"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml coconut milk","to":"80ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Leave the tapioca out and use 40g of rice flour on its own with the same 200ml of batter water. Cook it a full minute LONGER than the main version after it thickens — three minutes of stirring rather than two — and take it a touch thicker, because with no elastic starch in there the only thing holding the droplet's shape is how completely the rice starch has gelatinised. The ribbon test still applies; you simply want a slightly heavier ribbon. Everything else is unchanged, including the height, the ice water and the five minutes to firm. Use 80ml of coconut milk rather than 100ml — a softer droplet holds up better in a smaller pool of liquid than it does swimming in a large one. And build these glasses at the last possible moment and eat them straight away, because this version starts softening into the milk after about ten minutes where the full one holds twenty."}]}},{"name":"Es Cendol — Pressed from a Height, Set in Ice","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"The layers refusing to mix in the glass until a spoon goes through them and everything changes colour at once.","trivia":"The salt in the coconut milk is not seasoning and it is not a rounding-off touch — it is one half of a deliberate opposition, and taking it out collapses the dish in a way that is easy to demonstrate. Try a spoonful of unsalted coconut milk against a spoonful of the syrup and they read as two versions of sweet. Salt the milk properly and they become two different things that happen to be in one glass: a sweet, dark, faintly smoky syrup at the bottom, and a savoury, rich, almost soupy coconut on top, meeting in a gradient that changes every time the spoon goes in. That is also why the milk is never sweetened and the two are never stirred together in the kitchen. Half of Southeast Asia's coconut desserts are built on exactly this contrast, and the ones that get described as bland almost always turn out to be the ones where somebody sweetened the coconut milk out of an instinct that a pudding should be sweet all the way through."},{"name":"Dawet Ayu — the Drinking Version, No Ice Cubes","icon":"🥤","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":17,"howThisFeels":"A cold glass that is a drink first and a dessert second, and gone long before you expected it to be.","trivia":"The Central Javanese version is drunk rather than spooned, and the change is not simply a matter of putting it in a different glass — the arithmetic of the whole thing shifts. An iced dessert is deliberately built too strong, because a handful of ice is roughly a hundred millilitres of water that has not arrived yet, and it dilutes the glass steadily over the ten minutes it takes to eat. Take the ice out and there is nothing coming to dilute anything, so a base mixed at eating strength tastes cloying and thick from the first mouthful to the last. So the coconut milk goes up, the syrup goes in thinner, and everything is chilled hard beforehand instead of being cooled in the glass. It is the same correction a bartender makes between a drink built over ice and one shaken and strained, arrived at independently in a cart on a Javanese street, and it is a genuinely useful thing to understand about any cold drink you build.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml coconut milk","to":"160ml coconut milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No ice goes into the glass at all, so everything has to be cold before it is assembled — chill the coconut milk, the syrup and the bowl of cendol droplets in the fridge for at least two hours, and make the whole thing well ahead rather than on demand. Use 160ml of coconut milk instead of 100ml, salted the same way. Make the syrup thinner as well: melt the 40g of jaggery in 80ml of water rather than 50ml and do not simmer it down, so it pours easily and disperses through the milk instead of sitting under it in a layer. Then build it in a tall glass with the droplets first, the thin syrup over them and the cold coconut milk poured on top, and give it ONE gentle stir before it goes to the table — this version is meant to be roughly mixed rather than layered, because a drink cannot be excavated with a spoon. Drink it with a wide straw, or the droplets stay in the bottom of the glass and you have made a coconut drink with a surprise at the end."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Klepon","target":"indonesia-klepon","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Serabi Solo","target":"indonesia-serabi-solo","emoji":"🥥"},{"name":"Bika Ambon","target":"indonesia-bika-ambon","emoji":"🍰"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-acar-kuning","name":"Acar Kuning","nameAlt":"Turmeric quick pickle","aliases":["Acar Kuning","Acar","Acar Timun","Achar","Yellow Pickle","Indonesian Quick Pickle","Acar Ikan","Atjar Kuning"],"course":"side","type":["side","pickle","vegetables","no-cook","quick","budget","make-ahead","braai"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","braai","guests","make-ahead"],"ingredients":"80g cucumber · 60ml white vinegar · 60g carrots · 30ml water · 20g shallots · 15g jaggery · 15ml sunflower oil · 10g red chilli · 8g salt · 6g garlic · 6g fresh ginger · 4g ground turmeric","method":"Two decisions make this and they are both about water. The first is that you salt the vegetables, wring the water out of them and THROW IT AWAY. The second is that the brine goes over them boiling hot while the vegetables themselves are cold and completely raw, so that the heat only ever reaches the outside millimetre. Everything else is stirring sugar into vinegar.\n\nBEFORE ANY OF IT, THE HONEST LABEL. ⛔ THIS IS NOT A FERMENT. It looks like one, it sits in a jar like one, and it is not. A true pickle makes its own acid: salt holds the spoilage organisms back while lactic acid bacteria that were already on the vegetable work through the sugars over days at room temperature and drop the pH until nothing else can live there. An acar has the acid poured into it ready-made out of a bottle, and nothing is being cultured at all. That distinction is not academic, because it decides how you treat the jar. There is no culture establishing itself here, so time at room temperature buys you nothing and costs you everything — it is simply warm hours for whatever landed in the jar. Cool it and refrigerate it within the hour, keep it cold, and eat it inside two weeks. Do not leave it on a counter to develop, do not seal it and put it in a cupboard, and do not treat a recipe that has been near a stove for four minutes as though it had been processed for a shelf. Cold, and a fortnight.\n\nNOW CUT EVERYTHING THICK, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT LOOKS RIGHT. Cut the 80g of cucumber into batons about 1cm square, and if the seed core is large and watery, run a spoon down it and discard that — it holds the most water and the least crunch. Cut the 60g of carrots into batons the same size, and the 20g of shallots into thick wedges rather than rings. Slice the 10g of red chilli into fat diagonal pieces. Thin slices have far more cut surface per gram, which means they lose more water, take up more acid and go limp in about a day; thick batons stay crunchy for a fortnight. This is a keeping dish and it should be cut like one.\n\nSALT THEM AND THEN GET RID OF THE WATER. Toss the cucumber and carrot with 5g of the 8g of salt in a colander and leave them 30 minutes with a plate underneath. Liquid will pour out of them — a great deal of it, mostly from the cucumber, which is very nearly all water to begin with. Then rinse the vegetables briefly under cold water to take the surface salt off, and squeeze them dry, handful by handful, in a clean cloth. Squeeze harder than feels polite.\n\nTHE REASON IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ONE THE RUJAK CARD GIVES, and the pair is worth holding side by side. Rujak salts cut produce so that the liquid it draws out becomes the dressing, and the dish is finished within ten minutes of that happening. Here, exactly the same osmosis is running, and the water it produces is the enemy — because whatever comes out of the vegetable in the jar goes straight into the brine and dilutes it. An unsalted acar is fine on day one and by day three is sitting in a cloudy, weak, pale liquid that is no longer sour enough to be doing its job, on flavour or on safety. So you make the vegetables give the water up on your terms, on a plate, before they ever meet the vinegar. The same mechanism, used for the opposite purpose, and it is the single thing that separates a pickle that lasts a fortnight from one that lasts two days.\n\nMAKE THE YELLOW BRINE, AND START IT IN OIL. Pound or finely chop the 6g of garlic and the 6g of fresh ginger. Warm the 15ml of sunflower oil in a small pan over medium-low heat, add the garlic, ginger and the 4g of ground turmeric, and fry for 2 minutes until it smells cooked rather than raw and the oil has gone deep orange — turmeric gives its colour and much of its flavour up to fat far more readily than to water, which the Ayam Betutu card sets out in full. Then add the 60ml of white vinegar, the 30ml of water, the 15g of jaggery and the remaining 3g of salt, and bring it to a proper boil, stirring until the jaggery has completely dissolved. Let it boil hard for 1 minute. ⚠️ Boiling vinegar is unpleasant to stand over, so open a window and do not put your face in the steam.\n\nPUT THE VEGETABLES IN A JAR AND POUR THE BRINE OVER THEM BOILING. Pack the squeezed cucumber and carrot, the shallot wedges and the chilli into a clean jar or a bowl — cold, straight from the colander, entirely raw. Then pour the brine over while it is still at a rolling boil and press everything under with a spoon.\n\nHERE IS WHY THAT ORDER MATTERS, AND IT IS THE WHOLE TECHNIQUE. The heat is doing three jobs and all three depend on it being brief. It kills what is living on the surface of the vegetables and inside the jar, which is most of what an acidified pickle relies on for its fortnight. It drives brine into the outer layer fast, so the pickle tastes seasoned all the way through in hours rather than days. And it very lightly cooks the outside millimetre, which sets the pectin at the surface and firms it. What it does NOT do is cook the vegetable, because a jar of cold raw batons has enough thermal mass to pull that brine down below cooking temperature within a minute or so. The centre of every piece stays raw and hard. ⛔ The failure is simmering the vegetables IN the brine, which is what a great many recipes tell you to do — three minutes in acid at 100°C and carrots go soft and cucumber turns to translucent grey rag, and no amount of chilling brings either back.\n\nCOOL IT FAST, UNCOVERED, AND THEN REFRIGERATE. Leave the jar open on the counter until it is no longer warm to the back of your hand — half an hour at most — then lid it and put it in the fridge. Cooling it fast is part of keeping the crunch, since the vegetables carry on softening slightly for as long as the brine is hot, and a lidded jar of hot brine stays hot for a very long time. A warm kitchen is not a place to leave this standing.\n\nIT IS READY IN FOUR HOURS AND BETTER THE NEXT DAY. In four hours it is sharp, crunchy and edible. By the following day the turmeric has coloured everything evenly, the shallots have gone slightly translucent and lost their raw bite, and the acid has settled from harsh into bright. It holds that condition for about a week and then begins to soften, still perfectly good, until around two weeks when it is soft, cloudy and finished. Eat it cold, straight from the jar, in small quantities beside something rich — this is a seasoning that happens to be a vegetable, not a salad.","cookTime":"Cut 10 min · Salt-draw 30 min · Brine 6 min · Chill 4 hr","kcal":"~150 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g · Carbs 22g · Fat 7g · Sodium ~1450mg","storage":"⛔ FRIDGE ONLY, and this is the one instruction in the recipe with a real consequence behind it. An acar is acidified rather than fermented — the acid arrives from a bottle instead of being made in the jar by bacteria — so there is no established culture in there holding anything back, and no processing has been done to make it shelf-stable. It keeps 2 weeks in a clean sealed jar in the fridge and it is at its best in the first week; after that it stays safe and gets steadily softer, and by a fortnight the crunch has gone entirely. Always take it out with a clean dry spoon, because a spoon that has been in your food introduces exactly what the brine is there to exclude. Keep everything submerged; anything sitting proud of the liquid is the piece that will go first, so press it back down each time. ⚠️ Do not water-bath it, do not store it in a cupboard, and do not top an old jar up with fresh vegetables — start a new one, which takes ten minutes. It does not freeze under any circumstances: freezing bursts the cell walls that the whole dish is built on and it thaws as limp coloured rags in yellow water. If it smells yeasty, foams when opened, or the liquid has gone ropy or thick, it goes out.","chefNotes":"Cut everything THICK — 1cm batons, thick shallot wedges — because a keeping pickle cut thin goes limp in a day. Salt the cucumber and carrot for 30 minutes, then rinse and WRING THEM OUT: the water they give up would otherwise dilute the brine in the jar, which is what turns a fortnight's pickle into a cloudy two-day one. Fry the turmeric, garlic and ginger in oil before the vinegar goes in. Then pour the brine over BOILING, onto cold raw vegetables in the jar — never simmer the vegetables in it. The heat sterilises the surface, drives the brine into the outer layer and firms it, and a jar of cold batons drops it below cooking temperature within a minute, so the centres stay raw and hard. Cool it uncovered and get it into the fridge inside the hour. This is not a ferment: no culture, no counter time, two weeks cold.","pairsWith":"It exists to be the sharp thing on a plate of rich things, and it is close to compulsory beside anything grilled, fried or built on coconut milk — skewers, fried chicken, a slow-cooked spiced bird, a bowl of curry. A tablespoon is a serving; this is a seasoning wearing the shape of a side dish, and a plateful of it is unpleasant. It belongs on a braai table for the same reason a bowl of pickles belongs beside cold meat, and it is one of very few things there that can be made three days ahead and improves for it. Plain steamed white rice is its natural neighbour rather than its partner — the rice is blank, the acar is loud, and everything else on the plate sits between them.","goesWith":["Sate Ayam","Ayam Betutu","Nasi Uduk","Ayam Goreng Kalasan"],"trivia":"The elaborate Indonesian feast that much of the world pictures — twenty small dishes arriving at once, rice in the middle, a little of everything on one plate — is a Dutch invention, and it barely exists in Indonesia. Rijsttafel, literally rice table, was assembled in colonial households and on plantation estates in the nineteenth century: an existing Indonesian habit of eating several dishes with rice, scaled up into a piece of theatre, with a long line of servants each carrying one dish to the table. It was as much a display of wealth and reach as a way of eating, and it was aimed squarely at colonial appetites and colonial guests. What happened to it afterwards is the strange part. After independence the format faded in Indonesia, where it carried an obvious association nobody was keen to keep, while the Indo-Dutch communities who left for the Netherlands took it with them — so the rijsttafel is now a living institution in Dutch restaurants and an oddity in Indonesian ones, mostly kept alive for tourists. A cuisine's most famous format survives thousands of kilometres from the country whose name it carries, and it is worth knowing that the genuinely Indonesian version of many-small-dishes is something else entirely: the nasi padang counter, where the dishes are stacked in the window and you are charged for whichever ones you touch.\n\nAcar's place in that history is small and revealing. It is almost always on the table, it is almost never what anyone remembers, and it is the dish holding the whole arrangement together. Put twenty rich, oily, coconut-heavy, chilli-loaded dishes in front of somebody and the palate surrenders after about six mouthfuls; a single fork of something cold, sharp and crunching resets it, and the meal goes on. Every large cuisine has worked this out and arrived at its own version — a pickle, a relish, a wedge of lemon, a sharp slaw — and they are all doing the same structural job. The tell is that acar is one of the very few dishes on an Indonesian table that is not a variation of anything. It is the counterweight, and it is why the smallest bowl is the one to make first.","howThisFeels":"Squeezing a fistful of salted cucumber over the sink and being genuinely startled by how much water comes out of it.","leftovers":["(easy) The brine itself, once the vegetables are gone, is a finished dressing — shake it with a spoonful of oil and it goes straight onto any green salad, and it is sharper and more interesting than a vinaigrette you would have made on purpose.","(easy) Chopped small and folded through hot rice with a spoonful of the brine, which is a whole light lunch and takes two minutes.","(medium) Drained, chopped and stirred into mayonnaise with a little chilli for a fierce yellow dip or sandwich spread, which is roughly what a tartare sauce is and better than most of them.","(creative) Softening acar at the end of its fortnight blitzed with its own brine into a thin loose relish, then spooned over grilled fish or fried eggs — the crunch has gone anyway, so use the one thing it still has, which is acid."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Acar Timun — Cucumber and Shallot, No Brine Fry (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Clear, colourless and on the table in ten minutes, and gone off a satay plate faster than anything else on it.","trivia":"This is not a reduced acar kuning so much as the other, older and far more common thing — acar timun is what actually arrives beside a plate of skewers almost everywhere, and it is made in the quantity of one small bowl at a time rather than by the jar. Losing the turmeric, ginger and the frying step also changes what the dish is FOR. The yellow version is a keeping pickle built to sit in a fridge for a fortnight and be spooned out; this one is made in the ten minutes before eating and is meant to be finished the same day, which is why it is cut thinner, salted for less time and left crunchier and much sharper. It is worth knowing both, because the two are answers to different questions: one is a store cupboard, the other is a garnish.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g carrots"},{"item":"15ml sunflower oil"},{"item":"6g fresh ginger"},{"item":"4g ground turmeric"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No carrot, no oil, no ginger and no turmeric, which also means there is no frying pan involved at all. Cut the 80g of cucumber a little thinner than the main version — 5mm batons rather than 1cm — because this one is eaten today rather than kept, and salt it for 15 minutes rather than 30. Rinse and squeeze it exactly as described; that step earns its keep even over a few hours. Then simply stir the 60ml of white vinegar, the 30ml of water, the 15g of jaggery and the remaining salt together in a bowl until the sugar has dissolved, WITHOUT heating any of it, and pour it cold over the cucumber, the shallot wedges, the chilli and the finely chopped garlic. No boiling brine here: heat exists in the main recipe to buy two weeks of shelf life, and a bowl being eaten within a few hours does not need it and is crunchier without it. Let it stand 20 minutes, then eat it the same day. ⛔ Do not keep this one for a fortnight — nothing was heated and nothing was sterilised, so it is a two-day pickle at the outside, refrigerated."}]}},{"name":"Acar Kuning — Boiling Brine over Cold Raw Vegetables","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":15,"howThisFeels":"Brine going into the jar still boiling and the whole thing turning gold in front of you in about four seconds.","trivia":"The shallot wedges are the piece of this pickle that behaves least like the others, and it is worth knowing because it explains why they go in whole rather than sliced. An onion's cells hold a sulphur compound and an enzyme in separate compartments, and cutting is what introduces them to each other — which is where the raw, hot, eye-watering pungency comes from, and it is why a finely sliced onion is fierce and a thick wedge is mild. Both the heat of the brine and the acid then work on those same compounds, breaking them down over the first day, which is exactly why a shallot that tasted aggressive when it went into the jar tastes sweet and faintly translucent by the next morning. Cut them thin and you make far more of the pungency to begin with, and you also give the acid so much surface that the wedge collapses into a soft sliver within days. Thick, whole and slightly cruel-looking on day one is the correct condition."},{"name":"Acar Ikan Kuning — Fried Fish in the Pickle","icon":"🐟","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":44,"howThisFeels":"Cold fried fish that has been sitting in yellow brine overnight and is somehow better than it was hot.","trivia":"This version inverts the temperature rule the main recipe is built on, and the inversion is the lesson. Fish is fried first, and then EVERYTHING must be cold before the two meet — cold brine, cold fish. Pouring boiling brine over a fried fillet does exactly what pouring boiling anything over a fried thing does: the crust takes up water, steams from underneath and slides off in sheets, and you are left with bare fish in cloudy liquid. So the brine gets made and boiled as normal, for the shelf life, and then cooled completely before it goes anywhere near the pan. The dish that results is a keeping dish in the older sense of the phrase — fried fish submerged in acid was one of the standard ways of holding fish for a few days without a fridge anywhere it was hot, and the same idea turns up under different names all around the coasts of the world. It is also simply very good: the fish takes up the sour brine overnight and the vegetables take up a little of the frying.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"150g hake"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"15ml sunflower oil","to":"45ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 45ml of sunflower oil rather than 15ml — 30ml for frying the fish and 15ml for the brine as before. Pat the 150g of hake completely dry, salt it lightly, and shallow-fry it over medium-high heat for 3 to 4 minutes a side until the outside is properly firm and golden; hake is soft and flake-prone, so turn it once only and use a wide spatula. Lift it onto a rack and let it cool COMPLETELY, which takes about 20 minutes and is not optional. Make the brine and the salted vegetables exactly as described — including boiling the brine, which is doing the shelf-life job — and then cool the brine completely as well before assembling. ⛔ Both cold. Lay the fish in a shallow dish, pack the drained vegetables around and over it, and pour the cold brine over so everything is submerged. Refrigerate at least 4 hours and preferably overnight. Eat it cold, within 3 days rather than the fortnight the vegetable version keeps, because fried fish in brine is a much shorter proposition than pickled carrots."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Ayam Betutu","target":"indonesia-ayam-betutu","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Rujak","target":"indonesia-rujak","emoji":"🍍"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sayur-asem","name":"Sayur Asem","nameAlt":"Sour tamarind vegetable broth","aliases":["Sayur Asem","Sayur Asam","Sajoer Asem","Sour Vegetable Soup","Tamarind Vegetable Soup","Indonesian Sour Soup","Sayur Asem Sunda","Sayur Asem Jakarta"],"course":"side","type":["side","soup","vegetables","one-pot","budget","light","everyday","vegan"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","budget","light","lunch","meat-free"],"ingredients":"60g raw peanuts · 80g baby marrow · 60g green beans · 60g sweetcorn · 40g cabbage · 20g shallots · 15g tamarind paste · 12g jaggery · 10g red chilli · 6g garlic · 4g galangal · 4g salt · 2g bay leaves · 500ml water","method":"There is one instruction in this recipe that decides whether it works, and it is about WHEN, not what. The tamarind goes in at the END. Everything else — the vegetables, the aromatics, the sugar, the chilli — is a matter of taste and of what was in the house. The acid is a matter of physics, and putting it in early is the single mistake that turns this from a bright, clean, sour broth into a pot of chalky peanuts sitting in vegetables that will not soften no matter how long you leave them.\n\nFIRST, THE HONEST SHOPPING, BECAUSE TWO OF THE ORIGINALS ARE NOT ON A SHELF HERE. Sayur asem is built on labu siam — chayote, a pale green ridged squash — and very often on melinjo, the hard little seeds of the gnetum tree. Neither is realistically buyable here, and pretending otherwise is how a recipe wastes an afternoon. ✅ For chayote, baby marrow is the right stand-in and not a compromise you should feel bad about: both are mild, watery, thin-skinned and firm-fleshed, both hold their shape in a broth for exactly as long as you want them to and then go instantly to mush, and neither brings any flavour of its own — which is the point, since their job is texture and bulk in a broth that gets its whole character from somewhere else. ⛔ Melinjo has no stand-in and I am not going to invent one; it is faintly bitter and slightly chewy and nothing else behaves like it. Leave it out. The soup is complete without it. Long beans become green beans, cut short, and lose nothing.\n\nSTART THE PEANUTS FIRST, IN COLD UNSALTED WATER, AND GIVE THEM MUCH LONGER THAN YOU THINK. Put the 60g of raw peanuts in a pot with the 500ml of water and bring it to a bare simmer — the surface barely moving, not a rolling boil. Leave them 35 to 40 minutes. They are ready when one crushes flat against the roof of your mouth with no chalk in the middle and no resistance in the skin. ⛔ Do not add salt yet and do not add anything sour yet. This is the longest thing in the pot by a wide margin and it has to be finished before a single vegetable goes in, because nothing you do later can rescue an underdone peanut.\n\nWATCH WHAT HAPPENS TO THE WATER, BECAUSE THAT CLOUD IS THE ENTIRE BODY OF THE DISH. There is no coconut milk in sayur asem, no stock, no oil, no cornflour and nothing fried. The soup gets its weight from starch and protein leaching out of the peanuts over those forty minutes, which is why the water goes from clear to a faintly milky beige and why the finished broth has a soft roundness under the sourness instead of tasting like sharp water. This is also why you never drain the peanuts and start again with fresh water, which some recipes suggest to reduce the cloudiness — the cloudiness is not a flaw, it is the dish. If yours is thin, you either had too much water or not enough time.\n\nBRUISE THE AROMATICS, DO NOT POUND THEM. Flatten the 4g of galangal under the side of a knife until it splits and the smell jumps, slice the 20g of shallots and the 6g of garlic thin, cut the 10g of red chilli into fat diagonals, and add all of it to the peanut pot with the 2g of bay leaves. Simmer another 10 minutes. ⛔ A pounded paste is what you would do for a curry and it is wrong here — a paste clouds the broth into an opaque gravy, and sayur asem is meant to be a broth you can see through. Bruising splits the cells and lets the aromatics steep, so the flavour goes into the liquid while the pieces stay as pieces you can push aside on the spoon.\n\nNOW THE VEGETABLES, HARDEST FIRST, AND THEY GO IN QUICKLY ONE AFTER THE OTHER. The 60g of green beans cut into 3cm lengths and the 60g of sweetcorn go in first and get 6 minutes. Then the 80g of baby marrow in 1.5cm half-moons for 4 minutes. Then the 40g of shredded cabbage for the last 2 minutes only. The Gado-Gado card sets out the reasoning for staggering vegetables in full and it applies unchanged here, so I will not repeat it — the short version is that they are different vegetables and there is no single number of minutes that suits all four.\n\nTHE TAMARIND GOES IN LAST. THIS IS THE RECIPE. Off the heat, or with the pot barely ticking over, stir in the 15g of tamarind paste, the 12g of jaggery and the 4g of salt. Stir until the tamarind has completely dissolved and taste it — it should be sharply, almost aggressively sour, with the sweetness sitting underneath rather than beside it. Adjust with a little more of either. Then serve it. Do not simmer it for another twenty minutes to \"let the flavours marry\"; there is nothing left to marry and you will spoil it.\n\nHERE IS WHY THE ORDER MATTERS, AND IT IS WORTH UNDERSTANDING RATHER THAN JUST OBEYING. Vegetables are held rigid by pectin in their cell walls, and pectin is held in place partly by calcium bridges between its chains. Heating vegetables in ordinary near-neutral water breaks those chains down steadily, and that breakdown is what we call softening — it is the reason a carrot goes from snapping to bending. Drop the pH of the water and that breakdown slows down dramatically. Acid keeps the pectin chains intact and the cell walls firm, which is why a pot of beans simmered in tomato takes twice as long to go tender as the same beans in plain water, and why apple slices hold their shape in a tart made with lemon. So a tamarind pot from the beginning is not a pot of sour soup — it is a pot in which the peanuts stay chalky for an hour and the green beans stay squeaky and you slowly conclude that your stove is broken. ✅ Acid last means everything is already tender when the sourness arrives, and the sourness then arrives in the liquid where you want it and nowhere else.\n\nTHERE IS A SECOND REASON AND IT IS ABOUT FLAVOUR, NOT TEXTURE. Tamarind's sourness is bright and fruity when it goes in fresh, and long boiling flattens it — the fruit solids break down, the pulp's sugars take a little colour, and what was a clean high sour becomes a dull, dark, slightly stewed sourness that sits at the back of the mouth. The sour is the only thing this soup has. Adding it at the end and stopping is the difference between a broth that tastes lit up and one that tastes tired.\n\nEAT IT WARM RATHER THAN SCALDING, AND EAT IT WITH RICE. This is a side dish and a drink at the same time: a small bowl next to a plate of rice, something fried, and a spoonful of sambal, and the broth is spooned over the rice or drunk straight from the bowl between mouthfuls. ✅ It is noticeably better at 20 minutes off the stove than straight out of the pot, because very hot liquid mutes sourness and the whole point of this bowl is the sour. It is also better the next day, which almost nothing with green vegetables in it can claim — the vegetables have gone softer, but the acid that stopped them softening on the stove is still slowing them in the fridge.","cookTime":"Peanuts 40 min · Aromatics 10 min · Vegetables 12 min · Total ~1 hr","kcal":"~215 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 10g · Carbs 24g · Fat 9g · Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"This is one of very few green-vegetable dishes that is honestly better on day two, and the acid is the reason — the same low pH that stopped the vegetables softening on the stove keeps slowing them down in the fridge, so a cabbage that would be grey slime in an ordinary soup is still recognisably cabbage tomorrow. It keeps 3 days covered in the fridge and is at its best on day one and day two. Reheat it gently to just steaming, never to a boil: boiling it again does exactly what boiling the tamarind in from the start would have done and flattens the sourness into something dull. ✅ If it has gone a little flat anyway, the fix is 3g more tamarind paste stirred in off the heat, not more salt. ⛔ It does not freeze. Every vegetable in it is a high-water vegetable with nothing protecting its cell walls, and freezing bursts them all — you would thaw a pot of sour water with pale rags in it, and the peanuts would be the only survivors. If it smells fizzy or yeasty rather than sour, or the surface has gone slippery, it goes out; a sour dish does not announce spoilage as clearly as a bland one, so go by the smell of yeast rather than the taste of acid.","chefNotes":"The tamarind goes in LAST, off the heat, and everything about whether this works follows from that. Acid keeps pectin chains intact, so a sour pot is a pot where nothing ever goes tender — the peanuts stay chalky, the beans stay squeaky, and no amount of extra time fixes it. Start the raw peanuts in cold unsalted water and give them a full 35 to 40 minutes before a single vegetable goes in; the cloudy water they leave behind is the only body this soup has, so never drain it. Bruise the galangal and slice the shallots rather than pounding a paste — a paste turns a see-through broth into gravy. And serve it warm rather than scalding, because heat mutes sourness and sour is the whole dish.","pairsWith":"This is the sour, watery, cooling thing that goes next to the rich fried thing, and it is almost never the meal. A bowl of it beside a plate of rice, a piece of fried chicken and a spoonful of sambal is one of the most common lunches in western Java, and the logic is the same as a glass of something cold beside something greasy — the broth resets the mouth between bites and the meal keeps going. It is at its best against anything deep-fried or coconut-heavy, and it works beautifully beside grilled fish. It does not want another sour thing on the same plate, so a sharp pickle alongside is one sour too many and both lose. Plain steamed white rice is not optional here: the broth is thin and sharp and needs something blank underneath it, and half the pleasure is the moment the sour liquid soaks into hot rice.","goesWith":["Ayam Goreng Kalasan","Tempe Goreng","Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi"],"trivia":"The word that names this soup is doing three jobs at once, and the tree it points at is on the wrong continent. Asem — or asam — is simply the Indonesian and Malay word for sour. It is also the name of the tamarind. The language never bothered to separate the taste from the plant that most reliably delivers it, so an entire family of dishes is named after an adjective: sayur asem is, literally, sour vegetables. English did worse. Our word tamarind comes through Arabic as tamr hindī, the date of India, which manages to be wrong twice over: it is not a date, and the tree is not Indian. Tamarindus indica is native to tropical Africa, most likely the dry savannah belt south of the Sahara, and it travelled east so early and so completely that by the time anybody was writing names down, Arab traders had met it in India and reasonably assumed that was where it lived. Sanskrit had already called it the Indian date too. A tree from Sudan is known worldwide by a name meaning Indian fruit, and it flavours a soup in Java under a word that means sour, and nowhere in any of those three languages does the name contain a hint of where it actually came from.\n\nThe chemistry has the same slightly displaced quality. Most sour fruit is sour because of citric acid — lemons, oranges, pineapple. Tamarind is one of the very few that leans instead on tartaric acid, which is otherwise the signature acid of grapes and the thing that crusts the inside of a wine barrel. It is a rounder, softer, longer sourness than citric acid's sharp spike, and it is exactly why a squeeze of lemon cannot stand in for tamarind here: the shape of the sour is wrong, not just the flavour. 🌱 And if you want your own: a seed out of a block of tamarind pulp germinates readily in a warm Pretoria summer and grows into a genuinely beautiful feathery tree — but it will not give you a pod for the better part of a decade, and then it will keep giving them for well over a century. It is not a plant you grow for tonight's soup. It is a plant you grow for shade, and for whoever is cooking in your kitchen in 2040.","howThisFeels":"Cloudy peanut water turning bright and sharp the second the tamarind goes in, and tasting it twice because you can't quite believe that was the whole trick.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold from the fridge, straight from a mug, on a hot afternoon — it is astonishingly good this way and it is what half of Java does with the last of the pot.","(easy) Poured hot over yesterday's rice with a fried egg on top, which turns a side dish into a whole lunch and takes four minutes.","(medium) Strain the broth off, keep the vegetables aside, and use the sour liquid as the braising water for a piece of white fish — it needs about 8 minutes and no other seasoning at all.","(creative) Reduce the strained broth hard by half, then whisk in a spoonful of oil off the heat: you have a sour, savoury, peanut-thickened dressing that is superb over blanched greens or cold cooked potatoes, and it exists nowhere else."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sayur Asem Jakarta — Marrow, Cabbage and Peanut (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Four things and a pot of water making a soup that tastes like it took much more than it did.","trivia":"Stripping this back to marrow, cabbage and peanut is not a hardship version — it is closer to how the dish is actually cooked on most days, and the Jakarta style is genuinely thinner and sharper than the Sundanese one. The Betawi version of sayur asem leans harder on the sour and lighter on the vegetables, and often has no corn or beans in it at all, because a household soup was always made from what was already in the kitchen rather than from a list. The one thing that never gets cut is the peanuts, and now you know why: they are not an ingredient in this soup so much as the reason it has any body. Take everything else out and you still have a soup. Take the peanuts out and you have sour water.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g green beans"},{"item":"60g sweetcorn"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"60g raw peanuts","to":"40g raw peanuts"},{"from":"40g cabbage","to":"60g cabbage"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No green beans and no sweetcorn, and a little more cabbage to make up the bulk. Simmer 40g of raw peanuts in the 500ml of water for the full 35 to 40 minutes as described — this step never shortens, no matter how few other things are going in, because it is the step that makes the broth. Add the bruised aromatics for 10 minutes. Then the 80g of baby marrow for 4 minutes, the 60g of cabbage for the last 2, and the tamarind, jaggery and salt off the heat. ✅ With fewer vegetables in the pot the broth is proportionally sharper and thinner, so start with 12g of tamarind rather than 15g and add the rest by taste — the same amount of acid in a lighter soup lands harder."}]}},{"name":"Sayur Asem Sunda — Acid Last, Peanuts First","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"A green bean that still squeaks against your teeth in a broth that has been on the stove for an hour, and understanding exactly why.","trivia":"The Sundanese highlands of West Java are where this dish is most itself, and the reason is a whole regional habit rather than a recipe: Sundanese cooking is the most raw-vegetable-forward in Indonesia, built on lalab — piles of leaves, cucumber, beans and shoots eaten uncooked with sambal — in a country whose other cuisines cook almost everything. A sour, barely-simmered vegetable broth fits that instinct exactly, and it is why the Sundanese version keeps its vegetables so much firmer and greener than versions made further east. It is also why the acid-last rule was never a trick anybody had to be taught here. If your whole cuisine is built on vegetables that have barely been cooked, you would notice quite quickly that the sour pot never softens anything, and you would move the tamarind to the end without ever hearing the word pectin.","delta":null},{"name":"Sayur Asem Udang — Prawn and Shrimp Paste (Omnivore)","icon":"🦐","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":70,"howThisFeels":"Prawns going in for the last ninety seconds and curling tight while you watch, and the broth going from sour to sour-and-deep in the same moment.","trivia":"Adding prawns to a sour broth is a much older idea than it looks, and the sourness is doing something specific to them. Prawn flesh is loaded with free amino acids — far more than fish or meat — which is why prawns taste sweet and savoury at once and why they need so little help. Acid sharpens exactly that: a squeeze of something sour on seafood is not just a garnish habit, it lifts those amino acids forward the way salt does. The other half of the pairing is less flattering and more practical. Shrimp paste and dried shrimp exist because prawns spoil faster than almost any other protein — the same enzymes that make them delicious start working on them the moment they die — so coastal Southeast Asia has always had a way of turning a glut into something that keeps for a year. A pot with fresh prawns and a spoonful of shrimp paste in it therefore holds both halves of the same animal's story: the fast one and the slow one.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g prawns"},{"item":"10g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Two additions and one timing rule that matters more than either. Crumble the 10g of shrimp paste in with the bruised aromatics at the 10-minute steeping stage so it dissolves completely into the broth — it will smell startling for about a minute and then settle into a deep savoury background you would miss if it were gone. Then cook the vegetables exactly as described, and add the 80g of prawns for the LAST 90 SECONDS ONLY, after the cabbage and before the tamarind. ⛔ Prawns are done the instant they turn opaque and curl, and every second past that tightens them; a prawn that has simmered five minutes in a sour broth is a rubber comma. ✅ Add the tamarind off the heat as always, with the prawns already in — the acid firms their surface slightly and makes them read as even sweeter against it. This version does not keep as well as the vegetable one: eat it the same day, because reheated prawns are a poor trade for a soup that was otherwise better tomorrow."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Ayam Goreng Kalasan","target":"indonesia-ayam-goreng-kalasan","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Gado-Gado","target":"indonesia-gado-gado","emoji":"🥜"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-oseng-oseng-mercon","name":"Oseng-Oseng Mercon","nameAlt":"Firecracker beef, stir-braised nearly dry","aliases":["Oseng-Oseng Mercon","Oseng Mercon","Oseng Oseng Mercon","Mercon","Firecracker Beef","Yogyakarta Chilli Beef","Oseng Mercon Tetelan","Oseng Mercon Koyor"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","stew","one-pot","street-food","comfort","budget"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","weeknight","street food","comfort"],"ingredients":"150g beef shin · 60g red chilli · 20g shallots · 20g tomatoes · 15ml sunflower oil · 10g garlic · 10g lemongrass · 6g galangal · 5g jaggery · 4g salt · 2g bay leaves · 300ml water","method":"The heat is not a side effect of this dish, it is the dish — mercon means firecracker and the name is a warning, not a boast. So the useful thing to understand before you start is that there are exactly THREE levers on how hot this ends up, and the one everybody reaches for is the least effective of them. The three are: how much chilli, whether you pull the white pith out, and HOW MUCH OIL IS IN THE PAN. That last one is the real dial and almost no recipe mentions it.\n\n⚠️ THE SAFETY PART FIRST, AND IT IS NOT A DISCLAIMER TACKED ON THE END. You are about to fry 60g of ground raw chilli in a hot pan, and frying chilli puts capsaicin into the air. That is not a figure of speech — it is precisely the mechanism pepper spray works on, which is nothing more than capsaicin suspended in oil. So: open a window and put the extractor on before the paste hits the pan, do not stand over the pan and do not lean in to smell it, and get anybody with asthma out of the kitchen. You will cough. That is normal and it passes. ⛔ And when you handle the chilli, oil your hands first or wear a glove, because capsaicin is oil-soluble and barely water-soluble, which means rinsing your hands under the tap moves it around rather than removing it. Wash with dish soap or wipe with oil first, then soap. Do not touch your eyes for an hour. ✅ Have something dairy on the table — milk, yoghurt, or coconut milk — because casein actually lifts capsaicin off the receptors it is sitting on. Water does nothing at all.\n\nTHE BEEF IS FULLY COOKED BEFORE THE CHILLI EVER APPEARS, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Put the 150g of beef shin in a small pot with the 300ml of water, the 10g of lemongrass and the 6g of galangal both bruised flat under a knife, and the 2g of bay leaves. Simmer it, covered, at a bare tick — surface barely moving — for 1½ to 2 hours, until a piece pulls apart under gentle pressure from a fork but has not collapsed into threads. Then LIFT THE BEEF OUT and keep every drop of the liquid.\n\nHERE IS WHY, BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE THE DISH IS USUALLY LOST. An oseng is a dry stir-braise: high heat, a nearly dry pan, three or four minutes, everything tossed and coated. Nothing tough survives that treatment — not for four minutes, not for forty. Shin is one of the most collagen-heavy cuts on the animal, and collagen only turns into soft gelatin under long, wet, gentle heat; hit it hot and fast and it simply contracts and squeaks. So the wet part and the dry part of this recipe are two completely separate cooking processes that happen to occur in the same evening, and trying to merge them gives you chewy beef in a fierce sauce. Cook it soft first. Then make it hot.\n\nREDUCE THE COOKING LIQUID DOWN TO ABOUT 60ml AND KEEP IT BESIDE THE STOVE. Boil it hard, uncovered, until there is about four tablespoons left and it has gone slightly syrupy — that is dissolved gelatin from the shin, and it is the only sauce this dish gets. Throwing it away and using water later is the difference between a plate that glosses and one that looks dusty.\n\nGRIND THE CHILLI COARSE AND RAW, AND MAKE YOUR HEAT DECISION AT THIS EXACT MOMENT. Pound or blitz the 60g of red chilli, the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic and the 20g of tomatoes to a rough, still-flecked paste — coarse, not smooth, so there is texture in the finished dish. ✅ THIS is where you control the burn, and the thing worth knowing is that the heat is not in the seeds. It is concentrated in the white spongy placental tissue that the seeds are attached to; the seeds themselves are largely along for the ride and taste of very little. Scrape that white pith out with the tip of a spoon and you remove well over half the heat while losing almost none of the flavour or the colour. Leave it in and you have the real thing. There is no shame in a middle route — pith out of half of them.\n\nNOW THE OIL, WHICH IS THE LEVER THIS RECIPE IS ACTUALLY ABOUT. Capsaicin dissolves readily in fat and hardly at all in water. In a nearly dry pan with only 15ml of oil, the capsaicin stays largely where it was — concentrated in the chilli fragments themselves — so the burn arrives in SPIKES: one mouthful catches a hot flake and detonates, the next is merely warm. Put 45ml in instead and the capsaicin partitions out into the fat, the fat coats every piece of beef and then coats the inside of your mouth, and the identical amount of chilli now reads as a smooth, broad, slow burn that builds and lasts much longer. The total heat has not changed by a milligram. Its SHAPE has changed completely. Sharp and punchy, or smooth and long — you choose it with the oil jug, and you never touch the chilli count. ⛔ What you cannot do is cook the heat out. Capsaicin is stable well above any temperature a domestic pan reaches, so simmering for an hour reduces nothing; a dish that is too hot when you taste it will be exactly as hot at the table. Fix it with more beef and more rice, never with more time.\n\nFRY THE PASTE HARD FOR 3 TO 4 MINUTES. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil in a wide pan over medium-high, add the paste, and stir it more or less constantly. You are watching for two changes: the colour going from a bright pillar-box red to a duller brick, and the smell turning from raw, green and sinus-clearing to something toasty and round. ⛔ Do not wait for a pool of red oil to separate out at the edges — there is nowhere near enough fat in this pan for that to happen, and that is a rendang move belonging to a rendang quantity of oil. Colour and smell are your only two cues here.\n\nBEEF BACK IN, LIQUID IN, TWO MINUTES ON HIGH, AND IT MUST LOOK DRY. Tear or cut the cooked shin into rough bite pieces, add it to the paste with the reduced 60ml of liquid, the 5g of jaggery and the 4g of salt, and toss over high heat for 2 minutes until the liquid has all but vanished and every piece is coated and glossy. ✅ The finished dish is DRY. If there is sauce pooling in the pan you have not gone far enough — an oseng sits on the plate in a heap and does not run, and that concentration is why such a small portion is enough.\n\nTHEN TAKE IT OFF THE HEAT AND LEAVE IT ALONE FOR 5 MINUTES. Straight out of the pan it tastes sharper and more raggedly hot than it should, because the capsaicin has not finished moving into the fat and spreading through the dish. Five minutes standing evens it out noticeably, and the flavours underneath the heat — the galangal, the caramel from the jaggery, the gelatin — come forward instead of being trampled.\n\nSERVE IT SMALL, WITH A LOT OF PLAIN RICE AND SOMETHING COOL. A heaped tablespoon or two per person on a proper plate of rice is a serving, and it will feel like plenty. This is a main dish built with the intensity of a condiment, and the rice is not a garnish — it is the only thing standing between you and the pan.","cookTime":"Simmer 1 hr 45 min · Reduce 10 min · Oseng 6 min · Rest 5 min","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 31g · Carbs 9g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~1600mg","storage":"This is one of the very few dishes in this section that is genuinely better on day two and freezes properly, and both come down to what is NOT in it: no fresh green vegetables, no coconut milk, no dairy and no starch to go claggy. It keeps 4 days covered in the fridge. ⚠️ Expect it to taste HOTTER tomorrow — nothing has been added, but overnight the capsaicin finishes migrating into the fat and distributes evenly through the whole dish instead of sitting in chilli fragments, which is exactly the smooth-and-broad effect the oil lever produces on purpose. If that is more than you want, stir a spoonful of coconut milk through as you reheat. It freezes well for 3 months in a sealed tub — the collagen has already been converted to gelatin, so freezing has nothing left to ruin, and it thaws without the graininess that catches out most beef dishes. Reheat in a pan rather than a microwave, over medium heat with a splash of water, and drive it dry again before serving; a microwave leaves it wet in patches and the whole character of an oseng is that it is dry. ⛔ Do not store it in a container you care about or in anything with a soft plastic lid, because chilli oil stains and the smell does not leave.","chefNotes":"Cook the beef soft FIRST, in water, for a full 1½ to 2 hours — then make it hot. An oseng is a four-minute dry stir-braise and no tough cut has ever survived one; shin is nearly all collagen and it needs long wet heat to turn to gelatin. Keep the simmering liquid and reduce it to 60ml: that syrupy gelatin is the only sauce. The heat lives in the white pith the seeds are attached to, not the seeds — scrape it out and you lose over half the burn and almost no flavour. And the real dial is the OIL: capsaicin is fat-soluble, so 15ml gives sharp intermittent spikes and 45ml gives a smooth broad burn from the identical chilli. You cannot cook heat out — capsaicin laughs at frying temperature. Open a window before the paste hits the pan.","pairsWith":"Rice, and then more rice. This is a small dish with a large voice, and everything else on the plate is there to make it survivable rather than to compete with it — a mound of plain white rice, and then something soft, cool and dull on purpose. Fried tempeh is the classic partner because it is savoury and neutral and gives you something to chew between hits. Anything with coconut milk in it works beautifully alongside for a reason that is chemistry rather than taste: the fat carries capsaicin away from the receptors it is sitting on. A blanched or lightly dressed vegetable is welcome. ⛔ What does not work is another chilli dish beside it — a sambal on this plate is a joke played on your guests, and the second one is wasted anyway because your palate has already gone. Cold sweet tea or a glass of milk beats water, which does nothing at all against an oil-soluble molecule.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Tempe Goreng","Urap-Urap","Acar Kuning"],"trivia":"The number on the side of a hot sauce bottle is the strangest unit in food, because for most of its life it was not a measurement at all — it was a committee of people sipping sugar water until they stopped noticing. In 1912 a pharmacist named Wilbur Scoville, working for a pharmaceutical company on a capsicum muscle rub, needed some way to compare one batch of chilli extract with the next. His method was to dissolve a weighed amount of extract in alcohol, dilute it with sweetened water, and hand it to a small panel of tasters — usually five people — diluting further and further until a majority could no longer detect any heat at all. The dilution factor at that point became the number. Diluted one hundred thousand times before the burn vanished, and the extract is rated at one hundred thousand Scoville Heat Units. That is the whole test, and it was the industry standard for the better part of a century.\n\nIt has all the problems you would expect and one you might not. Tasters differ from each other; tasters get desensitised as the session goes on, so the numbers drift downward through an afternoon; and a panel of five that all like spicy food will rate a chilli lower than a panel that does not. That is a large part of why published Scoville figures for the same variety of chilli vary by a factor of two or three depending on which source you read — you are not reading a disagreement about chillies, you are reading a disagreement between long-dead tasting panels. Modern laboratories measure the capsaicinoids directly by liquid chromatography, which is precise and repeatable, and then MULTIPLY the result by a conversion factor to express it in Scoville units anyway, because that is the number the world understands. So a figure produced by a machine is still dressed in the units of a 1912 taste test. And the unit reaches further than food: police pepper spray is rated in the same currency, typically somewhere around two million Scoville units — which puts a can on a duty belt in roughly the same range as the hottest chillies people deliberately breed and eat. When this recipe tells you to open a window before the paste hits the pan, that is the comparison it has in mind.","howThisFeels":"The moment the paste hits the oil and the whole kitchen turns against you, and you cough and laugh and keep stirring anyway.","leftovers":["(easy) A spoonful on hot rice with a fried egg over it, which is what half of Yogyakarta eats for breakfast the morning after.","(easy) Stirred through plain instant noodles at the very end, off the heat — it is fierce, it takes ninety seconds, and it is far better than any sachet.","(medium) Loosened with a little coconut milk over low heat into an actual sauce, which tames it by a good third and turns a dry oseng into something you can serve over potatoes or greens.","(creative) Chopped fine and folded into a plain omelette mixture with a spoonful of the fat — the egg mutes the burn beautifully and you get a hot, savoury, beefy omelette that tastes nothing like either parent."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Oseng Mercon Tetelan — Cheap Cuts, Long Simmer (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":33,"howThisFeels":"The cheapest meat in the shop coming out of a long quiet simmer soft enough to pull with a spoon.","trivia":"This is not a downgraded version, it is the original. Tetelan is the Indonesian word for the offcuts left clinging to bone after butchering — scraps of meat, fat, sinew and gristle sold for very little because nobody could do much with them, and mercon was built on exactly that. The dish is a way of turning an unsellable cut into something people queue for, and it works because the two things it does to the beef are precisely the two things a scrap cut needs: hours of gentle wet heat to convert all that connective tissue into gelatin, and enough chilli that nobody is examining the texture too closely. The same logic built half the world's best-loved food, from oxtail to pho to boerewors, and it is worth remembering when a recipe tells you to use fillet.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef shin","to":"100g beef stew pieces"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use 100g of beef stew pieces rather than 150g of shin — the cheap offcuts with fat and sinew running through them are the honest original, and they cost roughly half. ✅ Two adjustments follow. Give them LONGER, not shorter: 2 to 2½ hours at a bare simmer, because a mixed cut has more connective tissue to convert and the whole point is to convert all of it. And skim the fat that rises during the simmer into a cup rather than discarding it, then fry the chilli paste in THAT instead of the sunflower oil — it is free, it carries far more flavour, and it is doing the same fat-soluble job with the capsaicin. ⚠️ Since the beef is 100g rather than 150g and the chilli stays at 60g, this version is proportionally hotter per mouthful. If that is not what you want, pull the pith from half the chillies rather than cutting the quantity, or the dish loses its body."}]}},{"name":"Oseng-Oseng Mercon — Beef Shin, Sharp and Nearly Dry","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":41,"howThisFeels":"A pan that looks almost dry and a plate that needs three times its own volume in rice.","trivia":"Almost everything in this section is older than the country it comes from. This one is not. Oseng-oseng mercon appeared in Yogyakarta in the late 1990s, credited to a street vendor working a small stall in the city centre, and it spread across Java in a decade — which makes it younger than the mobile phone and roughly the same age as the internet arriving in Indonesian homes. That is unusually visible for a dish. Most recipes reach us with their inventor lost centuries back, and we reconstruct their origins from trade routes and etymology and guesses; this one has a location, a decade and a queue that people still remember joining. It is a useful reminder that a cuisine is not a museum. Somewhere right now a vendor is inventing the dish that a recipe app will be explaining reverently in eighty years, and the thing that will carry it is the same thing that carried this one: it is very good, very cheap, and it makes people want to tell someone else about it.","delta":null},{"name":"Oseng Mercon Koyor — Tripe and Tendon, the Smooth Long Burn","icon":"🔥","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":55,"howThisFeels":"A burn that arrives late, spreads sideways and then simply stays, and you keep eating it anyway.","trivia":"Koyor is the Javanese word for the gelatinous connective bits — tendon, sinew, the soft translucent stuff around a joint — and a koyor version of this dish is where the oil lever is easiest to feel, because it is built to demonstrate it. With 45ml of oil instead of 15ml the identical 60g of chilli stops arriving in spikes and starts arriving as a single broad wave: capsaicin partitions into the fat, the fat coats everything including your mouth, and the burn becomes late, wide and remarkably persistent rather than sharp and intermittent. The tripe is doing its own version of the same trick. Its texture is nearly pure collagen, so after two hours of wet heat it is soft and slippery and holds the fat on its surface in a way lean muscle simply cannot, which means every piece of it is delivering a full dose. This is the version to cook when you want to understand the mechanism. It is not the version to cook for someone you have not warned.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g tripe"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"15ml sunflower oil","to":"45ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add 100g of tripe and raise the oil from 15ml to 45ml, and the two changes work together. Rinse the tripe well under cold running water, then simmer it alongside the beef shin in the same pot for the full 2 hours — it wants exactly the same long wet heat and finishes at exactly the same point, soft and slippery with no squeak left in it. ⚠️ It will smell strongly for the first twenty minutes; that passes, and it passes faster with the lid slightly off. Cut it into short ribbons before it goes back into the pan. Then fry the chilli paste in the full 45ml of oil. ✅ Watch what the extra fat does to the pan as well as to the burn: with three times the oil the paste fries more evenly and takes colour faster, so check it at 2½ minutes rather than 3½, and you may see a thin red rim of oil separate at the edge — at this quantity that is fine and it is not the same event as a rendang splitting. Finish exactly as the main version: high heat, 2 minutes, dry. The oil clings to the tripe and the whole dish comes out glossy rather than dusty."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥩"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bubur-ayam","name":"Bubur Ayam","nameAlt":"Chicken rice congee with a broth poured over","aliases":["Bubur Ayam","Bubur","Indonesian Chicken Congee","Chicken Congee","Rice Porridge","Bubur Ayam Jakarta","Bubur Nasi","Bubur Ayam Spesial"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","soup","breakfast","comfort","budget","one-pot"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["breakfast","everyday","family","comfort","budget","children"],"ingredients":"60g white rice · 150g chicken pieces · 20g spring onion · 20g prawn crackers · 20g fried peanuts · 15ml soy sauce · 10g fresh ginger · 10g fried shallots · 10g celery · 4g salt · 2g ground white pepper · 700ml water","method":"Every other rice recipe in this app is an instruction in how to keep the grain intact. This one is the opposite, and that inversion is the whole technique: bubur is the only rice dish where you deliberately DESTROY the grain, and if you treat the rice with the care rice normally deserves you will end up with a bowl of whole grains sitting in cloudy water, which is not congee. The second thing worth knowing before you start is that one pot of water is going to do three separate jobs, and most recipes throw two of them away.\n\nRINSE THE RICE ONCE AND THEN STOP. Put the 60g of white rice in a bowl, cover it with cold water, swirl it with your hand for about ten seconds and drain it. That is all. ⛔ Do NOT rinse it until the water runs clear. Every rice instruction you have ever read tells you to, and every one of them is right about a different dish: rinsing washes off the loose surface starch, and loose surface starch is what makes grains stick together, so for steamed rice you want it gone. Here that starch is your thickener. You are trying to get as much of it as possible into the water, not out of it, and the one quick rinse is only there to take dust off. Rinsing this rice properly is the single most common reason a home congee comes out thin.\n\nPOACH THE CHICKEN FIRST, IN ALL 700ml OF WATER, AND NEVER LET IT BOIL. Bruise the 10g of fresh ginger flat under a knife, cut the 20g of spring onion into two — whites and greens — and put the whites in a pot with the ginger, the 150g of chicken pieces and the full 700ml of cold water. Bring it up slowly to a bare simmer, where the surface trembles and you get a bubble every second or two, and hold it there for 25 minutes with the lid ajar. ⛔ A rolling boil is the mistake here, and it does two things at once: it tightens and shreds the muscle fibres so the meat comes out stringy and dry, and it churns the proteins and fat into a permanent grey cloud instead of letting them rise as scum you can lift off. ✅ Skim whatever grey foam collects in the first ten minutes and the finished broth will be clean and golden.\n\nLIFT THE CHICKEN OUT, SHRED IT, AND PUT THE BONES BACK. Take the pieces out with a slotted spoon and let them sit until you can handle them. Pull the meat off in coarse shreds along the grain — coarse, not fine, because fine shreds disappear into the congee and you want something to find. Then drop the skin and bones straight back into the pot and let them go another 10 minutes while you get on. That is where the body of the broth is: collagen in the skin and around the joints turning into gelatin, which is the difference between chicken-flavoured water and something that coats a spoon.\n\nNOW THE THING NOBODY LABELS PROPERLY — THAT ONE POT OF WATER HAS THREE JOBS. Job one it has already done: it cooked the chicken. Job two: strain it, measure about 600ml back into the pot and cook the rice in it, because rice cooked in chicken broth for three-quarters of an hour tastes of chicken all the way through in a way that no amount of stirring shredded meat in at the end can imitate. Job three: keep the last 100ml or so aside in a small jug, season it, and keep it HOT. At the table that goes over the assembled bowl as a thin, clear, separate broth — the kuah — and in Jakarta that pour is not optional, it is what the dish is. A stall hands you a bowl of thick congee and then floods it. ⛔ Cook the rice in fresh water and you have thrown away two of the three jobs and made a much duller breakfast for exactly the same effort.\n\nSIMMER THE RICE 40 TO 50 MINUTES WITH THE LID OFF. Bring the 600ml of broth and the drained rice to a bare simmer, lid off or well ajar so it reduces, and leave it largely alone for the first 25 minutes — just a scrape across the base every five minutes or so to make sure nothing is sticking.\n\nTHEN START STIRRING, AND UNDERSTAND WHAT STIRRING IS FOR, BECAUSE IT IS NOT ABOUT STICKING. Heat and water make the starch granules inside each grain swell up enormously — that is gelatinisation, and on its own it gives you soft rice in liquid. Turning that into congee needs a second, mechanical step: the swollen grains have to be physically RUPTURED so the long amylose molecules inside them spill out into the water, and it is dissolved amylose that thickens liquid. That is what the spoon is doing. Which is also why the timing is what it is — stirring in the first ten minutes accomplishes nothing at all, because the grains have not swollen yet and there is nothing inside them to release. From 25 minutes on, stir every couple of minutes. In the last 10 minutes stir often and press the grains against the side of the pot with the back of the spoon as you go. ✅ You are finished when you can no longer see an individual grain and the whole thing has the texture of very thick pouring cream.\n\nMAKE IT LOOSER THAN YOU WANT IT. THIS CATCHES EVERYONE ONCE. Congee thickens dramatically as it cools, and it keeps thickening — the amylose you just released starts re-associating into a firmer network the moment the temperature drops, which is the same process that makes day-old bread stale and cold rice hard. A bowl that looks perfect in the pot will be sliceable in ten minutes. So take it off the heat while it still slides off a tipped spoon in a slow ribbon. If it goes too far, a splash of the hot kuah stirred in fixes it instantly and you have some standing right there.\n\nSEASON AT THE END, WITH WHITE PEPPER. Off the heat, stir in the 4g of salt and the 2g of ground white pepper, and season the reserved kuah separately with a pinch of each. ⛔ Salt early and you cannot undo it: the pot loses roughly a third of its liquid over 50 minutes and takes all the salt with it, so what tasted right at the start is aggressive at the end. ✅ White rather than black pepper is not fussiness — white pepper is fermented and hulled before drying and gives a rounder, earthier, faintly musty heat with none of black pepper's sharp pine, and it is the flavour a bowl of bubur is supposed to have. It also disappears visually into a white bowl, which matters more than it should.\n\nASSEMBLE IN ORDER, BECAUSE THE TOPPINGS ARE HALF THE DISH AND NOT A GARNISH. Congee is deliberately bland — that is its function, the same way rice is — and everything that goes on top is either a texture or a salt. In this order: the congee into a wide bowl, then the shredded chicken in a heap on one side, the 15ml of soy sauce over the chicken rather than stirred through, the 10g of chopped celery and the spring onion greens scattered, the 20g of fried peanuts, then the 10g of fried shallots last of the soft things because they go limp fastest. ✅ Put the 20g of prawn crackers propped at the EDGE of the bowl, not on the surface — on the surface they are wet in thirty seconds; at the edge they stay crisp long enough to be worth having, and you break them in as you eat. Then pour the hot kuah over at the table, in front of whoever is eating it, and hand it over immediately.","cookTime":"Poach 35 min · Congee 50 min · Assemble 5 min","kcal":"~470 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 43g · Fat 17g · Sodium ~1900mg","storage":"The congee itself keeps 3 days covered in the fridge and it will set absolutely solid — firm enough to turn out of the tub in one piece, which is alarming the first time and completely normal. That is amylose re-associating as it cools, the same process that stales bread, and it reverses with heat and liquid: put it in a pan with a good splash of water or leftover broth, warm it gently and whisk or stir hard, and it comes back to a proper pouring texture in about four minutes. It always needs more liquid than you expect, so add it in stages. ⚠️ Store the congee and the toppings SEPARATELY and it is barely a compromise the next day; store them together and it is a sad grey bowl, because fried shallots, peanuts and crackers all exist for crunch and all of them are gone by morning. Keep the shredded chicken in a little of its own broth so it does not dry out. It freezes for 1 month and comes back rougher — slightly grainy rather than silky — so it is worth doing for a weekday breakfast and not worth doing for guests. ⛔ Do not leave it standing warm on the stove for hours: a thick starchy low-acid porridge at room temperature is one of the friendlier things you can offer a bacterium. Cool it and refrigerate it inside two hours, and reheat only what you are about to eat.","chefNotes":"Rinse the rice ONCE and stop — the surface starch every other rice recipe tells you to wash away is the only thickener this dish has. Poach the chicken in all 700ml at a bare simmer, never a boil, then use that same water three ways: it cooked the chicken, it cooks the rice, and the last 100ml is the hot kuah poured over at the table. Stir LATE, not early: the spoon's job is to physically rupture swollen grains so amylose spills out and thickens the liquid, and before 25 minutes there is nothing inside them to release. Take it off the heat looser than you want it, because it sets hard as it cools. Salt at the very end — the pot loses a third of its liquid. And prop the crackers at the edge of the bowl, never on the surface.","pairsWith":"This is a complete bowl and it does not want a plate of anything beside it, which makes it unusual in this section. What it wants instead is things to add: a spoonful of sambal on the rim, an extra ladle of broth, more fried shallots, more soy. A skewer of grilled chicken alongside is the one genuine addition, sold from the same carts and dipped into the congee between bites, and a sharp pickle on the side cuts the softness of the whole thing beautifully if you are eating it as lunch rather than breakfast. Sweet hot tea is the drink, and it is worth saying that this is breakfast food in Indonesia in the way that porridge is here — sold from a handcart at six in the morning, eaten standing up, and largely gone by nine. Late at night it is also the standard cure for having had too good an evening, which is a role it shares with congee everywhere it exists.","goesWith":["Sate Ayam","Sambal Terasi","Bawang Goreng","Acar Kuning"],"trivia":"The most useful thing to know about a chicken broth has nothing to do with technique. It is that the bird you can buy today is a fundamentally different animal from the one this recipe was designed around, and the difference is measured in weeks. A modern commercial broiler reaches slaughter weight — around 2kg — in roughly 35 days. In 1950 a chicken took something like three months to reach barely half that. Sixty years of breeding, and a much better understanding of poultry nutrition, has produced a bird that grows about four times faster than its great-grandparents, and that is a genuine achievement: chicken went from a Sunday luxury to the cheapest meat in the shop within a single lifetime, and a great many people eat protein because of it.\n\nBut broth is not made of meat. Broth is made of collagen — the connective tissue in skin, tendon, cartilage and around the joints — which dissolves into gelatin under long gentle heat and is the entire reason a good broth coats your lip and a bad one tastes like salty water. And collagen is built by TIME and by MOVEMENT. A bird that has existed for five weeks and barely walked has almost none of it. This is why your chicken stock can taste thin no matter how carefully you make it, and it is not your fault or your pot's. 🛒 The fix is on the shelf and it is cheap: in South Africa, look for a hard-body chicken, which is exactly what it sounds like — a mature, slower-grown bird, often a spent layer, sold specifically because it holds together through long cooking. It needs a good hour and a half rather than twenty-five minutes and the meat stays firmer, and the broth it gives you is a different substance entirely. Chicken frames and chicken feet do the same job for even less; feet in particular are almost pure collagen and will turn a pot to jelly in the fridge, which is precisely what you want under a bowl of congee. 🌱 And while you have the spring onions out for the topping: do not bin the white roots. Stand them in a glass of water on a sunny windowsill and they regrow within days, more or less indefinitely, which means the greens on top of tomorrow's bowl can be free.","howThisFeels":"Pressing the last whole grains against the side of the pot with the back of a spoon and watching the whole thing turn from soup into something that holds a ribbon.","leftovers":["(easy) Thinned with water and warmed gently, with a fresh scatter of shallots and spring onion — the crunch is the only thing that needs replacing.","(easy) Set congee cut into thick slabs straight from the tub, then shallow-fried in a little oil until crisp on both sides, which is a genuinely excellent thing and nobody expects it.","(medium) Loosened with broth and used as the base of a soup instead of the whole dish: it thickens a pot of vegetables beautifully and behaves like a roux you did not have to make.","(creative) Leftover kuah and congee whisked together over heat with a handful of grated cheese into an unlikely rice porridge that is somewhere between congee and risotto — not remotely authentic, very good, and the only way I know to use up 200ml of broth that is otherwise going in the bin."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bubur Jamur — Mushroom, No Chicken (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":23,"howThisFeels":"Mushrooms browned hard and dry until they smell like something roasted, going into a plain white bowl.","trivia":"A vegetarian bubur is not a modern accommodation, it is a fixture. Indonesia has a long-established Chinese-Indonesian Buddhist vegetarian tradition with its own restaurants, its own vocabulary and a genuine talent for making meatless versions of everything, and congee is where it is easiest — a bowl built on rice, broth and toppings is only as omnivorous as what you put on it. The interesting technical problem is not the protein but the BROTH, because a chicken broth's body comes from gelatin and there is no plant equivalent. So a vegan congee has to get its depth somewhere else: from mushrooms, which are loaded with free glutamates, and from browning them hard and dry before any liquid touches them. That browning is not optional the way it might be in a stew. Skip it and the bowl tastes of hot rice.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g chicken pieces"},{"item":"20g prawn crackers"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g mushrooms"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No chicken and no prawn crackers, and 80g of mushrooms instead — but the order changes, because there is no poaching stage to build the broth. Slice the mushrooms thick and dry-fry them in a wide pan over a HIGH heat with no oil and no salt at all, in a single layer, until they have given up their water, the pan is dry again and they have taken real brown colour and smell nutty. ⏱️ That is 8 to 10 minutes and it looks wrong for the first six. ⛔ Do not salt them early and do not crowd the pan — either one makes them release water and stew pale, and pale mushrooms have none of the savoury depth this bowl is relying on. Then add the bruised ginger, the spring onion whites and the 700ml of water to the same pan, scrape the base clean, and simmer 15 minutes. Strain, keep 100ml back for the kuah, and cook the rice in the rest exactly as described. Return half the mushrooms to the congee for the last 10 minutes and keep the other half back as the topping. ✅ Swap the crackers for a few extra fried peanuts, or use plain cassava crisps if you have them — you still need something that crunches, and that is the one job the crackers were doing."}]}},{"name":"Bubur Ayam Jakarta — Rinsed Once, Stirred Late","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":33,"howThisFeels":"A cart at six in the morning, a bowl too hot to hold properly, and clear broth going over the top of it in front of you.","trivia":"There is a quiet argument buried in this bowl about whether the congee should be stirred at all, and Indonesia takes a clear side. Broadly, the further north and east you go the more the porridge is left to break down on its own into something silky and uniform; the Jakarta version is stirred and pressed on purpose, and it is thicker and starchier than most of its cousins because of it. The tell is the kuah. If your congee is thick enough to stand a spoon in, you need a separate thin broth to pour over it, and if it is loose and soupy you do not — so a stall that hands you a jug is a stall that has committed to the thick version. The two decisions are the same decision, made twice. It also explains why a bubur bowl is always wide and shallow rather than deep: you are not eating a soup, you are eating a landscape with things arranged on it, and the broth arrives to flood the low ground.","delta":null},{"name":"Bubur Ayam Spesial — with Liver and Gizzard Skewers","icon":"🍢","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":37,"howThisFeels":"Two small skewers laid across a bowl of white porridge, and the smell of them arriving before the bowl does.","trivia":"Sate ati ampela — liver and gizzard skewers — is the topping that turns a bubur into a bubur spesial, and the reason it is liver specifically is economic before it is culinary. Offal is what a cart can afford to grill and still sell a bowl at breakfast prices, and a skewer of it delivers far more flavour per rand than the same money in muscle meat. The cooking problem is that liver is the least forgiving thing in the shop. It is about 70% water and has almost no connective tissue and almost no fat, so there is nothing in it to buffer heat — the window between pink-and-yielding and grey-and-chalky is genuinely about thirty seconds wide, and once it has closed nothing reopens it. That is the entire skill. Most people who think they dislike liver have only ever had it a minute past the point, and a piece taken off the heat while it is still faintly pink at the centre is a completely different food.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g chicken livers"},{"item":"4 bamboo skewers"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Two small skewers to lay across the bowl. Trim the 60g of chicken livers of any green-tinged patches or stringy connective bits — a green patch is bile and it is bitter enough to ruin the whole skewer. Soak them in cold water for 10 minutes, then pat them very dry: wet liver steams instead of colouring. Cut them into 2cm pieces, thread them onto the 4 bamboo skewers and brush them with a little of the soy sauce. ⚠️ Soak the bare ends of the skewers in water for 10 minutes first or they char through. Grill or dry-pan them over a HIGH heat for 60 to 90 seconds a side, no more, and take them off while the centre is still faintly pink. ⛔ There is no margin here at all: liver is about 70% water with no fat and no connective tissue to buffer the heat, so the step from tender to chalky is roughly thirty seconds and it does not reverse. ✅ Test one by pressing it — it should feel like the pad of your thumb, springy with a little give, not firm. Lay the skewers across the assembled bowl at the very end so they stay hot, and pour the kuah under them rather than over them."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Bawang Goreng","target":"indonesia-bawang-goreng","emoji":"🧅"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-soto-betawi","name":"Soto Betawi","nameAlt":"Jakarta beef soup with coconut milk and dairy","aliases":["Soto Betawi","Sroto Betawi","Jakarta Beef Soup","Betawi Soto","Coconut Beef Soup","Soto Betawi Susu","Soto Betawi Jeroan"],"course":"main","type":["main","soup","beef","stew","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["special","family","weekend","sunday","comfort","guests"],"ingredients":"150g beef brisket · 120ml coconut milk · 80g potatoes · 60g tripe · 60ml full-cream milk · 30ml sunflower oil · 20g shallots · 20g tomatoes · 20g spring onion · 10g macadamia nuts · 10g garlic · 10g fresh ginger · 10g lemongrass · 10g fried shallots · 10g lime · 6g coriander seeds · 6g galangal · 4g cumin · 4g salt · 2g cloves · 2g cinnamon · 2g ground nutmeg · 2g white pepper · 600ml water","method":"This soup has TWO fats in it and they are not interchangeable, they are not friends, and the entire recipe is an arrangement designed to keep them both intact. Coconut milk and cow's milk in one bowl is the thing that makes soto betawi itself, and it is also the thing that makes it go wrong: the two fall apart under completely different conditions, at completely different points, and almost every failed version of this dish is somebody having treated them as one ingredient. Get the ORDER right and nothing else about it is difficult.\n\nSO HERE IS THE SHAPE OF IT BEFORE YOU START. The meat is simmered in plain water for two hours and neither milk goes anywhere near the pot. Then the water becomes broth, the aromatic paste is fried in oil, the coconut milk joins at a gentle heat for the last fifteen minutes, and the dairy goes in OFF THE HEAT at the very end. The lime never enters the pot at all. Four separate stages, and once you know why each one exists you will never make it in the wrong order again.\n\nSIMMER THE MEAT IN WATER, AND SKIM IT IN THE FIRST FIFTEEN MINUTES. Put the 150g of beef brisket and the 60g of tripe — rinse the tripe well first under cold running water — into a pot with the 600ml of water, and bring it up SLOWLY to a bare simmer. For the first fifteen minutes a grey-brown foam will rise. Lift it off with a spoon and discard it, and keep doing it until it stops coming. ✅ That scum is coagulated protein and fine particles, and if you let a boil churn it back into the liquid it stays there permanently as a dull cloud with a faintly metallic edge. In most soups you would get away with it. Here you cannot, because a finished soto betawi is meant to be pale ivory — the colour of thin cream — and a cloudy broth under coconut milk does not read as creamy, it reads as dirty. Then hold it at a bare simmer, lid ajar, for 2 hours until the brisket gives easily under a fork and the tripe has lost its squeak entirely.\n\n⛔ AND DO NOT BOIL IT HARD AT ANY POINT. A rolling boil on brisket does the same thing it does to chicken: it tightens the muscle fibres so the meat shreds dry rather than sliced soft, and it emulsifies the fat into the liquid instead of letting it float where you can spoon it off or leave it. Bare simmer, two hours, patience. This part is entirely unskilled and entirely unhurried.\n\nWHILE THAT GOES, MAKE THE PASTE AND FRY IT PROPERLY IN OIL. Grind the 20g of shallots, 10g of garlic, 10g of fresh ginger, 10g of macadamia nuts, 6g of coriander seeds, 4g of cumin, 2g of cloves, 2g of cinnamon, 2g of ground nutmeg and 2g of white pepper to a paste with a splash of water. Bruise the 10g of lemongrass and the 6g of galangal flat under a knife but leave them whole. Heat the 30ml of sunflower oil in a wide pan over medium, add the paste with the bruised aromatics, and fry it, stirring, for 5 to 6 minutes until it darkens a shade, stops smelling raw and green, and the oil around the edge goes glossy.\n\nTHIS FRYING STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL AND THE REASON IS ABOUT SOLUBILITY. Almost everything that makes coriander seed, cumin, clove, cinnamon and nutmeg taste like themselves is fat-soluble, not water-soluble. Drop that paste straight into liquid and those compounds stay locked inside the ground particles — you get a soup that tastes raw and grassy, with a faint grittiness, and no amount of extra simmering fixes it because there is no fat for the flavour to move into. Five minutes in hot oil moves it all out into the oil, and the oil then goes into the soup. ⛔ Do not be tempted to fry the paste in the coconut milk instead. Coconut milk is an emulsion and frying is exactly what breaks it, and you would spend the fat you were trying to use.\n\nCOMBINE, THEN THE COCONUT MILK — GENTLY, AND FOR ONLY FIFTEEN MINUTES. Lift the meat out, slice the brisket across the grain into thick pieces and cut the tripe into short ribbons, then strain the broth into the paste pan and scrape the base clean. Add the 120ml of coconut milk and the 4g of salt, return the meat, and hold it at a bare quiver — steam, an occasional bubble, never a boil — for 15 minutes, stirring every couple of minutes. ⚠️ Coconut milk is fat suspended in water and held there by its own proteins, and a hard boil shears that emulsion apart: you get an oily slick on top and a thin watery layer beneath, and once split it does not come back. Stirring keeps it moving and gentle heat keeps it whole. ✅ Fifteen minutes is plenty — this stage is for the coconut to marry the broth, not to cook anything.\n\nNOW TAKE IT OFF THE HEAT COMPLETELY BEFORE THE DAIRY GOES IN. Wait a full minute so the surface stops steaming hard, then stir in the 60ml of full-cream milk. That is the last thing that happens in the pot.\n\nAND THIS IS THE ONE INSTRUCTION PEOPLE BREAK. Cow's milk behaves nothing like coconut milk. Its protein is casein, carried in tiny clusters that repel one another and therefore stay suspended — and what makes them stop repelling one another is ACID, not heat. Add milk to a hot pot that has tomato and lime in it and the casein clusters lose their charge, clump, and you get a soup with white grains through it and a slightly grainy feel on the tongue. Nothing reverses that. So: milk last, off the heat, into a pot that is hot but no longer boiling, and — this matters just as much — ⛔ THE LIME NEVER GOES IN THE POT. It goes into the individual bowl, squeezed at the table, where it can be as sharp as it likes over milk that is already sitting in a bowl and no longer being heated. Same for the tomato: it goes in raw, at the end, in the bowl.\n\nFRY THE POTATOES SEPARATELY AND ADD THEM AT THE TABLE. Cut the 80g of potatoes into 1.5cm cubes and shallow-fry them in a little oil over medium-high until deep golden and crisp at the corners, 8 to 10 minutes, then salt them lightly and keep them aside. ⛔ They must never be boiled in the soup. A potato simmering in broth sheds starch, which clouds the liquid and slightly thickens it into something gluey, and it collapses at the edges. Fried and added at the last second they keep a crisp outside and a floury middle, and that contrast against a soft creamy soup is half of why the dish is memorable. A stall keeps them in a separate tray for exactly this reason.\n\nBUILD THE BOWL, AND BUILD IT IN THIS ORDER. Sliced brisket and tripe into a wide bowl, the hot soup ladled over, then the fried potatoes, the 20g of tomatoes cut into raw wedges, the 20g of spring onion sliced, the 10g of fried shallots scattered on top, and the 10g of lime as a wedge on the rim for whoever is eating it to squeeze themselves. ✅ Eat it immediately, with rice on the side rather than in the bowl. It is very rich and the portion should be smaller than you think — a wide shallow bowl half-full is right, and a second helping is better than one large one going cold.","cookTime":"Simmer 2 hr · Paste 6 min · Coconut 15 min · Potatoes 10 min","kcal":"~620 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 36g · Carbs 22g · Fat 42g · Sodium ~1750mg","storage":"It keeps 3 days in the fridge and it is honestly better on day two, because the spice paste goes on dissolving into the fat overnight and the whole thing rounds out. ⚠️ BUT REHEAT IT GENTLY AND NEVER TO A BOIL — this is the one thing that undoes a good pot the next day. Both fats in it are fragile in different ways, the coconut emulsion splits under a hard boil and the casein curdles once acid and heat are both present, and a microwave delivers exactly the fierce uneven heat that does both at once. Use a pan over medium-low, stir it, and take it off when it steams. ✅ Store the potatoes, the raw tomato, the spring onion, the fried shallots and the lime SEPARATELY and every one of them survives; put them in the pot and by morning the potato has dissolved into starch, the shallots are wet paper and the lime has curdled your milk while you slept. The fat will set into a firm pale cap on top in the fridge, which is normal and is worth stirring back in rather than removing — it is where most of the spice flavour has ended up. ⛔ It does not freeze well. Frozen coconut milk separates on thawing and dairy goes grainy, so you would be thawing two broken emulsions at once. If you want to freeze something, freeze the strained meat and broth BEFORE either milk goes in — that freezes perfectly for 3 months, and finishing it later takes twenty minutes.","chefNotes":"Two fats, two completely different failure modes, and the order is the whole recipe. Simmer the meat in plain WATER for two hours, skimming hard in the first fifteen minutes — a cloudy broth under coconut milk reads as dirty, not creamy. Fry the spice paste in oil for five minutes, because almost everything that makes coriander, cumin, clove and nutmeg taste like themselves is fat-soluble and will never leave the powder in a watery pot. Coconut milk joins for the last fifteen minutes at a bare quiver, never a boil, or the emulsion shears apart for good. Then take the pot OFF the heat before the dairy goes in: casein curdles from acid more than from heat. And the lime never enters the pot — it goes on the rim of the bowl. Fry the potatoes separately and add them at the last second.","pairsWith":"Plain white rice on the side rather than in the bowl, and that distinction is worth keeping — this is a soup you eat with rice, not a soup poured over rice, and drowning the rice loses the contrast between the crisp potato and the creamy broth. A sharp pickle is close to compulsory beside it because the soup is genuinely fatty and something acidic on the same plate resets your mouth every few spoons. Sambal goes on the rim, not stirred in, so each person sets their own heat. A pile of fried shallots on the table for people to keep adding is how it is actually served. ⛔ What it does not want is another coconut dish beside it, or anything else rich — one bowl of this is already a substantial meal and a second creamy thing on the plate makes both of them feel heavy. Keep everything else around it sharp, plain or crisp.","goesWith":["Acar Kuning","Sambal Terasi","Bawang Goreng","Nasi Uduk"],"trivia":"There is something quietly strange about Jakarta's signature soup containing cow's milk, and it takes a detour through human genetics to see why. Most adults on earth do not comfortably digest lactose. We are all born making lactase, the enzyme that splits milk sugar, because we are all born on milk — but in most of the world that production winds down after weaning, which is the ordinary mammalian pattern. The ability to keep making it into adulthood, lactase persistence, is the ODDITY: a set of relatively recent mutations that spread through populations with long dairying histories in northern Europe, parts of the Middle East and parts of East Africa. Something like two-thirds of the world's adults have reduced lactase activity, and East and Southeast Asia are at the far end of that range — Indonesia among the highest. So a milk soup should be the last dish you would expect to find as the pride of a city of ten million people there.\n\nTwo things resolve it, and the second one is genuinely useful in a kitchen. The first is that dairy arrived in Southeast Asia in a TIN. Evaporated and condensed milk keep for years without refrigeration, travelled well, and were cheap and available long before fresh milk was any of those things, which is why so much of the region's dairy habit — in coffee, in tea, in desserts, in this soup — is tinned rather than fresh, and why a stall version of soto betawi is far more likely to use evaporated milk than a bottle from a fridge. The second is that lactose intolerance is a matter of DOSE, not of a switch. Most people who malabsorb lactose handle around 12 grams of it — roughly a cup of milk — with little or no trouble, and considerably more when it arrives with fat and protein and other food rather than on an empty stomach. Sixty millilitres of milk spread through a bowl of rich beef soup is a fraction of that. It is a rounding error, and it is why a dish that ought to be impossible is instead beloved.\n\nWhich means the coconut-only version further down this card is not a diet substitution or a compromise for anybody. It is the older way of building the soup, it is still how much of Indonesia makes it, and it is arguably the better bowl. The dairy is the newcomer. 🌱 And on the subject of things worth having to hand: the raw tomato wedges that go in at the end are the one component here that a supermarket does badly and a pot on a sunny stoep does brilliantly — a single plant will out-flavour anything you can buy, and a tomato with actual acidity in it is doing real work in a bowl this rich.","howThisFeels":"Stirring cold milk into a hot ivory pot that you have just taken off the flame, and holding your breath for a second in case it curdles — and it doesn't.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated gently in a pan with a fresh batch of fried potatoes and new shallots on top — the two things that never survive the night are the two things that take ten minutes to replace.","(easy) The meat lifted out and eaten cold with rice and sambal while you decide what to do with the broth, which is a perfectly respectable lunch and needs no cooking at all.","(medium) Strained broth used to cook rice instead of water, which gives you an outrageously good coconut-spiced rice for the price of nothing; keep the meat back and pile it on top.","(creative) Reduce the leftover soup hard by half in a wide pan and it stops being a soup and becomes a thick spiced gravy — spoon that over boiled potatoes or greens and nobody will identify what it used to be."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Soto Betawi Santan — Coconut Only, Cheap Cuts (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":64,"howThisFeels":"One fat instead of two, and a pot you can stop worrying about the moment it goes on the heat.","trivia":"This is the older soup and the one with fewer ways to fail. Before tinned milk was on every shelf, soto betawi was made with coconut milk alone, and a great deal of Indonesia still makes it that way by preference rather than by economy — the argument being that the dairy blurs the spices while coconut carries them. It is also the version to cook the first time you make this dish, for a reason that has nothing to do with authenticity: with the dairy gone, the pot has exactly one fragile thing in it instead of two, and the only rule left is not to boil it. You can put the lime and the tomato wherever you like. Nothing will curdle. Cook it this way once, taste it, then cook the dairy version and decide for yourself which you prefer — and be prepared for the answer to be this one.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef brisket","to":"120g beef stew pieces"},{"from":"120ml coconut milk","to":"150ml coconut milk"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"60g tripe"},{"item":"60ml full-cream milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No dairy and no tripe, and 120g of beef stew pieces in place of the brisket — cheap offcuts with fat and sinew running through them, which is what most households would have used anyway. ✅ Two things change for the better. Give the meat LONGER, 2½ hours rather than 2, because a mixed cut has more connective tissue to convert and it all wants converting. And since there is no casein in the pot, the acid rules relax completely: you may add the tomato to the pot for the last 5 minutes if you prefer it softened, and a squeeze of lime into the pot is now perfectly safe. Increase the coconut milk to 150ml to make up the body the dairy was providing, and keep the same rule that governs it — a bare quiver for the last 15 minutes, stirred, never a boil. ⚠️ With one fat carrying everything, the seasoning has to be checked more carefully at the end; taste for salt AFTER the coconut milk goes in, never before, because coconut milk mutes salt noticeably."}]}},{"name":"Soto Betawi — Coconut Early, Dairy Last","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":78,"howThisFeels":"A wide shallow bowl the colour of thin cream, with crisp potato corners still standing proud of it.","trivia":"The fried potato is the component people underestimate, and it is the reason the bowl is wide and shallow rather than deep. Every other element in this soup is soft — soft meat, soft tripe, a smooth creamy liquid — and a dish made entirely of one texture stops registering after about six spoonfuls, no matter how good it tastes. That is not preference, it is how palates work: contrast is what keeps attention. So the potato is fried hard and added at the last possible second purely to be the one thing that resists, and the raw tomato and the raw spring onion are there for the same structural reason. It also explains the shallow bowl. In a deep bowl the potatoes sink and go soft in thirty seconds, and you have lost the only crunch in the dish; in a wide one they sit half-proud of the surface and stay crisp long enough to matter. A serving vessel chosen to protect a texture is a small thing, and it is the difference between the bowl a stall hands you and the one you make at home.","delta":null},{"name":"Soto Betawi Jeroan — Tripe and Heart, with Tinned Milk","icon":"🫀","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":92,"howThisFeels":"Three different textures of the same animal in one bowl, and the heart being the surprise — firm, lean and clean, nothing like you expected.","trivia":"Jeroan means offal, and a soto betawi jeroan is the version a Jakarta stall sells to people who came for the offal rather than in spite of it. Heart is the one to know about, because it is the most misunderstood organ in the shop. It is not offal in the way liver or kidney are — it has no filtering function at all, it is simply a muscle that has worked continuously for the animal's entire life, and it tastes like exactly that: dense, lean, clean and beefy, with none of the mineral bitterness people brace for. What it does have is an enormous amount of myoglobin, the oxygen-storing protein that makes hard-working muscle dark, which is why it looks alarming and why it stays a deep red-brown however long you cook it. And because it is worked muscle with very little fat, it takes gentle heat and time the same way brisket does. Cooked briefly it is rubber; cooked for two hours in a quiet pot it is tender, sliceable and among the cheapest good beef you will ever buy. Swapping fresh milk for evaporated at the same time is not a coincidence — this is the stall version throughout, and the stall uses the tin.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g beef heart"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"60ml full-cream milk","to":"60ml evaporated milk"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add 80g of beef heart and swap the fresh milk for 60ml of evaporated milk. Prepare the heart properly first, because this is where people go wrong with it: trim away the tough outer membrane, any hard white sinew and the pale fatty valve tissue at the top, then rinse it well and cut it into 2cm slices. ⏱️ It goes into the pot at the START, alongside the brisket and tripe, and gets the full 2 hours — it is worked muscle with almost no fat and it needs every minute; ten minutes in a pan gives you rubber, two hours gives you something tender and sliceable. ✅ The evaporated milk is easier to handle than fresh, not harder: it has already been heat-treated, which makes its casein noticeably more stable, so it is markedly less inclined to curdle. Add it off the heat all the same — the habit is worth keeping — and be aware it is roughly twice as concentrated as fresh milk, so 60ml of it makes a distinctly richer and slightly sweeter bowl. ⚠️ Check the tin: evaporated milk is unsweetened and condensed milk is not, and condensed milk in this soup would be a disaster you would not recover."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Acar Kuning","target":"indonesia-acar-kuning","emoji":"🥒"},{"name":"Bawang Goreng","target":"indonesia-bawang-goreng","emoji":"🧅"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ayam-pop","name":"Ayam Pop","nameAlt":"Padang chicken fried pale on purpose","aliases":["Ayam Pop","Ayam Pop Padang","Padang White Chicken","Pale Fried Chicken","Ayam Pop Minang","Coconut Water Chicken"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","one-pot","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","special","guests","weekend","everyday"],"ingredients":"200g chicken pieces · 300ml coconut water · 100ml water · 30ml sunflower oil · 20g garlic · 10g shallots · 10g lime · 4g coriander seeds · 4g salt · 2g white pepper","method":"Every other fried thing in this section is an instruction in how to get colour. This one is an instruction in how to avoid it, and that is not a quirk — it is the entire dish. Ayam pop comes out of the fryer pale, matte and almost white, sitting on a plate next to a sambal so red it looks like a warning, and the contrast is deliberate. It is also the hardest instruction in this section to actually obey, because everything you have ever learned about frying will be shouting at you to leave it in for another minute.\n\nSO UNDERSTAND THE MECHANISM FIRST, BECAUSE THE WHOLE METHOD IS BUILT ON IT. Browning is the Maillard reaction — amino acids and sugars rearranging into hundreds of new brown, savoury compounds — and it needs three conditions at once: a surface that is DRY, a temperature well above about 140°C, and TIME. A piece of chicken lifted straight out of a braise has none of the three. It is saturated, and every bit of energy the hot oil delivers goes into boiling that water off rather than raising the temperature, so the surface sits pinned at around 100°C and physically cannot climb until it is dry. ✅ That is the loophole this dish lives in. The bird goes into hot oil while still wet, and comes out before the surface has dried — 30 to 45 seconds — so it firms and sets without ever reaching browning temperature. There is no skill in it. There is only nerve, and a clock.\n\nTAKE THE SKIN OFF, AND THIS IS ONE OF THE FEW TIMES THAT MAKES A CHICKEN DISH BETTER. Pull the skin off the 200g of chicken pieces and discard it or save it for something else. Two reasons, and both are structural rather than fussy. Skin is the part that browns first and fastest — it is fat and collagen and it dries ahead of the meat — so leaving it on makes the pale finish nearly impossible. And skin that has been braised for half an hour and then fried for forty seconds is genuinely unpleasant: too wet to crisp, too cooked to be soft, a flabby grey layer between you and the chicken. You would be keeping the worst possible version of it. Off it comes.\n\nBRAISE IT IN COCONUT WATER, AND CHECK THE CARTON BEFORE YOU BUY IT. Put the skinned chicken in a pot with the 300ml of coconut water, the 100ml of water, the 20g of garlic and the 10g of shallots both crushed flat, the 4g of coriander seeds lightly crushed, the 4g of salt and the 2g of white pepper. If you can get lime leaves, tear two and drop them in; they are worth having and they are not worth building the recipe around. Bring it to a bare simmer, lid on, and cook it for 25 to 30 minutes, turning the pieces once halfway, until the meat is fully cooked through and pulls slightly from the bone. ⛔ THE CARTON MATTERS AND THIS IS THE TRAP: most coconut water on a shelf here has added sugar, or is blended with fruit juice. Use it and you will coat the chicken in a thin sugar solution, and sugar in hot oil browns in about eight seconds no matter how wet the surface is. Buy plain, unsweetened coconut water and read the panel. If you cannot find it, the budget version below uses plain water and is honest about it.\n\nNOTICE WHAT IS NOT IN THAT POT. No kecap manis, no soy, no turmeric, no palm sugar, no chilli, no tamarind, nothing dark and nothing sweet. Almost every other Indonesian chicken braise has at least one of those and gets its character from them. This one is deliberately colourless, and it is also deliberately gentle: the flavour is garlic, coriander seed, white pepper and the faint sweetness of coconut water, and that is all. If it tastes underpowered to you at this stage, resist the urge to fix it. The bird is meant to be the quiet thing on the plate.\n\nLIFT IT OUT AND DO NOT DRY IT. Take the pieces out and let them drain in a colander for two minutes. Blot the loose drips off with paper — that is a safety measure, not a drying step — but ⛔ do NOT pat them dry, do not leave them uncovered on a rack, and do not let them sit for half an hour. You want the surface still damp when it hits the oil, because that dampness is the only thing standing between you and a brown chicken. ⚠️ A wet thing going into hot oil spits hard. Use a deep pan with the oil no more than 2cm up the side, keep a lid within reach, lower the pieces away from yourself with tongs rather than dropping them, and keep children out of the kitchen for this one minute.\n\nFRY 30 TO 45 SECONDS, AND WATCH THE CLOCK, NOT THE COLOUR. Heat the 30ml of sunflower oil in a small deep pan until a crumb dropped in fizzes vigorously — about 170 to 180°C. Slide in one or two pieces at a time, count, and take them out. ✅ The signals you are looking for are not visual in the way you expect: the surface goes from floppy and slightly translucent to firm and matte white, and the bubbling around each piece stays LOUD and vigorous the whole time. The moment that bubbling begins to slacken, the surface water has gone, the temperature is about to climb past 140°C, and browning starts within seconds. That slackening is your out-now signal, and if you are still deciding when you hear it you have already left it too long. ⛔ Do not crowd the pan — three pieces at once drops the oil temperature, extends the time and defeats the whole thing.\n\nREDUCE THE BRAISING LIQUID WHILE THE OIL HEATS. Strain the pot and boil the liquid hard, uncovered, down to about 60ml — six or seven minutes. It goes slightly syrupy and turns into a pale, savoury, faintly sweet sauce that tastes strongly of garlic and coriander seed. Spoon it over the chicken at the end. Throwing that liquid away is the most common waste in this recipe and it is where a third of the flavour lives.\n\nA WORD ABOUT DONENESS, BECAUSE THE COLOUR WILL WORRY SOMEBODY AT YOUR TABLE. This chicken is fully cooked. It was cooked in the braise; the fry contributes nothing at all to doneness and is purely about texture and surface. ✅ If pale chicken makes you uneasy — and it makes most people uneasy the first time, which is exactly what the Did You Know below is about — check it with a thermometer at the thickest part BEFORE it goes into the oil, not after: 74°C and you are done, and forty seconds in hot fat will not change that number. Judging this dish by eye is the one thing that does not work.\n\nSERVE IT WITH ALL THE COLOUR SOMEWHERE ELSE. A pale piece of chicken, the reduced pale sauce spooned over, a mound of plain white rice, a wedge of the 10g of lime, and then a fierce sambal in its own little heap on the side — red or green, but bright. Padang plates put every scrap of visual intensity beside the bird rather than on it, and that is not an accident of style. It is the reason the dish works.","cookTime":"Braise 30 min · Reduce 7 min · Fry 45 sec","kcal":"~340 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 6g · Fat 18g · Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"The braised chicken keeps 3 days in the fridge and it keeps beautifully, because it is sitting in a mildly salty, mildly acidic liquid that is doing exactly what a light brine does. ✅ THE RIGHT WAY TO STORE IT IS BRAISED BUT NOT FRIED: keep the pieces submerged in the strained braising liquid in a sealed tub, and do the 45-second fry on the day you eat it. That way the surface is still wet, the technique still works and the chicken has actually improved overnight. ⛔ Store it already fried and you have the opposite problem — the surface dries in the fridge, and a dried surface is precisely the thing that browns, so reheating it in a pan turns it into an ordinary piece of fried chicken. Perfectly nice. Not this dish. If it is already fried, reheat it covered in a low oven with a spoonful of the sauce over it rather than in a pan. The reduced sauce keeps 5 days on its own and sets to a light jelly in the fridge, which is dissolved collagen and is a good sign. Everything here freezes well for 3 months — freeze the braised chicken IN its liquid, thaw it in the fridge overnight, then fry. ⚠️ Never leave cooked chicken standing warm; cool it and refrigerate within two hours.","chefNotes":"Fry it and do not let it colour — that is the whole dish, and it works because Maillard browning needs a DRY surface. A piece straight out of a braise is saturated, so the oil's energy goes into boiling that water off and the surface stays pinned at about 100°C. Get it in and out in 30 to 45 seconds, before it dries. Watch the clock, not the colour, and listen: while the bubbling is loud you are safe, and the moment it slackens the water has gone and browning is seconds away. Take the skin off — it browns first and it is horrible braised-then-flash-fried. Buy UNSWEETENED coconut water and read the panel, because sugar browns in eight seconds however wet the surface is. And reduce the braising liquid to 60ml rather than binning it; a third of the flavour is in there.","pairsWith":"It is built to sit on a Padang plate, which means it is built to be the calm thing among loud ones. Plain white rice, a fierce sambal in its own heap on the side, and something sharp and crunching — a pickle, a raw cucumber, a squeeze of lime. Boiled cassava leaves are the classic green beside it. The rule worth keeping is that the chicken never gets sauced with anything dark or strong: everything else on the plate is intense and this is the piece you go back to between mouthfuls to reset. ⛔ It is the wrong dish to serve on its own with nothing beside it, because on its own it reads as plain — it needs the contrast to make sense, in the same way a slice of good bread needs something to be next to. Serve it with a rich, dark, chilli-heavy dish and it becomes the most-eaten thing on the table.","goesWith":["Sambal Terasi","Rendang","Acar Kuning","Nasi Uduk"],"trivia":"The first time you serve this, somebody will quietly wonder whether it is cooked. It is — but the doubt is not silliness, it is a genuine and very well-studied feature of how human beings taste, and this dish is one of the few in the world built deliberately to walk into it.\n\nWe do not taste with our mouths alone. Colour arrives before flavour does, sets an expectation, and then measurably changes what the mouth reports back. The most famous demonstration is a small, slightly wicked experiment run at the University of Bordeaux in 2001: fifty-four oenology students were given a white wine, then the same white wine dyed red with an odourless, tasteless colourant, and asked to describe both. Given the red glass, they overwhelmingly reached for red-wine vocabulary — the dark-fruit and spice descriptors — for a wine they had themselves described in white-wine terms minutes earlier. These were not casual drinkers; they were people training professionally to taste. The colour simply overrode them. The same effect runs everywhere it has been looked for: dye a lime drink orange and people identify it as orange, and food that looks pale is consistently rated as blander and less intensely flavoured than identical food that looks browned.\n\nWhich makes ayam pop quietly radical, because it is a dish that gives up that entire advantage on purpose. Brown crust is the single most reliable visual promise of savoury flavour a cook can make, and this recipe refuses to make it. What Padang cooking does instead is put every scrap of colour somewhere else on the plate — a red sambal, a green chilli relish, a dark curry alongside — and lets the pale bird be the resting place between them. The intensity is not missing, it has been relocated, and the plate as a whole is arranged for your eyes as carefully as for your mouth. It is a much more sophisticated bit of design than a piece of white chicken has any right to be, and it is worth explaining to whoever is looking doubtfully at their plate. 🌱 The one thing on that plate you can grow yourself is the chilli in the sambal: a chilli plant is a perennial in a Pretoria garden, crops for years off a single plant, and is happiest in a pot in full sun with a bit of neglect.","howThisFeels":"Counting to forty out loud over a pan of hot oil, taking out a piece of chicken that is still stubbornly white, and having to trust yourself.","leftovers":["(easy) Shredded cold into a bowl of rice with a spoonful of the reduced sauce and a lot of sambal — the chicken is mild enough to take any amount of heat you throw at it.","(easy) The reduced braising liquid poured over hot rice on its own, which is a small perfect thing and the reason you never throw it away.","(medium) Torn into a clear soup with the leftover braising liquid, some greens and a squeeze of lime — because the meat was poached rather than roasted it slips straight back into a broth without tasting reheated.","(creative) Shredded and folded into a cold salad with green mango, shallot and lime — pale mild poached chicken is a far better base for a sharp raw salad than anything browned, and this is the one leftover use where the missing crust is an advantage."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Ayam Pop Air Biasa — Plain Water Braise (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The same white chicken out of the same pan, and no one at the table can tell which pot it came from.","trivia":"Coconut water is close to half the cost of this dish and it is the first thing to go when it is not in the house, which is worth being honest about rather than precious. What it actually contributes is a faint sweetness and a very slight acidity — coconut water sits around pH 5, mildly sour — and the acidity does a small amount of tenderising at the surface over half an hour. It is a real contribution and it is a subtle one. What it is NOT is the reason the chicken comes out pale; that is entirely the wet-surface trick in the fry, and plain water delivers it identically. There is one genuine advantage to the water version too, and it is not nothing: with no sugar in the pot at all, there is nothing that can deposit on the surface and catch, so the fry becomes noticeably more forgiving. If you are making this for the first time, or making a lot of it at once, this is the safer pot.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml water","to":"400ml water"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"300ml coconut water"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No coconut water — 400ml of plain water instead, and everything else identical. ✅ Two small compensations, both worth making. Add the pared zest of the lime to the braise along with the garlic and coriander (keep the juice for the table), because the coconut water was contributing a faint fruity acidity and a strip of zest replaces it honestly. And add 3g of sugar — no more — dissolved in the braise, which restores the trace of sweetness. ⚠️ Three grams is safe; a spoonful is not, because sugar on the surface browns in seconds in hot oil however wet that surface is, and the entire point of this dish would be lost in the last forty seconds of cooking it. If that makes you nervous, leave the sugar out altogether. The dish is still very good and it is still the right colour, which matters more."}]}},{"name":"Ayam Pop — Coconut Water Braise, Forty-Second Fry","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":56,"howThisFeels":"A matte white piece of chicken next to a heap of scarlet sambal, and the plate looking like a photograph before you touch it.","trivia":"Coconut water is one of the odder things you can cook in, and the reason it turns up in Sumatran kitchens is simply that it was free. It is the liquid inside a young green coconut, and any household opening coconuts for their flesh had litres of it and no refrigeration, so it went into the pot. What is genuinely surprising is what it is: sterile inside an unopened nut, and close enough in mineral balance to blood plasma that it has been used as an emergency intravenous fluid in the field, in isolated documented cases where nothing else was available. That is not a party trick to attempt and it is not a health claim — it is a coincidence of chemistry, and a fairly startling one for something that arrives in a carton. In the pot it does much less dramatic work: a whisper of sweetness, a slight acidity that tenderises the surface a little, and a clean background that adds no colour at all, which is the one thing this recipe cannot do without.","delta":null},{"name":"Ayam Pop with Sambal Lado Mudo — Green Chilli Relish","icon":"🌶️","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":61,"howThisFeels":"A heap of rough green sambal beside a white bird, still warm, smelling of chilli that has been cooked rather than fried.","trivia":"Sambal lado mudo is the sambal that is supposed to be beside this chicken, and it does its own thing backwards. Sambal Terasi's card sets out the mortar laws in full and they hold here too — but this one is COOKED BEFORE it is pounded, not after, which is the opposite order and gives a completely different result. Boiling or steaming the green chillies, shallots and tomatoes first breaks their cell walls down and softens the raw, grassy, sinus-clearing edge that fresh chilli has, so what you end up with is round and hot rather than sharp and hot, and it stays a proper bright green instead of going dull. Pounding cooked vegetables is also physically easier and much coarser — nothing shreds, everything just gives — which is why a good lado mudo is a rough heap with recognisable pieces in it rather than a smooth paste. Green chillies rather than red is not a milder choice either, it is an unripe one: a green chilli is the same fruit picked early, so it is more vegetal and more bitter and often just as hot. 🌱 And it is the easiest thing on this plate to grow — a chilli plant is a perennial in a Pretoria garden and will crop for years from one pot in full sun.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"40g green chilli"},{"item":"20g tomatoes"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"10g shallots","to":"30g shallots"},{"from":"30ml sunflower oil","to":"45ml sunflower oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the sambal while the chicken braises. Put the 40g of green chilli whole, the 20g of tomatoes and 20g of the shallots (keep 10g back for the braise) into a small pot with just enough water to cover, bring to the boil and cook for 5 minutes until everything has slumped and the chillies have gone from bright to olive-green and soft. Drain them well. ✅ Then pound them in a mortar — coarse, with pieces still visible, roughly ten to fifteen good strokes and no more — with 2g of salt. ⛔ Do not blend this. A blender emulsifies it into a smooth green purée and the whole character of lado mudo is that it is rough. Heat 15ml of the sunflower oil until it shimmers and pour it over the pounded sambal while it is still hot, then stir once and stop; the hot oil sets the colour and carries the heat, and stirring it in fully makes it greasy rather than glossy. ⚠️ Cooking chilli releases capsaicin into the steam, so open a window and do not stand over the pot with your face in it. Serve it in a separate heap beside the chicken, never spooned over — the whole point of the plate is that the colour and the calm stay apart."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥩"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Acar Kuning","target":"indonesia-acar-kuning","emoji":"🥒"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-tempe-mendoan","name":"Tempe Mendoan","nameAlt":"Banyumas tempeh fritters, deliberately half-fried","aliases":["Tempe Mendoan","Tempeh Mendoan","Mendoan","Banyumas Tempeh Fritters","Purwokerto Mendoan","Soft Tempeh Fritters","Tempe Kemul"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","snack","street-food","fried","vegan","quick","budget"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","street food","quick","budget","picnic"],"ingredients":"400ml sunflower oil · 120g tempeh · 40g rice flour · 20g spring onion · 20g kecap manis · 10g cornflour · 10g red chilli · 6g garlic · 4g coriander · 4g coriander seeds · 4g salt · 2g white pepper · 100ml water","method":"Mendoan is the only fritter I know of that is named after being underdone, and the name is not an apology. In the Banyumasan dialect of central Java, mendo means half-cooked or soft, and a mendoan pulled out of the oil looks — to every instinct you own — like something you took out too early. It is pale. The batter is floppy. It droops over your fingers when you pick it up. That is the finished dish, and the thing worth understanding is that it is only POSSIBLE because of what the batter is made of.\n\n⚖️ FIRST, A DISTINCTION, BECAUSE THE AYAM POP CARD ALSO ENDS UP PALE AND IT IS NOT THE SAME TRICK. There, the pale finish comes from a wet surface holding the temperature down — the browning never gets a chance to start. Here the surface is not especially wet and the oil is very hot indeed. What stops this from becoming an ordinary crisp fritter is the FLOUR, and the timing is arranged around the flour rather than around moisture. Two dishes, two pale results, two completely different mechanisms.\n\nRICE FLOUR IS WHAT MAKES UNDER-FRYING LEGAL, AND WHEAT FLOUR WOULD RUIN IT. The 40g of rice flour and 10g of cornflour in this batter contain no gluten at all. That matters more than anything else in the recipe. A wheat-flour batter builds a gluten network the moment it is mixed, and that network needs proper, complete cooking to stop being dough — pull a wheat fritter out early and what you are eating is raw batter, pasty and adhesive on the roof of the mouth, and no amount of calling it soft will fix it. Rice-flour starch behaves completely differently: it gelatinises and sets into a tender, cohesive, faintly slippery film very early in the process and simply STAYS that way, with nothing raw about it, for a good while before it eventually dries and turns brittle. ✅ So the soft stage is a real, edible, deliberate destination in a rice-flour batter, and it does not exist at all in a wheat one. If you only have plain flour, make the crisp version at the bottom of this card instead. Do not make this one badly.\n\nSLICE THE TEMPEH THIN AND WIDE, AND UNDERSTAND THAT THE THICKNESS IS CALCULATED BACKWARDS. Cut the 120g of tempeh into large flat slices about 4 to 5mm thick and as wide as the block allows — think of a playing card rather than a chip. ⛔ Do not cut it into cubes or fingers. Real mendoan tempeh is pressed specially for this, thinner and broader than ordinary tempeh, and the reason is a piece of arithmetic: the batter can only survive about 90 seconds in hot oil before it starts to crisp, so the tempeh has to be thin enough to be completely hot and cooked through in that same 90 seconds. The slice thickness is not a preference, it is derived from the batter's clock. A 15mm slab of tempeh in a 90-second fry gives you a cold raw centre in a cooked coat. Thin, wide and floppy is the whole geometry of the thing.\n\nMAKE THE BATTER THIN — POURING-CREAM THIN, NOT PANCAKE THIN. Crush the 6g of garlic, the 4g of coriander seeds, the 4g of salt and the 2g of white pepper to a rough paste. Whisk that into the 40g of rice flour, the 10g of cornflour and the 100ml of water until completely smooth, then stir in the 20g of spring onion sliced thin, greens and all, and the 4g of coriander leaf roughly chopped. ✅ It should coat the back of a spoon in a thin translucent film and run off quickly, leaving the spoon still visible through it. If it sits on the spoon in an opaque layer, add water 10ml at a time. A thick batter gives you a doughnut with a bit of tempeh in it; a thin one gives you tempeh wearing a savoury green-flecked skin, which is what this is.\n\n⚠️ AND MIX IT JUST BEFORE YOU FRY. Rice flour does not stay suspended — it settles into a hard sludge at the bottom of the bowl within about ten minutes, and a batter that has stood is thin water on top and paste underneath. Whisk it again right before each batch.\n\nUSE PLENTY OF OIL, AND USE IT HOT. Heat the 400ml of sunflower oil in a small deep pan to 180°C — a drop of batter should sizzle immediately, rise straight to the surface and start bubbling hard, without browning in the first few seconds. That volume of oil is not extravagance and it is doing real work: a cold slice going into a small amount of oil crashes the temperature, and a batter fried in cool oil is exactly the failure this dish is most often accused of. ⛔ THERE IS A RIGHT AND A WRONG KIND OF SOFT, and the difference is grease. Pale-and-soft from a short fry in properly hot oil is tender and clean. Pale-and-soft from a long fry in lukewarm oil is sodden, because oil only enters food when the outward rush of steam is too weak to keep it out — and weak steam is exactly what cool oil produces. Hot and quick keeps the fritter dry inside. Cool and slow makes a sponge.\n\nFRY 60 TO 90 SECONDS, ONE OR TWO AT A TIME, AND TAKE THEM OUT PALE. Dip a slice, let the excess run off for a second, and lay it into the oil away from you. It will bubble furiously. Turn it once at about 40 seconds. ✅ You are finished when the batter has gone from translucent and wet-looking to opaque, matte and set — still cream-coloured, with perhaps the faintest gold at the very edges where the batter is thinnest — and it holds together when you lift it but bends under its own weight. ⛔ If it has gone evenly golden it is a perfectly good fritter and it is not a mendoan. Drain them on a rack rather than paper, and do not stack them.\n\nEAT THEM WITHIN ABOUT THREE MINUTES. This is not a dish that waits, and being honest about that is more useful than pretending otherwise. There is no crisp crust here to go soft, so nothing dramatic happens — but the batter firms noticeably as it cools and the whole pleasure of a mendoan is the warm, yielding, almost custardy state it is in straight out of the pan. On a Purwokerto street they are handed to you in paper and eaten standing up, which is not romance, it is timing.\n\nTHE DIP, AND THE RAW CHILLI ALTERNATIVE. Stir the 20g of kecap manis with the 10g of red chilli sliced into fine rings and a squeeze of water to loosen it. That is the whole sauce — sweet, dark, salty and hot, against something soft and mild. ✅ The other traditional route is simply to eat a whole raw bird's-eye chilli between bites, which sounds like showing off and is actually the better pairing: the fritter is gentle and slightly oily and a sharp raw green heat cuts it cleanly. Put both on the table and let people choose.","cookTime":"Slice 5 min · Batter 5 min · Fry 90 sec each","kcal":"~410 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 19g · Carbs 34g · Fat 23g · Sodium ~1350mg","storage":"⛔ THIS ONE DOES NOT KEEP, and I would rather say so plainly than give you a storage plan that produces a disappointing snack tomorrow. A mendoan exists in a specific state — warm, set, soft, not crisp — and that state lasts a few minutes. Cold, the batter goes dense and slightly leathery; reheated in an oven or a pan it does the one thing it was made not to do, which is crisp up, and you end up with an ordinary tempeh fritter. Fry what you will eat. ✅ What DOES keep is everything before the oil: sliced tempeh keeps 3 days sealed in the fridge, and the dry flour mix with the crushed garlic and spices keeps a week in a jar — add the water, the spring onion and the coriander only when you are ready to fry, since the greens go slimy in a wet batter within a couple of hours. That way the whole dish is five minutes from cold start. ⚠️ Cooled leftover frying oil can be strained through a fine sieve or a coffee filter and reused twice more for savoury frying — keep it in a sealed jar in a dark cupboard, and throw it out the moment it smells sharp or paint-like, or when it starts foaming rather than bubbling. If you do have cold mendoan and cannot bear to bin them, the leftovers list below has the one honest use.","chefNotes":"The batter must be RICE flour — that is what makes under-frying legal rather than raw. Rice starch sets into a tender cohesive film very early and stays that way; a wheat batter builds gluten and pulled early is simply dough. Slice the tempeh 4–5mm and wide, because the thickness is calculated backwards from the batter's 90-second clock. Batter thin enough to see the spoon through, and whisk it again right before each batch — rice flour settles to sludge in ten minutes. Oil HOT and plenty of it: pale-and-soft from a short fry in 180°C oil is tender, pale-and-soft from a long fry in lukewarm oil is a grease sponge, and they look identical going in. Out at 60–90 seconds while it is still cream-coloured and bends under its own weight. Eat within three minutes.","pairsWith":"It is a snack before it is anything else — handed over in paper, eaten standing up, with sweet tea. On a table it belongs beside things that are sharp or sour rather than beside other fried things, because two soft oily items on one plate cancel each other out. A sour vegetable broth alongside is a genuinely good lunch and the contrast is the point: something warm and yielding, something sharp and thin. It also works as the starter to almost any rice-and-sambal meal, since it is mild enough not to spend anyone's palate before the main event arrives. ⛔ Do not serve it as a side to a rich coconut-based dish — it disappears completely. And do not put it out on a platter twenty minutes before people sit down, which is the single most common way this dish is ruined by someone who cooked it perfectly.","goesWith":["Sambal Terasi","Sayur Asem","Bawang Goreng","Nasi Uduk"],"trivia":"Tempeh is a staple food with no known inventor, no origin story and almost no history — and for something eaten daily by tens of millions of people, that silence is genuinely strange. The earliest secure written mention of it appears in the Serat Centhini, a vast Javanese manuscript compiled in the early nineteenth century, and even there it turns up casually, in passing, as an ordinary thing needing no explanation — the way a modern cookbook would mention bread. Before that, essentially nothing. Java is not a place short of records: it has centuries of temple inscriptions, court documents and trade lists that itemise market goods, taxes and tribute in fussy detail. A food this important leaving no trace in any of them is a loud absence.\n\nThe likeliest explanation is that it simply is not very old. Tempeh needs soybeans in quantity, and soybeans are not native to Java — large-scale cultivation there is bound up with the growth of Chinese communities and the soy trade, which puts a floor under how far back the dish can reasonably go. Which leaves the most probable origin story as the least dramatic one imaginable: somebody, a few hundred years ago, in a household kitchen, left a batch of boiled soybeans wrapped and forgotten in a warm corner, came back to find them bound into a firm white cake, and was hungry enough or curious enough to try it. Nobody wrote the name down because at the time there was nothing to write down. The most famous fermented food in Southeast Asia was almost certainly invented by accident, by a person we will never identify, and the entire documentary record of it begins with a single unremarkable sentence in a manuscript from around 1814.\n\nMendoan itself carries its own small piece of language history, and this one you can taste. The name comes from the Banyumasan dialect word mendo, meaning half-done or soft — so the dish is called, quite literally, the half-cooked one. Almost every other food name in the world advertises what it is made of, where it comes from or how good it is. This one advertises its own undercookedness, which tells you the pale floppy result was never a compromise or a shortcut. It was the point from the very beginning, and somebody was confident enough about it to put it in the name. 🌱 One practical note on the coriander in the batter: it is the herb most likely to defeat you in a Pretoria summer, because coriander bolts to seed the moment it gets hot. Sow it in autumn and winter, keep it in afternoon shade, and cut the whole young plant at the base rather than picking leaves off it — treat it as a fast crop rather than a permanent plant and it will not let you down.","howThisFeels":"Lifting something pale and floppy out of hot oil, being absolutely certain you have got it wrong, and then eating it and understanding.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold mendoan chopped roughly and stirred through hot fried rice at the very end, where the softness stops being a flaw and becomes a savoury, chewy, tempeh-flecked texture.","(easy) Torn into a bowl of sour vegetable broth for two minutes — cold batter that has firmed up is very good at soaking, and this is the one reheating route that works with the dish rather than against it.","(medium) Sliced into ribbons and folded into a cold noodle salad with kecap manis, lime and raw shallot; nobody will guess it was fried yesterday.","(creative) Cut small and used as the crouton in a coconut-based soup, added right at the table so it keeps some structure — an unorthodox use that solves the actual problem, which is that a cold mendoan wants something wet and rich to live in."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mendoan Rumahan — Shallow Pan, Chilli Only (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":51,"howThisFeels":"A wide pan with barely a centimetre of oil in it, three fritters at a time, and a bowl of raw chillies on the side.","trivia":"Frying oil is a real cost that recipes are strangely quiet about — in this dish, at full depth, it is close to a quarter of what the plate costs before you have bought a single soybean. That is why the household version of almost every deep-fried street food is a shallow-pan version, everywhere in the world, and why street vendors can afford the depth and you cannot: they fry all day, so the oil is doing hundreds of jobs, while yours does one and then sits in a jar. The honest trade-off is temperature stability. A large volume of oil barely notices a cold slice going in; a shallow pan's temperature drops sharply and takes time to recover, which is exactly the condition that makes fritters greasy. So the shallow version is not simply the same dish for less money — it needs you to fry fewer at a time and wait between batches, and it swaps money for attention.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400ml sunflower oil","to":"100ml sunflower oil"},{"from":"120g tempeh","to":"100g tempeh"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"20g kecap manis"}],"addStep":[{"text":"100ml of oil in a wide pan rather than 400ml in a deep one, 100g of tempeh, and no kecap dip — just the sliced raw chilli on the side, which is the older pairing anyway. ✅ Three changes to how you fry, and they all follow from the smaller volume. Use the WIDEST pan you have, so the oil is about 1cm deep and there is plenty of surface — width buys you back some of the thermal stability that depth was providing. Fry ONE fritter at a time, never two, and let the oil come back up to temperature between them; you will hear it, because the sizzle returns to a proper vigorous hiss. And turn each one twice rather than once, at about 30 and 60 seconds, since only one face is submerged at a time. ⚠️ Watch the oil more closely than in the deep version — a shallow pan goes from 180°C to smoking much faster than a deep one, and burnt oil will make everything fried in it taste bitter. If it starts to smoke, take the pan off the heat entirely for a minute rather than turning the dial down."}]}},{"name":"Tempe Mendoan — Rice Flour, Ninety Seconds, Pale","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":77,"howThisFeels":"Something warm and yielding folding over your fingers, still too hot, eaten standing at the stove because it will not wait.","trivia":"There is a reason mendoan is a regional dish rather than a national one, and it is not the batter — it is the tempeh. Banyumas produces tempeh specifically FOR this, fermented in thin wide sheets rather than thick blocks, wrapped in banana leaf so it comes out broad and flexible and only a few millimetres thick. You cannot really buy that outside the region, and slicing an ordinary block thinly is the honest workaround rather than the real thing. It also explains something about how the dish travels: mendoan made elsewhere in Java is almost always thicker, and almost always fried a little longer to compensate, which drifts it steadily toward being an ordinary fritter. A dish built around a specialist ingredient tends to soften into a generic version of itself the further it gets from home, and the tell here is the timer. If a recipe tells you to fry mendoan until golden, it has already travelled too far.","delta":null},{"name":"Tempe Kemul — the Wonosobo Cousin, Fried Crisp","icon":"🟡","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":79,"howThisFeels":"The same tempeh, a yellow batter, three minutes instead of ninety seconds — and a snap when you break it.","trivia":"Kemul means blanket, and tempe kemul is the same idea taken in the exact opposite direction, which makes it the most useful thing on this card. It comes from Wonosobo, in the cold highlands a couple of hours from Banyumas, and it is thicker, yellow with turmeric, and fried until it genuinely crunches. Two towns, the same tempeh, the same rice flour, and two opposed doctrines about when to stop. Cooking both back to back teaches more about frying than any amount of reading: you use one batter, one pan and one ingredient, and the only variables are thickness and time. It is also the version to make if you only have plain wheat flour in the house, since a fully fried batter has no raw-dough problem to worry about — the whole argument for rice flour is about what happens when you stop EARLY, and this one does not stop early.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"2g ground turmeric"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"40g rice flour","to":"60g rice flour"}],"addStep":[{"text":"60g of rice flour rather than 40g, plus 2g of ground turmeric, and the same 100ml of water — so the batter comes out distinctly thicker, opaque, and properly yellow. ✅ Whisk the turmeric in with the dry flour rather than adding it at the end, so it disperses evenly instead of streaking. The batter should now coat the back of a spoon in a layer you cannot see through, and it should hang on the tempeh in a visible jacket rather than a skin. Fry at a slightly LOWER temperature — around 165 to 170°C rather than 180 — for 3 to 4 minutes, turning once, until it is deep gold and audibly crisp when tapped with the tongs. ⚠️ The lower temperature matters: a thicker batter at 180°C browns on the outside before the inside has finished cooking, and you get a crisp shell over a pasty layer. Lower and longer lets the whole thickness set. Drain on a rack and eat within about ten minutes, which is far more forgiving than the mendoan — a crisp fritter has a little grace period, a soft one has none. Serve with the same kecap and chilli dip."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Sayur Asem","target":"indonesia-sayur-asem","emoji":"🥣"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sambal-matah","name":"Sambal Matah","nameAlt":"Balinese raw sliced shallot and lemongrass sambal","aliases":["Sambal Matah","Sambal Mentah","Balinese Raw Sambal","Raw Shallot Sambal","Lemongrass Sambal","Bali Sambal Matah"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","sambal","condiment","no-cook","quick","vegetables","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","guests","quick","braai","summer"],"ingredients":"60g shallots · 30ml coconut oil · 15g birds eye chillies · 10g lemongrass · 10g lime · 5g shrimp paste · 3g salt","method":"Matah means raw, and that is the first thing this sambal tells you about itself. Nothing here is pounded, almost nothing is cooked, and there is no paste at any point — it is a bowl of things cut very small and tossed together, which makes it far closer to a salad than to any other sambal in this section. But the fact that actually governs how you make it is a different one, and it took me a while to see it: ⚖️ EVERY AROMATIC IN THIS BOWL IS SOMETHING YOU WOULD NORMALLY TAKE OUT OF THE DISH BEFORE SERVING. Lemongrass gets bruised, simmered and fished out. Lime leaf gets torn in and lifted off. They are infusion aromatics — you borrow their flavour and discard the fibre. Here you eat all of it. And the whole method follows from that, because a thing you chew has to be cut in a way that makes it chewable, and each one needs a different cut.\n\nSTART WITH THE LEMONGRASS, BECAUSE IT IS THE ONE THAT GOES WRONG. Take the 10g of lemongrass and use only the bottom third of the stalk — the pale, dense, slightly bulbous part. Cut the dry woody base off, and then peel away the outer two or three layers with your fingers until what is left is pale, tight and a little glossy. ✅ THE TEST: a slice of the part you keep should BEND when you press it, not snap. If it snaps, peel another layer. Then slice it ACROSS the stalk, as thin as you can physically manage — under 1mm, thin enough to be almost translucent. ⛔ This is not fussiness and it is the single thing that ruins a home-made matah. Lemongrass fibre runs the length of the stalk in long tough bundles, so a 2mm slice is a bundle of splinters and you will be picking them out of your teeth; sliced at half a millimetre across the grain, those same fibres are cut short enough to be soft, and the piece disappears in the mouth leaving only the flavour. Sharp knife, and take your time — this is four minutes of careful work and it is most of the dish.\n\nNOW THE SHALLOTS, IN RINGS, WITH A SHARP KNIFE. Peel the 60g of shallots and slice them across into rings about 1mm thick, separating them as you go. Shallots are the bulk of this sambal — well over half of it by weight — so this really is a shallot salad with seasoning in it, rather than a seasoning with a bit of shallot. ✅ A SHARP knife matters here for a purely mechanical reason: a blunt blade crushes and tears rather than cutting, so it ruptures far more cells and you get a wetter, harsher, more aggressive result from the identical bulb. (The Acar Kuning card sets out what cutting actually does to an onion's chemistry, and it applies unchanged here; the point in this bowl is simply that a clean cut gives you a cleaner sambal.)\n\nTHE CHILLIES ARE SLICED, NOT CRUSHED, AND THAT CHANGES HOW THE HEAT ARRIVES. Slice the 15g of bird's eye chillies across into fine rings, seeds and all. Because nothing is pounded, the heat in a matah is not distributed evenly through a paste — it sits in discrete rings, so you get bright hot flashes against mild sweet shallot rather than a uniform burn. That intermittent quality is characteristic and it is why matah eats hotter than its chilli weight suggests. ⛔ If that is too much, reduce the number of chillies rather than removing the seeds, since fishing seeds out of rings this small is a waste of an afternoon.\n\n⚠️ THE LIME LEAF, AND THE HONEST SOUTH AFRICAN ANSWER. A proper Balinese matah has Thai lime leaves — the knobbly-skinned lime, and most shops here still label the leaves kaffir lime leaves — in it, shredded into hair-fine green threads and eaten like the rest. If you can find them — and you occasionally can — roll two leaves into a tight cigar, cut out the tough central rib first, and shred them across at under 1mm. ✅ If you cannot, do NOT leave the citrus out and do not substitute an ordinary bay leaf, which is a different plant entirely and tastes nothing like it. Pare the zest off the 10g of lime in wide strips with a peeler, taking no white pith, stack the strips and shred them as fine as you possibly can, then use them exactly as you would the leaf. It is not identical — lime leaf has a floral, almost perfumed note that zest does not reach — but it is the same job done honestly, it is eaten the same way, and it needs the same hair-fine cut for the same reason.\n\nTOSS EVERYTHING WITH SALT AND LIME JUICE, WITH YOUR HANDS. Put the shallot rings, lemongrass, chilli rings and shredded zest in a bowl with the 3g of salt and the juice of the 10g of lime. Crumble in the 5g of shrimp paste — the Sambal Terasi card explains what toasting it does and why, and it holds here; toast it or use it raw as you prefer, but crumble it very fine so it disperses. Then work it gently with your fingertips for about half a minute, lifting and turning rather than squeezing. ⛔ Do not stir it hard with a spoon and never crush it against the side of the bowl. You are trying to distribute, not to combine.\n\nPOUR HOT OIL OVER IT, AND ACCEPT THAT THIS IS THE ONLY COOKING THAT HAPPENS. Heat the 30ml of coconut oil in a small pan until it shimmers and a shred of shallot dropped in sizzles immediately, then pour it straight over the bowl and toss once. It will hiss and the smell will change instantly from sharp and raw to something rounder. ✅ What the oil is doing is taking the aggressive raw squeak off the shallot and carrying the flavour across the whole bowl so one small spoonful tastes of everything — it is not there to cook anything, and it does not. Coconut oil rather than sunflower is worth it here if you have it, because it is solid at cool room temperature and firms slightly as the sambal stands, which gives matah its characteristic slightly clinging texture rather than a puddle of oil at the bottom.\n\nLET IT STAND TEN MINUTES, THEN EAT IT. It is at its best about ten minutes after the oil goes on and it stays excellent for a couple of hours. ⚠️ It is NOT better tomorrow, and this is worth knowing before you make a double batch: the shallots go on releasing water into the bowl and softening, the lemongrass slowly loses its perfume to the oil, and by the next day you have a milder, wetter, flatter thing that is perfectly pleasant and is no longer a matah. Make what you will eat, and make it in the ten minutes before you sit down.","cookTime":"Slice 12 min · Toss 1 min · Hot oil 2 min · Stand 10 min","kcal":"~180 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g · Carbs 8g · Fat 16g · Sodium ~1250mg","storage":"It keeps 2 days in a sealed jar in the fridge and it is genuinely good on day one — but be clear about what happens to it, because this is a dish that fades rather than spoils. Salt keeps drawing water out of the shallots, so the bowl gets progressively wetter and the shallot rings go from crisp to limp; and the lemongrass and citrus give their volatile oils up into the coconut oil, so the sambal gets rounder and less bright with every hour. By day two it is a mild, oily, pleasant shallot relish. It is not sambal matah. ✅ If you want it ahead, do the slicing ahead instead: shallots, chillies and lemongrass can all be sliced, kept in separate closed tubs in the fridge for a day, and tossed with the salt, lime and hot oil in the two minutes before serving. That gets you the whole benefit of preparation with none of the fade. Take it out of the fridge 20 minutes before eating, because coconut oil sets hard and cold matah is dull. ⛔ It does not freeze under any circumstances — every component is a thin raw slice held together by its own cell walls, and freezing bursts all of them; you would thaw a bowl of grey wet threads in oil.","chefNotes":"Every aromatic in this bowl is something you would normally fish out and throw away, and here you eat all of it — so the cut is the whole recipe. Lemongrass: bottom third only, peel until a slice BENDS instead of snapping, then slice across the grain at under 1mm or you are eating splinters. Lime leaf or pared lime zest: rib out, rolled, shredded hair-fine. Shallots in 1mm rings, and use a sharp knife, because a blunt one crushes and gives you a wetter harsher sambal from the same bulb. Nothing is pounded, ever. Toss with your fingertips, not a spoon. Then hot oil over the top — that is the only heat in the dish and its job is to take the raw squeak off and spread the flavour, not to cook. Ten minutes to rest, two hours at its best, and it is not better tomorrow.","pairsWith":"It goes on grilled and fried things, and it is at its very best on anything that came off a fire — grilled fish above all, where the sharp raw shallot and the citrus cut straight through smoke and char. It is also outstanding on a braai table for exactly that reason and is one of the few things there that takes fifteen minutes and no cooking. A spoonful on plain rice is a meal on its own if you are eating alone and cannot be bothered. ⛔ Where it does not work is on anything wet: stirred into a soup or a curry it dissolves into the liquid and the whole texture — the point of the dish — vanishes, and you have spent twelve minutes of knife work on nothing. Keep it on the side, in its own small bowl, and let people spoon it over dry food. It is also the wrong sambal for a dish that already has strong citrus in it; one lime note per plate.","goesWith":["Sate Lilit","Ayam Betutu","Pepes Ikan","Nasi Uduk"],"trivia":"The shallots that make up most of this bowl grow in some of the most fertile soil on earth, and the reason for that fertility is also the reason people living on it periodically lose everything. Indonesia sits along the most volcanically active stretch of the Ring of Fire, with around 130 active volcanoes — more than any other country. Java, an island roughly the size of England, supports something like 150 million people, which makes it one of the most densely populated large islands anywhere, and it manages that almost entirely because of what its volcanoes have done to its dirt.\n\nThe mechanism is worth knowing because it is not the obvious one. Volcanic ash is not fertile when it lands. Fresh ash is essentially powdered glass — sterile, sharp, chemically locked up, and catastrophic for anything growing under it. What makes it valuable is weathering: over decades and centuries, rain and heat break that volcanic glass down into clay minerals with an enormous surface area, and those minerals hold water and nutrients far better than ordinary soil does. The result is a soil type called andosol, and it is remarkably good at growing exactly the shallow-rooted, hungry, intensively farmed crops that Indonesian agriculture runs on — shallots and chillies very much among them. So the fertility arrives on a delay. A farmer planting shallots on the flank of a volcano today is farming a disaster that happened to somebody's great-grandparents, and the eruption that would ruin this season's crop is the one that will make the soil extraordinary for a generation nobody in the field will meet. That trade — ruin now, abundance later, repeated indefinitely — is the whole reason a village stays put somewhere that everybody outside it considers obviously mad.\n\n🌱 And on the subject of shallots, they are the easiest thing on this list to grow and almost nobody here does. A shallot is not really an onion in the way you plant one: it multiplies. Push a single healthy bulb into decent soil in autumn, pointy end just proud of the surface, and by early summer it will have divided into a clump of six or eight — you lift the clump, keep the best few bulbs back, and plant those again. They want sun, a bit of compost and not much water, they store for months in a paper bag in a dark cupboard, and after the first once-off bulb you never buy them again. For a household that gets through this much shallot, that is a genuinely useful piece of arithmetic.","howThisFeels":"Twelve minutes of nothing but a sharp knife and a board, and a smell coming off the pile that makes you want to eat it before the oil is even hot.","leftovers":["(easy) Spooned over a fried egg on rice, which is the standard Balinese answer to what happens to yesterday's matah and needs no explanation.","(easy) Stirred through hot noodles right at the end with a splash of the oil from the bottom of the jar — the residual heat wakes the lemongrass back up for a few minutes.","(medium) Used as a marinade rather than a sambal: rub day-old matah into fish or chicken pieces an hour before grilling, where the softening that ruined it as a relish is exactly what you want.","(creative) Fry the whole of yesterday's matah in a dry pan over medium heat until the shallots go golden and the oil separates — it stops being sambal matah entirely and becomes a deeply savoury cooked relish, which is a much better outcome than pretending the raw one survived the night."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sambal Matah Polos — No Terasi, Sunflower Oil (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Five things, a knife, and something on the table in fifteen minutes that tastes like it should have been harder.","trivia":"Leaving the shrimp paste out is not a compromise version, and it is worth saying so clearly because vegan sambal recipes are usually apologising for something. A great many matah in Bali have no terasi in them at all — it is the version most often served alongside grilled fish, on the sensible grounds that the fish is already bringing the savoury depth and a second strong marine note would just be shouting. What the terasi actually contributes is glutamate and a fermented funk, and without it the sambal is noticeably cleaner, sharper and more citrus-forward, which some people prefer outright. If you want some of that savoury weight back without the shrimp paste, an extra pinch of salt and a slightly heavier hand with the lime will do most of the work, because what you are missing is largely intensity rather than a specific flavour.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"30ml coconut oil","to":"30ml sunflower oil"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"5g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No shrimp paste, and sunflower oil in place of coconut. ✅ Everything about the cutting is unchanged — the lemongrass still needs to bend rather than snap, and it still gets sliced under 1mm across the grain, because that has nothing to do with which oil you use. Two small adjustments make up the difference. Add an extra 1g of salt, since the terasi was carrying some of the seasoning as well as the savoury depth. And add the lime juice in two stages: half at the tossing stage, half after the hot oil has gone on and the bowl has stood five minutes, which keeps the citrus bright rather than letting all of it soften into the oil at once. ⚠️ Sunflower oil stays liquid at room temperature where coconut oil firms up, so this version sits looser in the bowl and will pool a little at the bottom — toss it again just before it goes to the table rather than adding less oil, because the oil is what carries the flavour across everything."}]}},{"name":"Sambal Matah — Sliced Raw, Hot Oil Over","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"Hot oil hitting raw shallot and the whole kitchen changing smell in a second and a half.","trivia":"Sambal matah is much younger than it looks, and the story of how it spread is a modern one. Within Bali it was a local, largely domestic thing — a raw sambal from the island's own kitchens rather than a dish with any wider reputation — until Bali's tourism industry expanded through the second half of the twentieth century and restaurant kitchens went looking for the things that were most distinctly Balinese rather than generically Indonesian. Matah was perfect for that: it is visually striking, it needs no cooking, it holds for a service, and it tastes like nowhere else. It travelled out to Jakarta and then internationally as Balinese food became a category, and it is now probably the single best-known Balinese dish outside the island. What is unusual is how little it changed on the way. Most dishes get blunted as they travel — pounded smoother, cooked longer, made safer — and matah's whole identity is a set of things it refuses to do, which turns out to be surprisingly hard to erode. There is no simpler version of it to drift toward.","delta":null},{"name":"Sambal Matah Lengkap — Double Lemongrass, Lime Leaf, Toasted Terasi","icon":"🍃","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"A bowl that is more green thread than shallot, and a smell of lemongrass that arrives from the next room.","trivia":"The full-strength version tips the balance so that the aromatics stop being seasoning and become the substance — roughly a quarter of the bowl by weight is lemongrass and shredded citrus leaf, which is an amount that would be absurd in almost any other dish on earth. It works for one specific reason: lemongrass's flavour is dominated by citral, the same compound that gives lemon peel its character, and citral is intensely aromatic but genuinely mild on the palate. It is not hot, not bitter and not especially strong-tasting; it just smells enormous. So you can eat a quantity of it that looks unreasonable on the plate without the sambal becoming overwhelming, which is not true of, say, an equivalent weight of ginger or garlic. That gap between how much you can SMELL and how much you can comfortably EAT is the space this version lives in, and it is why a lengkap matah reads as fragrant rather than fierce.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"10g lemongrass","to":"20g lemongrass"},{"from":"10g lime","to":"20g lime"},{"from":"5g shrimp paste","to":"8g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Double the lemongrass to 20g and the lime to 20g, and go to 8g of shrimp paste — and since there is twice as much of the difficult ingredient, the peeling standard has to be twice as strict. ⚠️ At this quantity a single under-peeled stalk will put splinters through the whole bowl, so peel each one until a test slice bends, and if in doubt take another layer off; you will lose some weight to the compost and it is worth it. ✅ Toast the shrimp paste for this version rather than using it raw: flatten it into a thin disc, dry-toast it in a pan over medium heat for about 90 seconds a side until it darkens and smells sharp and roasted, then crumble it in. The Sambal Terasi card sets out why toasting does what it does and that reasoning holds here unchanged. If you have Thai lime leaves, use four rather than two, ribs removed and shredded hair-fine, and keep the pared lime zest as well — the two are not interchangeable and the full version has both. ⏱️ Give this one a full 15 minutes to stand rather than 10, because there is more fibre for the oil to work through."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sate Lilit","target":"indonesia-sate-lilit","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Ayam Betutu","target":"indonesia-ayam-betutu","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-es-teler","name":"Es Teler","nameAlt":"Iced avocado and coconut fruit cup","aliases":["Es Teler","Es Teller","Iced Avocado Dessert","Indonesian Fruit Ice","Avocado Coconut Ice","Es Teler Alpukat"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","drink","no-cook","cold","summer","quick","sweet"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["summer","everyday","family","guests","children","quick"],"ingredients":"250ml water · 100ml coconut milk · 100g avocado · 80g pineapple · 60ml coconut water · 40ml condensed milk · 20g jaggery","method":"There is no cooking in this at all, which makes it tempting to treat as a list rather than a recipe. It is not. Everything that decides whether es teler is wonderful or a disappointing puddle happens in the sixty seconds of putting it together, and the governing fact is one most people never think about: ⚖️ ICE IS AN INGREDIENT, AND EVERY GRAM OF IT THAT MELTS IS WATER YOU DID NOT CHOOSE TO ADD. A glass built carelessly is a glass that dilutes itself while you carry it to the table.\n\n⚖️ AND A NOTE ON WHAT THIS IS NOT. The Es Cendol card is built on keeping two things separate — sweet syrup and salted coconut, deliberately never stirred, so each spoonful is different from the last. Es teler is the opposite dish and it is worth knowing the two side by side: everything here IS combined, it is a fruit salad in a glass, and the aim is that every mouthful tastes the same as the one before it. Two Indonesian iced desserts, two opposed intentions.\n\nFIRST, FREEZE THE WATER THE NIGHT BEFORE. Pour the 250ml of water into a tray and freeze it solid. That is the ice, and it is worth being deliberate about it rather than grabbing whatever is in the freezer door — ice made from water that has been sitting open in a freezer for a month tastes of the freezer, and in a dish this delicate you will notice. ✅ If your water is heavily chlorinated, boil it and let it cool before freezing; boiling drives off most of the chlorine and the ice comes out cleaner-tasting and clearer.\n\nCHILL EVERY SINGLE COMPONENT BEFORE ANYTHING MEETS THE ICE. THIS IS THE WHOLE TECHNIQUE. The coconut milk, the coconut water, the condensed milk and the fruit all go into the fridge for at least two hours beforehand. ⛔ Pour room-temperature liquid over ice and the ice immediately gets a second job it should never have been given: instead of merely HOLDING the cold, it has to CREATE it, and the only way ice can cool something is by melting. A glass built from warm components loses a third of its ice inside two minutes, and that ice becomes water sitting in your dessert. ✅ Build it cold-into-cold and the ice has nothing to do but sit there, so it lasts, and what you drink at the bottom of the glass tastes like what you tasted at the top.\n\nMAKE THE LIQUID UP COMPLETELY, IN A JUG, BEFORE YOU TOUCH A GLASS. Melt the 20g of jaggery in a splash of hot water until it dissolves, then let it cool completely — grated palm sugar will not dissolve in a cold liquid no matter how long you stir, and you will end up with gritty sweet sludge at the bottom. Combine the cooled syrup with the chilled 100ml of coconut milk, the 60ml of coconut water and the 40ml of condensed milk, whisk it smooth and taste it. ⚠️ It should taste slightly TOO sweet and slightly TOO strong in the jug, because the ice is going to dilute it by design as you eat. A liquid that tastes perfectly balanced before it meets ice will taste thin and watery within five minutes.\n\nAND THE REASON THE LIQUID IS MIXED IN A JUG RATHER THAN IN THE GLASS IS PURELY MECHANICAL. Once the glass is built, you cannot stir it. There is a very ripe avocado in there and a spoon going round the glass turns it into green streaks through the milk in about three strokes. So the seasoning decision, all of it, is made in the jug where you can still taste and adjust. This is the same reasoning as a cook salting a soup rather than salting the bowls.\n\nSPOON THE AVOCADO, DO NOT CUBE IT, AND USE ONE THAT IS ALMOST TOO SOFT. Take 100g of avocado — roughly half a large one — and scoop it out in rough, ragged spoonfuls straight into the glass. ⛔ Do not cut it into neat cubes. This matters more than it sounds: the avocado in es teler is not there as a fruit, it is there as a TEXTURE, and its job is to behave like cream you can chew. Ragged spoon-torn pieces have soft, irregular surfaces that blur into the coconut liquid around them, which is what you want; neat cubes have sharp edges that stay visually and texturally separate, and the glass reads as a fruit salad with avocado in it rather than as one thing. ✅ And it must be RIPE — properly ripe, yielding under a thumb, at the stage where you would be slightly nervous keeping it another day. A firm avocado is grassy, faintly squeaky and slightly bitter, and there is nothing else in this glass strong enough to hide it.\n\n⚠️ THE TWO INGREDIENTS YOU CANNOT BUY HERE, AND WHAT TO DO ABOUT EACH. A proper es teler has young coconut flesh and ripe jackfruit in it, and neither is realistically available in South Africa. They get different answers and it is worth being straight about which is which. **Young coconut flesh** — the soft translucent jelly scraped out of a green coconut — has NO stand-in. Mature coconut is hard and crunchy, desiccated coconut is dry fibre, and neither is remotely the same thing. Leave it out. ✅ What you CAN buy is the coconut water, which is the liquid from exactly that nut, so in this recipe it does double duty and carries the flavour even though the texture is missing. **Ripe jackfruit** is a different case: yellow, sweet, slightly fibrous, with a distinctive aroma somewhere between banana and bubblegum. Pineapple is an honest stand-in for the JOB — sweet, acidic, fibrous enough to hold its shape in cold liquid, and available everywhere all year — while being no substitute at all for that aroma, which nothing replicates. ⛔ Do not buy tinned young jackfruit for this; that is green unripe jackfruit sold as a meat substitute, it is savoury, and it would be a genuinely unpleasant surprise in a dessert.\n\nCRUSH THE ICE, AND KNOW WHAT THAT COSTS YOU. Wrap the cubes in a clean tea towel and hit them with a rolling pin, or pulse them briefly in a blender. ⚠️ A shaved-ice machine is what a street stall uses and it is what makes the slushy version people photograph, and there is no honest way to do it at home here — a blender gives crushed, not shaved, and the two are not the same. Crushed is the right answer for a home kitchen and the glass is excellent. But understand the trade: crushing ice multiplies its surface area enormously, and melting only happens at the surface, so crushed ice melts several times faster than the same weight of whole cubes. **That is the reason this dish is assembled at the last possible second and not a minute before.**\n\nBUILD IT AND HAND IT OVER IMMEDIATELY. Avocado in the bottom of a tall glass, the 80g of pineapple cut into rough 1.5cm pieces on top, crushed ice filling the glass, then the cold liquid poured over the ice so it runs down through everything. Pandan is traditional and hard to buy — if you have a leaf, bruise it and steep it in the warm syrup, then remove it. Serve it with a long spoon and tell people to eat it now.","cookTime":"Freeze overnight · Chill 2 hr · Assemble 3 min","kcal":"~390 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 5g · Carbs 44g · Fat 22g · Sodium ~90mg","storage":"⛔ ASSEMBLED, IT KEEPS FOR ABOUT FOUR MINUTES, and there is no version of this where a made-up glass survives in a fridge — the ice melts, the liquid separates, the avocado oxidises to a dull brown-grey at the edges and the whole thing becomes a sad diluted soup. Never build it ahead. ✅ What keeps beautifully is every component separately, which is how a stall runs it: the sweetened coconut liquid keeps 3 days in a sealed jug in the fridge and honestly improves overnight as the palm sugar and coconut settle into each other; the cut pineapple keeps 3 days; the ice keeps indefinitely. That means a made-ahead es teler is a two-minute job at the moment somebody wants one, which is much better than a made-ahead glass. ⚠️ The avocado is the only thing that cannot be prepped in advance — cut surfaces go brown within about half an hour and no amount of lime juice really stops it in something this pale — so scoop it straight into the glass as you build. If you must, scoop it into the cold liquid itself and cover the surface, where the milk keeps most of the air off for an hour or so. ⛔ Nothing here freezes as a finished dish: coconut milk splits on thawing and avocado goes waxy and grey.","chefNotes":"Ice is an ingredient, and every gram that melts is water you did not choose to add. So chill every single component to fridge-cold BEFORE anything meets the ice — warm liquid forces the ice to create the cold rather than hold it, and the only way ice can do that is by melting. Mix the whole liquid in a jug and taste it there, because once the glass is built you cannot stir it without turning a ripe avocado into green streaks. Make that liquid taste slightly too sweet and too strong: the ice will dilute it by design. Dissolve the palm sugar in hot water and cool it, or it stays as grit at the bottom. Spoon the avocado in ragged pieces rather than cubing it — it is a texture, not a fruit. And crush the ice at the last second, because crushing multiplies the surface area and melting only happens at the surface.","pairsWith":"It is a dessert and a drink at the same time and it does not really sit beside other food — it comes after, or it comes on its own in the middle of a hot afternoon, which is what it is for. Where it genuinely belongs is at the end of a heavy, chilli-forward, coconut-rich meal: it is cold, sweet, fatty and completely uncomplicated, and it puts out a fire that a hot pudding would only feed. It is also the right thing after a braai for the same reason. ⛔ Do not serve it alongside another coconut dessert, since the whole plate then tastes of one thing, and do not serve it with coffee — the temperature clash is unpleasant and the sweetness makes the coffee taste sour. Sweet iced tea is wrong too, for the same reason. Water, or nothing.","goesWith":["Es Cendol","Klepon","Bika Ambon","Serabi Solo"],"trivia":"For most of human history ice in the tropics was not difficult to get — it was impossible. And then, for about eighty years, it arrived by sailing ship from Massachusetts, and the entire business rested on a single fact of geometry.\n\nIn 1806 a young Bostonian named Frederic Tudor loaded a brig with blocks cut from a New England pond and sailed it to the Caribbean, on the reasoning that ice was worthless where it was and priceless where it was not. Almost everyone told him the cargo would be seawater by arrival. It was not. He lost money for years on everything except the physics, and by the 1830s the trade had reached India, and through the middle of the century ice cut from frozen ponds outside Boston was being sold in ports across South and Southeast Asia — packed in sawdust, which New England's timber mills produced by the mountain and threw away. Purpose-built ice houses went up in colonial port cities specifically to hold it, and in a great many places a cold drink was, for the first time ever, a thing that could be bought.\n\nThe reason it worked is the same reason this recipe tells you to crush the ice at the last second. Melting happens only at the SURFACE of a piece of ice, and a large block has a tiny amount of surface for the amount of ice inside it. Halve the size of every piece and you double the surface area for the same weight; crush a cube into eighths and you have multiplied its exposed surface eightfold, and it will vanish roughly that much faster. Tudor was not insulating his cargo so much as exploiting scale: a 100kg block is, in effect, insulated by its own bulk, and if you sail fast enough you arrive with most of it. The trade died around the turn of the twentieth century, killed off entirely by mechanical refrigeration, and it is now almost completely forgotten — which is a strange fate for an industry that once shipped frozen pond water halfway around the world at a profit. The next time you decide whether to use cubes or crushed, you are making the same calculation Tudor made, on a scale of about one glass.\n\n🌱 One growing note, because it is the ingredient in this recipe you are least likely to find and most likely to succeed with: pandan. It is nearly impossible to buy fresh here and it is genuinely easy to grow — a clumping, spiky-leaved plant that is happy in a large pot in warm shade, needs regular water and almost nothing else, and multiplies by throwing out offsets you can pull off and pot up. It will not survive a hard Highveld frost, so it wants to come under cover or onto a stoep in winter. One plant supplies a household indefinitely, and there is no dried or bottled version that comes close to a fresh leaf.","howThisFeels":"Cold coconut milk running down through crushed ice and finding the avocado at the bottom, and the glass going misty in your hand while you carry it.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover sweetened coconut liquid straight over ice on its own is a perfectly good cold drink and takes no work at all.","(easy) The same liquid poured over cubed fruit and frozen in a tray makes a rough granita — scrape it with a fork every half hour and it comes out flaky rather than solid.","(medium) Blitz any leftover liquid with a very ripe banana and freeze it in moulds for an outstanding ice lolly, which is where a household with children should be sending this in the first place.","(creative) Use the coconut liquid instead of milk in a pancake or serabi batter the next morning — the palm sugar caramelises in the pan and gives you something far better than the batter you would otherwise have made."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Es Teler Santan — Coconut and Palm Sugar Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"Darker, less sweet, tasting much more of coconut, and somehow more grown-up than the tinned-milk version.","trivia":"Condensed milk is doing three separate jobs in the standard glass and it is worth knowing all three before you take it out, because you have to replace each one. It sweetens; it thickens, since it is milk with about 60% of its water removed; and it adds dairy fat, which is what makes the liquid cling to a spoon rather than run off it. Palm sugar covers the sweetness and brings a caramel, slightly smoky depth that condensed milk does not have. Extra coconut milk covers the fat, and coconut milk is a good deal fattier than most people assume — the thick tinned kind is comparable to single cream. Body is the one that needs a decision: nothing here thickens the way reduced milk does, so this version is honestly thinner, and rather than fight that the sensible move is to use less ice and serve it in a smaller glass. It is not a lesser dessert. It is a cleaner and more coconut-forward one, and in Indonesia it long predates the tin.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40ml condensed milk"},{"item":"60ml coconut water"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml coconut milk","to":"120ml coconut milk"},{"from":"20g jaggery","to":"30g jaggery"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No condensed milk and no coconut water — 120ml of coconut milk and 30g of jaggery instead, and use the thick tinned kind rather than a carton drink, which is mostly water. ✅ Make the syrup properly for this version, because it is now carrying all the sweetness: dissolve the 30g of jaggery in 30ml of hot water, then keep it on a low heat for 2 minutes until it darkens very slightly and smells faintly of caramel, then cool it completely. That short cook is the difference between sweet and interesting. ⚠️ Add a small pinch of salt to the coconut milk — no more than a couple of grains between your fingers. Coconut milk without dairy alongside it tastes flat and slightly chalky, and salt is what lifts it; this is a seasoning move, not a flavour, and it should not be detectable as salty. ⛔ Use LESS ice than the main version, about two-thirds, and a smaller glass. Without condensed milk the liquid is thinner and the same volume of ice will thin it past the point of pleasure."}]}},{"name":"Es Teler — Chilled Cold, Assembled at the Last Second","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Handing somebody a misted glass and watching them find the avocado with the spoon and look up.","trivia":"Avocado in a sweet drink is the thing that stops people in their tracks, and the surprise is entirely a matter of where you grew up. Avocado is a fruit — botanically a large single-seeded berry — and it is very sweet-adjacent in most of the world's kitchens: it turns up in milkshakes across Brazil, in ice cream in Vietnam and the Philippines, in sweetened juices throughout Indonesia, and as a dessert ingredient far more often than as a savoury one. The savoury-only convention is a fairly local habit that spread from Mexican and then North American cooking, and it is the historical outlier rather than the rule. What makes it work in a glass is that avocado has almost no sugar and almost no acid and a great deal of fat — about 15% by weight, most of it unsaturated — which is nearly the composition of cream. So it does not taste like fruit in the mouth. It behaves like dairy, which is exactly the job it has been given here.","delta":null},{"name":"Es Teler Mangga — with Ripe Mango","icon":"🥭","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Three soft things in one glass — avocado, mango and coconut — and the pineapple being the only thing with an edge on it.","trivia":"Adding mango is not a South African improvisation; mango turns up in Indonesian iced desserts constantly, and here it is closer in spirit to the missing jackfruit than pineapple is. Ripe mango and ripe jackfruit share a chemical family in their aroma — both lean heavily on esters, the compounds responsible for what we read as tropical, fruity, faintly sweet-scented smells, and both carry a distinct note that sits somewhere near banana. That is why a glass with mango in it smells much more like the real thing than one with pineapple alone, even though pineapple is closer on texture. The catch is that mango is soft where jackfruit is fibrous, so this version is noticeably more yielding overall — which is why the pineapple stays in rather than being replaced. Keep at least one thing in the glass that has some resistance to it, or the whole dessert becomes one uninterrupted soft mouthful and stops being interesting halfway down.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g mango"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add 80g of ripe mango, cut into rough 1.5cm pieces like the pineapple, and keep the pineapple rather than swapping it out — the glass needs one component with resistance in it. ✅ Choose the mango by smell rather than by squeeze: a ripe one is fragrant at the stem end and gives slightly under gentle pressure at the shoulders, and a mango that smells of nothing will taste of nothing however soft it is. ⏱️ Cut it no more than an hour ahead; mango loses its perfume to the air surprisingly fast once cut, and it is the aroma you are buying here. ⚠️ Mango brings noticeably more sugar than pineapple does, so cut the jaggery in the syrup from 20g to 12g and taste the jug before you commit — this version tips into cloying more easily than any other on the card. Layer it in the glass with the avocado at the bottom, mango and pineapple over it, then the ice and the liquid."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Es Cendol","target":"indonesia-es-cendol","emoji":"🥤"},{"name":"Klepon","target":"indonesia-klepon","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Rujak","target":"indonesia-rujak","emoji":"🍍"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ayam-taliwang","name":"Ayam Taliwang","nameAlt":"Lombok chicken, flattened and glazed on the fire","aliases":["Ayam Taliwang","Ayam Bakar Taliwang","Lombok Grilled Chicken","Taliwang Chicken","Spatchcock Chilli Chicken","Ayam Taliwang Pelalah"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","grill","street-food","braai","comfort"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["braai","family","weekend","guests","special","summer"],"ingredients":"220g whole chicken · 25g shallots · 20g birds eye chillies · 20g tomatoes · 15ml sunflower oil · 12g garlic · 10g jaggery · 10ml lime juice · 8g galangal · 4g shrimp paste · 4g salt","method":"⚖️ A CHICKEN IS THE WRONG SHAPE FOR A FIRE, and almost everything that goes wrong with grilled chicken comes from ignoring that. It is a lopsided object made of two meats that want different things: the breast is thin, pale, lean and finished at around 65°C, while the thigh is thick, dark, worked muscle full of connective tissue that only becomes properly good somewhere past 75°C. Put a whole bird over coals and you are asking one fire to hit two targets that are ten degrees and several centimetres apart. Something is always wrong. Ayam Taliwang's answer is to stop treating it as a bird at all and make it one flat thing of roughly one thickness — and then everything else on this card follows from that decision.\n\nBUTTERFLY IT, AND CUT THE BACKBONE OUT RATHER THAN CUTTING THROUGH THE BREAST. Sit the bird breast-down on a board. With sturdy kitchen scissors, cut all the way up one side of the backbone and then all the way up the other, and lift the backbone out — keep it for stock, it is the best part of the carcass for that. Turn the bird over, put the heel of your hand on the breastbone and press down firmly until you hear it give. It will crack, and that is the point: the keel bone is what holds the bird in its A-shape, and once it is broken the whole thing lies flat. ✅ Then make a small cut on the inside of each thigh, right down to the bone. That one small cut does a disproportionate amount of work — it opens the thickest, slowest part of the bird to the heat so that it stops being the piece everyone is waiting for.\n\nTHE RESULT SHOULD BE ROUGHLY ONE THICKNESS ALL OVER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE ARGUMENT. A flattened bird is about 2.5 to 3cm at the thigh and not far off that across the breast, so the two now finish within a few minutes of each other instead of fifteen. It also cooks in around half the time of a whole roasted bird for exactly the same reason — heat has half as far to travel. ⚠️ Which is why the SIZE of the chicken matters more here than in any other recipe on this card. Taliwang is made with a small young bird; flatten a big supermarket broiler and the thigh is still 4cm thick, which no amount of butterflying rescues over direct coals. ✅ Buy the smallest whole chicken you can find, and if the only birds available are large, cut it into pieces and grill those instead — a jointed bird is an honest substitute and a blackened one is not.\n\nSALT IT AND OIL IT, AND PUT NOTHING ELSE ON IT YET. Rub the flattened bird all over with the 4g of salt and the 15ml of sunflower oil, and leave it at room temperature for 30 minutes while you make the paste and light the fire. ⛔ THE CHILLI PASTE DOES NOT GO ON NOW. This is the single most common way this dish is ruined and it is worth understanding rather than obeying, so it gets its own paragraph below.\n\nMAKE THE PASTE. Pound or blitz the 25g of shallots, the 20g of bird's eye chillies, the 20g of tomatoes, the 12g of garlic, the 8g of galangal and the 4g of shrimp paste to a coarse red paste. ✅ Kencur — a small aromatic rhizome — is what a Lombok kitchen would use here and it is not available in South Africa; galangal stands in one for one, which is Tina's ruling and it holds across the file. Fry the paste in a small dry-ish pan over medium heat for 5 minutes until it darkens and loses its raw edge, then stir in the 10g of jaggery and the 10ml of lime juice off the heat. It should be thick enough to sit on a spoon and just loose enough to brush. Set it aside.\n\n⚖️ NOW THE REASON THE PASTE WAITS, AND IT IS ARITHMETIC. That paste is roughly a third sugar and shallot by weight, and sugars caramelise and then burn somewhere around 160 to 170°C. The surface of a grate directly over hot coals is well past 300°C. So a sugared surface over direct fire has SECONDS of margin, not minutes — while the thigh underneath it needs twenty-five minutes to get to 75°C. Put the paste on at the start and the arithmetic is simply impossible: you will have a black, bitter, acrid crust wrapped around raw thigh meat, and there is no way to cook your way out of it. ⛔ This is different from Ayam Betutu, where a huge volume of paste goes on before a long slow cook — that works precisely because the heat there is low and indirect and never reaches burning temperature. Over direct coals it cannot.\n\nCOOK THE BIRD NAKED FIRST — SKIN DOWN, WEIGHTED. Get your coals to a medium heat: you should be able to hold a hand 15cm above the grate for about five seconds, no longer. Lay the bird skin-side down and put a weight on it — a foil-wrapped brick, a heavy pan, a flat stone. Leave it 12 to 15 minutes, then turn it and give the bone side 10 to 12 minutes.\n\n✅ AND THE WEIGHT IS NOT ABOUT SQUASHING IT, IT IS ABOUT CONTACT. An unweighted flattened bird still touches the grate in a few high spots and arches away everywhere else, so you get dark stripes and pale valleys and a great deal of the skin never really cooks. Pressed down, the skin is in continuous contact — and contact conducts heat far faster and far more evenly than the radiant heat crossing the gap does. It is the same reason a toasted sandwich in a press browns evenly and one in a pan does not. The pressed skin also renders its fat properly instead of steaming, which is the difference between crisp and rubbery.\n\nGLAZE TWICE, RIGHT AT THE END, AND WATCH IT LIKE A HAWK. When the bird is nearly done — the juices at the thigh running clear, or a thermometer reading 70°C at the thickest part — brush a generous coat of paste over the bone side and give it 2 minutes. Turn it, brush the skin side, and give that 2 minutes. Then brush the skin side ONCE MORE and give it only 60 to 90 seconds, just long enough to set and catch in a few places. ⚠️ Move the bird to a cooler part of the fire for the glazing if your coals are fierce, and stay standing over it. The moment you smell anything sharp or acrid rather than sweet and toasty, it is going past caramelised and you have about ten seconds. ✅ Two thin coats are far better than one thick one: a thick layer stays wet underneath, slides off in the eating and never sets, while two thin coats bond to the skin and to each other.\n\nREST IT 5 MINUTES, THEN CUT IT UP AND SPOON THE REST OF THE PASTE OVER. Take it off, let it sit five minutes on a warm plate — it is a lean small bird and five minutes is enough — then cut it into pieces through the joints. Any paste left in the pan goes over the top at the table, unheated, where it is bright and sharp rather than caramelised. ✅ Serve it with plain rice and something raw, cold and green beside it. It is a very hot, very sweet, very savoury piece of chicken and it needs an ally.","cookTime":"Butterfly 10 min · Salt rest 30 min · Grill 25 min · Glaze 6 min · Rest 5 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g · Carbs 14g · Fat 28g · Sodium ~1500mg","storage":"It keeps 3 days in the fridge and it is one of the better things to find in there cold — a glazed grilled bird eats very well straight from the fridge, and the paste firms up into something closer to a relish clinging to the skin. ⚠️ Reheating is where it can go wrong: the glaze is mostly sugar, so a hot oven or a pan will take it from caramelised to burnt in the time it takes the meat to warm through. Use a moderate oven, 160°C, covered with foil, for about 12 minutes, and take the foil off only for the last two if you want the skin back. ⛔ Never reheat it over direct coals or under a grill — you would be putting an already-caramelised surface back over 300°C with nothing left to lose. ✅ The paste on its own keeps 2 weeks in a sealed jar in the fridge under a thin film of oil, and it is worth making triple: it is a finished condiment as it stands, and having a jar of it turns any grilled meat into most of this dish. It freezes in an ice-cube tray for 6 months. The cooked chicken freezes for 2 months, but the glaze goes slightly grainy and dull on thawing, so freeze it only if you plan to shred it rather than serve it whole.","chefNotes":"A chicken is the wrong shape for a fire — the breast is done at 65°C and the thigh wants past 75°C, and no single fire hits both. Butterfly it, crack the keel bone flat, and slash the inside of each thigh to the bone: now it is one thickness and the two finish together. Use the SMALLEST bird you can find, because flattening a big broiler still leaves a 4cm thigh that direct coals cannot reach. Weight it while it cooks — that is about CONTACT, not compression: pressed skin conducts heat evenly and renders instead of steaming. And the paste is a third sugar, which burns at 160°C over a 300°C grate, so it goes on only in the last six minutes, in two thin coats rather than one thick one. Stand over it. When it smells sharp instead of toasty you have about ten seconds.","pairsWith":"In Lombok this arrives with plecing kangkung beside it and the pairing is close to a rule rather than a suggestion — cold, sharp, raw-tasting greens against a hot sweet glazed bird, and each one makes the other make sense. Plain white rice is essential and not negotiable: the glaze is intense enough that a plate of this on its own is exhausting after four mouthfuls, and rice is what turns it into a meal rather than a challenge. A raw sliced sambal or a cold pickle alongside works for the same reason. ⛔ Do not serve it with anything else sweet, anything else glazed, or anything else fiercely chilli-hot — the plate loses all its shape and everything tastes like the loudest thing on it. And do not serve it with a rich coconut dish; this is already a big, oily, sweet piece of food and it wants sharpness beside it, not more weight.","goesWith":["Plecing Kangkung","Sambal Matah","Nasi Uduk","Acar Kuning"],"trivia":"Lombok, where this dish comes from, has the strangest harvest in Indonesia, and it happens once a year on a date that a lunar calendar predicted for centuries before anybody understood the biology behind it.\n\nOn one morning in February or March, thousands of people wade into the sea off Lombok's south coast before dawn, carrying nets and buckets, to catch nyale — thin, brightly coloured, wriggling sea worms that appear at the surface in extraordinary numbers for a few hours and then are gone for another year. They are gathered by the bucketful, and they are eaten: fried, wrapped in banana leaf and grilled, or stirred into a savoury dish with chilli and coconut. The festival is called Bau Nyale, catching the nyale, and it is one of the biggest events in the Sasak calendar.\n\nThe biology is stranger than the festival. What surfaces is not the animal. Palolo worms live in crevices in the reef, and when the time comes each one grows a specialised rear section packed entirely with eggs or sperm — and then that section DETACHES and swims to the surface on its own to spawn, while the front half of the worm stays safely in the reef and simply grows a new one for next year. The sea fills with millions of independently swimming reproductive halves. And the timing is the remarkable part: the swarming is locked to the lunar cycle with a precision that lets it be predicted a year in advance, to within a day or two, and the Sasak lunar calendar has been calling the date for generations. It is the same phenomenon that makes the palolo rise a fixed festival in Samoa and Fiji as well, thousands of kilometres away and on a different month — every population reading its own moon. The Sasak explanation is a legend about a princess called Mandalika who threw herself into the sea rather than choose between the princes competing for her, and returned as the worms so that everyone could have her. Which is, if you think about it, not a bad description of what is actually happening out there.\n\n🌱 A smaller thought, on the garlic in the paste: garlic is planted from a clove rather than a seed, in autumn, pointy end up, about 5cm down — and each clove becomes a whole bulb by early summer. That means once you have a variety you like, you can keep the best bulbs back each season and plant your own line indefinitely without ever buying garlic again. It is one of the few crops where a single once-off purchase genuinely is the last one.","howThisFeels":"The crack of the keel bone giving way under your hand, and knowing from that sound alone that the rest of the evening is going to work.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold from the fridge with rice and a squeeze of lime, which is arguably better than it was hot and needs nothing done to it.","(easy) The meat pulled off and stirred through fried rice at the very end, where the sticky glaze coats the grains and does most of the seasoning for you.","(medium) The stripped carcass and the backbone you saved simmered for 90 minutes into a stock that already carries chilli, galangal and shallot — a soup base with a head start that you would struggle to build on purpose.","(creative) Shredded into a cold salad with raw shallot, cucumber and a spoonful of the leftover paste loosened with lime juice, which turns a heavy grilled dish into something light enough for a hot afternoon."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Ayam Taliwang Wajan — Heavy Pan, No Fire (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"A brick wrapped in foil sitting on top of a chicken in a frying pan, which looks ridiculous and works perfectly.","trivia":"Cooking this indoors is not a lesser version so much as a more controllable one, and the reason is the very thing that makes charcoal difficult: a fire has one temperature and you cannot turn it down. A heavy pan on a dial gives you something coals never will — the ability to drop the heat the moment the glaze goes on, which is precisely the moment this dish is most likely to be lost. What you give up is smoke, and it is worth being honest that smoke is a real ingredient here rather than a romantic detail. Grilling over coals deposits phenolic compounds on the surface that genuinely change the flavour and cannot be faked; a pinch of smoked paprika gets you a suggestion of it and no more. So the trade is a real one in both directions: outdoors you get flavour you cannot otherwise have, indoors you get control you cannot otherwise have, and on a weeknight the second one is often worth more.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"220g whole chicken","to":"180g whole chicken"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"4g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A smaller bird, no shrimp paste, and no fire at all. Use the heaviest frying pan you own, ideally cast iron, over a medium heat with a thin film of the oil. ✅ Lay the flattened bird skin-side down and weight it with a foil-wrapped brick or a second heavy pan — the weight matters even more here than over coals, because a pan is perfectly flat and rewards full contact absolutely. Give it 14 to 16 minutes skin-side down without moving it at all, then turn and give the bone side 12 minutes with the weight back on. ⚠️ Fat will render out and the pan will get busy; pour some off into a cup if it starts spitting, and keep the extractor on. ✅ Then TURN THE HEAT DOWN to low before the paste goes anywhere near the pan, which is the whole advantage of doing it this way — glaze, 2 minutes, turn, glaze, 2 minutes, and a final thin coat for 1 minute. Without the shrimp paste the paste is cleaner and sharper and a little less deep; add an extra 2g of salt to compensate for the seasoning it was carrying."}]}},{"name":"Ayam Taliwang — Butterflied, Weighted, Glazed Twice","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"A flat bird lying under a brick over coals, and a smell of caramelising chilli that brings the neighbours to the fence.","trivia":"The dish is named after a place that is smaller than you would expect from how famous it became — Karang Taliwang, a neighbourhood in the city of Mataram on Lombok, rather than a region or a people. Almost every dish in this section is named for an island, an ethnic group, an ingredient or a technique; this one is named after a few streets. What it points to is how much Indonesian food is genuinely LOCAL in origin before it is national: a recipe belonging to one kampung, sold from one cluster of warungs, that happened to be good enough to travel. The other thing the name marks is a boundary. Lombok cooking is noticeably fiercer and more chilli-forward than Balinese cooking immediately to its west, despite the islands being visible from one another, and Taliwang is where a lot of people first notice that. Two islands separated by about 35km of water, and a genuinely different threshold for what counts as hot.","delta":null},{"name":"Ayam Taliwang Pelalah — Double Chilli, Shorter Glaze","icon":"🔥","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":43,"howThisFeels":"A glaze that goes from red to dark in the time it takes to turn around, and knowing you have to stay put.","trivia":"Pelalah is the Lombok word for the fierce version and it is worth knowing that the extra chilli changes the COOKING and not just the eating, which is why it needs its own instructions. A paste with twice the chilli in it has more solid matter and proportionally less water, and water is what protects a glaze — as long as there is moisture evaporating out of the surface, the sugars underneath are held near 100°C and cannot caramelise. A drier paste loses that shield much sooner, so it sets faster, catches faster and burns faster, and a window that was four minutes becomes closer to two. The same thing is true of any thick sauce you have reduced hard before glazing with it, which is a useful general lesson hiding inside a very specific dish: the drier the coating, the shorter your margin, and reducing a glaze to make it cling is also reducing the time you have to work with it.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"20g birds eye chillies","to":"40g birds eye chillies"},{"from":"8g galangal","to":"12g galangal"},{"from":"4g shrimp paste","to":"6g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Double the chillies to 40g, and go up on the galangal and shrimp paste to keep the paste in balance rather than letting it become pure heat. ⚠️ Fry the paste a little longer — 7 minutes rather than 5 — because there is more solid matter to cook the rawness out of, and a raw chilli edge is far more obvious at this concentration. ⛔ THEN SHORTEN EVERY GLAZE STEP. The window drops from 2 minutes to about 75 seconds per coat, and the final setting coat to 40 seconds, because a drier paste has less water shielding its sugars and goes from set to burnt much faster. Move the bird to the coolest part of the fire before the first coat goes on rather than after. ✅ Have the plate ready and the tongs in your hand before you start glazing this version — there is genuinely no time to go and fetch anything, and the difference between the best version of this dish and a bitter one is about fifteen seconds of attention."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Plecing Kangkung","target":"indonesia-plecing-kangkung","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Sambal Matah","target":"indonesia-sambal-matah","emoji":"🌶️"},{"name":"Ayam Betutu","target":"indonesia-ayam-betutu","emoji":"🍗"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sayur-lodeh","name":"Sayur Lodeh","nameAlt":"Javanese vegetables in thin and thick coconut","aliases":["Sayur Lodeh","Sayur Lodhe","Javanese Coconut Vegetable Stew","Lodeh","Coconut Vegetable Soup","Sayur Lodeh Jawa"],"course":"side","type":["side","soup","vegetables","one-pot","comfort","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["everyday","family","budget","lunch","comfort","meat-free"],"ingredients":"300ml water · 150ml coconut milk · 80g baby marrow · 60g young jackfruit · 60g green beans · 60g aubergines · 40g cabbage · 20g shallots · 10g garlic · 10g red chilli · 10g lemongrass · 8g galangal · 5g jaggery · 4g salt · 4g shrimp paste · 2g bay leaves","method":"Most vegetable pots are ordered by hardness — the slow things first, the quick things last. This one is not, and that is the thing to understand before you start. ⚖️ IN A COCONUT POT THE RUNNING ORDER IS SET BY WHAT EACH VEGETABLE DOES TO THE COCONUT, NOT BY HOW LONG IT TAKES TO SOFTEN. Get that the wrong way round and you end up with a pot of perfectly cooked vegetables sitting in a broken, oily, curdled liquid, which is the only way this dish really fails.\n\n⚖️ THE HARDNESS QUESTION IS ALREADY ANSWERED ELSEWHERE. The Gado-Gado card sets out staggered vegetable timing in full and it applies here without modification, so I am not going to repeat it — assume it, and pay attention instead to the second running order sitting on top of it, which is the one nobody writes down.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY BREAKS COCONUT MILK IS SALT AND ACID, NOT HEAT ALONE. Coconut milk is fat held in suspension by coconut proteins, and those proteins keep the fat droplets apart because they carry an electrical charge that makes them repel one another. Two things cancel that charge. **Acid** neutralises it directly. **Salt** screens it — dissolved ions crowd around the proteins and mask the charge, so the droplets stop pushing each other away. Either one on its own makes the emulsion far more fragile; either one COMBINED with heat and it breaks readily, and once it has broken into an oil slick over thin grey water it does not come back. ✅ So the two most dangerous ingredients in this pot are the two most innocent-looking: the salt and the tomato. Salt goes in at the END. Anything sour goes in at the end or not at all.\n\nUSE THE COCONUT TWICE, IN TWO DIFFERENT STRENGTHS. THIS IS THE JAVANESE ANSWER. Split the 150ml of coconut milk: put 100ml into a jug with the 300ml of water and stir — that is your thin coconut, santan encer, and it is the cooking liquid. Keep the remaining 50ml back, undiluted, as the thick coconut, santan kental, and it goes in at the very end. ⚖️ The reason is dilution as protection: thin coconut is mostly water with a little fat in it, so there is very little emulsion to shear apart and it will take a long simmer quite happily. Thick coconut is a concentrated emulsion and would break under the same treatment in minutes. Two strengths, two jobs — one cooks the vegetables, the other enriches the finished pot — and it is why a proper lodeh tastes far more of coconut than the amount in it would suggest.\n\nBRUISE THE AROMATICS AND START THE THIN COCONUT. Flatten the 10g of lemongrass and the 8g of galangal under a knife, slice the 20g of shallots, 10g of garlic and 10g of red chilli, and crumble in the 4g of shrimp paste. Put all of it into a pot with the thin coconut and the 2g of bay leaves, and bring it to a bare simmer. ⛔ AND DO NOT PUT A LID ON IT. A lid on a coconut pot does two bad things: it drips condensed water back onto the surface, and more importantly it traps heat so the liquid boils harder than you think it is, and localised boiling against the base is exactly where an emulsion shears. Lid off, the whole way, and stir every couple of minutes. That stirring is not about sticking either — it is about not letting any one part of the pot get much hotter than the rest.\n\nNOW THE VEGETABLES, HARDEST FIRST. The 60g of young jackfruit goes in first and gets 15 minutes — it is dense and starchy and it is the slowest thing here by a distance. ⚠️ Make sure you have bought young or green jackfruit in brine or water, which is a savoury vegetable, and not ripe jackfruit in syrup, which is a sweet fruit; the tins look similar and the mistake is unrecoverable. Then the 60g of green beans cut short and the 60g of aubergines in 2cm cubes for 8 minutes, then the 80g of baby marrow in half-moons for 4, then the 40g of cabbage shredded for the last 2. The whole time, lid off, bare simmer, occasional stir.\n\n⚠️ AND KEEP AN EYE ON THE AUBERGINE, WHICH IS THE SNEAKY ONE. Aubergine drinks liquid rather than releasing it, so a pot that looked right when it went in can be noticeably thick and short of liquid ten minutes later — and a thicker pot heats less evenly and is more likely to catch and break. Top it up with a splash of hot water if it tightens, not cold, and not more coconut.\n\nSEASON AT THE VERY END, AND THIS IS THE STEP THE WHOLE RECIPE HAS BEEN PROTECTING. Off the heat, or with the pot barely ticking, stir in the reserved 50ml of thick coconut, the 5g of jaggery and the 4g of salt. Let it come back to steaming — no further — and taste it. ✅ Adjust the salt now, in small pinches, tasting between each. ⛔ It is genuinely worth resisting the urge to salt earlier, even though everything in you will want to, because salt at the start turns a forty-minute pot into a coin toss. If it needs more depth rather than more salt, a little more crumbled shrimp paste dissolved in a spoonful of hot liquid will do it without adding sodium to the pot at the wrong moment.\n\nIF IT DOES SPLIT, HERE IS THE HONEST TRUTH ABOUT FIXING IT. You will see a thin sheen of clear oil separating out and the liquid underneath going watery and slightly grey. ⛔ It cannot be fully rescued and anyone who tells you otherwise is selling something. What you CAN do is take it off the heat immediately, let it cool for five minutes, and whisk in a tablespoon of fresh thick coconut milk vigorously off the heat — the new intact emulsion will carry some of the loose oil back into suspension and the pot will look and eat considerably better. It will not be as good as one that never split. Serve it and say nothing.\n\nEAT IT WARM, NOT HOT, WITH RICE. Lodeh is a side dish and a very gentle one — mild, sweet, savoury, soft — and it exists to sit beside things with more edge than it has. It is better after ten minutes off the stove, and it is better still the next day.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Aromatics 5 min · Vegetables 30 min · Finish 3 min","kcal":"~290 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 6g · Carbs 21g · Fat 20g · Sodium ~1300mg","storage":"It keeps 3 days in the fridge and it is one of those pots that genuinely improves overnight — the aromatics finish dissolving into the fat and the whole thing rounds out. ⚠️ REHEAT IT GENTLY AND LID OFF, exactly as you cooked it. Everything that could break the emulsion on the stove can break it again in the reheating, and by now the salt is already in there, so a cold pot is MORE fragile than it was during cooking, not less. Medium-low, stirred, taken off the moment it steams. ⛔ A microwave is the wrong tool: it heats in fierce local hot spots, which is precisely the condition that shears a coconut emulsion, and you will take a good pot out of the fridge and a broken one out of the machine. ✅ The fat will set into a firm pale layer on top when it is cold — that is normal and it should be stirred back in as it warms rather than lifted off, since most of the aromatic flavour has dissolved into it. It does not freeze: frozen coconut milk separates on thawing and every vegetable in here is a soft high-water one whose cell walls burst, so you would thaw a pot of oily water with rags in it. If it smells sour or fizzy rather than sweet and coconutty, it goes out.","chefNotes":"In a coconut pot the running order is set by what each vegetable does to the COCONUT, not by how hard it is. What breaks coconut milk is salt and acid — both cancel the electrical charge that keeps the fat droplets apart — so salt goes in at the very END and anything sour goes in last or not at all. Use the coconut twice at two strengths: thin (diluted with the water) as the long-simmering cooking liquid, because there is barely any emulsion to shear; thick and undiluted stirred in off the heat at the end. Lid OFF the whole time — a lid traps heat, and localised boiling against the base is where an emulsion breaks. Watch the aubergine, which drinks liquid rather than releasing it. And if it does split, it cannot be fully rescued.","pairsWith":"It is the mild, soft, forgiving thing on a Javanese plate and it wants loud company — rice first, then something fried, salty or fiercely chilli-hot. Fried tempeh beside it is the standard everyday pairing and the contrast of crisp against soft is most of the pleasure. A spoonful of sambal on the rim rather than stirred in lets each person decide how much edge they want, which matters because lodeh itself is deliberately gentle. It also sits well beside grilled or fried chicken, where its sweetness balances char. ⛔ What it cannot do is share a plate with another coconut dish — two santan pots at one meal and everything tastes the same, and a rich curry beside it makes the lodeh disappear entirely. It also does not want a sour dish next to it, which is a shame because they are natural partners in theory; in practice one sour side per plate, and if you are serving a tamarind soup, serve this another day.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Tempe Goreng","Sambal Terasi","Ayam Goreng Kalasan"],"trivia":"The coconut sitting in this pot is one of the few crops whose DNA can be read as a shipping record, and what it says is that the whole thing happened twice.\n\nWhen researchers genotyped well over a thousand coconut palms from across the tropics, they expected a single domestication with everything else radiating out from it. What they found instead were two clearly distinct genetic populations, which means coconuts were brought under cultivation independently in two separate places — one in the Pacific basin, one around the Indian Ocean, in the region of India and Sri Lanka. Two groups of people, with no knowledge of each other, looked at the same wild tree and did the same work of selecting and planting it, and the difference between their results is still legible in a nut you can buy today.\n\nWhat makes it a shipping record is what happened afterwards. The Pacific type travels with the Austronesian expansion, the extraordinary seaborne movement of people that populated the islands from Taiwan through the Philippines and Indonesia and eventually out to Polynesia and Madagascar — the palm went in the canoes because a coconut is food, drink, rope, roofing and a container all at once, which is close to a survival kit for an ocean crossing. The Indian Ocean type moves the other way, carried west along Arab and Indian trade routes to East Africa. And then there is a lovely anomaly: coconuts on the Caribbean side of Central America turn out to carry Pacific ancestry rather than Atlantic, which fits Spanish ships bringing them across from the Philippines on the Manila route and planting them on the far side. A tree's genome recording a specific colonial shipping lane is not a thing you expect from a soup ingredient.\n\nThe reason the genetics was needed at all is the best part. Coconuts FLOAT, and a fallen nut can survive months at sea and germinate on a beach a thousand kilometres away entirely on its own. So for a long time nobody could separate where humans had carried the coconut from where the ocean had simply taken it — the plant's own dispersal ability was hiding the human story underneath. Only the genetics could tell the two apart, and it turns out that almost everywhere the coconut matters most, it arrived in a boat with somebody. 🌱 On a much smaller scale, the baby marrow in this pot is the single easiest thing to grow in a Pretoria garden and the one most likely to defeat you by succeeding — one healthy plant in a sunny bed will produce more than a household can eat, they double in size if you look away for two days, and every experienced gardener has a story about giving them away in bulk. Plant one. Not three.","howThisFeels":"Stirring the last of the thick coconut into a pot that has been quietly simmering all afternoon, and watching the whole thing turn pale and glossy in one go.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated gently and poured over rice with a fried egg on top, which is a complete lunch and the standard Javanese answer.","(easy) Blitzed smooth with a stick blender into a thick coconut vegetable soup — the split-risk disappears once it is blended, and a pot that has gone slightly too soft is improved by it.","(medium) Drained of most of its liquid and used as a filling for a pancake or a folded roti, with the liquid served alongside to dip.","(creative) Reduce the strained liquid hard in a wide pan until it is thick and the oil separates on purpose, then fry the drained vegetables back into it — you have deliberately broken it, which turns a soft stew into a rich dry coconut vegetable dish and is a genuinely different meal."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sayur Lodeh Polos — Garden Vegetables Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"Whatever was in the vegetable drawer, a tin of coconut milk, and a pot that tastes like somebody planned it.","trivia":"This is what lodeh actually is in most Javanese kitchens on most days — not a fixed recipe but a method applied to whatever is in the house, which is why no two versions of it agree and why arguing about the correct vegetables misses the point entirely. The dish is a technique with a shopping list attached loosely. What that means practically is that the substitution rules are generous in a way almost nothing else in this section is: anything with a firm flesh that holds its shape works in the early slot, anything soft and leafy works in the last two minutes, and the pot will be good. The one real constraint is not to use anything sour — no tomato, no unripe fruit, nothing pickled — because the acid is what threatens the coconut, and that constraint is exactly the same whether you are cooking a careful version or clearing out a fridge.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g young jackfruit"},{"item":"4g shrimp paste"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"40g cabbage","to":"60g cabbage"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No jackfruit and no shrimp paste, and a little more cabbage to make up the bulk. ✅ Losing the jackfruit also loses the 15-minute opening stage, so this version is a genuinely quicker pot — start with the green beans and aubergine and you are finished in about 18 minutes rather than 35. ⚠️ Losing the shrimp paste costs more than it looks like it should, because terasi was carrying the savoury depth rather than just seasoning. Replace it with 40g of mushrooms sliced and added with the beans: mushrooms are loaded with free glutamates and they do a recognisably similar job in a coconut pot, which is why they turn up in vegan versions of half the dishes in this file. ⛔ Do NOT compensate with extra salt instead — salt is the thing this recipe spends forty minutes protecting the coconut from, and more of it at the end will make the pot taste seasoned rather than deep, which is a different problem entirely."}]}},{"name":"Sayur Lodeh — Thin Coconut First, Thick Coconut Last","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A pot with no lid on it all afternoon, stirred every few minutes, filling the house with something soft and sweet.","trivia":"Sayur lodeh has a second life in Yogyakarta as something closer to a civic instruction than a recipe. There is a long-standing Javanese tradition of the palace calling for households across the city to cook a lodeh made with a specific set of vegetables at times of pestilence or upheaval — a shared act performed simultaneously in thousands of kitchens, with each vegetable in the list carrying its own symbolic reading. It was invoked during epidemics in the past and it was raised again in recent memory, and the striking thing about it is the choice of dish. Not a ceremony, not a procession, not something rare or expensive. A cheap everyday vegetable pot that nearly every household already knew how to make and already had most of the ingredients for. Whatever you think about the symbolism, the practical logic is hard to argue with: an instruction that everybody can actually follow, on the same evening, using what is already in the kitchen, is a genuinely effective way of making a scattered city do one thing together.","delta":null},{"name":"Sayur Lodeh Komplit — with Tempeh and Tofu","icon":"🟫","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":74,"howThisFeels":"Cubes of tofu that have been sitting in coconut for twenty minutes and taste of nothing but this pot.","trivia":"Adding tempeh and tofu turns a side dish into a main, and the two behave completely differently in the pot, which is worth knowing before you cut them. Tofu is a sponge with almost no flavour of its own, so it ABSORBS — twenty minutes in a coconut pot and a cube of it tastes more like the pot than the pot does, which is the whole reason it earns a place. Tempeh is the opposite: it is dense, fully colonised by mould through every gap, and it has a strong nutty savoury flavour that it CONTRIBUTES rather than takes on, and it stays firm and slightly chewy no matter how long it simmers. So one of them is there to carry the dish's flavour and the other to add its own, and putting both in gives you two entirely different textures from what looks like the same category of ingredient. The traditional move is to fry the tempeh first, which is not about crispness — nothing stays crisp in a wet pot — but about developing flavour that then leaches out into the liquid.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g firm tofu"},{"item":"50g tempeh"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add 60g of firm tofu and 50g of tempeh, both cut into 2cm cubes, and treat them differently because they are doing different jobs. ✅ Fry the tempeh first in a little oil over medium-high heat for about 4 minutes until it takes real colour on at least two faces — that browning develops flavour that then leaches into the pot, and it is worth the extra pan even though nothing here stays crisp. Add it to the pot with the green beans so it has 8 minutes to give that flavour up. ⚠️ The tofu goes in LAST, with the cabbage, for the final 2 minutes only, and handle it gently — it needs no cooking at all, only time to absorb, and stirring it in early breaks the cubes into crumbs that cloud the liquid. ✅ Press the tofu between two plates with a weight on top for 15 minutes before you cut it, which drives out water and leaves room for it to take the coconut up instead. This version is a main course rather than a side, so serve it with rice and nothing else needs to be on the table."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tempe Goreng","target":"indonesia-tempe-goreng","emoji":"🟫"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sayur Asem","target":"indonesia-sayur-asem","emoji":"🥣"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-bebek-goreng","name":"Bebek Goreng","nameAlt":"Madura fried duck, simmered soft then crisped in its own fat","aliases":["Bebek Goreng","Bebek Goreng Madura","Bebek Sinjay","Bebek Goreng Surabaya","Indonesian Fried Duck","Fried Duck","Bebek Goreng Sambal Pencit"],"course":"main","type":["main","duck","fried","braised","street-food","spiced","special"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","weekend","guests","special","sunday"],"ingredients":"200g duck legs · 300ml water · 20g shallots · 12g garlic · 10g galangal · 8g lemongrass · 8g macadamia nuts · 6g red chilli · 5g coriander seeds · 4g salt · 4g ground turmeric · 3g jaggery · 2 bay leaves","method":"⚖️ THE FAT IS UNDER THE SKIN, NOT ON IT — SO CRISPING A DUCK IS A DRAINING PROBLEM, NOT A BROWNING ONE. This is the single thing to understand before you light anything, because every instruction below follows from it. A duck is a waterfowl, and it carries a continuous layer of fat between the skin and the meat, 3 to 5mm thick, laid down as insulation and buoyancy. A chicken has nothing like it. And while that fat is still there, liquid and hot, the skin sitting on top of it is FLOATING — held a hair off the pan, with a layer of boiling fat underneath conducting heat away as fast as the pan delivers it. Boiling anything is pinned near 100°C. Browning does not begin properly until well past 140°C. So the skin cannot go brown, cannot go crisp, and will not, no matter how hot you make the pan or how long you leave it. You are not trying to cook the skin. You are trying to get rid of what is underneath it, and the browning happens by itself in the last four minutes once there is nothing left in the way.\n\n⚖️ AND IT IS A DIFFERENT PIN FROM AYAM POP'S. That card explains at length what a WET surface does to browning — water boiling off holds the surface at 100°C and no colour can form. Same physics here, different liquid: there the wetness is the whole dish and you exploit it, here the fat is the obstacle and you drain it. The argument is not repeated on this card. Go and read it there.\n\nSTART BY SIMMERING, AND UNDERSTAND WHY THE ORDER IS FIXED. Duck legs are working muscle in a way supermarket chicken simply is not — a duck walks, paddles and swims for its whole life, and its legs are dense with connective tissue. That is a good thing and it is also a clock: collagen only becomes gelatin under long, gentle, wet heat, and nothing in a frying pan will ever do it. Fry a raw duck leg and you get a chewy leg with a nice skin. So the leg is cooked in water first, and only then is it dried, rendered and browned. ⚖️ The Bubur Ayam card explains why a 35-day broiler has almost no collagen and gives thin stock; a duck is the exact opposite problem, and it wants time rather than a better bird.\n\nMAKE THE BUMBU FIRST, AND KEEP IT SHORT ON PURPOSE. Pound or blitz the 20g of shallots, the 12g of garlic, the 10g of galangal, the 8g of macadamia nuts, the 6g of red chilli, the 5g of coriander seeds toasted dry for a minute first, the 4g of ground turmeric and the 4g of salt into a rough paste. Slice the 8g of lemongrass thinly across the grain before it goes in, because it is woody and a blade will not break the fibres down lengthways. The macadamias stand in for candlenut and do what they do everywhere in this cooking — fat and starch for body, not flavour. ⚖️ This is a DELIBERATELY short paste, and it is worth saying why: Ayam Betutu is built on base genep, the complete spice base, at roughly a quarter of the weight of the meat. That is that dish. Here the duck is the loudest thing in the pan by a distance, and a huge paste would only be boiled away in the water. Betutu's card owns the big paste. This one does not compete with it.\n\nSIMMER 60 TO 75 MINUTES, AND NEVER LET IT BOIL. Put the 200g of duck legs into a pan just wide enough to hold them flat, add the bumbu, the 300ml of water, the 3g of jaggery and the 2 bay leaves, and bring it up to the barest tremble — a bubble every second or two, no more. Lid on, and leave it alone. ⚠️ A rolling boil does two things you cannot undo: it agitates the meat off the bone, and you need these legs INTACT to lie skin-down in a pan afterwards, and it churns the rendering fat into the liquid as an emulsion instead of letting it float clear where you can lift it off. ✅ It is done when a skewer slides into the thickest part of the thigh and meets no resistance at all — not \"a little\", none. Under-simmered duck cannot be rescued later. Top up with HOT water if it drops below halfway up the legs.\n\n⚖️ NOW THE LIQUID, AND THIS IS WHERE THIS CARD PARTS COMPANY WITH KALASAN. Ayam Goreng Kalasan is also a braise-then-fry, and there the braising LIQUID is the prize — reduced hard and turned into the crisp lace on top, which is the whole argument of that dish. ⛔ Do not do that here. This liquid is thinner, saltier and far fattier, and reduced it goes from savoury to harsh in about two minutes. Here the prize is the FAT. Lift the legs out onto a rack, pour the liquid into a bowl or a mug and put it in the fridge. In an hour the fat sets into a firm pale cap on top and lifts off in one piece — that is pure duck fat, and it is the best thing in this recipe that you did not have to buy. Under it is a savoury jelly that is proper stock. Both are covered in the storage note.\n\nDRY THE SKIN, AND GIVE IT LONGER THAN FEELS NECESSARY. Pat the legs completely dry, then leave them uncovered on a rack for at least 30 minutes — in the fridge overnight is better and is what I would do if there is time. Fridge air is dry and it pulls moisture out of the skin the way a drying room does. ⚠️ Surface water is simply one more thing your pan has to boil off before anything useful can start, and a duck leg straight out of a simmer is soaked.\n\nSCORE THE SKIN — THROUGH THE SKIN AND THE FAT, AND STOP AT THE MEAT. With the tip of a sharp knife, cut a lattice about 1cm apart across the skin side, at a shallow angle. ✅ You are cutting escape routes so the melting fat has somewhere to go instead of sitting in a sealed pocket. ⛔ Do not cut into the meat. The moment you break into muscle you have opened a channel for the juices to run out, and the leg will dry as it fries. The cue is feel rather than depth: skin and fat give softly under the blade, and meat is firm and grippy. If you are not confident, cut shallower — a light scoring that only reaches the fat is still doing most of the job.\n\n⛔ NOW THE INSTRUCTION THAT SOUNDS WRONG: PUT IT IN A COLD, DRY PAN. No oil — none, and there is none on the ingredient list. Lay the legs skin-side down in a cold heavy pan and only then turn the heat on, low. Every instinct says a hot pan, and a hot pan is exactly wrong here: fierce heat seizes and contracts the skin proteins immediately, sealing the surface shut and trapping the fat behind it, and you end up frying a leg with all of its insulation still inside. A cold start gives the fat a slow, unhurried head start while the skin is still open and soft, and it runs out before the skin sets.\n\nRENDER 10 TO 14 MINUTES ON LOW, AND POUR THE FAT OFF AS IT COMES. Keep the heat low enough that you hear a gentle, steady sizzle rather than a spit. Fat will pool fast. ✅ Pour it off into a heatproof cup three or four times as it collects — a 200g pair of legs gives up 30 to 50ml, which is a startling amount to see leave a piece of meat. This is not tidiness. Fat left in the pan means the skin is now shallow-frying in a puddle at boiling point, which is the pinned-at-100°C problem all over again, and it is the reason so much home-cooked duck skin comes out beige and rubbery. ⚠️ Keep the poured fat. It is the by-product that makes this dish quietly economical.\n\nTHE SKIN TELLS YOU WHEN IT IS READY, AND THE CUE IS THICKNESS, NOT COLOUR. Lift a leg with tongs every few minutes and look at it. At first the skin is thick, pale and slack. It will slowly go thinner, tighter and faintly translucent, and the fierce bubbling will quieten right down — that quietening is the sound of the last of the fat leaving, and it is the same acoustic cue Ayam Pop uses for the opposite purpose. ✅ From that moment you are finally past the pin, and colour arrives quickly.\n\nTURN THE HEAT UP AND BROWN IT — 3 TO 4 MINUTES, AND WATCH IT. Raise the heat to medium-high for the last few minutes and let the skin go properly deep gold and crisp, pressing the legs down with a spatula so the whole surface is in contact rather than arching away. ⚖️ Ayam Taliwang's card explains why contact browns and a gap does not, and the same is true in a pan. Then turn them over for 60 seconds on the flesh side, purely for colour. ⛔ Nothing here needs cooking — the duck was cooked an hour ago. If it browns unevenly, that is a contact problem, not a time problem, so press rather than wait.\n\nREST 5 MINUTES ON A RACK, NEVER A PLATE. A rack, always: a crisp skin set down on a flat plate sits in its own steam and softens within two minutes, which is a sad way to lose fourteen minutes of rendering. Serve it with hot rice, something sharp and cold beside it, and the pan wiped out — there should be almost nothing left in it.","cookTime":"Bumbu 10 min · Simmer 70 min · Dry 30 min · Render 14 min · Brown 4 min · Rest 5 min","kcal":"~540 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 40g · Carbs 9g · Fat 37g · Sodium ~1450mg","storage":"The cooked legs keep 3 days in the fridge and are one of the better things to find in there, because the hard work is already done and cannot be undone — the fat has gone and it does not come back. ✅ Reheating actually WORKS on this one, which is rare for anything fried: put the leg skin-side down in a dry cold pan, heat gently for 5 to 6 minutes, then turn the heat up for 2, and the skin re-crisps almost exactly as it did the first time. ⛔ Not the microwave, which steams the skin soft in about forty seconds and leaves you with a boiled duck leg. Cooked legs freeze for 2 months; thaw fully in the fridge before the pan or the outside crisps while the middle is still cold. ✅ THE TWO BY-PRODUCTS ARE WORTH MORE THAN THE LEFTOVERS. The rendered fat, strained through a fine sieve into a clean jar, keeps 3 months in the fridge and close to a year in the freezer — it is a genuinely superior frying medium with a high smoke point, and potatoes roasted in it are the standard argument for keeping it. The jellied stock under the fat cap keeps 4 days in the fridge and 3 months frozen; it is salty and spiced, so treat it as a seasoning base rather than a plain stock and taste before adding any salt to whatever it goes into. ⚠️ Do not keep the simmering liquid unseparated — the fat protects the jelly underneath while it sits, but once stirred back together it is neither one thing nor the other and sours faster.","chefNotes":"Duck fat sits UNDER the skin, so crisping is a draining job, not a browning job — while the fat is there the skin floats on boiling liquid, pinned near 100°C, and no pan can brown it. So: simmer the legs first, 60 to 75 minutes at a bare tremble and never a boil, until a skewer meets no resistance at all. Dry the skin properly, 30 minutes uncovered or overnight in the fridge. Score a 1cm lattice through skin and fat and STOP at the meat. Then the instruction that sounds wrong — start in a COLD dry pan with no oil, because a hot pan seizes the skin shut and traps the fat behind it. Render low for 10 to 14 minutes and pour the fat off three or four times as it comes; fat left in the pan just puts the skin back in a puddle. When the skin goes thin and translucent and the bubbling quietens, turn the heat up and brown it for 3 to 4 minutes, pressing it flat for contact. Rest on a rack, never a plate. And keep the fat — it is the best thing in the recipe you did not pay for.","pairsWith":"Hot white rice is not optional here, and neither is something sharp. This is rich, salty, fatty food eaten with the fingers, and it wants a pickle or a raw sambal on the side rather than another cooked dish — a spoonful of something fierce and a heap of cold cucumber does more for the plate than any accompaniment you could cook. In Madura and East Java it arrives with rice, a sambal, raw cucumber and basil, and nothing else at all. A coconut rice underneath turns it into a proper Sunday plate. ⛔ Do not put it next to anything else rich, creamy or coconut-based — the whole table goes heavy and the duck stops being the event. And do not serve it with gravy of any kind; the skin you spent fourteen minutes on softens the moment anything wet lands on it.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Acar Kuning","Sambal Terasi","Plecing Kangkung"],"trivia":"The duck comes from an island that is better known for salt, and the salt is a lesson in patience that a cook can actually use. Madura, off the north-east tip of Java, supplies a very large share of Indonesia's salt, and the reason is weather rather than sea — all seawater is roughly three and a half percent salt, everywhere on earth, so a salt coast is not made by the water. It is made by a long reliable dry season, flat low-lying land and clay that will not drain. Madura is drier and harsher than Java across the strait, which is poor luck for rice and very good luck for salt.\n\nAnd the process is not drying. It is SEPARATION, and the order is fixed. Seawater is walked through a chain of shallow ponds, growing more concentrated at every stage, and as it concentrates the dissolved minerals crystallise out one after another in a strict sequence: first the calcium carbonate, then calcium sulfate — the gypsum, which is why the early ponds are scraped clean and the scrapings thrown away — and only then, over a fairly narrow window, sodium chloride, the salt you actually want. ⚠️ Keep going past that window and the magnesium salts come out, and those are bitter, harsh, and so hygroscopic that they pull water out of the air and turn a jar of salt into a damp lump. So a salt-maker's real skill is knowing exactly when to harvest and when to drain off the remaining liquor, which has its own name — the bittern. ✅ Which is a genuinely useful thing to know at a shop shelf: sea salt that tastes faintly bitter, or that clumps damp within a fortnight, has been taken too far down that sequence. The good stuff was pulled at the right moment by somebody watching a pond.\n\n🌱 And one nudge, because the lemongrass in the bumbu is by a wide margin the most expensive thing per gram on this ingredient list and it should not be. Lemongrass roots from a supermarket stalk. Stand a fresh stalk with its base intact in a glass of water on a windowsill, change the water every couple of days, and within two weeks it puts out white roots; pot it up and it becomes a clump grass that keeps dividing. It loves a Pretoria summer, sulks in a hard frost and comes back from the crown, and one plant will supply a household indefinitely. Same arithmetic as the heirloom garlic: one stalk you were going to cook anyway, and you never buy it again.","howThisFeels":"Pouring a third cup of hot fat out of a pan you never put oil in, and finally hearing the sizzle go quiet.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated skin-side down in a dry cold pan, 5 minutes low and 2 minutes high — one of the very few fried things that genuinely comes back, because the fat is already gone and cannot re-soften it.","(easy) The meat pulled off the bone, still cold, folded through hot rice with a spoonful of sambal and a squeeze of lime, which needs no reheating at all.","(medium) The bones and any scraps simmered for an hour with the jellied stock from under the fat cap, then strained — a spiced duck broth with a serious head start, and noodles in it are supper.","(creative) Cold shredded duck fried hard in a spoonful of its own kept fat until the edges catch and go brittle, then piled onto rice with raw shallot and cucumber — the fat you saved cooking the meat it came off, which is the whole dish making its own argument."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Bebek Goreng Rumahan — Short Bumbu, Own Fat (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":44,"howThisFeels":"Four things in a pot, an hour of doing nothing, and a jar of fat left over that feels like getting paid.","trivia":"This version makes the economics of the dish visible, and they are stranger than they look. The two lines that come out are the galangal and the macadamia, which between them cost more per gram than the duck does — the same pattern as the Pepes budget fork, where the expensive things were never the protein. But the real point is at the other end. A pair of duck legs gives up 30 to 50ml of rendered fat, and duck fat is not a scrap: it is sold in jars at a price that would make you blink, it keeps for months, and it is a better frying medium than anything on this ingredient list. So the cheapest version of this recipe is also the one that most obviously PRODUCES something. You cook a meal and you finish with a jar of cooking fat you did not have that morning, which is a kind of household arithmetic that has mostly been forgotten and used to be the entire reason anybody kept a dripping tin.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"10g galangal"},{"item":"8g macadamia nuts"},{"item":"3g jaggery"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"200g duck legs","to":"150g duck legs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A smaller portion, no galangal, no macadamia and no jaggery — so the bumbu is shallot, garlic, chilli, coriander, turmeric, lemongrass and salt, pounded exactly as described. ✅ Expect a looser, thinner paste without the nut in it, which matters not at all here because it is going into 300ml of water rather than clinging to anything. What you lose is the galangal's resinous pine note, and the honest compensation is acid rather than another spice: squeeze lime over the finished legs at the table. ⚠️ Nothing about the technique changes and nothing about it should — the simmer, the drying, the scoring, the cold pan and the pouring-off are the entire dish, and they cost nothing. ✅ One genuine adjustment for the smaller legs: start checking the skewer at 55 minutes rather than 60, and take 2 minutes off the render, because a lighter leg has proportionally less fat to lose and will reach the thin translucent stage sooner."}]}},{"name":"Bebek Goreng Madura — Simmered, Rendered, Fried in Its Own Fat","icon":"🦆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":67,"howThisFeels":"Skin that shatters under the first press of a fork over meat that gives way like a braise, which should not be possible in one piece of bird.","trivia":"Bebek goreng is a Madurese dish that mostly is not eaten on Madura, and that tells you something about the island. Madura is limestone and hard going — thin soil, a short wet season, poor rice country — so for centuries Madurese have gone out rather than stayed in, and they went as traders, drovers, salt-makers and cooks. The result is that Madurese food is everywhere in East Java and Madurese warungs are a fixture in every Javanese city, so the word MADURA on a sign in Surabaya is not a claim about where the duck was raised. It is a claim about who is doing the cooking, which is a much older and more useful kind of label than the ones we are used to reading. The same migration carried sate Madura, soto Madura and this duck across the country, and it is why a small dry island with no agricultural advantages has a wildly disproportionate presence on Indonesian menus.","delta":null},{"name":"Bebek Sinjay — with Sambal Pencit (Green Mango)","icon":"🥭","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":71,"howThisFeels":"A mouthful of sharp raw mango straight after a mouthful of duck skin, and the plate resetting itself.","trivia":"The green mango sambal is not a garnish and it is not there for the flavour combination, it is there to do a mechanical job on your tongue — and the way it works is not what \"cutting through the richness\" implies. Acid does not dissolve fat; that phrase is a comfortable myth. What acid actually does is trigger salivation, hard and immediately, and saliva physically rinses the film of fat off the surface of the tongue so the next mouthful lands on a clean palate instead of a coated one. Richness is not being neutralised chemically. It is being WASHED OFF, mouthful by mouthful, which is why the effect is instant and why it stops working the moment you stop eating the sambal. ✅ Unripe mango is unusually good at this because it is high in both citric and malic acid while carrying almost no sugar to soften the effect. ⛔ And it is why this sambal must stay RAW and cold — Ayam Pop's lado mudo is cooked before it is pounded, deliberately, to take the raw grassy edge off a green chilli sambal, and doing that here would cook off exactly the sharpness you are relying on.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"40g green mango"},{"item":"20g tomatoes"},{"item":"10g birds eye chillies"},{"item":"3g shrimp paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cook the duck exactly as described, and make the sambal while it simmers so it has an hour in the fridge — this one improves with a little sitting, unlike a matah. ✅ Peel the 40g of green mango and cut it into fine matchsticks rather than grating it: grated mango weeps and turns to slush within twenty minutes, while matchsticks stay crisp and keep the texture that is half the point. ⚠️ Use a mango that is genuinely UNRIPE — hard, pale, squeaky under the knife, sour with no perfume at all. A half-ripe one brings sugar, and sugar is the one thing that cancels the effect this sambal exists for. ⛔ Pound the 10g of bird's eye chillies, the 3g of shrimp paste and the 20g of tomatoes to a coarse paste and then STOP — the mango is folded in whole at the end, never pounded, or you have made a purée. The mortar laws and the toasting of the shrimp paste belong to the Sambal Terasi card and are not repeated here; follow them there. ✅ Fold the mango matchsticks through with a pinch of salt, taste, and leave it 30 minutes so the salt draws a little juice and the whole thing loosens into itself. Serve it cold, in a separate bowl, with a spoon — never over the duck, or the skin softens within a minute."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Ayam Betutu","target":"indonesia-ayam-betutu","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Acar Kuning","target":"indonesia-acar-kuning","emoji":"🥒"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-kue-lapis","name":"Kue Lapis","nameAlt":"Steamed layer cake, one thin layer at a time","aliases":["Kue Lapis","Kue Lapis Sagu","Kue Lapis Gula Merah","Indonesian Layer Cake","Steamed Layer Cake","Kuih Lapis","Nine Layer Cake"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","steamed","vegan","coconut","budget","celebration"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","party","celebration","everyday"],"ingredients":"100ml coconut milk · 50ml water · 45g tapioca starch · 25g jaggery · 20g rice flour · 15g white sugar · 2g salt","method":"⚖️ EVERY LAYER HAS TO BE SET ENOUGH NOT TO BLEND, AND STILL FRESH ENOUGH TO BOND. That window is about two minutes wide, it opens and closes eight times in one cake, and everything else on this card is arranged around it.\n\nMiss it in one direction and the layers merge. Pour new batter onto a layer that has not set and the two simply mix at the boundary — you get a muddy gradient with a stripe at each end, which is not a layer cake, it is one cake with a memory of stripes. Miss it in the other direction and the cake comes apart. Leave a layer steaming far too long, or let the steamer cool between pours, and the starch at that surface finishes gelatinising and begins to firm and retrograde; the next layer then sits ON it instead of bonding INTO it, and what you cut into is a stack of separate discs. ⚠️ That failure is confusing, because peeling kue lapis apart layer by layer is the traditional and correct way to EAT it — but you are peeling a bonded cake along its grain, which takes a small deliberate effort. A cake that falls apart on the knife has failed.\n\nMAKE THE BATTER, AND MAKE IT IN TWO BOWLS. Warm the 25g of jaggery with the 50ml of water in a small pan over low heat, stirring until it has completely dissolved, then take it off and let it cool to lukewarm — ⚠️ grated jaggery will not dissolve in a cold batter, ever, and undissolved grit falls to the bottom of the bowl and turns up as dark speckles in what should be a clean pale layer. In a separate bowl whisk the 45g of tapioca starch, the 20g of rice flour, the 15g of white sugar and the 2g of salt with the 100ml of coconut milk until completely smooth, then strain it — thirty seconds through a sieve now saves you a lump that will show as a blister in a 3mm layer. Divide that white batter into two roughly equal bowls and stir the cooled jaggery syrup into ONE of them. You now have a pale bowl and a dark bowl, and no colouring in the house. ⚖️ The proportion of rice flour to tapioca starch is what sets the texture here, and Es Cendol's card argues that out in full — body from the rice flour, spring from the tapioca. It is not re-argued here.\n\n⚠️ AND YOU MUST STIR EACH BOWL AGAIN BEFORE EVERY SINGLE POUR. Tapioca starch does not stay suspended for even a minute; left standing it drops to the bottom as a dense white sludge with thin liquid above it, so the third layer would be mostly water and the eighth mostly starch. Tempe Mendoan's card makes the same point about rice flour settling in ten minutes — this is the same problem, roughly ten times faster, and it is the commonest reason a home kue lapis has layers of visibly different thickness and firmness. A quick stir with the ladle before each pour. Every time. No exceptions.\n\nGET THE STEAMER GOING PROPERLY BEFORE THE TIN GOES IN. You want a hard, constant, generous steam — a bare tremble is not enough here, because the tin is full of cold batter and each new pour drops the temperature again. Grease a small tin, about 12cm, lightly with oil, and warm it in the steamer for 2 minutes before the first pour so the first layer starts setting immediately rather than sitting cool and separating. ⛔ WRAP THE STEAMER LID IN A CLOTH, and do it now rather than after you have ruined a layer. Condensation collecting on a bare lid drips onto a just-set surface and makes a small crater; the next pour fills that crater, and it shows in the finished slice as a lens-shaped flaw right through the cake. A cloth-wrapped lid absorbs it. Lift the lid AWAY from you and to one side each time.\n\n⚖️ THE LAYERS ARE 3mm BECAUSE OF HOW BADLY TAPIOCA CONDUCTS HEAT, NOT BECAUSE THIN LOOKS BETTER. Tapioca gel is a poor conductor — once the top of a layer sets, that gel insulates everything below it. Pour a 10mm layer and the top skins over in three minutes while the middle is still liquid, and then you are pouring layer two onto a thin skin floating on batter, which collapses into exactly the muddy merge described above. So a ladleful is roughly 3mm deep, and the thickness is derived from the material rather than chosen. Use ONE measure — the same ladle, the same fill, every time — because unequal layers are the single most obvious tell of a home-made one, and it is a free thing to get right.\n\nNOW BUILD IT: DARK, PALE, DARK, PALE — 5 MINUTES EACH. Pour the first dark layer into the warm tin, cover, and steam 5 minutes. ✅ The test is TOUCH, not the clock: press the surface lightly with a fingertip and it should feel firm and slightly tacky and leave no batter on your finger. If it is still wet, give it 90 seconds more; if it looks dry and matte at the edges, you have gone past the window and the next layer will not key onto it properly. Stir the pale bowl, pour the next layer straight onto the hot set surface, cover, 5 minutes. Keep going, alternating, until both bowls are empty — eight layers from this quantity, ending on a dark one.\n\n⚠️ GIVE THE FINAL LAYER LONGER — 15 TO 20 MINUTES. Every layer up to now has been finished from above as well as below, by the layer poured on top of it. The last one has nothing over it and has to set entirely from underneath and from the steam, so it needs three times as long. ✅ It is done when a skewer into the centre comes out clean and the whole surface is uniformly firm and glossy rather than tacky.\n\n⛔ THEN COOL IT COMPLETELY, AND COMPLETELY MEANS AT LEAST 4 HOURS. This is not patience for its own sake and it is the step most people skip. Warm tapioca gel is soft, sticky and gluey; it drags on a blade, smears the stripes into each other and tastes of nothing much. It firms and turns properly springy and translucent only as it cools, and that cooling set is what makes a clean-edged stripe possible at all. Room temperature, uncovered, then a couple of hours in the fridge if the kitchen is hot. Bika Ambon's card makes the same argument about cutting a set starch too early and is worth reading beside this one.\n\nCUT IT WITH A WET KNIFE, OR WITH THREAD. Tapioca is extremely adhesive and a dry blade tears rather than cuts. ✅ Wipe the blade and dip it in cold water between every single cut, or oil it lightly — a plastic knife is genuinely better than a steel one here, and a length of clean cotton thread pulled down through the cake is better than both. Cut into diamonds or small squares; this is rich, dense and chewy, and a large slice is nobody's friend.\n\n🌿 IF YOU HAVE PANDAN, USE IT FOR THE PALE HALF. Simmer a knotted leaf in the coconut milk, cool, remove it, and the pale layers come out faintly green and smell like nothing else. It is not sold reliably in South Africa, so it stays out of the ingredient list rather than being pretended at — and the cake is complete without it. ⛔ Do not reach for vanilla essence as a substitute. It is a different smell entirely and it makes the cake taste like a supermarket biscuit.","cookTime":"Batter 15 min · Steam 8 layers × 5 min · Final layer 20 min · Cool 4 hrs","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g · Carbs 62g · Fat 9g · Sodium ~800mg","storage":"This is one of the better-keeping sweets in the file and it wants to be kept correctly. ⛔ DO NOT REFRIGERATE IT once it is properly cool and cut, or at least not for long: tapioca and rice starch both retrograde in cold, which is the same staling process that makes day-old bread firm, and a kue lapis left two days in the fridge goes hard, dull and slightly crumbly at the edges. ✅ In a sealed container at room temperature it holds 2 days and is arguably better on day two, when the layers have fully set and peel apart cleanly. In a Pretoria summer, or if the kitchen is genuinely warm and humid, refrigerate it rather than risk it — but then take it out a good hour before serving and let it come all the way back to room temperature, at which point most of the firmness reverses and it is close to new again. That gentle warming back is worth knowing generally: starch retrogradation is largely undone by heat. It freezes for 2 months, wrapped tightly in one block rather than in slices, and should be thawed at room temperature and never in a microwave, which turns the outside gluey while the middle is still cold. ⚠️ It does not need reheating at all and should not have it — this is a room-temperature sweet.","chefNotes":"Every layer must be set enough not to blend and fresh enough to bond, and that window is about two minutes wide. Too wet and the batters merge into a gradient; too dry or too cool and the cake delaminates into separate discs. Five minutes a layer, tested by TOUCH — firm, slightly tacky, nothing on the finger. Stir the bowl before every single pour, because tapioca starch drops to sludge in under a minute and your last layers will otherwise be pure starch. Wrap the steamer lid in a cloth or condensation pits the surface and shows as a lens in the slice. Layers are 3mm because tapioca gel insulates — a thick layer skins over with liquid underneath. Use one ladle, the same fill, every time. The final layer needs 15 to 20 minutes because nothing is being poured on top of it. Then cool it 4 full hours before it goes anywhere near a knife, and cut with a wet blade or a thread.","pairsWith":"Strong black coffee or plain unsweetened tea, and not much else. It is dense, sweet and chewy, so it belongs on a table of small sweet things eaten in small pieces rather than at the end of a big meal — a plate of it alongside other steamed rice-flour sweets is exactly right, and that is how it is actually served, at a party or a gathering rather than as a dessert course. ⛔ Do not serve it with cream, ice cream or any sauce: it is already rich with coconut and its whole appeal is a clean chewy bite and a set of visible stripes, both of which anything wet destroys. ✅ Cut it small and put out more than looks sensible — it disappears at a rate that surprises people who have just watched somebody spend an hour making it.","goesWith":["Klepon","Serabi Solo","Es Cendol","Black tea"],"trivia":"The dish is properly called kue lapis SAGU, and sago is one of the strangest staple foods on earth — because it is starch harvested from the TRUNK of a palm, not from a seed, a grain or a root.\n\nA sago palm spends ten to fifteen years doing one thing: pumping starch into its own trunk. It is not building a food store for a bad season, the way a potato does. It is saving up for a single enormous terminal flowering — the palm flowers once, spends the entire contents of its trunk doing it, and then dies. ✅ Which makes the harvest a race against the plant's own biology, and it is the part that stops people in their tracks: the tree has to be felled JUST BEFORE it flowers. Leave it a few months too long and the starch has been converted and spent on the flower spike, and fifteen years of stored carbohydrate is simply gone. A grower reads the tree — a change in the crown, the first sign of the spike — and cuts it down at the last possible moment.\n\nThe payoff for all that waiting is remarkable. One mature trunk yields somewhere between 150 and 300kg of starch, which makes sago among the highest carbohydrate yields per hectare of any crop in the world — and it does it in freshwater swamp that will grow nothing else, with no ploughing, no planting, no irrigation and no fertiliser. Across Maluku and Papua, where rice does not grow well, sago is the staple, and it is a staple that is essentially gathered rather than farmed. It is also worth knowing that it is not the same plant as the ornamental \"sago palm\" sold in garden centres, which is a cycad and genuinely poisonous.\n\n⚖️ AND THE HONEST SUBSTITUTION IS AT THE END OF THAT STORY. Real sago flour is not stocked in South Africa. Tapioca starch is, and it is the standard stand-in everywhere the two meet — the textures are close enough that a great many kue lapis in Indonesia are made with tapioca too. But they come from completely different plants: one is the pith of a swamp palm's trunk, the other is the root of a cassava shrub, a South American plant carried across the world by Portuguese ships. Two starches that behave almost identically in a steamer, from two plants that could hardly be less alike.\n\n🌱 Which points at the nudge, because cassava is the one you can actually grow. It is not planted from seed — you plant a STICK. A 20 to 25cm length of mature stem, pushed into warm soil at an angle, roots and shoots within a few weeks, and eight to ten months later you dig up the roots. ⚠️ It is frost-tender and a hard Highveld winter will cut it down, so in Pretoria treat it as a summer annual: plant sticks in spring, harvest before the cold. Every plant you lift gives you next season's planting material from its own stems, which is the same arithmetic as the heirloom garlic — one start, and then never again. ⛔ And know the rule that goes with it: cassava root must always be peeled and thoroughly cooked, never eaten raw.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid for the eighth time in forty minutes and seeing another clean stripe appear, like something being built rather than baked.","leftovers":["(easy) Straight from the container at room temperature with coffee, which is what it is for — it needs nothing done to it and is better on day two.","(easy) Cut into small diamonds and put out on a plate for guests, where a slightly firm day-old cake peels apart into layers more cleanly than a fresh one does.","(medium) Cubes pan-fried in a dry non-stick pan for 2 minutes a side until the outside blisters and goes chewy-crisp while the middle stays soft — the one hot use that works, and it works because tapioca browns rather than melting.","(creative) Cubes dropped into a glass of iced coconut milk with palm sugar syrup, where they behave like the chewy jellies in a cold Indonesian drink and the layers show through the glass."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kue Lapis Putih — Plain White, No Palm Sugar (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Eight identical white layers that you can only see when you tear it, which turns out to be its own small pleasure.","trivia":"Taking the palm sugar out removes the stripes and it does NOT remove the dish, which is worth saying plainly because the layers are usually described as the whole point. A plain white kue lapis is a real and common thing — it is what a lot of households actually make — and eating one teaches you something the striped version hides: the layers are still completely there, you simply cannot see them until you tear it apart, and the peeling is the pleasure rather than the picture. The other quiet economy is the coconut milk, which is by a distance the most expensive line on this card. Dropping it from 100ml to 70ml and making up the difference with the sugar syrup gives a slightly firmer, chewier, less rich cake with a cleaner tapioca springiness — closer to the older, plainer versions of this sweet, and a bit less like a modern bakery one.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"25g jaggery"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml coconut milk","to":"70ml coconut milk"},{"from":"15g white sugar","to":"25g white sugar"}],"addStep":[{"text":"No jaggery at all, less coconut milk, and a little more white sugar to carry the sweetness. ✅ Dissolve the 25g of white sugar in the 50ml of water over low heat exactly as you would have done with the palm sugar, cool it, and stir it into the single bowl of batter — you are not dividing it in two this time, so there is one bowl and one ladle. ⚠️ Everything else is identical and nothing about the technique relaxes: same 3mm layers, same 5 minutes, same touch test, same stir before every pour, same 20 minutes on the last layer, same 4 hours of cooling. ⚖️ In fact the touch test matters MORE here, because you have lost your visual cue — with two colours you can see at a glance whether a new pour has sat cleanly on top or bled into the layer beneath, and with one colour you cannot. Trust the finger and the clock. ✅ The lower coconut content also sets a shade firmer, so start checking the final layer at 15 minutes rather than 18."}]}},{"name":"Kue Lapis Gula Merah — Eight Layers, Dark and Pale","icon":"🍰","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Pulling one warm brown layer off the top with your fingers before anyone else has been offered a piece.","trivia":"The alternating colours here come from palm sugar rather than from a bottle, and that is not a purist's choice — it is the older way and it does something a dye cannot. The dark layers are not merely darker, they taste different: palm sugar carries the caramel and faintly smoky notes left over from boiling down the sap, plus a mineral edge and a trace of acidity, so a bite crosses two flavours as well as two colours. A pink-and-white kue lapis coloured with a few drops of colouring is a real and extremely popular thing across Indonesia and Malaysia — many people's childhood version is bright pink — but every layer in it tastes exactly the same, and the cake becomes a purely visual object. This one is the version where the stripes are made of flavour, and it is also the reason the layer order matters slightly: ending on a dark layer puts the stronger-tasting one on top, where it is the first thing the mouth meets.","delta":null},{"name":"Kue Lapis Enam Belas — Sixteen Layers, Thick Coconut","icon":"🥥","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"An hour and a half at the stove for a cake you can count the layers of across the room, and doing it anyway.","trivia":"Doubling the layers is not showing off, it is the version that proves the technique — and it also runs into a limit that is worth understanding before you start. Sixteen layers at half the thickness means each one sets faster, so the steaming time per layer drops to around three minutes rather than five, and the window between set-enough and too-dry narrows with it. The cake stops being forgiving. What you get in exchange is real: thinner layers mean more boundaries, and every boundary is a place where the cake can be peeled, so a sixteen-layer kue lapis eats quite differently from an eight-layer one made of the same batter — springier, more delicate, and far more satisfying to pull apart. This is also where the extra coconut earns its place. A richer batter sets slightly softer, which counteracts the firmness that comes from having twice as many set surfaces stacked up, and without it a sixteen-layer cake can come out faintly rubbery. More layers and more fat go together; more layers on their own do not.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"40ml coconut cream"},{"item":"10g desiccated coconut"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"45g tapioca starch","to":"55g tapioca starch"}],"addStep":[{"text":"55g of tapioca starch rather than 45g, plus 40ml of coconut cream stirred into the batter with the coconut milk, and 10g of desiccated coconut held back for the top. ✅ Divide as before into a dark bowl and a pale bowl, then use a ladle HALF the size — or half-fill the same one — so each layer goes on at roughly 1.5mm. ⚠️ Drop the steaming time to 3 minutes a layer and start using the touch test from about 2 minutes 30, because the window is genuinely tighter here and a thin layer goes from wet to over-dry in well under a minute. ⛔ Do not be tempted to speed it up by pouring two layers close together — that is the merge failure, and at this thickness it ruins the whole point of the version. Sixteen layers takes about an hour and there is no way around it. ✅ Steam the 10g of desiccated coconut with a pinch of salt for 3 minutes in a small bowl beside the tin while you work, then scatter it over the final layer and give that last layer the full 20 minutes so the coconut sets INTO the surface rather than sitting loose on top of it. Cool for the full 4 hours; a taller cake needs every minute of it."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Klepon","target":"indonesia-klepon","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Es Cendol","target":"indonesia-es-cendol","emoji":"🥤"},{"name":"Bika Ambon","target":"indonesia-bika-ambon","emoji":"🍯"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ikan-woku","name":"Ikan Woku","nameAlt":"Manado fish, cooked under a fistful of herbs","aliases":["Ikan Woku","Woku","Woku Belanga","Cakalang Woku","Manado Fish","Ikan Woku Manado","Rica-Rica Ikan"],"course":"main","type":["main","fish","braised","herb","spiced","quick","light"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","sunday","guests","everyday","summer"],"ingredients":"150g snoek · 100ml water · 40g tomatoes · 30g basil · 25g spring onion · 20g shallots · 15ml sunflower oil · 12g red chilli · 10g garlic · 10ml lime juice · 8g fresh ginger · 8g lemongrass · 6g galangal · 5g ground turmeric · 4g salt","method":"⚖️ THE HERBS ARE A VEGETABLE IN THIS DISH, NOT A SEASONING, AND THE QUANTITY IS THE ENTIRE RECIPE. Thirty grams of basil and twenty-five grams of spring onion for one portion is, near enough, a whole supermarket packet of basil per person. It looks like a typo. It is not. Manado cooking on the north-eastern arm of Sulawesi uses herbs at the volume other cuisines use onions, and if you scale the herbs down to what feels sensible you will produce a perfectly nice spiced fish that is not woku. ✅ Every instruction below follows from that one decision, because cooking with 30g of leaves is a genuinely different job from cooking with 3g.\n\nTHE FIRST CONSEQUENCE: BUILD THE POT DRIER THAN LOOKS RIGHT. There is only 100ml of water in this recipe and it will look mean in the pan. That is deliberate — 30g of leaves and 40g of tomato release a surprising amount of liquid as they collapse, and the herbs finish the sauce rather than sitting in one. ⚠️ Add the amount of liquid your instincts want and you will get a thin herb soup with a piece of fish in it, and there is no way back: you cannot reduce it later without overcooking the fish, which is the thing this dish protects most carefully.\n\n⚖️ AND CHOOSE THE FISH FOR THE POT, WHICH MEANS NOT HAKE. This is a wet, spiced, jostling braise, and a soft flaky white fish disintegrates in it — you lift out shreds and the sauce goes cloudy with fragments. Woku is made in Manado with cakalang, which is skipjack tuna: firm, oily, dense-fleshed, and able to sit in a hot sauce for twelve minutes and come out in pieces you can still see. ✅ Snoek is the honest South African answer and a genuinely good one — firm, oily, robust, and it holds together exactly the way this pot needs. ⛔ Two other cards in this file use hake and are right to: Pepes Ikan steams it sealed in a parcel where nothing moves, and Sate Lilit pounds it to a paste on purpose. Neither of those is this. **A fish that is correct in one dish can be the wrong ingredient in the next one, and hake is the wrong ingredient here.** Yellowtail, kingklip or firm fish steaks all work; anything that flakes at a fork's touch does not.\n\nPOUND THE PASTE, AND KEEP THE HERBS OUT OF IT. Into the mortar go the 20g of shallots, the 12g of red chilli, the 10g of garlic, the 8g of fresh ginger, the 6g of galangal, the 5g of ground turmeric and the 4g of salt. Pound to a coarse paste rather than a smooth one — this is a rustic pot and a silky paste makes the sauce look processed. Bruise the 8g of lemongrass along its length with the flat of a knife and leave it whole; it goes in as an infusion aromatic and comes out at the end. ⛔ The basil and the spring onion do NOT go anywhere near the mortar. They are the vegetable, and pounding them would turn the vegetable into a seasoning, which is the exact inversion this dish exists to avoid.\n\n⛔ DO NOT CHOP THE BASIL. STRIP IT BY HAND. Pull the leaves off the stems with your fingers and leave them whole, tearing only the very largest ones. Cutting and bruising ruptures the cells and lets the plant's own enzymes get at the exposed surfaces, and basil goes black at a cut edge within minutes — the same browning reaction as a cut apple. ⚠️ At 3g nobody notices. At 30g the pot goes visibly dark and the flavour turns flat and stewed instead of bright. ✅ Keep the tender top stems, which are good eating and full of flavour, and throw the woody lower stems into the pot whole to be fished out later — they carry plenty of aroma and no texture worth having.\n\nFRY THE PASTE PROPERLY — 5 TO 6 MINUTES, UNTIL IT DARKENS AND SMELLS COOKED. Heat the 15ml of sunflower oil over medium heat and fry the paste, stirring, until it loses its raw sharp edge, deepens in colour and begins to smell rounded rather than pungent. ⚠️ There is not enough oil in this pan for it to split and separate, and it is not supposed to — Rendang's card owns that stage and this one is nothing like it. Colour and smell are your only two cues here. Then add the bruised lemongrass, the woody basil stems and the 40g of tomatoes cut into thick wedges, and cook another 2 minutes until the tomato has slumped but not dissolved.\n\nADD THE WATER AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HERBS. Pour in the 100ml of water, bring it to a gentle simmer, and stir in HALF the basil and half the 25g of spring onion, cut into 3cm lengths. ⚖️ THIS IS THE SPLIT, AND IT IS THE TECHNIQUE: the two halves are doing completely different jobs. This first half is being sacrificed. It will collapse, give up its aromatic oils and its water into the sauce, go dark, and effectively disappear — and that is what makes the sauce taste of basil all the way through instead of merely having basil in it. Two minutes is enough.\n\nLAY THE FISH IN AND LEAVE IT ALONE — 10 TO 12 MINUTES. Cut the 150g of snoek into two or three large pieces and settle them into the sauce in one layer, spooning some over the top. ⛔ Do not stir once the fish is in. Tilt the pan and spoon instead; a spoon dragged through a pot of fish is how a braise becomes a hash. Keep it at a bare simmer with the lid off — ✅ it is done when the flesh has gone from translucent to opaque all the way through and a fork slid into the thickest part meets no resistance, and the sauce has tightened to something that coats rather than pools. If the pan looks dry before the fish is done, add hot water 20ml at a time, never cold.\n\nTHEN THE SECOND HALF OF THE HERBS, OFF THE HEAT, WITH THE LID ON FOR TWO MINUTES. Take the pan off the stove entirely, add the 10ml of lime juice, scatter the remaining basil and spring onion over the top, and put the lid on. ✅ These leaves are not being cooked. They are being wilted by residual heat, and they stay whole, green and recognisably a leaf you bite. ⚖️ The lid is doing one specific thing and it is borrowed openly rather than invented: basil's aroma compounds are volatile, so in an open pot they leave with the steam — Pepes Ikan's card is built entirely on trapping exactly those volatiles inside a sealed parcel for twenty minutes. This is that principle borrowed for two minutes at the end of an open pot, and it is worth reading that card for the full argument.\n\n⚠️ AND THE LIME GOES IN OFF THE HEAT, EVERY TIME. Acid drives off delicate aromatics faster over a flame and dulls the green of the herbs on contact with heat. Off the stove it stays sharp, and sharp is what a rich herbal pot needs to stop it going heavy.\n\nFISH OUT THE LEMONGRASS AND THE WOODY STEMS, AND SERVE IT IMMEDIATELY. This is not a dish that improves standing on a warm stove; the finishing herbs are only right for about ten minutes before they go dull and olive. Rice underneath, and something sharp on the side. ✅ Spoon the sauce over generously — after all that, most of the flavour is in it.","cookTime":"Paste 12 min · Fry 8 min · Simmer 12 min · Finish 2 min off the heat","kcal":"~380 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g · Carbs 12g · Fat 21g · Sodium ~1420mg","storage":"It keeps 2 days in the fridge and it is worth being precise about what survives and what does not, because they are different halves of the same dish. ✅ The SAUCE keeps beautifully and is arguably better on day two, once the spice paste has settled into it. The fish is fine. ⛔ The finishing herbs do not survive at all — they go dark olive and taste stewed within a few hours, which is the fate of every green herb left sitting in a warm acidic sauce. So store it knowing that, and fix it on the plate: reheat gently, covered, over a low heat or in short microwave bursts until just hot through, then throw in a fresh small handful of torn basil and sliced spring onion off the heat with the lid on for two minutes exactly as you did the first time. Five grams of fresh herbs completely resets it, and this is the rare leftover that can be made to taste new rather than merely acceptable. ⚠️ Do not boil it to reheat — the fish is already cooked and a second hard heat turns snoek dry and cottony. It does not freeze well: the fish goes woolly and the sauce separates slightly, and the herbs are gone anyway. ✅ What DOES freeze is the fried spice paste on its own, in an ice-cube tray for 6 months, which turns this into a fifteen-minute supper — and it is worth making a triple batch of the paste while the mortar is already dirty.","chefNotes":"The herbs are a vegetable here and the quantity IS the recipe — 30g of basil for one portion is not a typo, and scaling it down gives you a nice spiced fish that is not woku. Because of that, build the pot DRY: only 100ml of water, because the leaves and tomato release plenty and the herbs finish the sauce rather than sitting in one. Choose a firm oily fish — snoek, yellowtail, or tuna steaks — and NOT hake, which disintegrates in a wet jostling braise. Strip the basil by hand and never chop it: at 30g, cut edges go black within minutes and the pot turns dark and stewed. Then split the herbs in half and use them twice. Half goes in early and is sacrificed — it collapses into the sauce and is what makes the whole thing taste of basil. Half goes in at the very end, off the heat, with the lid on for two minutes, so it wilts without cooking and stays a leaf you can bite. Lime off the heat too. Never stir once the fish is in — tilt and spoon.","pairsWith":"Plain white rice, and plenty of it — this is a fiercely flavoured, herb-heavy, chilli-hot pot and rice is what makes it a meal instead of an assault. Beyond that it wants very little: a plate of something raw and cold, a cucumber, a wedge of lime. In Manado it arrives with rice, a fresh chilli sambal on the side for people who want more heat, and cassava or sweet potato leaves boiled plain. ⛔ Do not serve it with anything coconut-based or creamy, which flattens the herbs completely and wastes the entire point of the dish. ⛔ And do not put another herb-forward dish beside it; two of these on one table cancel each other out. ✅ What it genuinely does want is a cold beer or something sharp to drink, because it is hot in the chilli sense and the heat builds across a plate.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Acar Kuning","Plecing Kangkung"],"trivia":"The island this dish comes from is shaped like nothing else on earth, and the reason is that it is not really one island at all.\n\nSulawesi has four long arms radiating from a small centre, separated by deep gulfs, and the shape has always been the first thing anybody notices about it on a map. The explanation turns out to be that the arms were once separate. Sulawesi is a collision — fragments of continental crust from two entirely different origins, jammed together and welded into one landmass. The western part came from Sundaland, the Asian side of the world; parts of the eastern and south-eastern arms arrived from the Australian side, carried across on moving plates over tens of millions of years. So the four arms are not one island's peninsulas. They are the seams of a wreck.\n\n⚖️ AND THAT ACCIDENT PUT THE ISLAND IN THE STRANGEST BIOLOGICAL POSITION ON THE PLANET. Sulawesi sits in the middle of Wallacea — the zone between Asian and Australian life where neither one wins. Deep water on both sides meant animals could rarely cross in either direction even when sea levels dropped and joined up half of Southeast Asia into dry land. What that isolation produced is a roll-call of animals that exist nowhere else: the babirusa, a pig whose upper tusks grow upward straight THROUGH the skin of its own snout and curve back over its face; the anoa, a buffalo the size of a large dog; and the maleo, a bird that has given up sitting on its eggs altogether. ✅ The maleo buries them instead — in volcanically heated sand or sun-warmed black beaches, using the ground itself as the incubator, and the chick digs its way out fully feathered and able to fly almost immediately, having never met a parent. An island made by geology, keeping birds that hand their eggs back to it.\n\nThe cooking follows the same logic as the map. Manado, out on the north-eastern arm, cooks almost nothing like Makassar down on the south-western one — different fish, different heat, different herbs, a thousand kilometres apart by road around two gulfs. The local word rica simply means chilli, which is why the fiery Manado dishes are called rica-rica; woku is its herb-led sibling from the same kitchens, and the two are often set against each other on the same table. One is built on chilli, one on green leaves, and both belong to a region that ended up culturally and biologically distinct largely because getting there is difficult.\n\n🌱 Which brings us to the basil, and to the single strongest argument for a pot on the windowsill in this entire book. This recipe needs 30g of it per person, and basil is the most expensive thing on the ingredient list by a long way — several times the cost of the fish, gram for gram. It is also one of the easiest plants there is. Take a stem from a supermarket bunch, strip the lower leaves, stand it in a glass of water on a sunny sill, and it will root within a week or so; pot it up and pinch the growing tips out regularly, always just above a pair of leaves, and it branches into a bush instead of bolting to flower. ⚠️ Two Pretoria specifics: it is frost-tender, so it is a spring-to-autumn plant here and worth taking cuttings from in March to carry indoors, and it wants afternoon shade in real summer heat or it flowers and goes bitter. ✅ And the honest scale: for this dish one plant is not enough. Grow three or four, pinch them hard, and a pot of woku stops being an expensive supper.","howThisFeels":"Putting a lid on a pan that is already off the heat, and lifting it two minutes later into a wall of hot basil.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheated gently, covered, with a fresh small handful of torn basil and spring onion thrown in off the heat at the end — five grams of fresh herbs completely resets it, and this is the rare leftover that can taste new.","(easy) The fish flaked into hot rice with the sauce spooned over, eaten as a rice bowl, which needs nothing but a squeeze of lime.","(medium) The sauce alone, without any fish left in it, loosened with a little hot water and used to braise eggs — crack them straight in, lid on, 5 minutes, and it is a completely different supper.","(creative) The cold sauce and flaked fish stirred through a fresh green salad as the dressing, with the sharpness turned up by extra lime — the herb volume that made it a hot pot makes it an unusually good cold one."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Woku Rumahan — Short Paste, Fewer Herbs (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":37,"howThisFeels":"The same hot green smell out of a pan that cost half as much, and nobody at the table doing the arithmetic.","trivia":"This is the version that admits what the full one costs, and where the money actually is — which is not where anyone expects. The fish is the cheapest major component here. The basil is the expensive one: at supermarket prices a 30g portion of it costs more than the snoek it is going on, which is a genuinely strange sentence to write about a herb and a fish. Galangal and lemongrass are the other two, both bought by the piece and both used by the gram. So this fork cuts the herb volume rather than the fish, and it is honest about the consequence: with 10g of basil instead of 30g you have a spiced fish braise with basil in it rather than a herb dish with fish in it, which is a real and noticeable difference and still a very good supper. ✅ The trade is the one that makes the grow-your-own note above worth acting on — the ONLY way to have the full-herb version cheaply is to grow the herb, and once you do, this budget fork stops existing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"6g galangal"},{"item":"8g lemongrass"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g snoek","to":"100g snoek"},{"from":"30g basil","to":"10g basil"},{"from":"25g spring onion","to":"10g spring onion"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A smaller piece of fish, a third of the basil, no galangal and no lemongrass. ⚖️ The technique does not relax anywhere and should not: still strip the basil by hand and never chop it, still split it in half, still finish off the heat with the lid on for two minutes. ✅ In fact the SPLIT matters more at this quantity, not less — with only 10g of basil the temptation is to throw it all in at the end so you can see it, and that is exactly wrong. Half still goes in early and disappears into the sauce, because at any quantity that early half is what makes the sauce taste of basil rather than merely carry it. ⚠️ Losing the galangal and lemongrass takes out the resinous, citrus-pine top notes, and the honest compensation is acid rather than more spice: push the lime juice up to 15ml and add a strip of pared lime zest to the pot while it simmers, fished out at the end with the woody stems. ✅ The smaller piece of fish also cooks faster — start checking it at 8 minutes rather than 10."}]}},{"name":"Ikan Woku Belanga — Herbs Twice, Off the Heat at the End","icon":"🌿","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":70,"howThisFeels":"Lifting out a piece of fish that has held together perfectly and finding a whole green leaf stuck to the side of it.","trivia":"Belanga is the clay pot, and the full name of the dish — woku belanga — distinguishes it from woku daun, the version wrapped in leaves and grilled instead. That distinction is worth knowing because it tells you something about what the herbs are for in each. In the leaf-wrapped version the herbs are sealed in with the fish and everything happens in a closed space, so the leaves infuse and nothing escapes. In the pot version the pot is open, the aromatics are free to leave with the steam, and the cook has to compensate with timing — which is precisely why this recipe splits the herbs and puts a lid on for two minutes at the very end. The same dish, two vessels, and the vessel decides the method. It is also why woku belanga is the one that travelled and became the version people know: a pot is a pot anywhere, and a good leaf is a local ingredient.","delta":null},{"name":"Cakalang Woku — Skipjack Tuna, the Manado Original","icon":"🐟","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":101,"howThisFeels":"Dark dense fish in a green sauce, cut with a knife rather than flaked with a fork.","trivia":"Cakalang is skipjack tuna and it is the fish this dish was built around, which matters more than authenticity for its own sake — it changes how you cook it. Tuna is dense, dark, low in connective tissue and very lean in the loin, and it behaves less like other fish and more like a piece of red meat: it goes from succulent to dry and chalky in about a minute, and it does that at a lower temperature than white fish does. So this fork simmers for less time, not more, despite the fish looking sturdier. ✅ The other thing skipjack explains is why Manado cooking is so heavily spiced and so chilli-forward in the first place. North Sulawesi's other great use for cakalang is cakalang fufu — the fish split, skewered on a bamboo frame and hot-smoked hard until it is dry enough to keep for weeks without refrigeration — and a strongly smoked, strongly savoury fish needs a sauce loud enough to stand next to it. The chilli and the herb volume are not decoration. They evolved beside a fish that could shout.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"10g birds eye chillies"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g snoek","to":"150g tuna steak"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Tuna steaks instead of snoek, plus 10g of bird's eye chillies pounded into the paste for the proper Manado heat level. ⚠️ THE TIMING CHANGES AND IT CHANGES DOWNWARD. Cut the tuna into large chunks rather than thin steaks, and give it 5 to 6 minutes in the simmering sauce, not 12 — it is dense and lean, and it goes from juicy to chalky faster than any white fish will. ✅ The doneness cue is different too: pull it when the outside is opaque and the very centre is still faintly rosy, because it carries on cooking in a hot sauce for a minute or two after the pan is off the stove. Overcooked tuna cannot be rescued by sauce, however good the sauce is. ⛔ Do not stir it at all — dense tuna chunks jostled in a pan shed their corners and cloud the sauce. Tilt and spoon. ✅ If you can only find tinned tuna, this is one of the rare dishes where it genuinely works: drain it well, skip the simmer entirely, and fold it through the finished sauce off the heat along with the second half of the herbs."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Pepes Ikan","target":"indonesia-pepes-ikan","emoji":"🐟"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sate-padang","name":"Sate Padang","nameAlt":"Minangkabau skewers under a thick rice-flour gravy","aliases":["Sate Padang","Satay Padang","Sate Padang Panjang","Sate Pariaman","Padang Satay","Minangkabau Satay","Beef Satay with Yellow Gravy"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","beef","grill","street-food","spiced","skewers","braai"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","street food","guests","braai","weekend"],"ingredients":"150g beef stew pieces · 400ml water · 25g rice flour · 20g shallots · 12g red chilli · 10g garlic · 8g fresh ginger · 8g galangal · 8g lemongrass · 6g ground turmeric · 5g coriander seeds · 4g salt · 3g ground cumin · 2g cinnamon · 2 bay leaves · 4 bamboo skewers","method":"⚖️ IN EVERY OTHER SATE THE SKEWER IS THE DISH AND THE SAUCE IS THE ACCOMPANIMENT. HERE IT IS THE OTHER WAY ROUND. Sate Padang is a gravy that happens to arrive with meat in it, and a stallholder is judged almost entirely on the thick, matte, ochre-coloured sauce ladled over the top. So the meat gets one paragraph of technique and the gravy gets the rest of the card, which is the correct proportion.\n\n⚖️ AND THE GRAVY IS THICKENED WITH RICE FLOUR, WHICH DOES NOT DISSOLVE. IT SUSPENDS. That one fact is where every problem with this sauce comes from, and almost all of them happen in the thirty seconds the slurry goes into the pot.\n\nBOIL THE MEAT FIRST, AND BOIL IT IN ONE PIECE. Put the 150g of beef into a pot with the 400ml of water, the 2 bay leaves, the bruised 8g of lemongrass and 2g of the salt, and simmer gently, lid on, for 60 to 90 minutes until a skewer meets no resistance. ⛔ Do NOT cut it up first. This is the one piece of skewer technique that belongs to this card rather than to Sate Ayam: small pieces simmered loose for an hour give everything they have to the stock and come out stringy and grey, while a whole piece holds its juice and — the practical part — slices cleanly afterwards into flat even pieces that thread properly. Cut it hot, into 2cm flat slices across the grain, once it comes out. ⚖️ Sate Ayam's card owns the threading, the bare handle and the two-zone fire, and none of it is repeated here.\n\n⚖️ THE STOCK IS NOT A BY-PRODUCT, IT IS THE SAUCE. Ayam Goreng Kalasan already makes the full argument for never pouring a braising liquid away, and it is taken as read — except that here it goes further than that card does, because the liquid is not merely reused, it IS the dish. Strain it, and skim the fat off the top properly with a spoon. ⚠️ Loose fat and starch in the same hot pot is what makes a gravy look greasy and broken rather than glossy and thick, so this is worth two minutes.\n\nFRY THE PASTE, THEN LET IT MEET THE STOCK. Pound the 20g of shallots, the 12g of red chilli, the 10g of garlic, the 8g of fresh ginger, the 8g of galangal, the 5g of coriander seeds, the 6g of ground turmeric, the 3g of ground cumin and the 2g of cinnamon into a coarse paste. Fry it in a dry-ish pan for 5 minutes until it darkens and smells cooked rather than raw, then pour the strained stock in and simmer 10 minutes so the spices open into the liquid. Taste and add the remaining salt now — ⚠️ season BEFORE the starch goes in, because once it is thick, salt sits on the surface and takes a great deal of stirring to distribute, and stirring is the one thing you will shortly want to do less of.\n\n⛔ NOW THE SLURRY, AND THE TWO RULES ARE COLD AND SLOW. Mix the 25g of rice flour with about 60ml of COLD water — taken from the measured 400ml at the start, or fresh — until completely smooth. ⚠️ Stir it again immediately before you pour, because rice flour settles into a solid pad at the bottom of a cup within a minute or two; Kue Lapis's card makes the same point about tapioca and it is the same fault. Then pour it into the simmering stock in a THIN steady stream while stirring constantly.\n\n⚖️ WHY DUMPING IT IN CANNOT WORK, MECHANICALLY: the instant dry-ish flour hits hot liquid, the starch on the outside of each little clump gelatinises and forms a skin — and that skin is waterproof. You now have a lump of dry flour in a sealed jacket, and no amount of whisking will open it. You are not mixing flour into water; you are racing gelatinisation, and a thin stream into moving liquid is how you win. ⚖️ Es Cendol's card cooks rice flour to a paste FROM COLD, where lumping is impossible because everything heats together — this is the opposite problem, introducing starch into liquid that is already hot, and it is the harder of the two.\n\n⚖️ THEN WAIT TWO FULL MINUTES BEFORE YOU JUDGE IT — THIS IS WHERE MOST PEOPLE RUIN THE SAUCE. Rice starch gelatinises somewhere around 70 to 78°C, noticeably higher than wheat flour. So for the first minute after the slurry goes in, almost nothing appears to happen: the pot looks thin, the cook concludes it has not worked, adds more slurry — and then all of it reaches temperature at once and you have wallpaper paste in a pan. ✅ Add the slurry, keep it at a gentle simmer, stir slowly, and give it two minutes on a clock before you form any opinion at all. Judge it late, or you will judge it twice.\n\n✅ THEN COOK IT OUT — 3 TO 4 MINUTES MORE. Once thickened it still tastes chalky and raw; a few minutes at a bare simmer takes that off and it is the difference between a gravy and a paste in a bowl.\n\n⚖️ AND KNOW THAT A STARCH SAUCE HAS A PEAK AND THEN GOES BACKWARDS, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF EVERY OTHER SAUCE IN THIS BOOK. A reduction gets thicker the longer you cook it, so longer is always safer. This one does not work that way at all. The granules swell and thicken, they reach a peak — and then, held at a hard boil and stirred vigorously, they rupture and the sauce THINS again and turns slightly slimy in the mouth. ⛔ So there is a window and you come off the heat inside it: gentle simmer, minimal stirring once it has thickened, and stop while it still coats the back of a spoon in a layer you can draw a clean line through. ✅ This also explains a rule that looks arbitrary — anything sour goes in OFF the heat at the very end, because acid breaks starch chains and will thin a finished gravy in front of you.\n\n⚖️ RICE FLOUR AND NOT CORNFLOUR, AND YOU CAN SEE THE DIFFERENCE ACROSS A ROOM. Rice starch sets matte, opaque, dense and slightly short — it clings to a skewer, holds its shape on the plate and stays thick as it cools. Cornflour sets glossy, translucent and springy, and firms into a wobble in the fridge. ⛔ A Padang gravy that looks shiny has been made with the wrong starch. If rice flour is genuinely unavailable, plain wheat flour is a closer substitute here than cornflour is, cooked out for longer.\n\nTHREAD AND GRILL — 2 MINUTES A SIDE, AND THE FIRE DOES NOT COOK ANYTHING. Soak the 4 bamboo skewers, thread the sliced boiled beef on tightly, and grill over hot coals or in a smoking-hot ridged pan for about 2 minutes a side, purely for char and smoke. ✅ The meat has been cooked for an hour and a half already; the fire contributes colour and nothing else. ⚖️ Ayam Pop's card explains at length why doneness is checked BEFORE a fire and never judged by eye afterwards, and that argument holds exactly here.\n\nSERVE IT DROWNED. Skewers on the plate, gravy ladled over generously so it pools rather than dresses, and fried shallots on top. This is not a sauce you dip into — the entire arrangement is designed so that every mouthful has gravy on it, and a Padang stall will pour more on if you look like you are running short.","cookTime":"Simmer 90 min · Paste 5 min · Gravy 15 min · Grill 4 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 33g · Carbs 26g · Fat 20g · Sodium ~1500mg","storage":"The two halves keep differently and should be stored apart. ✅ The gravy keeps 4 days in the fridge and freezes for 3 months, and it is worth making double while the mortar is out — it is the long part of the recipe and it reheats far better than most starch sauces because rice flour holds up in a way cornflour does not. ⚠️ It will set almost solid in the fridge, which is normal and is not a fault: that is starch retrogradation, the same firming that happens to a day-old loaf. Loosen it with hot water, a splash at a time, over a LOW heat. ⛔ Do not boil it hard to thin it out — that pushes it past the peak described in the method, the granules rupture and you get a thin slimy sauce that cannot be brought back. Gentle heat and added water only. The cooked meat keeps 3 days on or off the skewers and can be grilled straight from cold, which is genuinely convenient: the whole dish becomes a ten-minute job if the gravy and the boiled beef are already in the fridge, which is more or less how a stall runs it. ✅ Boiled beef also freezes well for 2 months in its own strained stock rather than dry, which stops it going woolly. ⚠️ Do not freeze it already threaded — bamboo splinters after a freeze and thaw.","chefNotes":"The gravy is the dish and the meat is the vehicle, so most of the care goes into the sauce. Boil the beef in ONE piece for 60 to 90 minutes and slice it afterwards — pieces simmered loose go stringy and grey. The stock IS the gravy: strain it and skim the fat properly, because loose fat plus starch is what makes a gravy look broken. Rice flour does not dissolve, it suspends: mix it with COLD water, stir it again right before pouring, and add it in a thin stream to moving liquid, because dumped in, the outside of each lump gelatinises into a waterproof skin around dry flour. Then WAIT two full minutes before judging the thickness — rice starch gelatinises late, around 70 to 78°C, and the panic-second-addition is what turns this sauce into paste. Cook it out 3 to 4 minutes. Then stop, because a starch sauce peaks and then goes BACKWARDS: held at a hard boil it ruptures and thins. Anything sour goes in off the heat, since acid breaks starch chains. Rice flour, never cornflour — matte and opaque is right, glossy is the wrong starch.","pairsWith":"It is street food and it is eaten with something starchy underneath to catch the gravy — in Padang that is ketupat or lontong, compressed rice cakes, and at home a bowl of plain hot rice does the same job perfectly well. ✅ Fried shallots on top are not optional in any meaningful sense; they are the only crisp thing on a plate of soft meat and thick sauce, and the dish feels one-note without them. Beyond that it wants almost nothing, because the gravy is already carrying chilli, turmeric, ginger, galangal and cinnamon and there is no room for another loud thing. ⛔ Do not serve it with peanut sauce of any kind — that belongs to a different sate entirely and the two sauces on one plate is a genuine mess. ✅ A cold pickle or some raw cucumber on the side is welcome and is about the only addition that helps.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Acar Kuning","Bawang Goreng","Sambal Terasi"],"trivia":"Padang food comes from a people whose houses are built to move, in a place where the ground does not stay still.\n\nWest Sumatra sits directly on the Sumatran Fault, one of the most seismically active landscapes on earth, and the Minangkabau built for it centuries before anyone used the phrase earthquake engineering. The rumah gadang — the big house, with those extraordinary roofs that sweep up into sharp horn-shaped peaks at each end — is traditionally built with **no nails at all**. The timbers are jointed and pegged, and the whole frame is designed to flex: the posts do not go into the ground but stand on flat stone footings, so in a quake the building slides and sways on its stones and absorbs the movement instead of resisting it and snapping. ✅ It is the same principle modern base isolation uses under hospitals and bridges, arrived at by carpenters, and it is the reason old rumah gadang have survived earthquakes that flattened newer buildings around them.\n\nThe roof shape is said to represent buffalo horns, and the name Minangkabau is popularly read as *menang kerbau* — victorious buffalo — from a legend in which a war with a Javanese kingdom was settled not by fighting but by a buffalo contest that the Minangkabau won by cunning. ⚖️ The house is worth knowing about for a second reason, and it is the more surprising fact: the Minangkabau are the largest matrilineal society in the world. Ancestral property, land and the rumah gadang itself pass from mother to daughter, and the house belongs to the women of the lineage. It is even built to reflect that — bays are added along its length as daughters marry, each getting her own chamber, so the building physically grows with the family that owns it. A house that expands sideways for daughters and sways on stones in an earthquake is a remarkable amount of thinking to find in one roofline.\n\nAnd the food culture that came out of West Sumatra has its own famous piece of design. A proper Padang restaurant does not hand you a menu. The waiter arrives carrying a tower of a dozen or more small plates stacked up his arm and sets every one of them on your table — rendang, curries, greens, fried things, sambals — and you eat whatever you feel like. ✅ You are charged only for the plates you actually touch, and the rest go back untouched to be served to the next table. It is a system that trades enormous trust and turnover for zero ordering time, and it is why Padang food spread across Indonesia faster than any other regional cuisine: the format is as portable as the recipes.\n\n🌱 On the turmeric and ginger, both of which are expensive per gram and both of which are the same trick: you grow them from a piece bought to cook with. Choose a plump rhizome with visible buds — the little pale nubs — break it into pieces with a bud or two each, and plant them flat and shallow, about 5cm deep, in a big pot of loose soil in spring. ⚠️ Both want warmth, moisture and shade from the harshest afternoon sun, and both die back completely in a Highveld winter, which is not the plant failing but the plant telling you it is ready: lift the pot once the leaves have yellowed, and you will find several times what you planted. ✅ Fresh turmeric in particular is worth the pot, because ground turmeric is easy to buy here and fresh turmeric is very nearly impossible — and they are not the same ingredient. Keep back your best rhizomes for next spring and the cycle pays for itself indefinitely.","howThisFeels":"Ladling a sauce thick enough to stand a spoon in over four small skewers, and knowing the skewers were never really the point.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold gravy loosened with hot water over a low heat and poured over fresh rice with a fried egg, which is a complete supper and does not need the meat at all.","(easy) The boiled beef grilled straight from the fridge — two minutes a side — so a plate of this is ten minutes from cold if both halves are already made.","(medium) The gravy thinned rather further with stock and used as a soup base, with noodles and greens dropped into it; it is essentially a finished spice base already.","(creative) Thick cold gravy spread on toasted bread with the beef sliced over it, which sounds wrong and is excellent — the rice-flour set means it behaves like a savoury paste rather than running off."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sate Padang Rumahan — Short Spice, One Pot (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Four skewers, a thick yellow sauce and nothing clever, on a Tuesday, for the price of a loaf of bread.","trivia":"The cuts of beef this dish was built on are the cheap ones, and that is the whole reason the technique exists rather than an economy applied to it later. An hour and a half of gentle simmering is exactly what shin, chuck and offal need and exactly what a tender expensive steak cannot survive — put rump through this method and you get grey string. ✅ So the budget version is not a lesser Sate Padang, it is closer to the original: this is a dish designed to make a small amount of hard-working meat feed people, with a thick starch-bound sauce doing the job of stretching it further. What comes out of the spice list here is the top layer rather than the foundation — galangal, cumin and cinnamon are the aromatics, while turmeric, chilli, ginger, garlic and shallot are the structure. Take out the foundation and it stops being Padang; take out the top layer and it is simply a plainer version of the same thing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"8g galangal"},{"item":"3g ground cumin"},{"item":"2g cinnamon"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef stew pieces","to":"100g beef stew pieces"},{"from":"8g lemongrass","to":"4g lemongrass"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A smaller piece of beef, half the lemongrass, and no galangal, cumin or cinnamon — so the paste is shallot, chilli, garlic, ginger, turmeric and coriander, which is still a proper Padang foundation. ⚖️ Nothing about the gravy relaxes and nothing should: cold slurry, thin stream into moving liquid, two full minutes before you judge it, cook it out, and come off the heat before the peak. That sequence is free and it is the entire dish. ✅ With less beef the stock is lighter, so reduce the water to 300ml before the flour goes in rather than adding more flour to compensate — thickening a weak stock harder just gives you thick weak stock, and reducing concentrates what is actually there. ⚠️ Keep the full 25g of rice flour despite the smaller volume; you want the sauce properly thick, and this fork has less meat to hide behind. ✅ Fried shallots on top matter more here than in any other version, because they are now doing all of the crunch and a good part of the flavour."}]}},{"name":"Sate Padang Panjang — Yellow Gravy, Rice Flour Slurry","icon":"🍢","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":44,"howThisFeels":"Drawing a spoon through the pot and watching the line stay open for a second before it closes.","trivia":"There are three recognised styles of this dish and they are argued about in the way people argue about barbecue, which is to say seriously and by town. Padang Panjang, up in the highlands, makes the yellow one: turmeric-forward, thick, aromatic rather than fierce, and it is the version most people outside West Sumatra have eaten. Pariaman, on the coast, makes it red and much hotter. Padang city itself sits between the two, in colour and in heat, and is sometimes described as the diplomatic version. ✅ What is genuinely interesting is that the difference is almost entirely in the GRAVY rather than in the meat or the method — the same boiled beef, the same skewers, the same fire, the same rice-flour thickening — which tells you plainly where the identity of the dish lives. Three towns, one technique, and an argument about a sauce.","delta":null},{"name":"Sate Pariaman — Red, Fierce, with Tripe","icon":"🌶️","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":55,"howThisFeels":"A dark red sauce that stains the rice, and the first mouthful telling you the coast does not compromise.","trivia":"Offal is not an economy in Sate Padang, it is the traditional composition — tongue, tripe and heart alongside the beef, and a stall will ask you which you want. Tripe in particular earns its place for a textural reason rather than a thrifty one: it has almost no flavour of its own but a springy, slightly chewy resistance that is the exact opposite of the long-simmered beef beside it, so a skewer with both on it is more interesting than a skewer with either alone. ⚠️ It does need honest handling — tripe sold in South Africa is usually cleaned and part-cooked, but it still wants a good hour and a half, and undercooked tripe is genuinely unpleasant in a way that undercooked beef is not. The red colour here is dried chilli rather than fresh, which is worth knowing as a general principle: dried chillies give a deeper, sweeter, more brick-coloured heat with much less of the grassy sharpness fresh ones bring, which is why the Pariaman version reads as darker and rounder even at a much higher chilli weight.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g tripe"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"12g red chilli","to":"25g red chilli"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Add 80g of tripe and double the chilli to 25g. ✅ Rinse the tripe well and cut it into large pieces, then simmer it in the same pot as the beef for the full 90 minutes — it wants every minute and it will not be ready sooner. ⚠️ It gives up a fair amount of its own liquid and a distinct smell in the first twenty minutes; skim the surface a couple of times early on and both settle down completely. Slice it after boiling into strips roughly the size of the beef slices, and thread the two alternately so every skewer carries both textures. ⛔ Do not thread tripe and beef on separate skewers — the point of it is the contrast within a mouthful. ✅ For the deeper red, use dried chillies if you have them: soak 25g in hot water for 20 minutes, drain, and pound them into the paste in place of the fresh. The colour is markedly better and the heat is rounder. ⚠️ Fry the paste a little longer at this chilli weight, 7 minutes rather than 5, because there is more solid matter to cook the rawness out of — and keep the extractor on and the window open, since frying that quantity of chilli will make everyone in the house cough."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Ayam Pop","target":"indonesia-ayam-pop","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-sate-klathak","name":"Sate Klathak","nameAlt":"Yogyakarta mutton grilled on iron spokes, salt and pepper only","aliases":["Sate Klathak","Satay Klathak","Sate Kambing Klathak","Bicycle Spoke Satay","Yogyakarta Goat Satay","Sate Klathak Kuah Gulai","Iron Skewer Satay"],"course":"main","type":["main","mutton","grill","braai","street-food","quick","special"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["braai","family","weekend","guests","special"],"ingredients":"180g mutton · 6g salt · 4g black pepper","method":"⚖️ THE SKEWER IS THE COOKING TOOL HERE, NOT THE HANDLE — AND THAT ONE SENTENCE IS THE WHOLE DISH. Sate klathak is grilled on iron: traditionally on bicycle spokes, which is not a piece of local colour or a hard-up improvisation. It is the reason the recipe works, and once you see why, every other decision on this card follows from it.\n\nSteel conducts heat hundreds of times better than wood does. A metal spoke lying across hot coals reaches fire temperature along its entire length within about a minute, and it carries that heat straight into the **geometric centre** of every chunk of meat threaded onto it. That centre is the exact place that is normally last to cook — it is the whole reason a kebab comes off the fire charred outside and pink in the middle. Here it is being heated from the inside at the same time as the outside, by a rod running through it. ⛔ Bamboo can do none of this. Wood is an insulator; a soaked bamboo skewer is a wet stick that steams, chars and then protects the meat around it. It is the correct tool for a different job, and it is the wrong tool for this one.\n\n⚖️ AND BECAUSE OF THAT, THE MEAT IS CUT BIG — WHICH INVERTS EVERY OTHER SATE IN THIS BOOK. Cut the 180g of mutton into large 3 to 4cm cubes. ⛔ Sate Ayam's card is explicit that you slice thigh across the grain into flat 5mm pieces and never cubes, and it is right — over a bamboo skewer all the heat arrives from the surface, so thin is the only way the middle ever cooks before the outside burns. This card does the exact opposite for the exact opposite reason. Heat is arriving from the middle as well, so a big chunk is not a problem; it is the point. ✅ Big chunks stay juicy in a way thin slices cannot, and that juiciness is most of why people queue for this.\n\n⚠️ USE STAINLESS STEEL, AND READ THIS BEFORE YOU RAID THE SHED. Ordinary stainless-steel barbecue skewers work identically and are what most people should use — a bicycle spoke is simply a thin, rigid, free piece of steel with a threaded end that stops the meat sliding off. ⛔ NEVER use galvanised or zinc-plated wire, fencing wire, or coated wire of any kind. Heated zinc gives off fumes that make people genuinely ill, and no dish is worth it. If you do not know what a piece of metal is, do not put food on it and do not put it in a fire. Stainless steel, or a proper skewer.\n\n⚠️ AND THE SKEWER WILL BE FEROCIOUSLY HOT ALONG ITS WHOLE LENGTH, WHICH IS NOT TRUE OF BAMBOO. There is no cool handle end. This is the direct consequence of the thing that makes the dish work, and it catches people out every time. **Tongs, always, and never fingers.** Lay them down on a metal tray or a plate rather than a wooden board, which they will scorch. Keep children well back from the fire while these are cooking, and count them off the grill so none gets left leaning somewhere.\n\nSEASON IT WITH SALT AND PEPPER AND NOTHING ELSE, AND MEAN IT. Toss the cubes with the 6g of salt and the 4g of black pepper, coarsely cracked rather than finely ground, and leave them 20 minutes at room temperature. ✅ That is the entire seasoning. No kecap manis, no turmeric, no bumbu, no marinade, no glaze — which in an Indonesian sate is close to heresy and is exactly what the dish is known for. ⚖️ There are two reasons and both are worth having. The first is a claim about the ingredient: klathak is made with young goat and it exists to taste of goat, so anything loud enough to cover it defeats the purpose. The second is technical and it is the one that matters at the fire — **there is no sugar anywhere in this recipe, so there is nothing that can burn.** Sate Ayam has to build two heat zones and glaze late because kecap manis is a third sugar and turns bitter over high heat in under a minute. With salt and pepper you can put the meat over the fiercest part of the coals and leave it there, which is precisely what this dish needs.\n\n✅ COARSE PEPPER, NOT FINE, AND IT IS NOT FUSSINESS. Finely ground pepper on a surface that is about to sit over fierce coals scorches and goes acrid and dusty. Cracked coarse, it stays in fragments big enough to survive, and you get bursts of pepper rather than a uniform grey heat over everything.\n\nTHREAD TIGHT, TWO OR THREE CHUNKS TO A SPOKE. Push the cubes on so they touch each other — gaps let the exposed metal between them get hotter than the meat and it will scorch what it touches. Two or three chunks per spoke, no more, so each skewer stays manageable with tongs.\n\nBUILD A FIERCE FIRE AND USE IT. You want coals you can only hold a hand over for two or three seconds — hotter than almost anything else in this file. ⚖️ Ayam Taliwang's card explains why a sugared glaze cannot survive a fire like that, and it is the same physics read from the other end: with no sugar on the surface, this is the one Indonesian grill where the fiercest heat is an advantage rather than a hazard, and a timid fire actively makes it worse by giving the outside time to dry out before the inside is done.\n\nGRILL 6 TO 8 MINUTES IN TOTAL, TURNING EVERY 90 SECONDS OR SO, AND CHECK BY CUTTING. ⚠️ This is where the conduction catches people out in the other direction: **klathak reaches doneness noticeably sooner than your eyes expect**, because the middle has had a head start the entire time. A chunk this size on bamboo would need twelve minutes and would still be underdone in the centre. ✅ So do not judge it by char. At 6 minutes take one skewer off, cut a chunk in half, and look: you want it hot all the way through with the very centre still faintly pink and juicy, not grey. If the outside looks perfect and the middle is grey, the fire was too weak and it took too long. ⛔ Do not cook mutton on a spoke to well done — the whole reason for the technique is a big juicy chunk, and cooking the juice out of it gives you an expensive dry cube on a stick.\n\nREST 3 MINUTES, ON THE SKEWERS, ON A WARM PLATE. Not long — these are small pieces and they cool fast — but three minutes lets the juices settle back through the chunk instead of running out at the first bite. ⚠️ And the metal is still conducting during the rest, which means the meat carries on cooking a little more than a bamboo skewer would allow. Factor that in: pull it a shade before you think you should.\n\n✅ SERVE THEM STILL ON THE SPOKES, WITH RICE AND SOMETHING SHARP. In Yogyakarta they are laid straight into a bowl of thin goat curry, which is the version at the bottom of this card. Plain is how you should eat the first one.","cookTime":"Cut 10 min · Season 20 min · Grill 8 min · Rest 3 min","kcal":"~450 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 41g · Carbs 2g · Fat 31g · Sodium ~1900mg","storage":"Cooked mutton keeps 3 days in the fridge and it is worth pulling the chunks off the skewers before they go in, because cold metal in a container is awkward and the meat keeps perfectly well loose. ⚠️ Reheating is the weak point of this particular dish and it is honest to say so: the entire appeal is a big chunk that is juicy in the middle, and a second heat is exactly what takes that away. ⛔ Never reheat it over the fire again or under a grill — the outside is already fully cooked and the only thing left for fierce heat to do is dry it. ✅ The best route by a distance is not to reheat it at all: mutton is very good cold, sliced thin against the grain, with something sharp. If you must have it hot, slice it first and warm it through for one minute in a hot sauce or gravy, where the liquid protects it. It freezes for 2 months but comes back noticeably drier, so freeze it only if the plan is to slice it into something wet. ✅ Raw seasoned cubes, on the other hand, are excellent to prepare ahead: salted and peppered, covered, they hold 24 hours in the fridge and are genuinely better for it, because the salt has time to work into the middle of a 4cm chunk rather than sitting on the outside. ⚠️ Do not go much past a day, or the surface goes tacky and cures rather than seasons.","chefNotes":"The skewer is the cooking tool, not the handle. Steel conducts heat into the geometric centre of each chunk while bamboo insulates — so this is the one sate where the meat is cut BIG, in 3 to 4cm cubes, and Sate Ayam's flat 5mm slices would be wrong. ⛔ Stainless steel or proper skewers only, never galvanised or coated wire, and the whole skewer will be scorching hot with no cool end, so tongs and never fingers. Salt and coarse-cracked pepper is the entire seasoning, and the technical payoff is that with no sugar anywhere there is nothing to burn — so you can use the fiercest coals in the book and leave it there. Grill 6 to 8 minutes, turning often, and check by CUTTING one open rather than by char, because the conduction means it is done sooner than your eyes expect. Centre faintly pink and juicy. Rest 3 minutes, and pull it a shade early because the metal keeps cooking it while it rests.","pairsWith":"Rice, and something sour or sharp, and very little else — the seasoning here is so deliberately bare that anything complicated beside it makes the whole plate incoherent. ✅ A cold pickle is close to essential: mutton is rich and fatty and a sharp vinegary vegetable resets the palate between chunks in a way nothing else on the table will. A raw sliced sambal works for the same reason. In Yogyakarta the standard is rice, a bowl of thin goat curry, sliced raw cabbage and tomato, and a cup of very sweet tea, which sounds like an odd drink beside mutton and is actually the local answer to the fat. ⛔ Do not serve it with peanut sauce or kecap manis — you would be putting back exactly what the dish spent its whole identity removing. ⛔ And do not put it next to another grilled meat; a table with two grills on it wastes the one that is subtler, and here that is this one.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Acar Kuning","Sambal Matah","Bawang Goreng"],"trivia":"Yogyakarta's other great piece of ironwork is a dagger, and some of them were forged from metal that fell out of the sky.\n\nThe keris is the wavy-bladed Javanese dagger, and it is not primarily a weapon — it is an heirloom, a status object and, in the older understanding, a thing with a character of its own, passed down, named, and looked after. What makes a good one is the **pamor**: the pale, watery, fingerprint-like pattern running through the blade. That pattern is not etched on or inlaid. It is structural. The smith forge-welds two different metals together in alternating layers — ordinary iron and a nickel-rich metal — then folds and re-forges the billet many times over, so the two become hundreds of interleaved layers running through the finished blade. ✅ The pattern you see is the cross-section of that folding, and it is only revealed at the very end, when the blade is treated with an acidic mixture that eats into the iron and leaves the nickel standing very slightly proud and paler. The acid does not make the pattern. It uncovers one that was built in.\n\n⚖️ AND THE NICKEL IS THE REMARKABLE PART. Nickel-bearing iron is not something Java had a supply of, and the traditional source for the finest pamor was **meteoric iron** — specifically, for a long period, material from a meteorite that fell near Prambanan in the eighteenth century and was taken into the keeping of the court at Surakarta, where it was portioned out over generations to make blades. Nickel-iron meteorites are fragments of the cores of shattered protoplanets, so a high-status keris quite literally contains metal that formed in the middle of a small world that broke apart before this one had finished assembling. When that ran short, smiths moved to imported industrial nickel, which is what most modern pamor is. It is a rare case of a legend and a metallurgical fact being the same story.\n\nWhich is a long way of saying that a city with that much history in iron working out that a thin steel rod cooks meat from the inside is not an accident. And the name of the dish is worth having too: klathak is onomatopoeic — the sound of coarse salt crackling and popping as it hits the coals under the meat. The dish is named after a noise.\n\n💡 One thing about the pepper, since there are only three ingredients here and pepper is one of them. Black, white and green peppercorns are all the same fruit off the same vine; the difference is entirely when they are picked and what is done to them afterwards. Green is the unripe berry preserved before it can dry. Black is the unripe berry left to dry, its skin shrivelling and darkening. **White is the FULLY RIPE berry soaked in water for days until the outer flesh ferments and rots away, then rubbed off, leaving the pale seed inside.** That is why white pepper has that distinctive slightly funky, barnyard note that black pepper does not — it is not a milder pepper, it is a fermented one, and people who dislike it are usually reacting to the ferment rather than to the pepper. Worth knowing next time a recipe specifies one over the other, because they are genuinely not interchangeable.","howThisFeels":"Lifting a red-hot steel rod off the coals with tongs and understanding, from the weight and the heat coming off it, that the metal has been cooking the meat from the middle the whole time.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold, sliced thin against the grain, with a sharp pickle and bread — mutton is genuinely good cold and this avoids the reheating problem entirely.","(easy) Sliced into a hot bowl of noodle soup at the last second, where the broth warms it through in under a minute without drying it.","(medium) Chopped small and fried hard with rice, cold from the fridge, so the fat renders out and re-crisps the edges — dry leftover mutton is improved rather than punished by this.","(creative) The cold chunks sliced and folded into a sharp raw salad of cabbage, tomato and shallot with plenty of lime, which is more or less what is already on the plate beside it in Yogyakarta, rearranged."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Klathak Polos — Fewer Chunks, No Curry (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Three things on the shopping list and a fire, which is the shortest route to a good supper in this whole book.","trivia":"This is the cheapest record in the file to SHOP for and one of the more expensive to eat, and the gap between those two facts is the honest lesson in it. There are three ingredients. There is no paste, no coconut milk, no aromatic bought by the piece and used by the gram — none of the small costs that quietly make up half the price of most cards here. And yet almost the entire cost is the mutton, which means this is one of the very few dishes where buying better meat genuinely is the only upgrade available and cutting the meat is the only saving available. ✅ There is a real cook's advantage hiding in that. A three-line recipe has nowhere to hide, so it is the best possible dish to learn a fire on: if it comes out badly you know precisely which of the four variables was wrong — the cut, the size, the heat, or the time — because there is nothing else in the pot to blame.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g mutton","to":"130g mutton"},{"from":"6g salt","to":"4g salt"}],"addStep":[{"text":"A smaller portion and proportionally less salt — and nothing else changes, because there is nothing else to change. ✅ Cut the 130g into the same 3 to 4cm chunks rather than making them smaller to get the same number of skewers; **the chunk size is the recipe** and shrinking it to 2cm turns this into an ordinary kebab that happens to be on metal. Fewer, bigger pieces, always. ⚠️ Watch the timing more closely with less meat on the grill: a lightly loaded fire runs hotter, because there is less cold meat absorbing the heat, so start cutting one open to check at 5 minutes rather than 6. ✅ Serve it with more rice and a bigger pile of pickle rather than more meat — that is how a smaller portion is made to feel like a full plate anywhere in the world, and it is genuinely how it is eaten."}]}},{"name":"Sate Klathak — Salt, Pepper, Steel","icon":"🔩","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36,"howThisFeels":"Two enormous chunks on a bare metal rod with nothing on them but salt, arriving at the table looking almost rude.","trivia":"The bicycle spoke is the detail everyone repeats about this dish, and the interesting question is not why a spoke but why a bicycle. Java runs on bicycles in a way that is hard to overstate — the Dutch brought the roadster bicycle and it never left, so the onthel, the heavy black single-speed Dutch-pattern bike, is still a familiar object across Javanese towns, and Yogyakarta in particular is a bicycle city. Which means that for a warung cook in the second half of the twentieth century, a bicycle spoke was the most available thin steel rod in existence: rigid, cheap or free, roughly the right length, and helpfully finished at one end with a threaded nipple that stops the meat sliding off. ✅ It was not chosen for its romance. It was chosen because it was lying around, it did the job better than bamboo, and somebody noticed. That is how most good kitchen technique actually arrives, and it is worth remembering the next time a recipe insists on a specialist tool.","delta":null},{"name":"Sate Klathak Kuah Gulai — Sitting in Thin Goat Curry","icon":"🍛","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"Two skewers laid across a bowl of pale yellow curry, half in and half out, which is exactly how it is served and looks like nothing else.","trivia":"The gulai is deliberately THIN, and that is the opposite of what most people expect from an Indonesian curry — it is closer to a soup than a sauce, and it arrives in a separate bowl with the skewers laid across or dropped straight in. The reason is that it is not there to flavour the meat, which has already been decided, permanently, by salt and pepper and fire. It is there to do two other jobs: to carry the rice, and to put the aromatics BACK on the table in a place where they cannot interfere with the grilling. ✅ It is a genuinely clever piece of menu design when you look at it as a whole — the spice complexity that every other sate builds into a marinade or a glaze has simply been relocated into a bowl beside the fire, where none of it can burn. The meat gets to taste of meat, the spices get to taste of spices, and the eater combines them at the last possible moment. Nothing has been given up; it has been moved.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"150ml water"},{"item":"100ml coconut milk"},{"item":"20g shallots"},{"item":"10g garlic"},{"item":"6g ground turmeric"},{"item":"5g coriander seeds"},{"item":"4g fresh ginger"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Grill the sate exactly as described — nothing about it changes, and in particular ⛔ do not be tempted to brush any of the curry onto the meat, which would undo the entire dish. Make the gulai while the fire settles. ✅ Pound the 20g of shallots, the 10g of garlic, the 4g of fresh ginger, the 5g of coriander seeds toasted dry for a minute first, and the 6g of ground turmeric into a paste, and fry it in a dry-ish pan for 5 minutes until it darkens and smells cooked. Add the 150ml of water, simmer 10 minutes, then add the 100ml of coconut milk and hold it at a bare quiver for 10 minutes more. ⛔ Never let it boil once the coconut milk is in — Sayur Lodeh's card sets out exactly what heat and salt do to a coconut emulsion, and it applies here without modification. ⚠️ Keep it genuinely thin, at about the consistency of a light soup: if it starts to reduce and thicken, add hot water back. A thick gulai turns this into a curry with meat in it, and the whole architecture of the dish depends on the bowl staying a broth. ✅ Season it at the very end, and season it LIGHTLY — the meat is already carrying 6g of salt and the two arrive in the same mouthful. Ladle it into a wide bowl and lay the skewers across the top."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Sate Ayam","target":"indonesia-sate-ayam","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Sate Padang","target":"indonesia-sate-padang","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Acar Kuning","target":"indonesia-acar-kuning","emoji":"🥒"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-ayam-tangkap","name":"Ayam Tangkap","nameAlt":"Acehnese fried chicken buried under a haystack of crisp leaves","aliases":["Ayam Tangkap","Ayam Tangkap Aceh","Acehnese Fried Chicken","Ayam Temurui","Caught Chicken","Aceh Curry Leaf Chicken","Ayam Tangkap Daun"],"course":"main","type":["main","chicken","fried","herb","street-food","spiced","special"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","special","weekend","sunday"],"ingredients":"400ml sunflower oil · 200g chicken pieces · 25g curry leaves · 15ml lime juice · 12g garlic · 10g shallots · 8g fresh ginger · 6g ground turmeric · 5g salt · 4g birds eye chillies","method":"⚖️ THE LEAVES ARE NOT A GARNISH. THEY OUTWEIGH NOTHING AND THEY OUT-VOLUME EVERYTHING — a proper ayam tangkap arrives as a small mountain of dark crisp leaves with pieces of fried chicken hidden somewhere inside it, and you eat the leaves. By the fistful. That is the dish, and if you put a tasteful scattering of six leaves on top of some fried chicken you have not made a restrained version of it, you have made something else.\n\n⚖️ AND FRYING A LEAF IS A DEHYDRATION RACE MEASURED IN SECONDS, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME PROBLEM AS FRYING ANYTHING ELSE ON THIS SHELF. A leaf is a sheet of water held in a very thin flat structure, so its surface area relative to its volume is enormous. Drop it into hot oil and essentially all of its moisture leaves at once: it spits hard for two or three seconds, goes quiet, turns from glossy green to dark and matte, and is done. **Ten seconds, start to finish.** ⛔ The Ayam Goreng Kalasan card has a version buried in crisp fried galangal and makes the full argument there for an aromatic being a texture as well as a flavour — but grated galangal is a dense fibrous shred and takes 60 to 90 seconds. A leaf has a tenth of that. Do not carry the timing across; the two look similar on a plate and behave nothing alike in the pan.\n\n⚠️⚠️ WHICH MAKES THE SAFETY NOTE THE MOST IMPORTANT PARAGRAPH ON THIS CARD, AND I WOULD RATHER SPEND IT HERE THAN ON TECHNIQUE. Putting wet leaves into 180°C oil is the single most violent thing anyone does in this whole file. All that water flashing to steam at once throws oil out of the pan, and a leaf carries a surprising amount of water for something so light. ✅ So: **the leaves must be BONE DRY before they go anywhere near the oil.** Wash them well ahead, shake them out hard, pat every one between two cloths, and then spread them in a single layer to air-dry for at least 30 minutes — an hour is better. ✅ Use a deep pan, no more than a third full of oil. ✅ Add them a SMALL handful at a time, dropped in low and away from you, never tipped in from a height. ✅ Have a lid or a spatter guard in your other hand before you start, and stand back rather than leaning over the pan. ⛔ Never put anything wet into hot oil, and never leave the pan while it is hot. Children and pets out of the kitchen for this one.\n\nMARINATE THE CHICKEN FIRST, AND CUT IT SMALL. Chop the 200g of chicken pieces into small bone-in chunks — smaller than you would for any other fried chicken, roughly 4cm, so that a piece is a mouthful or two rather than a course. ✅ Small pieces are not an affectation: this is eaten with the fingers out of a shared pile, and it means the chicken cooks through in the same short window that keeps the outside from over-darkening. Pound the 12g of garlic, the 8g of fresh ginger, the 4g of bird's eye chillies and the 5g of salt to a paste, mix it with the 6g of ground turmeric and the 15ml of lime juice, and rub it into the chicken. Leave it 30 minutes at room temperature, or up to 4 hours in the fridge.\n\n⚠️ THEN DRY THE CHICKEN AGAIN BEFORE IT FRIES. A wet marinade is exactly what you have just spent thirty minutes putting on, and it is also the thing that will steam rather than fry. Lift the pieces out, let the excess run off, and pat them with paper — you want the seasoning clinging to the surface, not sitting on it in a film of liquid.\n\nHEAT THE 400ml OF OIL TO 170°C AND FRY THE CHICKEN 8 TO 10 MINUTES. That is a real depth of oil and it is doing real work — small bone-in pieces dropped into a shallow pan crash its temperature and end up greasy. ⚖️ Tempe Mendoan's card sets out precisely why cool oil makes a grease sponge and hot oil does not, and it holds here without modification. Fry in two batches rather than crowding, turning once, until the pieces are deep gold and cooked through to the bone. ✅ Check one: cut into the thickest piece to the bone and look for no pink at all and clear juices, since bone-in chicken is not a thing to guess at. Lift onto a rack, never onto paper on a plate — a rack keeps the underside crisp.\n\n⛔ NOW SKIM THE OIL BEFORE THE LEAVES GO IN, AND DO NOT SKIP THIS. Lift out every scrap of loose garlic, chilli and chicken debris with a slotted spoon, because those fragments are already dark and they have about eight seconds of life left in them. A burnt fleck stuck to a fried leaf tastes acrid and there is no picking it off. ✅ Let the oil come back up to temperature — around 180°C now, slightly hotter than for the chicken, because the leaves want to go in and out fast rather than sit.\n\nFRY THE LEAVES — 8 TO 12 SECONDS, A HANDFUL AT A TIME, AND STAND OVER THEM. Drop in a small handful of the 25g of curry leaves. They will spit hard, then quieten within a couple of seconds. ✅ The cue is SOUND, not colour: **when the fierce bubbling drops away to almost nothing, the water is gone and they are done.** Lift them straight out with a spider into a sieve or onto a rack. ⛔ Leave them a further ten seconds and they go from dark green and fragrant to black and bitter, and unlike most burnt things there is no gradual warning — the whole handful turns together. Do them in three or four batches and give the oil a moment between each. Fry the 10g of shallots, sliced thin, in the same way at the very end: 30 to 40 seconds, and out while still pale gold, because they carry on darkening off the heat.\n\n✅ THE LEAVES SHOULD SHATTER, NOT BEND. A properly fried curry leaf crushes to fragments between your fingers and smells intensely of itself — far stronger fried than raw, because the frying drives off the water and concentrates everything left. ⚠️ If they are leathery rather than brittle, they went in damp or the oil was too cool, and they will go soft within minutes. There is no fixing them; fry the next handful hotter and drier.\n\n⚖️ A WORD ON THE SECOND LEAF, HONESTLY. A real Acehnese ayam tangkap uses two leaves: temurui, which is the curry leaf, and pandan, cut into short lengths and fried alongside it. Fresh pandan is not reliably sold in South Africa, so it is not on the ingredient list and it is not faked with anything. ✅ The dish stands up completely on curry leaves alone — they are the dominant flavour in the original too. If you do grow pandan or find it frozen, cut 20g into 4cm lengths, dry it as carefully as the curry leaves, and fry it FIRST and for slightly longer, 20 to 25 seconds, because a pandan leaf is thicker and tougher than a curry leaf and needs it.\n\nBUILD IT AS A PILE AND SERVE IT AT ONCE. Tip the chicken into a wide bowl or onto a platter, pour every one of the leaves over it so the chicken genuinely disappears, scatter the fried shallots, and take it to the table immediately. ⚠️ Crisp leaves last about fifteen minutes in the open and rather less on a humid Highveld evening; they take up moisture from the air and from the chicken underneath and go limp. ✅ This is a dish you fry while people are already sitting down.","cookTime":"Marinate 30 min · Dry leaves 60 min · Fry chicken 10 min · Fry leaves 2 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g · Carbs 6g · Fat 38g · Sodium ~1650mg","storage":"⛔ THE LEAVES DO NOT KEEP AND NOTHING WILL MAKE THEM. They are crisp for about fifteen minutes and there is no storage advice that changes it — an airtight jar buys perhaps an hour if the kitchen is dry, and a fridge kills them outright because it is a damp environment and they take up moisture faster than they lose it. ✅ Fry the quantity that is going to be eaten. The chicken is a different matter and keeps 3 days in the fridge; it is very good cold, and it reheats well in a hot dry pan or an oven at 200°C for 8 minutes, which brings the skin back. ⚠️ Do not microwave it. ✅ What genuinely works if you want this on a weeknight is to split the job: marinate and fry the chicken a day ahead, refrigerate it, then reheat it while a fresh handful of leaves fries in two minutes at the last second. The leaves are the fast part of the recipe, so making them the only fresh part is the sensible division. ✅ Leftover frying oil, cooled and strained through a fine sieve, is worth keeping in a sealed jar in a dark cupboard and reusing twice more for savoury frying — it will smell strongly of curry leaf, which is a bonus rather than a problem. ⚠️ Throw it out the moment it smells sharp or paint-like, or foams rather than bubbles. ⛔ Never pour hot oil into a bin or down a drain; cool it completely first.","chefNotes":"The leaves are the dish, not the garnish — the chicken should genuinely disappear under them. Frying a leaf is a dehydration race: it is a sheet of water with enormous surface area, so it spits, quietens and is DONE in about ten seconds, where Kalasan's crisp galangal shreds take 60 to 90. ⚠️ Which makes drying them the safety step, not a nicety: wet leaves in 180°C oil throw oil out of the pan. Bone dry, a small handful at a time, dropped in low and away from you, spatter guard in the other hand. Marinate the chicken, then DRY it again before it fries, and cut it small and bone-in so it is finger food. Fry the chicken at 170°C, then SKIM every scrap of debris out of the oil before the leaves go in, or a burnt fleck welds itself to a leaf. Leaves at 180°C: the cue is SOUND — when the fierce bubbling drops to nothing, they are done, and ten seconds later they are black and bitter with no warning. They should shatter, not bend. Then pile everything up and serve immediately, because crisp leaves last about fifteen minutes.","pairsWith":"Plain white rice and a wet sambal, and that combination is not a suggestion so much as the structure of the meal — everything on this plate is dry, crisp and intense, so it needs something soft underneath it and something sharp beside it or it becomes hard work halfway through. ✅ In Aceh it comes with rice, a fierce sambal, and often a bowl of thin sour soup on the side, and the sourness is doing exactly what the lime in the marinade does, only more of it. A cold pickle works just as well. ⛔ Do not serve it with anything else fried — two crisp things on one table cancel each other out and this one loses, because its texture is the more delicate of any two. ⛔ And do not put a sauce ON it, ever: the leaves have about fifteen minutes of crispness and a spoonful of anything wet ends that instantly. Sauces go beside it, in a bowl, and the eater dips the chicken and not the pile.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Sambal Terasi","Acar Kuning","Plecing Kangkung"],"trivia":"There are South Indian curry leaves in the middle of an Acehnese dish, and the reason is a wind that only blows one way for six months at a time.\n\nThe entire Indian Ocean trading world ran on the monsoon, and the monsoon is not weather in the way we usually mean it — it is a wind system that **reverses twice a year**, blowing steadily from the south-west for months and then steadily from the north-east for months. For a sailing ship that is not an inconvenience, it is a timetable. You could sail from India to Sumatra on one monsoon, and then you could not go home. ⚖️ **So you waited — for the wind to turn, which meant a season, often the better part of half a year.** And that single fact is why the port cities on those routes are the way they are. Traders did not visit them; they LIVED in them, for months at a time, every year, for centuries. They rented houses, learned the language, married locally, buried relatives, argued theology and cooked their own food with whatever grew nearby. A port on a monsoon route does not merely exchange goods. It absorbs people.\n\nAceh sits on the northern tip of Sumatra, right at the mouth of the Strait of Malacca, which is the doorway between the Indian Ocean and everything east of it — and it was the first part of Southeast Asia to take up Islam, arriving by exactly that route, with exactly those traders. It has been called Serambi Mekkah, the verandah of Mecca, ever since. ✅ So when a curry leaf turns up in an Acehnese kitchen, and cardamom and cumin turn up in Acehnese curries in a way they simply do not in Javanese ones, it is not borrowing or fusion. It is the residue of several centuries of South Indian and Arab households living there half the year because the wind would not let them leave.\n\nAnd Aceh used that position with some nerve. In the 1560s the Sultanate, under pressure from the Portuguese, sent an embassy across the Indian Ocean to the Ottoman court in Istanbul asking for military help — and got it, in the form of cannon and gunners who made the journey to Sumatra. Ottoman artillery, on the far side of the Indian Ocean, at the request of a Sumatran sultan, in the sixteenth century. The world was smaller and much better connected than it is usually given credit for, and the connection ran on a wind.\n\n🌱 And now the useful part, because this recipe asks for 25g of curry leaves and curry leaves are among the most expensive things per gram in this entire book — while also being a plant that grows extremely well right here. The curry leaf tree is a small, fast, forgiving tree that does beautifully in Pretoria; it is already common in South African gardens, particularly where there is an Indian family who has been growing one for forty years, and the usual way to get one is to ask. ✅ It wants full sun, a big pot or a warm sheltered spot, and not much else. ⚠️ It hates cold wet feet and a hard frost will knock it back, so a pot you can move against a north-facing wall in winter is the safe route on the Highveld. Pick sprigs rather than individual leaves and it bushes out. ✅ One established tree will supply this dish, and every curry in the house, indefinitely — and unlike almost everything else in the file, dried curry leaves are genuinely a poor substitute for fresh, so growing it is not a saving so much as the only way to have the real ingredient at all.","howThisFeels":"Digging through a mountain of hot black leaves with your fingers to find the chicken, and eating half the leaves on the way.","leftovers":["(easy) The chicken cold, straight from the fridge, which is genuinely excellent and sidesteps the leaf problem entirely.","(easy) Reheated in a 200°C oven for 8 minutes with a fresh handful of leaves fried while it warms — two minutes of work and it is the whole dish again.","(medium) The meat stripped off the bones and stirred through fried rice with any surviving leaves crushed in at the end, where they behave like a spice rather than a texture.","(creative) Leftover leaves, even soft ones, blitzed with salt into a coarse green powder and kept in a jar — it is an intense curry-leaf seasoning for eggs, rice or roast vegetables, and it turns the one component that cannot be stored into the one that keeps longest."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Ayam Tangkap Rumahan — Shallow Pan, Fewer Leaves (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"A smaller pile, a shallower pan, and the same startling smell filling the whole house.","trivia":"The two costs in this dish are the two things nobody thinks of as costs: the oil and the leaves. Between them they come to more than the chicken does, which is an unusual sentence about a meat dish and is worth knowing because it changes what you would sensibly cut. Frying oil at full depth is close to a quarter of the plate here, and curry leaves are among the priciest ingredients per gram in the whole book. ✅ So this fork cuts both, and it is honest that cutting the leaves is the real loss — with 10g instead of 25g you have fried chicken with crisp leaves on it, which is very good, rather than a plate of leaves with chicken in it, which is the actual dish. The oil is the easier cut and costs less in quality, provided you accept the trade that comes with a shallow pan: less thermal stability, so fewer pieces at a time and a wait between batches. **Money for attention**, the same bargain the Mendoan card makes, and the same reason street versions of fried food are deep and home versions are shallow everywhere in the world.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"400ml sunflower oil","to":"150ml sunflower oil"},{"from":"200g chicken pieces","to":"150g chicken pieces"},{"from":"25g curry leaves","to":"10g curry leaves"}],"addStep":[{"text":"150ml of oil in the widest pan you own rather than 400ml in a deep one, less chicken, and 10g of leaves. ✅ Width buys back some of the thermal stability that depth was providing, so use the biggest pan available and fry the chicken in three small batches rather than two, letting the oil recover between them — you will hear it come back to a proper vigorous sizzle. ⚠️ Turn the chicken more often, three or four times rather than once, because only part of each piece is submerged at any moment. ⛔ The leaves do NOT get an easier ride in a shallow pan and this is the one place to be careful rather than economical: a smaller volume of oil is more violent when water hits it, not less, because the temperature swing is sharper. Same rules, more strictly — bone dry, a very small pinch at a time, and stand back. ✅ Fry them in four tiny batches rather than two. And keep the full 5g of salt despite the smaller portion; a leaner plate needs the seasoning more, not less."}]}},{"name":"Ayam Tangkap Aceh — Buried in Curry Leaves","icon":"🌿","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":67,"howThisFeels":"Setting down a bowl where the chicken is completely invisible and watching somebody work out that they are supposed to eat the pile.","trivia":"The name is the strangest thing about the dish and nobody can settle it. Tangkap means caught, or seized, so ayam tangkap is the caught chicken — and there are at least three explanations in circulation. One says it refers to a chicken caught and cooked at short notice for an unexpected guest, which is the sort of hospitality story every cuisine keeps. Another says the pieces are cut small precisely so that a bird could be dispatched, chopped and fried before the visitor got bored, making the name a comment on speed. The most enjoyable version claims the pile of leaves is the point of the name: the chicken has been hidden in the undergrowth, and eating the dish is the act of catching it. ✅ There is no way to settle which is right, and the honest thing is to say so rather than pick the prettiest — but the third one is the only one that describes what is actually in front of you, which is a bird you genuinely have to hunt for with your fingers.","delta":null},{"name":"Ayam Tangkap Temurui Penuh — Double the Leaves","icon":"🍃","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":84,"howThisFeels":"A bowl that looks like someone emptied a hedge into it, and the first fistful telling you it was exactly right.","trivia":"Fifty grams of curry leaves for one portion sounds like a joke and it is roughly what a good Acehnese warung actually serves, which raises the obvious question: why is a herb edible in that quantity when almost none of them are? The answer is that frying changes what the leaf IS. Raw curry leaf is tough, waxy and faintly bitter, and nobody eats fifty grams of it. Fried, the water is driven off in seconds and what remains is essentially a wafer — brittle, weightless, and carrying a concentrated version of the aromatic oils that were always there. ✅ Crucially the bitterness drops away with the moisture while the aroma stays, so the flavour goes UP as the harshness goes down, which is not how most ingredients behave when you cook them hard. It is the same reason a fried sage leaf is pleasant and a raw one is medicinal. The volume you can comfortably eat is set by texture rather than by flavour, and frying resets the texture completely.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"20g shallots"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"25g curry leaves","to":"50g curry leaves"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Double the curry leaves to 50g and add a further 20g of shallots, sliced thin, so the pile carries sweetness and a second texture as well as volume. ⚠️ The drying step is now the long pole in the recipe and it must not be rushed — 50g of leaves spread in a single layer needs a good hour, and two layers stacked on a tray will not dry properly at all, so use two trays. ⛔ More leaves means MORE batches, not bigger ones. Six or seven small handfuls, never three big ones: a large handful drops the oil temperature, and leaves fried in cooling oil go leathery instead of brittle, which is the failure this version is most likely to hit. ✅ Give the oil twenty seconds to recover between batches and listen for the sizzle to come back sharp. ✅ Fry the shallots last of all and lift them out pale — with this much else in the bowl they are easy to forget and they burn faster than anything else in the pan. ⚠️ Salt the pile lightly from a height once it is assembled rather than salting the leaves in the pan; salt sprinkled onto hot fried leaves draws moisture straight back out of the air and softens them."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Ayam Goreng Kalasan","target":"indonesia-ayam-goreng-kalasan","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Nasi Uduk","target":"indonesia-nasi-uduk","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Sambal Terasi","target":"indonesia-sambal-terasi","emoji":"🌶️"}]},
{"id":"indonesia-dendeng-balado","name":"Dendeng Balado","nameAlt":"Beef beaten to a sheet, dried, fried to a shatter, buried in chilli","aliases":["Dendeng Balado","Dendeng Batokok","Dendeng Balado Padang","Crispy Beef Balado","Minangkabau Crispy Beef","Dendeng","Beef Dendeng"],"course":"main","type":["main","beef","fried","dried","spiced","street-food","special"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"southeast-asia","country":"Indonesia","occasion":["family","guests","special","weekend","sunday"],"ingredients":"300ml sunflower oil · 150g beef · 40g red chilli · 25g shallots · 20g tomatoes · 10g garlic · 10ml lime juice · 5g coriander seeds · 5g salt · 4g jaggery · 2 bay leaves","method":"⚖️ YOU ARE NOT TENDERISING THIS BEEF. YOU ARE CHANGING ITS GEOMETRY SO THAT IT CAN DRY. That distinction is the whole card, and it is worth getting straight before you pick up a mallet, because the instinct when you flatten meat is that you are breaking fibres to make it soft. Here the fibres are almost beside the point. What matters is **thickness**, because drying is limited entirely by how far water has to travel to reach a surface. Water in the middle of a 10mm slice has a long, slow journey and will never all get out — the outside case-hardens first and traps the rest inside. Water in the middle of a 2mm sheet is already practically at the surface. ✅ Beat it thin enough and the whole piece can dry through; leave it thick and it cannot, no matter how long you give it.\n\n⚖️ WHICH MAKES THIS THE EXACT OPPOSITE OF SATE KLATHAK, AND THE PAIR IS WORTH READING TOGETHER. That card cuts beef into deliberately enormous 3 to 4cm chunks, because a steel skewer delivers heat into the middle and the centre is being cooked from the inside. There is no help from inside here — nothing is conducting anything into the middle of this meat. **So the only way to deal with the centre is to abolish it.** Two cards, opposite instructions, same underlying question: how far does heat, or water, have to travel.\n\nSLICE ACROSS THE GRAIN, AS THIN AS YOU CAN, THEN BEAT IT THINNER. Put the 150g of beef in the freezer for 20 minutes first — a firm, part-frozen piece slices far more evenly than a soft one, and evenness matters more here than in almost any other recipe on the shelf. Cut it **across** the grain into slices about 5mm thick. ⚠️ Across, not along: cutting across the grain leaves short fibres, and short fibres are what allow the finished sheet to shatter cleanly under a tooth. Cut it lengthways and you get strings that bend and pull instead of breaking.\n\nNOW BEAT EACH SLICE TO ABOUT 2mm — AND USE THE FLAT SIDE. Lay the slices between two sheets of baking paper or inside a bag, and work them out from the middle with the **flat** face of a mallet, a rolling pin or the base of a small pan. ⛔ Not the spiked side of a tenderiser. Those spikes are designed to sever fibres and they will punch the sheet full of holes, and a holed sheet tears apart in the oil and drops half of itself to the bottom of the pan. ✅ You want an intact, even, slightly translucent sheet — hold one up and you should just about see the shape of your hand through it. Uneven sheets are the single commonest failure: the thin parts burn while the thick parts are still leathery, in the same pan, in the same forty seconds.\n\nSEASON, THEN DRY — AND THIS IS THE STEP PEOPLE SKIP. Pound the 10g of garlic, the 5g of coriander seeds toasted dry for a minute first, the 5g of salt and the 4g of jaggery to a paste with the 10ml of lime juice, and rub it into the sheets. Leave 30 minutes. Then dry them: ✅ spread them in a single layer, not touching, on a rack, and give them **2 to 3 hours in a low oven at 80°C with the door propped slightly open**, or a full day in dry moving air, or a couple of hours in genuine hot Highveld sun with the rack raised and covered in mesh. ✅ They are ready when they feel dry and stiff to the touch, darkened, and bend slightly rather than flopping — not brittle yet, that comes in the pan.\n\n⛔ AND DO NOT SKIP STRAIGHT TO FRYING A WET MARINATED SHEET, WHICH IS WHAT MOST HOME ATTEMPTS DO. A wet sheet in hot oil does not fry, it boils: the water leaving the surface holds it at 100°C, it goes grey and leathery rather than crisp, and it will never recover. ⚖️ Ayam Tangkap's card is built on the same physics from the other side — a leaf can be dried IN the pan because it is a sheet with no interior, so its water is gone in ten seconds. **Meat has an interior, so the drying has to happen before the pan rather than in it.** That is the entire reason this recipe has a three-hour step in the middle of it.\n\nMAKE THE BALADO WHILE IT DRIES, AND REDUCE IT PROPERLY. Pound the 40g of red chilli, the 25g of shallots and the 20g of tomatoes to a coarse paste — coarse, so it stays a relish rather than becoming a sauce. Fry it in 3 tablespoons of the oil over medium heat for 8 to 10 minutes with the 2 bay leaves, stirring, until it darkens, smells sweet rather than sharp, and — the cue that matters — **the oil visibly separates and pools red at the edges of the pan.** ⚠️ That separation is not a garnish, it is the doneness signal: it means the water has gone out of the paste, and a watery sambal is what turns a crisp sheet soggy in under a minute. ⚖️ Ayam Pop's card owns the argument for cooking a chilli sambal rather than serving it raw, and it is not repeated here.\n\nFRY THE SHEETS — 40 TO 60 SECONDS, AND WATCH THEM. Heat the remaining oil to 180°C, properly hot. Slide in one or two sheets at a time, no more; they need room, and a crowded pan drops the temperature and gives you the leathery failure again. ✅ They will bubble hard for about twenty seconds, then the bubbling drops away sharply — that is the last of the water going — and they will darken and stiffen within a few seconds after that. Lift them out onto a rack the moment the fierce bubbling stops. ⛔ Ten seconds too long and beef this thin goes bitter and black, and unlike a thick cut there is no forgiving margin at all.\n\n✅ THE TEST IS THAT IT SHATTERS, NOT THAT IT BENDS. Let one cool for a minute and snap it. A properly made sheet breaks cleanly with an audible crack and splinters at the edge. If it bends, folds or chews, it went into the oil damp — the drying was short, not the frying. ⚠️ Do not fix it by frying it longer, which only burns the outside. Put the rest back to dry for another hour and try again.\n\nCOAT IT AT THE VERY LAST SECOND AND CARRY IT STRAIGHT TO THE TABLE. Take the balado off the heat, tip in the fried sheets, and turn them through it two or three times — no more. ⚠️ You have perhaps five minutes before the moisture in the sambal starts working into the beef and softening it, which is why this is assembled as people sit down rather than held warm. ✅ Some cooks serve the sambal in a separate bowl for exactly this reason and it is a perfectly legitimate way to eat it, though the dish is named for the coating and something is lost. **Balado means it wears the chilli. Serve it wearing the chilli, and serve it fast.**","cookTime":"Slice + beat 30 min · Marinate 30 min · Dry 2–3 hrs · Sambal 10 min · Fry 5 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 36g · Carbs 11g · Fat 33g · Sodium ~1750mg","storage":"This is a dish with a split personality in the fridge and it is worth knowing which half you are storing. ⛔ Once coated it is at its best for about five minutes and is soft within the hour — coated leftovers keep 2 days and are perfectly pleasant, but they are chewy-savoury rather than crisp and there is no bringing the shatter back. ✅ **Stored UNCOATED, the fried sheets are a different proposition entirely**: completely cool, in a genuinely airtight jar with the lid on tight, they hold their crispness for a week at room temperature and are one of the best things in the house to find at ten at night. ⚠️ Completely cool is not a suggestion — a jar closed on warm beef sweats and the whole batch goes limp by morning. ✅ The balado sambal keeps 2 weeks in the fridge under a film of its own oil and freezes for 3 months, so the sensible way to run this dish is to keep the two halves apart and marry them a plateful at a time. ✅ Undried, un-fried marinated sheets freeze well for 2 months laid flat between sheets of paper, and can go straight into the drying oven from frozen. ⚠️ Do not refrigerate the fried sheets — a fridge is a damp place and they soften faster in there than they do on the counter.","chefNotes":"You are not tenderising the beef, you are changing its geometry so it can dry — drying is limited by how far water has to travel, so a 2mm sheet can lose all of it and a 10mm slice never will. Slice ACROSS the grain (short fibres shatter, long fibres bend), part-freeze it first for even slices, and beat it with the FLAT face, never the spiked side, which holes the sheet so it tears in the oil. Then dry it properly — 2 to 3 hours at 80°C with the door ajar — because a wet sheet in hot oil boils rather than fries and goes grey and leathery for good. Reduce the balado until the oil visibly separates: a watery sambal sogs a crisp sheet in under a minute. Fry 40 to 60 seconds, one or two at a time, and lift them out the moment the fierce bubbling stops. The test is that it SHATTERS. If it bends, the drying was short — fry it longer and you only burn it. Coat at the last second and serve immediately.","pairsWith":"Hot white rice, and quite a lot of it, because this is intense, salty, chilli-hot and dry, and rice is the thing that makes it a meal rather than a very good snack. ✅ Something cool and wet on the side earns its place too — cucumber, a simple pickle, or a plain boiled green — since every other element on the plate is concentrated. In Padang it turns up on the stacked-plate table alongside rendang and gulai, and it plays the crisp part in a spread where almost everything else is soft and saucy, which is exactly the role to give it at home. ⛔ Do not put it beside another crisp fried thing; it will lose. ⛔ And do not serve it with a wet gravy poured over — the entire point is a sheet that breaks under a tooth, and any liquid beyond its own clinging sambal undoes three hours of drying in about ninety seconds.","goesWith":["Nasi Uduk","Acar Kuning","Plecing Kangkung","Rendang"],"trivia":"Almost every culture that ever had more meat than it could eat in a week arrived independently at the same answer, and the differences between their answers are more interesting than the similarity.\n\nDried meat turns up as biltong, as jerky, as charqui in the Andes, as pastirma across the eastern Mediterranean, as bakkwa in southern China, and as dendeng here — and none of them borrowed it from the others. They are convergent inventions, because the underlying problem is universal and the physics only permits one solution. What differs is how each one gets there: some cure with salt and hang in moving air, some use vinegar, some press and smoke, some sweeten heavily. ✅ Dendeng is one of the few that adds a second act — it is dried like the others, and then it is **fried**, which no other member of the family does as standard, and that is what turns a chewy preserved food into something that shatters.\n\n⚖️ AND THE SCIENCE UNDERNEATH IS NOT WHAT MOST PEOPLE ASSUME. Salt and sugar do not preserve food by poisoning bacteria. They preserve it by taking the **water** away from them. Microbes cannot use water that is chemically bound to something else, and both salt and sugar are extremely good at binding it — so what actually matters is a measure called water activity, the amount of water still free to be used, on a scale where pure water is 1.0. ✅ Below roughly 0.85, essentially nothing that causes food to spoil can grow, no matter how much total water is technically present. That single number explains an enormous amount: why honey never goes off, why a fruitcake keeps for a year, why jam is safe until you open it, and why a heavily sweetened dendeng is exactly as safe as a heavily salted one despite tasting nothing alike. It also explains the failure mode — a sheet that was left too thick is dry on the outside and above 0.85 in the middle, which is precisely where things go wrong.\n\nWhich is why the beating matters as much as it does. Every millimetre you take off the sheet is water activity you can actually reach.\n\n🌱 The nudge here is a tree, and it is the one that pays back longest. There is lime juice in the marinade and lime turns up across this entire cuisine, and citrus does genuinely well in Pretoria — the Highveld gets the cold winter nights that citrus needs to colour and sweeten properly, and the summers are long enough to ripen fruit. ✅ A lime or lemon tree in a large pot, or in a sunny sheltered corner with a wall behind it, will fruit within a few years and then keep going for decades. ⚠️ The one thing to get right is frost while it is young: the first two or three winters are when a citrus dies, so plant it where it gets morning sun and shelter, or keep it potted and move it, and mulch the roots. ⚠️ And go for a grafted tree from a nursery rather than growing from a pip — a pip takes the better part of a decade and often does not come true. ✅ After that it is close to free fruit forever, and there is no ingredient in this cuisine that a lime does not improve.","howThisFeels":"Snapping a piece of beef in half and hearing it crack like a biscuit, which is not a sound meat is supposed to make.","leftovers":["(easy) Uncoated sheets straight from the jar as a snack with a cold drink — a week of crispness makes this the most useful leftover in the file.","(easy) Crumbled over hot rice with a spoonful of the kept sambal, which needs no cooking at all and is a full lunch.","(medium) Broken into shards through a sharp raw salad of cucumber, shallot and lime, where it behaves like a crouton that happens to be beef.","(creative) Blitzed to a coarse rubble with a little of the dried sambal and kept as a savoury dust for eggs, noodles or roast vegetables — softened leftovers that had lost their crunch are perfect for this, so nothing is ever wasted."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Dendeng Rumahan — Shallow Pan, Less Chilli (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":29,"howThisFeels":"Three hours of doing nothing, five minutes of doing everything, and a plate that cost less than it has any right to.","trivia":"This is a dish built to make a small amount of meat go a long way, and beating it thin is the oldest trick in that book — 100g of beef spread out to 2mm covers a startling amount of plate, and because it is intense and crisp rather than soft and mild, a small quantity satisfies in a way the same 100g in a stew would not. ✅ That is worth understanding as a principle rather than a saving: concentrated, crunchy, strongly seasoned food eats bigger than its weight, which is why almost every cuisine with a history of scarcity has a crisp salty preserved thing in it. The chilli is the other lever, and it is a real one here — 40g for a single portion is a genuinely large amount of fresh chilli and it is one of the more expensive lines on the card. Cutting it to 25g still gives you a proper balado; it simply gives you one that a cautious eater can finish.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"5g coriander seeds"},{"item":"4g jaggery"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef","to":"100g beef"},{"from":"300ml sunflower oil","to":"120ml sunflower oil"},{"from":"40g red chilli","to":"25g red chilli"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Less beef, less chilli, a shallow pan, and no coriander or palm sugar in the marinade — so it is garlic, salt and lime, which is a perfectly honest dendeng cure. ⚠️ Losing the jaggery costs you a little of the dark caramel edge AND a little of the preservation, so with this version keep the finished sheets in the jar for four or five days rather than a week. ✅ The shallow pan is the real change: 120ml of oil in a wide frying pan will fry a 2mm sheet perfectly well because there is almost nothing to it, but you must do them **one at a time** and let the oil come back between each, and turn each sheet once halfway rather than leaving it. ⛔ Do not be tempted to shorten the drying to save time — that is the one step with no substitute, it costs nothing but patience, and it is what separates this dish from a piece of fried beef. ✅ If anything, dry it slightly longer here: less sugar in the cure means slightly more free water left in the sheet."}]}},{"name":"Dendeng Balado — Beaten Thin, Dried, Fried, Coated","icon":"🥩","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":49,"howThisFeels":"Holding a slice of raw beef up to the window and being able to see daylight through it.","trivia":"There is a close cousin of this dish that takes the opposite decision at the last moment, and the pair of them are the clearest lesson in Minangkabau cooking you can get from two recipes. Dendeng batokok is beaten too — batokok means beaten — but it is grilled rather than dried and deep-fried, so it stays thick, chewy and juicy, and it is usually served under a green chilli sambal instead of a red one. Same cut, same mallet, same region, and a completely different eating experience: one shatters, one yields. ✅ Which tells you that the beating is genuinely not about tenderness in either dish, because if it were, the two would taste much the same. The mallet is doing something structural, and each cook then decides whether to chase crispness or chase juice. You cannot have both, and pretending otherwise is how people end up with beef that is neither.","delta":null},{"name":"Dendeng Kering — Double-Fried for the Jar","icon":"🫙","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":56,"howThisFeels":"A jar on the shelf that everyone in the house knows about and pretends not to.","trivia":"Frying something twice to make it keep sounds like a contradiction and is not, and the reason is the same water activity number as before. The first fry drives off the free water at the surface and in the outer layers; a rest lets the remaining moisture in the very centre migrate outward and even itself out, which it will always do given twenty minutes; and the second, shorter, cooler fry removes that redistributed water too. ✅ One long fry cannot achieve the same thing, because the outside burns long before the middle has had time to move its water anywhere. It is exactly the mechanism behind twice-cooked chips, applied to meat. ⚠️ What you are making at that point is genuinely shelf-stable in a way the single-fried version is not, and it stops being a dish and becomes a store-cupboard thing — which is what dendeng was for in the first place, long before anyone put a fresh chilli sambal on it and served it hot.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"10g red chilli"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef","to":"200g beef"}],"addStep":[{"text":"More beef, a little more chilli, and the sheets are fried twice so they keep properly. ✅ Fry them the first time at 180°C for 30 to 40 seconds — slightly SHORTER than the single-fry version, since they are going back in — and lift them onto a rack. ⚠️ Then rest them a full 20 minutes and do not rush it: that pause is the whole mechanism, and it is when the moisture still sitting in the centre migrates outward and spreads itself evenly through the sheet where the second fry can reach it. ✅ Drop the oil to about 160°C and fry them again for 30 seconds, until they are a shade darker and completely rigid. They should feel weightless. ⛔ Do not coat these ones. Cool them completely — completely — and put them into a properly airtight jar, where they will keep two to three weeks rather than one. ✅ Serve the balado sambal alongside in a small bowl and let people dip a piece at a time, so the jar stays crisp and only what is being eaten ever gets wet."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Rendang","target":"indonesia-rendang","emoji":"🥘"},{"name":"Ayam Pop","target":"indonesia-ayam-pop","emoji":"🍗"},{"name":"Acar Kuning","target":"indonesia-acar-kuning","emoji":"🥒"}]}
];
